{"parentUuid":"214450d0-f220-47bf-8c00-90d11127ae2d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"fe3259f3-06b4-4656-9c7c-f91ae09159c7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LPMXMWWQKoBJAGecdjDEa4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t004 [D] Sunk-Cost & Anchoring Bias Detector in Disruption Recovery (`detect_mi :: `base-research.md` (Section 1.3) highlights how **Sunk-Cost Escalation** and **Anchoring Bias** derail engineering projects: teams and agents keep pouring iterations into a failing approach because they already invested checkpoints into it, rather than re-planning (`[1/M']`). In `AIKit`, `scripts/disruption_recovery.py` already \n2\t008 [D] Declarative 3-Zone Self-Modification Constitution (`forbidden` / `prop :: In `syntra_kernel` (`artifacts/self_mod_policy.toml`, `src/agi_core/self_mod_policy.rs`, `src/utilities/self_mod_gate.rs`, and `src/utilities/baseline_snapshot.rs`), repository paths are partitioned into three explicit governance zones for autonomous agents: 1. `forbidden`: Files an autonomous agent must never modify without exp\n3\t026 [D] Pre-Composed Cognitive Package Blueprints (`manifest` + Domain Knowled :: In `melasistema/intentio`, `packages/` (`hook_analyzer`, `product_pitch_lab`, `cartoon_universe`) solve the \"blank canvas\" problem for domain workspaces: instead of initializing an empty directory, `./intentio init <package>` deploys an opinionated **Knowledge Package** containing: 1. A declarative `manifest` (`name`, `version`,\n4\t028 [D] Anti-Drift Compaction Validator, Role-Attribution Guard & 40-Word Sess :: Vendor compaction commands run a tool-free analysis then emit a fixed-section summary that keeps user security constraints verbatim and never credits assistant-quoted `user: ...` text to the real user. A separate recap prompt yields under 40 words: goal, current task, one next action. This stops two failure modes of context comp\n5\t035 [D] Weighted Rule-Quality Rubric with Stub-Text Detection & Minimum-Score  :: Each rule file is scored on weighted dimensions (specific prompts, sources, examples, verification section, pass/fail thresholds, exceptions when nuanced) and regex checks reject generic stub wording. Files under a minimum score are excluded from consumption. AIKit's `.aikit/guidelines.md` check only validates well-formedness, n\n6\t036 [D] Violation Baseline Ratchet (Legacy Debt Tolerated, Monotonic Decrease  :: A committed baseline JSON records known violations per file so a new strict check can be switched on without a flag day: old violations pass, new ones fail. The same idea appears as an expected-failure list for an external conformance suite. This lets AIKit tighten a gate on existing adopter projects incrementally.\n7\t042 [D] Shortcut-Debt Ledger (shortcut: ceiling, trigger markers; no-trigger r :: A deliberate shortcut is marked with a comment naming its limit and when to upgrade it. A one-shot scan collects every marker into rows (`file:line, what, ceiling, upgrade`) and tags markers without an upgrade trigger as `no-trigger`. This turns \"unnamed suppression\" bans (PRINCIPLE-007 R3) into something positive: a shortcut is\n8\t043 [R] Anchored Invariant-Phrase Canary for Generated Rule Copies :: One canonical rule text feeds many host adapters; a script compares copies after normalizing frontmatter and CRLF, and also asserts a list of load-bearing phrases is present in the longer source of truth. AIKit generates `AGENTS.md`/`GEMINI.md` capsules by compression, so the risk is a compression that silently drops a binding c\n9\t053 [D] Skill / Tool Description Linter (Trigger Phrase, Constraints, Examples :: The guide recommends specific tool descriptions that say when to use the tool, enum-constrained parameters, and examples. AIKit's skills (`SKILL.md` frontmatter `description`) are selected by an agent from the description alone, so a vague description means the skill never triggers. A linter makes that checkable.\n10\t065 [D] CRLF & Control-Character Log Injection Shield (`log_safe`) :: User inputs, external tool outputs, and network headers written to terminal outputs or log streams can carry `\\r` and `\\n` characters, allowing attackers to forge log lines (e.g. inject fake `[OK] Checkpoint passed` entries) or ANSI terminal escape sequences. `log_safe` escapes control characters, replaces CRLF with literal `\\r`\n11\t069 [R] Multithreaded Subprocess Spawn Safety (`posix_spawn` Eligibility Helpe :: When running Python subprocesses in multithreaded tools or background workers, CPython's standard `fork()` implementation can deadlock or crash with SIGSEGV (signal 11) if background threads hold internal libc/system framework locks. CPython switches to the crash-safe `posix_spawn` system call only when specific criteria are met\n12\t070 [R] Pair-Safe, Anchor-Durable Compaction Planner with Fold-Ratio Gate :: A compaction pass chooses the first message to keep verbatim, then replaces everything before it with a summary. Naive index-based cutting silently drifts as the transcript grows, cuts between an assistant tool call and its result, or leaves an orphaned tool result whose owning call was folded away. Agno's planner is pure over a\n13\t071 [D] Backend-Representability Path Guard with Durable Atomic Writes :: Two different path normalizations on one path are a security bug: a grammar that accepts `ａ．` or `a.` and an on-disk map that NFKC-folds and rstrips dots will silently collapse distinct legal names, and a fullwidth-dot sequence can fold into `..` after the grammar has already approved it. Agno validates a strict grammar (NFC, no\n14\t072 [D] Isolated Regex Scan Subprocess with Hard Deadline and Position-Only IP :: `re` has no timeout, and a thread stuck in the regex engine cannot be interrupted. A user- or model-supplied pattern such as `(a+)+$` against a long line can hold the GIL and wedge the whole process, including the gate that was supposed to protect the workspace. Agno runs the scan in a child process launched as `python -I <scrip\n15\t077 [D] Search-Cost Knowledge-Map Optimizer with Published Complexity Metrics :: AIKit's generated codebase map is a tree of modules that both humans and agents navigate, but nothing says whether a given level of nesting is worth its cost. PageIndex prices navigation explicitly: reading a collapsed node costs its whole span `S(v)`; routing through an expanded node costs `R(v) = 1` plus the most expensive bra\n16\t078 [D] Assign-Once Byte-Budget Filename Pipeline :: Repositories, tags and export bundles are named from user- or model-supplied text, and every operating system disagrees about what a legal name is. PageIndex separates the problem into two functions with different jobs: `sanitize_filename` runs **once**, before a storage key is assigned, and may replace illegal characters, fold \n17\t079 [D] Bounded Range-Set Selector Grammar with Canonical Re-Compression :: CLI selectors such as \"lines 1-3,7\" or \"gates test,hygiene\" are user input that is frequently malformed and occasionally hostile: `1-1000000000` materializes a billion integers inside the caller before any downstream cap is reached. PageIndex parses one grammar shared by the SDK and the tool layer, validates the whole string wit\n18\t084 [D] Generated-Artifact Provenance Gate with Volatile-Field Projection :: AIKit already generates its codebase map and knowledge base and already has `update_knowledge_base.py --check`. What it lacks is the general rule the source establishes: **a derived artifact must be regenerable and must prove it is current**, and the comparison must be insensitive to fields that legitimately change on every run.\n19\t085 [D] Pairwise Description-Collision Detector with Review-Allowlist Reasons :: AIKit's skills, rules and commands are chosen by an agent from their descriptions, so two descriptions that compete for the same request cause silent misrouting — and no per-item linter can see it, because each description is individually valid. The source compares every pair: TF-IDF vectors over the description plus slug tokens\n20\t086 [D] Canonical Parser with an AST-Based Single-Parser Guard :: The source's most expensive bug was not a missing feature: three independent hand-rolled \"YAML-ish\" parsers each accepted a different subset of scalar styles and silently truncated 604 of 817 descriptions to their first line, with no error and no warning. The durable fix has three parts. First, **one canonical reader** owns extr\n21\t091 [D] Fail-Closed Change-Record Classifier with Object-Resolved Sizes and Di :: AIKit gates work by looking at the working tree, not at a described change set. That is fine locally and wrong for anything that must be justified later: a path can be spelled two ways, a mode can change silently (`100644` → `120000` makes a file a symlink), a diff can claim a small size for a large blob, and a generated directo\n22\t092 [D] Impact-Scoped Validation Plan with Policy Digest and Exact Receipt Ver :: Running every gate on every change is safe and slow; running a subset chosen by intuition is fast and unsound. The source makes the subset itself a reviewed artifact. A versioned policy file declares which path classes exist (`catalog`, `editorial`, `runtime`, `web`), which files and prefixes belong to each, which checks each cl\n23\t098 [D] Layered Settings Resolver with Per-Key Merge Policy, Unoverridable Den :: AIKit already layers configuration — repository `kilo.json`, a project `.aikit/project.json`, the global config directory, and command-line flags — but the precedence is expressed only by the order in which the code happens to read the files. The source shows both the model and its sharp edges. A resolver should (a) walk a decla\n24\t104 [D] Destructive-Command Coverage Matrix for Subprocess Call Sites :: AIKit shells out in roughly a dozen places (git, tests, gates, hooks). Each call site is a place where a path, a branch name or a user-supplied selector can become an argument to a destructive command. A settings-level `ask` list — the source's approach — protects the *interactive* surface only, matches command text by prefix, a\n25\t105 [R] Turn-Scoped Search-Evidence Gate for First-Touch Edits :: An agent that edits a file it has never looked at is guessing. Telling it not to guess is advisory; a gate that requires *evidence gathered in this turn* is not. The source implements that gate for a single harness and documents every decision behind it. A first-touch edit is denied unless the current turn contains a completed t\n26\t110 [D] Harness Adapter Capability Registry with Compliance Scorecard Gate :: AIKit targets several agent runtimes. Each has a different config location, a different notion of \"project\" vs \"user\", and a different set of hook or permission features. Without one table, each adapter's support is discovered by reading its code, and the differences become silent: an adapter simply does nothing for an event the\n27\t112 [D] Fixpoint Identifier Normalization as a Single Source of Truth :: AIKit mints identifiers in several places — project names in `project.json`, gate ids, ledger task ids, map node ids, skill ids — and each place normalizes slightly differently. The failure mode is subtle and expensive: two spellings of one entity become two entities, or one entity becomes unreachable under the spelling a user t\n28\t113 [D] Reverse Blast-Radius Traversal with a Declared Relation Contract :: Before a change, the useful question is \"what will this break?\" — and the useful answer is a list of consumers with a path, not a count. The source answers it by walking **incoming** edges over a declared relation set, seeding from a file or symbol query, and reporting for each hit the depth, the relation traversed, and the **ed\n29\t115 [D] Stable Community Identity Across Recomputation :: AIKit's map groups modules into areas, and recomputing that grouping is where a navigational aid quietly becomes noise: if cluster ids and names reshuffle on every run, every link, ADR and handoff that referenced \"area 7\" rots, and a reviewer cannot tell a real reorganisation from a numbering change. The source treats cluster id\n30\t119 [D] Calibrated Token Bound with Offline Oracle and Ratio Margin :: AIKit enforces token and character budgets on what it sends to a model (`PRINCIPLE-012`), but it counts with a character heuristic and treats that number as if it were the model's count. The source shows why that is unsound and what it costs: two tokenizers disagree by roughly ±2%, the sign depends on content, and an under-count\n31\t125 [D] Singleton-Access Boundary Enforced by AST with Justified Allowlist :: Hidden global state is what makes a codebase untestable and its behaviour order-dependent, and it usually arrives one convenient import at a time. The source fixes it structurally. Process-wide singletons (database handle, document engine, cache client) are resolved **once at the assembly points** and passed down through an expl\n32\t126 [D] Injective Canonical Payload Framing for Signed Records :: Everything AIKit signs, hashes or compares depends on one hidden assumption: that two different logical records never serialise to the same bytes. The source violates that assumption twice in the same module. `hash_state(parts)` joins components with `|` and hashes the result, so `[\"a|b\", \"c\"]` and `[\"a\", \"b|c\"]` collide — and t\n33\t133 [D] Projection-Recorded-As-Event with Coverage Precedence and Rebuild :: AIKit keeps derived state next to its canonical state: the codebase map, the knowledge base, area labels and handoff summaries are all computed from sources of truth. Today a derived file that is damaged or stale is either regenerated from scratch or trusted because it exists; there is no record of *which* projection the project\n34\t138 [D] Unknown-Commit-Outcome Signal for Atomic Writes :: AIKit's atomic-write rule says temp file, fsync, rename. The part that is easy to get wrong is what