nd nothing verifies that it was. GAAI closes the gap with three small pieces: the plan declares an `impl_model` per story; the wrapper that actually invokes the model logs the **observed** tag and provider per phase into an append-only JSONL; and a pre-push hook com\n40\t158 [D] Commit-Pinned Remote Module Sync with Local-Edit Guard :: aiFlows' FlowVerse loader resolves a repository and revision to a concrete commit hash, caches the module under that hash, and refuses to overwrite a sync directory that the user has modified. The pattern turns a mutable remote dependency into an auditable pin: the same revision can be re-resolved, a changed commit is visible, a\n41\t159 [R] Declarative Key-Transform Pipeline for Imported Records :: aiFlows composes data shaping from small named operations (`key_select`, `key_rename`, `key_set`, `key_delete`, `key_copy`, `key_match_input`) instead of embedding one-off Python. For a registry that imports records from many external ledgers, the same idea gives a reviewed, testable mapping file: each import declares which keys\n42\t162 [D] Trace DAG Normalization with Bounded Summaries :: The source turns raw ADK events into a stable graph: one model node, one node per tool event, edges in call order, and short input/output summaries. The value for AIKit is a portable execution trace for dogfood and subagent runs: a reviewer can see *what happened* without reading raw payloads, and the trace can be diffed across \n43\t163 [R] Visible Tool-Plan Intent Strings without Raw Arguments :: The source translates a tool call into a human sentence (`\"capture_visual_crop\"` becomes \"zoom into region (x,y)\"), so a user can follow an autonomous loop without reading tool JSON. For AIKit's subagent and dogfood surfaces, a stable intent layer makes progress messages reviewable and keeps raw arguments, paths and secrets out \n44\t176 [D] Quality-Delta Gate with Fix-or-Justify Acknowledgements :: The source checks a change for twelve quality kinds and reports *only what got worse*: duplication, a new private copy of a shared utility, a sign-flipped near-duplicate, a function that absorbed a second job. A finding is either fixed or justified in a written acknowledgement; the acknowledgements ledger survives across tasks, \n45\t177 [D] Symbol-Pinned Notes Surfaced on Lookup with Staleness :: ripwire lets an agent leave a gotcha note pinned to a symbol; a later lookup surfaces it automatically. That is institutional memory between contexts that never met. AIKit's knowledge base is document-oriented; a symbol note attaches a short, reviewed invariant (\"this parser must reject a leading dash; the CLI turns it into a me\n46\t178 [D] Byte-Identity Determinism Gate for Generated Artefacts :: The source's strongest invariant is that the same tree produces the same bytes on every run, regardless of thread count or scheduling. It enforces this with fixed partitioning, canonical reduction order, sorted paths and a byte-diff gate. AIKit generates `MATRIX.md`, the AST map, capsule files and license registers; today a dict\n47\t179 [D] Floor-vs-Zero Evidence Contract with Refusal and Continuation :: The source refuses to let an incomplete answer look complete. A caller count is a floor because dynamic dispatch and callbacks are invisible; `0` means \"none found\", never \"none exists\"; ambiguity and unresolved names are counted; a selector that names nothing refuses with a did-you-mean instead of returning zero; every truncati\n48\t182 [D] Environment-Fingerprinted Run Manifest for Dogfood and Agent Runs :: The source stamps each QA session with a GUID, a human-sortable folder, the scene, the Unity version, the app version, a start time and a strictly increasing sequence. A run is therefore reproducible and auditable. AIKit's dogfood and subagent runs need the same: a manifest that records the command, the input digests, the Python\n49\t186 [R] Reflection Plugin Loader with Assembly/Type Allow-List :: The source builds AI components from a GUI by reflecting over assemblies and types. Reflection is powerful and dangerous: a name from a config file can load an arbitrary type or run a static initializer. A clean-room loader loads only from an explicit root, requires an entrypoint that implements a declared protocol, and checks a\n50\t192 [D] Delta-Read Cache with a Content-Fingerprint Guard :: The source remembers what an agent read and returns only the diff when the same file is read again, while a fingerprint guard catches a file that changed between the fingerprint and the read. For AIKit, a `read` helper with the same contract would reduce repeated file dumps in long sessions and, more importantly, give the agent \n51\t193 [D] Context Quality Score with Weighted Signals and Honest Unavailability :: The source grades context quality from seven signals and shows a letter grade. The reusable part is not the exact weights but the shape: each signal is measured independently, a missing signal is `unavailable` rather than zero, weights are explicit, and the grade is derived from a documented band. For AIKit this can score a task\n52\t195 [D] Savings Ledger Separating Metered and Estimated Value :: The source reports savings in three tiers and keeps metered actions separate from estimates. That honesty matters: a compressed output with a measured before/after character count is evidence, while \"repeat reads avoided\" is a model. AIKit should record every context-saving action with a tier, an evidence field and a before/afte\n53\t198 [D] Completion Distillation Packet :: Magic Context captures memory \"for free\": the historian already reads the whole history to compress it, so it lifts durable facts in the same pass instead of asking the agent to remember. AIKit has the analogue of that raw history — the task ledger already records every checkpoint with `--why --benefits --risks --mitigation`, th\n54\t199 [D] ADR Locator Verification Gate :: Magic Context's dreamer re-verifies each memory against the current codebase: it maps a memory to the files that back it, then checks paths, configs and patterns, fixing or removing what has drifted. AIKit has the same staleness problem in a sharper form. Its ADR register is 500+ KB of accepted decisions, many of which name a mo\n55\t200 [D] Machine-Evaluable Deferred Conditions :: Magic Context's `ctx_note` is a scratchpad for intentions that are not yet actionable; a note resurfaces at natural boundaries, and \"smart notes\" carry an open-ended condition the dreamer watches for. AIKit already has the seed of this: `--handoff \"next: ...; state: ...; failed: ...; open: ...\"` is parsed by `parse_handoff` and \n56\t201 [D] Deterministic Checkpoint Decay Rendering :: Magic Context renders old compartments at lower fidelity as the window tightens, using a deterministic rule rather than an LLM, so the same history always renders the same way. AIKit's `--resume` does the opposite of graceful: it prints the interrupted task, its plan, and recent checkpoints, but a long task's history either crow\n57\t204 [D] Knowledge Delta Merge with Archive :: OpenSpec's most transferable rule is that a change never edits the spec directly: it produces a delta (`ADDED`/`MODIFIED`/`REMOVED` requirements), and only archiving merges that delta into the living spec, leaving a dated copy of the delta behind. AIKit approximates this with an append-only ADR register, but ADRs are narrative d\n58\t206 [D] Explore-Before-Plan Phase Artefact :: OpenSpec separates a no-stakes `/opsx:explore` from `/opsx:propose`: exploration reads the code, weighs options and produces a recommendation without committing to a plan, and only then does a change folder get created. AIKit has an `explorer` subagent and a Phase 2 plan checkpoint, but nothing forces an option comparison before\n59\t210 [D] Progressive-Disclosure Skill Index :: Acontext's retrieval rule is that an agent should see a cheap index and fetch content by tool use, not receive everything up front. AIKit's Tier 1 budget is built on the same insight, but its skill surface does not exploit it: `.agents/skills/aikit/SKILL.md` is a single long runbook injected through the host capsule, and as more\n60\t212 [D] Hash-chained task ledger :: Each ledger entry stores `prev` and `digest`, where `digest = sha256(canonical_json({prev, index, ...entry fields}))` with sorted keys, compact separators and `ensure_ascii=True`. Verification recomputes the chain and reports the first broken index. A silent edit of an old checkpoint, plan or `--why` then fails verification inst\n61\t214 [D] Plan-scope overreach gate with path globs :: ToolReplay loads a scope file with an exact-match allow-list and flags every call outside it as `permission-overreach`. We extend the idea from tool names to **tool plus path**: the Phase 2 plan declares allowed path globs, and a gate compares the staged diff (and, where available, the tool-call transcript) with them. This enfor\n62\t215 [D] Replay-consistency lint for dogfood transcripts :: `replay.py` walks a transcript once. It keeps the first response seen for each canonical call (tool plus sorted-key args). A later identical call with a different response is `non-determinism`, and the first such index is the divergence point. An identical call and response with no mutator between them is `redundant-call`. Lines\n63\t217 [D] Rule-ablation review against harness rot :: The list highlights the ablation method for instructions: strip the prompt, add back only the lines that fix a repeated failure, and repeat at each model release. Instructions encode corrections for behaviours a model may no longer have, so they go stale. For AIKit, each Tier 1 capsule line and each principle rule gets a record \n64\t218 [R] Isolated vs forked subagent context rule :: A listed multi-agent write-up gives a decision rule. **Fork** workers that continue an in-progress investigation, so they inherit the supervisor's context. **Isolate** verifiers that must judge independently, so they start fresh and cannot be anchored by the author's reasoning. AIKit already delegates to `explorer`, `worker`, `a\n65\t220 [D] Harness checklist as an automated gate :: `templates/HARNESS_CHECKLIST.md` is a manual review list: explicit tool permissions (allowed, restricted, not allowed), verification commands that are correct, no ambiguous instructions, long-lived state in files rather than the prompt, milestones with verification commands, scope boundaries written down, and minimum permissions\n66\t222 [D] Four-type gate taxonomy with bounded revision loops :: `references/gates.md` maps every checkpoint to one of four types. **Pre-flight** blocks entry on a missing precondition with no partial work. **Revision** loops back to the producer with specific feedback under an iteration cap, and escalates early if the issue count does not fall between iterations (stall detection). **Escalati\n67\t223 [R] Adversarial verifier with mandatory severities :: Checker agents open with a \"FORCE stance\": assume the work is flawed until evidence proves otherwise. They list the ways checkers go soft (crediting intent, accepting a decision reference without checking its full scope, accepting \"v1\" scope cuts, letting six passing dimensions hide a failed seventh). Every finding must carry `B\n68\t224 [D] Machine-greppable predicate context cited by ID :: `CONTEXT.md` states operational facts as single-line predicates (`CLASS.subkey=value`). Agent briefs must cite predicates by ID and never paraphrase, and a meta rule (`brief-must-cite-doc`) enforces this. A glossary of modules and seams sits beside the predicates, and chronological notes go in a separate log at the bottom.\n69\t225 [D] Tests-only gap auditor with read-only implementation :: The \"Nyquist auditor\" receives a list of validation gaps. For each gap it writes a minimal behavioural test that can fail, runs it, debugs the test for at most 3 iterations and reports what actually happened. Implementation files are read-only: the agent may create only tests, fixtures and a validation record, and an implementat\n70\t226 [D] Requirement-to-task coverage check for plans :: The plan checker verifies a plan before execution by going backward from the goal. Every requirement must map to at least one task, tasks must actually deliver the requirement and not only reference it, dependencies must not be broken or circular, artifacts must be wired together, scope must fit the context budget, and the plan "}]},"uuid":"4aae8dc1-154c-4a35-bd6e-7e4e5d5260a6","timestamp":"2026-10-29T18:48:03.927Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\YUNUS-~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\c--Users-Yunus-Emre-Desktop-portable-agent-kit\\05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787\\scratchpad\\ak_mech.txt","content":"004 [D] Sunk-Cost & Anchoring Bias Detector in Disruption Recovery (`detect_mi :: `base-research.md` (Section 1.3) highlights how **Sunk-Cost Escalation** and **Anchoring Bias** derail engineering projects: teams and agents keep pouring iterations into a failing approach because they already invested checkpoints into it, rather than re-planning (`[1/M']`). In `AIKit`, `scripts/disruption_recovery.py` already \n008 [D] Declarative 3-Zone Self-Modification Constitution (`forbidden` / `prop :: In `syntra_kernel` (`artifacts/self_mod_policy.toml`, `src/agi_core/self_mod_policy.rs`, `src/utilities/self_mod_gate.rs`, and `src/utilities/baseline_snapshot.rs`), repository paths are partitioned into three explicit governance zones for autonomous agents: 1. `forbidden`: Files an autonomous agent must never modify without exp\n026 [D] Pre-Composed Cognitive Package Blueprints (`manifest` + Domain Knowled :: In `melasistema/intentio`, `packages/` (`hook_analyzer`, `product_pitch_lab`, `cartoon_universe`) solve the \"blank canvas\" problem for domain workspaces: instead of initializing an empty directory, `./intentio init <package>` deploys an opinionated **Knowledge Package** containing: 1. A declarative `manifest` (`name`, `version`,\n028 [D] Anti-Drift Compaction Validator, Role-Attribution Guard & 40-Word Sess :: Vendor compaction commands run a tool-free analysis then emit a fixed-section summary that keeps user security constraints verbatim and never credits assistant-quoted `user: ...` text to the real user. A separate recap prompt yields under 40 words: goal, current task, one next action. This stops two failure modes of context comp\n035 [D] Weighted Rule-Quality Rubric with Stub-Text Detection & Minimum-Score  :: Each rule file is scored on weighted dimensions (specific prompts, sources, examples, verification section, pass/fail thresholds, exceptions when nuanced) and regex checks reject generic stub wording. Files under a minimum score are excluded from consumption. AIKit's `.aikit/guidelines.md` check only validates well-formedness, n\n036 [D] Violation Baseline Ratchet (Legacy Debt Tolerated, Monotonic Decrease  :: A committed baseline JSON records known violations per file so a new strict check can be switched on without a flag day: old violations pass, new ones fail. The same idea appears as an expected-failure list for an external conformance suite. This lets AIKit tighten a gate on existing adopter projects incrementally.\n042 [D] Shortcut-Debt Ledger (shortcut: ceiling, trigger markers; no-trigger r :: A deliberate shortcut is marked with a comment naming its limit and when to upgrade it. A one-shot scan collects every marker into rows (`file:line, what, ceiling, upgrade`) and tags markers without an upgrade trigger as `no-trigger`. This turns \"unnamed suppression\" bans (PRINCIPLE-007 R3) into something positive: a shortcut is\n043 [R] Anchored Invariant-Phrase Canary for Generated Rule Copies :: One canonical rule text feeds many host adapters; a script compares copies after normalizing frontmatter and CRLF, and also asserts a list of load-bearing phrases is present in the longer source of truth. AIKit generates `AGENTS.md`/`GEMINI.md` capsules by compression, so the risk is a compression that silently drops a binding c\n053 [D] Skill / Tool Description Linter (Trigger Phrase, Constraints, Examples :: The guide recommends specific tool descriptions that say when to use the tool, enum-constrained parameters, and examples. AIKit's skills (`SKILL.md` frontmatter `description`) are selected by an agent from the description alone, so a vague description means the skill never triggers. A linter makes that checkable.\n065 [D] CRLF & Control-Character Log Injection Shield (`log_safe`) :: User inputs, external tool outputs, and network headers written to terminal outputs or log streams can carry `\\r` and `\\n` characters, allowing attackers to forge log lines (e.g. inject fake `[OK] Checkpoint passed` entries) or ANSI terminal escape sequences. `log_safe` escapes control characters, replaces CRLF with literal `\\r`\n069 [R] Multithreaded Subprocess Spawn Safety (`posix_spawn` Eligibility Helpe :: When running Python subprocesses in multithreaded tools or background workers, CPython's standard `fork()` implementation can deadlock or crash with SIGSEGV (signal 11) if background threads hold internal libc/system framework locks. CPython switches to the crash-safe `posix_spawn` system call only when specific criteria are met\n070 [R] Pair-Safe, Anchor-Durable Compaction Planner with Fold-Ratio Gate :: A compaction pass chooses the first message to keep verbatim, then replaces everything before it with a summary. Naive index-based cutting silently drifts as the transcript grows, cuts between an assistant tool call and its result, or leaves an orphaned tool result whose owning call was folded away. Agno's planner is pure over a\n071 [D] Backend-Representability Path Guard with Durable Atomic Writes :: Two different path normalizations on one path are a security bug: a grammar that accepts `ａ．` or `a.` and an on-disk map that NFKC-folds and rstrips dots will silently collapse distinct legal names, and a fullwidth-dot sequence can fold into `..` after the grammar has already approved it. Agno validates a strict grammar (NFC, no\n072 [D] Isolated Regex Scan Subprocess with Hard Deadline and Position-Only IP :: `re` has no timeout, and a thread stuck in the regex engine cannot be interrupted. A user- or model-supplied pattern such as `(a+)+$` against a long line can hold the GIL and wedge the whole process, including the gate that was supposed to protect the workspace. Agno runs the scan in a child process launched as `python -I <scrip\n077 [D] Search-Cost Knowledge-Map Optimizer with Published Complexity Metrics :: AIKit's generated codebase map is a tree of modules that both humans and agents navigate, but nothing says whether a given level of nesting is worth its cost. PageIndex prices navigation explicitly: reading a collapsed node costs its whole span `S(v)`; routing through an expanded node costs `R(v) = 1` plus the most expensive bra\n078 [D] Assign-Once Byte-Budget Filename Pipeline :: Repositories, tags and export bundles are named from user- or model-supplied text, and every operating system disagrees about what a legal name is. PageIndex separates the problem into two functions with different jobs: `sanitize_filename` runs **once**, before a storage key is assigned, and may replace illegal characters, fold \n079 [D] Bounded Range-Set Selector Grammar with Canonical Re-Compression :: CLI selectors such as \"lines 1-3,7\" or \"gates test,hygiene\" are user input that is frequently malformed and occasionally hostile: `1-1000000000` materializes a billion integers inside the caller before any downstream cap is reached. PageIndex parses one grammar shared by the SDK and the tool layer, validates the whole string wit\n084 [D] Generated-Artifact Provenance Gate with Volatile-Field Projection :: AIKit already generates its codebase map and knowledge base and already has `update_knowledge_base.py --check`. What it lacks is the general rule the source establishes: **a derived artifact must be regenerable and must prove it is current**, and the comparison must be insensitive to fields that legitimately change on every run.\n085 [D] Pairwise Description-Collision Detector with Review-Allowlist Reasons :: AIKit's skills, rules and commands are chosen by an agent from their descriptions, so two descriptions that compete for the same request cause silent misrouting — and no per-item linter can see it, because each description is individually valid. The source compares every pair: TF-IDF vectors over the description plus slug tokens\n086 [D] Canonical Parser with an AST-Based Single-Parser Guard :: The source's most expensive bug was not a missing feature: three independent hand-rolled \"YAML-ish\" parsers each accepted a different subset of scalar styles and silently truncated 604 of 817 descriptions to their first line, with no error and no warning. The durable fix has three parts. First, **one canonical reader** owns extr\n091 [D] Fail-Closed Change-Record Classifier with Object-Resolved Sizes and Di :: AIKit gates work by looking at the working tree, not at a described change set. That is fine locally and wrong for anything that must be justified later: a path can be spelled two ways, a mode can change silently (`100644` → `120000` makes a file a symlink), a diff can claim a small size for a large blob, and a generated directo\n092 [D] Impact-Scoped Validation Plan with Policy Digest and Exact Receipt Ver :: Running every gate on every change is safe and slow; running a subset chosen by intuition is fast and unsound. The source makes the subset itself a reviewed artifact. A versioned policy file declares which path classes exist (`catalog`, `editorial`, `runtime`, `web`), which files and prefixes belong to each, which checks each cl\n098 [D] Layered Settings Resolver with Per-Key Merge Policy, Unoverridable Den :: AIKit already layers configuration — repository `kilo.json`, a project `.aikit/project.json`, the global config directory, and command-line flags — but the precedence is expressed only by the order in which the code happens to read the files. The source shows both the model and its sharp edges. A resolver should (a) walk a decla\n104 [D] Destructive-Command Coverage Matrix for Subprocess Call Sites :: AIKit shells out in roughly a dozen places (git, tests, gates, hooks). Each call site is a place where a path, a branch name or a user-supplied selector can become an argument to a destructive command. A settings-level `ask` list — the source's approach — protects the *interactive* surface only, matches command text by prefix, a\n105 [R] Turn-Scoped Search-Evidence Gate for First-Touch Edits :: An agent that edits a file it has never looked at is guessing. Telling it not to guess is advisory; a gate that requires *evidence gathered in this turn* is not. The source implements that gate for a single harness and documents every decision behind it. A first-touch edit is denied unless the current turn contains a completed t\n110 [D] Harness Adapter Capability Registry with Compliance Scorecard Gate :: AIKit targets several agent runtimes. Each has a different config location, a different notion of \"project\" vs \"user\", and a different set of hook or permission features. Without one table, each adapter's support is discovered by reading its code, and the differences become silent: an adapter simply does nothing for an event the\n112 [D] Fixpoint Identifier Normalization as a Single Source of Truth :: AIKit mints identifiers in several places — project names in `project.json`, gate ids, ledger task ids, map node ids, skill ids — and each place normalizes slightly differently. The failure mode is subtle and expensive: two spellings of one entity become two entities, or one entity becomes unreachable under the spelling a user t\n113 [D] Reverse Blast-Radius Traversal with a Declared Relation Contract :: Before a change, the useful question is \"what will this break?\" — and the useful answer is a list of consumers with a path, not a count. The source answers it by walking **incoming** edges over a declared relation set, seeding from a file or symbol query, and reporting for each hit the depth, the relation traversed, and the **ed\n115 [D] Stable Community Identity Across Recomputation :: AIKit's map groups modules into areas, and recomputing that grouping is where a navigational aid quietly becomes noise: if cluster ids and names reshuffle on every run, every link, ADR and handoff that referenced \"area 7\" rots, and a reviewer cannot tell a real reorganisation from a numbering change. The source treats cluster id\n119 [D] Calibrated Token Bound with Offline Oracle and Ratio Margin :: AIKit enforces token and character budgets on what it sends to a model (`PRINCIPLE-012`), but it counts with a character heuristic and treats that number as if it were the model's count. The source shows why that is unsound and what it costs: two tokenizers disagree by roughly ±2%, the sign depends on content, and an under-count\n125 [D] Singleton-Access Boundary Enforced by AST with Justified Allowlist :: Hidden global state is what makes a codebase untestable and its behaviour order-dependent, and it usually arrives one convenient import at a time. The source fixes it structurally. Process-wide singletons (database handle, document engine, cache client) are resolved **once at the assembly points** and passed down through an expl\n126 [D] Injective Canonical Payload Framing for Signed Records :: Everything AIKit signs, hashes or compares depends on one hidden assumption: that two different logical records never serialise to the same bytes. The source violates that assumption twice in the same module. `hash_state(parts)` joins components with `|` and hashes the result, so `[\"a|b\", \"c\"]` and `[\"a\", \"b|c\"]` collide — and t\n133 [D] Projection-Recorded-As-Event with Coverage Precedence and Rebuild :: AIKit keeps derived state next to its canonical state: the codebase map, the knowledge base, area labels and handoff summaries are all computed from sources of truth. Today a derived file that is damaged or stale is either regenerated from scratch or trusted because it exists; there is no record of *which* projection the project\n138 [D] Unknown-Commit-Outcome Signal for Atomic Writes :: AIKit's atomic-write rule says temp file, fsync, rename. The part that is easy to get wrong is what a caller should do when the rename succeeded but the *durability* step failed: retrying may be harmless, but reporting \"failed\" is false, and reporting \"succeeded\" is unproven. Maka models this as a third outcome. After the rename\n140 [D] Measured Risk Assessment with Refusal Outcome and Escalation Triggers :: AIKit's `PRINCIPLE-007` already requires an impact assessment on a Track C plan, but the assessment is prose the caller writes, and nothing connects it to the change actually being made. AI Governor specifies a version that can be computed: enumerate the target, the affected components, the modified functions and the existing te\n141 [D] Multi-Feature File Inventory with Surgical-Change Guard :: The most expensive AI regressions come from editing a file that serves several features through one shared dispatch path: a change made for one feature silently alters another. AI Governor names the detection signals precisely — centralizing names (`Manager`, `Handler`, `Router`, `Modal`), functions over 100 lines with more than\n142 [D] Rule-Metadata Feedback Loop from False-Positive Applications :: A rule corpus rots in one specific way: rules that are never selected, and rules that are selected for the wrong task. AI Governor's retrospective names both explicitly. It requires each task plan to carry an `[APPLIES RULES: ...]` annotation; after implementation, the agent must identify which applied rules were genuinely usefu\n147 [D] Base-Held Trust Manifest with Closed Ordered Rejection Taxonomy :: Every gate AIKit runs is defined by files that live in the same commit as the change being judged, which means a change can edit the gate that judges it. GAAI closes that by *address*: the policy is read from the **base commit**, so the candidate does not get a vote. Three things make it implementable. First, a **three-schema sp\n153 [D] Declared-vs-Observed Execution Attestation :: A plan says \"this task will be executed by the primary model\", and nothing verifies that it was. GAAI closes the gap with three small pieces: the plan declares an `impl_model` per story; the wrapper that actually invokes the model logs the **observed** tag and provider per phase into an append-only JSONL; and a pre-push hook com\n158 [D] Commit-Pinned Remote Module Sync with Local-Edit Guard :: aiFlows' FlowVerse loader resolves a repository and revision to a concrete commit hash, caches the module under that hash, and refuses to overwrite a sync directory that the user has modified. The pattern turns a mutable remote dependency into an auditable pin: the same revision can be re-resolved, a changed commit is visible, a\n159 [R] Declarative Key-Transform Pipeline for Imported Records :: aiFlows composes data shaping from small named operations (`key_select`, `key_rename`, `key_set`, `key_delete`, `key_copy`, `key_match_input`) instead of embedding one-off Python. For a registry that imports records from many external ledgers, the same idea gives a reviewed, testable mapping file: each import declares which keys\n162 [D] Trace DAG Normalization with Bounded Summaries :: The source turns raw ADK events into a stable graph: one model node, one node per tool event, edges in call order, and short input/output summaries. The value for AIKit is a portable execution trace for dogfood and subagent runs: a reviewer can see *what happened* without reading raw payloads, and the trace can be diffed across \n163 [R] Visible Tool-Plan Intent Strings without Raw Arguments :: The source translates a tool call into a human sentence (`\"capture_visual_crop\"` becomes \"zoom into region (x,y)\"), so a user can follow an autonomous loop without reading tool JSON. For AIKit's subagent and dogfood surfaces, a stable intent layer makes progress messages reviewable and keeps raw arguments, paths and secrets out \n176 [D] Quality-Delta Gate with Fix-or-Justify Acknowledgements :: The source checks a change for twelve quality kinds and reports *only what got worse*: duplication, a new private copy of a shared utility, a sign-flipped near-duplicate, a function that absorbed a second job. A finding is either fixed or justified in a written acknowledgement; the acknowledgements ledger survives across tasks, \n177 [D] Symbol-Pinned Notes Surfaced on Lookup with Staleness :: ripwire lets an agent leave a gotcha note pinned to a symbol; a later lookup surfaces it automatically. That is institutional memory between contexts that never met. AIKit's knowledge base is document-oriented; a symbol note attaches a short, reviewed invariant (\"this parser must reject a leading dash; the CLI turns it into a me\n178 [D] Byte-Identity Determinism Gate for Generated Artefacts :: The source's strongest invariant is that the same tree produces the same bytes on every run, regardless of thread count or scheduling. It enforces this with fixed partitioning, canonical reduction order, sorted paths and a byte-diff gate. AIKit generates `MATRIX.md`, the AST map, capsule files and license registers; today a dict\n179 [D] Floor-vs-Zero Evidence Contract with Refusal and Continuation :: The source refuses to let an incomplete answer look complete. A caller count is a floor because dynamic dispatch and callbacks are invisible; `0` means \"none found\", never \"none exists\"; ambiguity and unresolved names are counted; a selector that names nothing refuses with a did-you-mean instead of returning zero; every truncati\n182 [D] Environment-Fingerprinted Run Manifest for Dogfood and Agent Runs :: The source stamps each QA session with a GUID, a human-sortable folder, the scene, the Unity version, the app version, a start time and a strictly increasing sequence. A run is therefore reproducible and auditable. AIKit's dogfood and subagent runs need the same: a manifest that records the command, the input digests, the Python\n186 [R] Reflection Plugin Loader with Assembly/Type Allow-List :: The source builds AI components from a GUI by reflecting over assemblies and types. Reflection is powerful and dangerous: a name from a config file can load an arbitrary type or run a static initializer. A clean-room loader loads only from an explicit root, requires an entrypoint that implements a declared protocol, and checks a\n192 [D] Delta-Read Cache with a Content-Fingerprint Guard :: The source remembers what an agent read and returns only the diff when the same file is read again, while a fingerprint guard catches a file that changed between the fingerprint and the read. For AIKit, a `read` helper with the same contract would reduce repeated file dumps in long sessions and, more importantly, give the agent \n193 [D] Context Quality Score with Weighted Signals and Honest Unavailability :: The source grades context quality from seven signals and shows a letter grade. The reusable part is not the exact weights but the shape: each signal is measured independently, a missing signal is `unavailable` rather than zero, weights are explicit, and the grade is derived from a documented band. For AIKit this can score a task\n195 [D] Savings Ledger Separating Metered and Estimated Value :: The source reports savings in three tiers and keeps metered actions separate from estimates. That honesty matters: a compressed output with a measured before/after character count is evidence, while \"repeat reads avoided\" is a model. AIKit should record every context-saving action with a tier, an evidence field and a before/afte\n198 [D] Completion Distillation Packet :: Magic Context captures memory \"for free\": the historian already reads the whole history to compress it, so it lifts durable facts in the same pass instead of asking the agent to remember. AIKit has the analogue of that raw history — the task ledger already records every checkpoint with `--why --benefits --risks --mitigation`, th\n199 [D] ADR Locator Verification Gate :: Magic Context's dreamer re-verifies each memory against the current codebase: it maps a memory to the files that back it, then checks paths, configs and patterns, fixing or removing what has drifted. AIKit has the same staleness problem in a sharper form. Its ADR register is 500+ KB of accepted decisions, many of which name a mo\n200 [D] Machine-Evaluable Deferred Conditions :: Magic Context's `ctx_note` is a scratchpad for intentions that are not yet actionable; a note resurfaces at natural boundaries, and \"smart notes\" carry an open-ended condition the dreamer watches for. AIKit already has the seed of this: `--handoff \"next: ...; state: ...; failed: ...; open: ...\"` is parsed by `parse_handoff` and \n201 [D] Deterministic Checkpoint Decay Rendering :: Magic Context renders old compartments at lower fidelity as the window tightens, using a deterministic rule rather than an LLM, so the same history always renders the same way. AIKit's `--resume` does the opposite of graceful: it prints the interrupted task, its plan, and recent checkpoints, but a long task's history either crow\n204 [D] Knowledge Delta Merge with Archive :: OpenSpec's most transferable rule is that a change never edits the spec directly: it produces a delta (`ADDED`/`MODIFIED`/`REMOVED` requirements), and only archiving merges that delta into the living spec, leaving a dated copy of the delta behind. AIKit approximates this with an append-only ADR register, but ADRs are narrative d\n206 [D] Explore-Before-Plan Phase Artefact :: OpenSpec separates a no-stakes `/opsx:explore` from `/opsx:propose`: exploration reads the code, weighs options and produces a recommendation without committing to a plan, and only then does a change folder get created. AIKit has an `explorer` subagent and a Phase 2 plan checkpoint, but nothing forces an option comparison before\n210 [D] Progressive-Disclosure Skill Index :: Acontext's retrieval rule is that an agent should see a cheap index and fetch content by tool use, not receive everything up front. AIKit's Tier 1 budget is built on the same insight, but its skill surface does not exploit it: `.agents/skills/aikit/SKILL.md` is a single long runbook injected through the host capsule, and as more\n212 [D] Hash-chained task ledger :: Each ledger entry stores `prev` and `digest`, where `digest = sha256(canonical_json({prev, index, ...entry fields}))` with sorted keys, compact separators and `ensure_ascii=True`. Verification recomputes the chain and reports the first broken index. A silent edit of an old checkpoint, plan or `--why` then fails verification inst\n214 [D] Plan-scope overreach gate with path globs :: ToolReplay loads a scope file with an exact-match allow-list and flags every call outside it as `permission-overreach`. We extend the idea from tool names to **tool plus path**: the Phase 2 plan declares allowed path globs, and a gate compares the staged diff (and, where available, the tool-call transcript) with them. This enfor\n215 [D] Replay-consistency lint for dogfood transcripts :: `replay.py` walks a transcript once. It keeps the first response seen for each canonical call (tool plus sorted-key args). A later identical call with a different response is `non-determinism`, and the first such index is the divergence point. An identical call and response with no mutator between them is `redundant-call`. Lines\n217 [D] Rule-ablation review against harness rot :: The list highlights the ablation method for instructions: strip the prompt, add back only the lines that fix a repeated failure, and repeat at each model release. Instructions encode corrections for behaviours a model may no longer have, so they go stale. For AIKit, each Tier 1 capsule line and each principle rule gets a record \n218 [R] Isolated vs forked subagent context rule :: A listed multi-agent write-up gives a decision rule. **Fork** workers that continue an in-progress investigation, so they inherit the supervisor's context. **Isolate** verifiers that must judge independently, so they start fresh and cannot be anchored by the author's reasoning. AIKit already delegates to `explorer`, `worker`, `a\n220 [D] Harness checklist as an automated gate :: `templates/HARNESS_CHECKLIST.md` is a manual review list: explicit tool permissions (allowed, restricted, not allowed), verification commands that are correct, no ambiguous instructions, long-lived state in files rather than the prompt, milestones with verification commands, scope boundaries written down, and minimum permissions\n222 [D] Four-type gate taxonomy with bounded revision loops :: `references/gates.md` maps every checkpoint to one of four types. **Pre-flight** blocks entry on a missing precondition with no partial work. **Revision** loops back to the producer with specific feedback under an iteration cap, and escalates early if the issue count does not fall between iterations (stall detection). **Escalati\n223 [R] Adversarial verifier with mandatory severities :: Checker agents open with a \"FORCE stance\": assume the work is flawed until evidence proves otherwise. They list the ways checkers go soft (crediting intent, accepting a decision reference without checking its full scope, accepting \"v1\" scope cuts, letting six passing dimensions hide a failed seventh). Every finding must carry `B\n224 [D] Machine-greppable predicate context cited by ID :: `CONTEXT.md` states operational facts as single-line predicates (`CLASS.subkey=value`). Agent briefs must cite predicates by ID and never paraphrase, and a meta rule (`brief-must-cite-doc`) enforces this. A glossary of modules and seams sits beside the predicates, and chronological notes go in a separate log at the bottom.\n225 [D] Tests-only gap auditor with read-only implementation :: The \"Nyquist auditor\" receives a list of validation gaps. For each gap it writes a minimal behavioural test that can fail, runs it, debugs the test for at most 3 iterations and reports what actually happened. Implementation files are read-only: the agent may create only tests, fixtures and a validation record, and an implementat\n226 [D] Requirement-to-task coverage check for plans :: The plan checker verifies a plan before execution by going backward from the goal. Every requirement must map to at least one task, tasks must actually deliver the requirement and not only reference it, dependencies must not be broken or circular, artifacts must be wired together, scope must fit the context budget, and the plan ","numLines":70,"startLine":1,"totalLines":272}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"214450d0-f220-47bf-8c00-90d11127ae2d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main"}
