blic"}]}},"live_cwd":"/c/Users/Yunus-Emre/Desktop/portable_agent_kit","platform":"windows"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4fdf6972-f6ea-471b-8f17-1abec0f93909","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"afc9d0ae-b0fe-440e-be5c-aa052b832cb7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"fe3259f3-06b4-4656-9c7c-f91ae09159c7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LevzruTbVAMVV12EhkbiYt","type":"tool_result","content":"71\t227 [R] Decision records with rationale and rejected alternatives :: A decision is a first-class ledger event: the choice, the rationale and the **rejected alternatives**. At session start, relevant prior decisions are injected into context, which prevents the \"yesterday we chose SQLite, today the agent proposes Postgres\" failure. A comparison table in the README claims this beats MEMORY.md files\n72\t228 [D] Separate history chain from live coordination state :: History (decisions, tasks, verdicts) is append-only and chained. Liveness (claims, heartbeats) is not, because it is live and not historical. Each session writes a small heartbeat snapshot, refreshed as it works and deleted on clean exit. A snapshot that outlives its session means the session died, so peers can see \"stale\", and \n73\t229 [D] Cross-provider review pinned to a commit SHA :: `edda review` has a second provider's agent review a committed branch and records a result with the reviewed SHA, reviewer session, observed model, findings and cost (measured or marked unmeasured). Optional spec and gate evidence qualify the verdict. A related command records a clean-SHA receipt for a local command run.\n74\t231 [D] Deletion tombstones with a recorded reason :: When Edda's garbage collector removes a stored blob, it appends a tombstone with the reason (`retention`, `quota`, `purge_archive` or `manual`). The history then never silently loses data: a missing item can be explained.\n75\t232 [D] Portable ledger and memory interchange export :: The update section names memory interchange formats and portable agent schemas as a growing theme. AIKit keeps its state in `task_ledger.json`, the ADR register and `knowledge/`, readable by every host through scripts. A versioned, documented export (one JSON document: tasks, checkpoints, ADR index, guidelines) would let another\n76\t235 [D] Skill evaluation harness for trigger and outcome :: The benchmarks section lists tools that generate and evaluate skills and measure skill performance on real workflows, plus a guide to testing skills. The idea: treat `.agents/skills/aikit/SKILL.md` as code with tests. A fixture file lists prompts that should and should not trigger the skill (checked against the `description`) an\n77\t236 [D] Third-party skill intake scanner :: \"Treat skills like code\" becomes a gate. Before a third-party skill is added to a skills directory, a scanner reads its `SKILL.md` and bundled scripts for risky patterns: shell execution of downloaded content, network calls to unknown hosts, reads of credential paths, instructions that tell the agent to ignore rules or disable c\n78\t237 [R] Typed source labels in the intake register :: Each list entry starts with a type label (`Library:`, `Blog:`, `Paper:`) and one line of value. Idea Catcher reviews so far cover very different repo kinds (frameworks, lists, tools). A `kind` field on each review record (`framework`, `tool`, `awesome-list`, `survey`) would make the matrix filterable.\n79\t238 [D] Contract-keyed incremental regeneration :: Cached work is reused only when every input of the contract matches: source hashes, ownership, model and prompt version. A prompt-version bump alone does not invalidate everything. If a snapshot is missing or incompatible, the system falls back to doing the work.\n80\t239 [D] Staged candidates with precondition-checked approval :: Generated content can be staged as a candidate instead of being written directly. Approval re-validates, requires that the candidate body is unchanged since it was staged, and checks a recorded precondition on the target (it still looks as it did when the candidate was made). Batch approval records its intent first so an interru\n81\t240 [R] Computed, never-persisted dossier freshness :: Freshness is derived on demand from a snapshot with three orthogonal signals (source-derived status, contradicted, archived) and is never stored, so it cannot go stale itself. For us: each dossier records its analysed commit, and review records hold the date. A pure function over that data classifies each dossier as current, age\n82\t241 [D] Narrow storage interfaces over local-disk state :: Since 1.5.0, behaviour (what changed, whether a candidate can be approved) is separate from persistence. Features use narrow per-feature interfaces, and a local-disk implementation holds file handles, locking, atomic writes, path confinement, symlink handling and crash recovery. The interface does not promise POSIX semantics, an\n83\t242 [D] Read-only persona reviewers with a synthesizer :: Several narrow reviewers run in parallel, each with one lens and no write tools: a naive reader who flags undefined terms, a structural checker, a style checker, an implementation-readiness checker and a design critic. A synthesizer merges and de-duplicates their reports for the primary agent, which applies only approved fixes. \n84\t243 [D] Design-versus-code drift report :: For each design document, the drift skill reads its intent, locates the named files and symbols (asking the author to add links when it cannot), reads the current code and lists divergences: missing features, incomplete models (fields in the design but not the class), logic shortcuts (hard-coded values where dynamic logic was pl\n85\t244 [D] Brief-to-draft design gate with human approval :: EGM separates thinking from writing with explicit artifacts. Brainstorming ends in a saved brief; drafting produces the design section by section and pauses after each for the human; fresh reviewers then challenge it; the human approves before implementation. It is advertised for features that need design decisions and not for s\n86\t245 [D] Ship AIKit as a multi-host plugin :: One repository carries manifests for several hosts (`.claude-plugin`, `.codex-plugin`) plus a shared set of skills and agents, and installs with a single command per host, including Antigravity (`agy plugin install <repo URL>`). A distributions folder and a harness guide cover host differences, including a rule to stop if the ho\n87\t247 [D] Append-only JSONL ledger writes :: CubeLoop's checkpointer writes only new messages, so cost per checkpoint is constant, unlike a full snapshot on every change. For AIKit, `task_ledger.json` is rewritten whole on each checkpoint (temp, fsync, replace). As history grows, each write costs more and a crash during a write touches all history.\n88\t252 [R] allow / deny / ask permission triad with failure fallbacks :: The tools-and-permissions checklist calls for each tool to have a defined `allow`, `deny` or `ask` outcome, plus defined behaviour on interruption, fallback, and failure of a sibling call in a parallel batch. Permissions are one explicit decision function and not scattered conditions.\n89\t253 [R] Source-map line for every extracted idea :: Both books end with a source-map appendix that links claims to the places they come from. Our dossiers name repos but rarely the exact file or section behind each idea, so a later reader cannot quickly verify one.\n90\t254 [D] Approval-gated learning capture on correction :: When the user corrects the agent, it states what went wrong and proposes a one-line rule in a fixed form (`[LEARN] Category: rule`) with a category from a closed list, then waits for approval before saving. Trigger phrases (\"remember this\", \"don't do that again\") start the flow.\n91\t255 [R] Tool-call budgets by task size :: A context-discipline rule sets tool-call budgets per task size (quick fix 20, bug fix 30, feature 50, large feature 80), tells the agent to wrap up at 80% of budget, to read before write, not to re-read unchanged files, to stop after tests pass, and to iterate at most twice on one failure.\n92\t256 [D] File-based STOP switch for autonomous loops :: Touching one file (`STOP`) halts every research loop. Loops check for it at the top of each iteration. It needs no process signalling, works across hosts and operating systems, and is easy to explain.\n93\t258 [D] Touched-surface optional audits :: Some checks only matter when a change touches what they inspect (packaging, release, mirrors). They are not part of the default regression set, but become mandatory when the diff touches their surface. A manifest maps each optional audit to the path globs that trigger it.\n94\t259 [D] Core regression tier for the fast loop :: The \"default regression set\" is deliberately small and capability-based: install, runtime entry, routing, truth artifacts. It protects what must keep working, and avoids wording and layout tests that break on harmless edits.\n95\t260 [R] Host parity matrix enforced by a test :: GSD publishes what each command, workflow, agent role and hook becomes on each host, and a test fails when a new surface is added without a disposition for that host (supported, adapted, or explicitly unsupported with a reason). New features therefore cannot silently skip a host.\n96\t261 [D] Optional capability registry :: Beyond the core, GSD has named, independently maintained capabilities (for example drift, gap analysis, nyquist, broken-windows, markdown linting, numerics steering) that a user opts into, discovered through a registry. The core stays small.\n97\t263 [D] Plan-fits-context fit check per milestone :: The plan step verifies that each plan fits a fresh context window, because quality degrades as context fills (context rot), and each executor then starts clean. The fit check happens before execution, not after degradation shows.\n98\t264 [D] Compile-time lint for agent instruction Markdown :: Playbooks programs are Markdown with a fixed structure, and a compile step parses them to an AST, resolves imports and checks expressions before anything runs. Errors surface at compile time with a location, not as odd model behaviour later.\n99\t271 [D] Strict Declarative Reference Resolution :: Spelltest resolves cross-references inside its YAML in the most forgiving possible way. In `yaml_tests.py`, a user's metric list is built with `[defined_metrics[name] for name in metric_names if name in defined_metrics]`: a typo in a metric name does not fail, it **silently removes that metric from the evaluation**, and the run \n100\t273 [D] Plan Attestation Drift Detector :: AIKit's plan lives in the ledger, and the ledger is protected by a lock and atomic writes — against corruption, not against **the agent's belief diverging from it**. The failure mode is real and unguarded: an agent reads the plan in Phase 2, the context compacts, the agent reconstructs the plan from memory, and it proceeds again\n101\t274 [D] Worker Journal with Shared Tick :: AIKit's rule is that sub-agents never write the ledger (PRINCIPLE-008 R6), and the implementation enforces it with a global lock over one JSON document. That is correct for integrity and wrong for observability: a sub-agent's findings, failures and file touches are invisible to the orchestrator until it summarizes them in its ow\n102\t275 [D] Environment Doctor with Always-Zero Exit :: `plan-doctor.sh` exists because every mechanism in that project can fail **silently and only on some machines**: a path canonicalizer that emits `C:\\` style output makes resolution go dark, a `.planning/` directory can exist while nothing resolves, an attestation file can sit where the hooks do not look. The script runs one pass\n103\t276 [R] Selector Binding Refusal :: planning-with-files documents a bug class it had to fix explicitly: *\"Explicit selectors are bindings, not hints (issue #237). This script WRITES ledger rows into the plan dir it picks, so a legacy cwd fallback after a rejected selector files another plan's run history.\"* In other words, when an operator names a plan and the nam\n104\t280 [D] Untracked Local Overlay Layer :: `headroom learn` mines failed sessions and writes corrections to an instruction file, and its default target is `CLAUDE.local.md` — local and gitignored — with the tracked `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md` as explicit opt-ins. That default encodes a governance judgement worth adopting: **machine- and person-specific tuning must not enter \n105\t281 [D] Signal-Preserving Truncation Contract :: Headroom's demo claim is precise: a 10,144-token log compresses to 1,260 tokens **and the `FATAL` line survives byte for byte**. That is a contract, not a capability — the compressor commits to preserving a declared class of lines, and the benchmark verifies it. AIKit truncates constantly and makes no such promise: gate output i\n106\t282 [R] Plugin Contract Test Harness :: The source gives every server plugin its own test file (auth, caching, compression, rate limiting, logging, metrics, graceful shutdown). That is better than no tests, but it does not prove the plugins share a contract. A single harness defines required hooks (`register`, `startup`, `shutdown`), optional hooks, ordering and isola\n107\t290 [D] Trace-Only Learning Loop with Evidence-Linked Lesson Candidates :: ACE's `TraceAnalyser` learns from pre-recorded traces without re-running the task. For AIKit, the same idea applies to dogfood and subagent transcripts: mine repeated failure patterns offline and produce *lesson candidates* with evidence event ids, rather than mutating rules during a live run. A candidate is reviewed by a human \n108\t292 [D] Implementation-Keyed Persistent Memo :: CocoIndex's memo key is the whole idea: `hash(input) + hash(function source)`. The second half is what most caches get wrong, and it is what makes a cache safe to keep across versions. AIKit has the unsafe half: `_extract_symbols_uncached` is memoized in `_SYMBOL_CACHE` keyed by `(file_path, content, strict)`, but the cache is *\n109\t295 [D] Declared Cadence Before Work :: Trailmark's `cadence-first` picks Tier 1/2/3 per block **before** execution: trivial work gets a plan-only path, complex refactors get the full chain, hot-fixes skip the loop. The justification is economic, not aesthetic — a protocol that taxes a one-line fix gets bypassed entirely, and then nothing is enforced. AIKit has exactl\n110\t296 [D] Pre-Flight Reuse Table with Symbol Proof :: Trailmark's `library-first` step is a table with one row per unit of work: *does something in the repository already do this, and if not, how many lines will it take?* The framing matters — \"writing your own is faster than finding someone else's, so without the table it wins by default\" — and it attacks the most common form of a\n111\t297 [D] Irreversible Action Classifier :: Trailmark's rule for publishing, deploying and deleting is a **stop**, not a warning: \"the skill halts and waits for a human. Not a warning printed as it goes; a stop.\" AIKit encodes the same intent as prose in PRINCIPLE-018 (no force-push, explicit staging, never destructive recovery) and implements it narrowly in a few call si\n112\t298 [D] Claim Falsification Gate :: BMAD's `claims-check` states the principle plainly: the plan is \"the change's own account of itself: testimony, not evidence — a claim repeated in a code comment is still the same claim, not confirmation.\" The reviewer extracts each checkable claim (what the change does, what it preserves, ordering, parity with existing code) an\n113\t299 [D] Deletion Intent Audit :: BMAD's `deletion-check` asks a question almost no automated review asks: for each removed or replaced chunk, *did it carry behaviour or a contract that the change neither re-established nor intentionally retired?* Pure renames and whitespace are excluded; findings are regressions, orphaned references and newly dead code. AIKit c\n114\t300 [D] Linted Architecture Spine :: BMAD ships an architecture *spine* template plus `lint_spine.py` and a reviewer gate, so the architecture document is not free prose: it has required parts, and a script proves they are present and coherent. AIKit's `knowledge/architecture.md` is hand-maintained prose with a freshness check that only proves the file exists — no \n115\t301 [D] Path-Scoped Rule Capsules :: Game Studios ships 14 rule files that are **scoped to path globs** — gameplay code, engine code, UI, network, scene and asset files each load their own standards, applied when a file matching the glob is edited. The gain is both relevance (the model sees the rule that applies to the file in front of it) and budget (rules for cod\n116\t302 [D] Pre-Code Artefact Budget :: Game Studios reports that its `standard` rigor mode produced 30 documents before the first line of code and was ranked **last** by blind reviewers and a human playtest, while `minimal` produced one document and still shipped a working, tested game. The lesson is not \"less process\" — it is that each artefact must justify its cost\n117\t305 [D] As-Of Ledger Projection :: Viseur's ability to scrub to any frame is the feature that makes a log useful for diagnosis. AIKit's `--resume` only ever shows the present: the active task, its latest plan and recent checkpoints. Yet the question a post-mortem asks is \"what did the agent know when it made the wrong decision at checkpoint 4\" — and that state ex\n118\t306 [D] Risk-Weighted Test Ordering :: The prototype includes a test-prioritisation component: not all tests are equal, so run the ones most likely to expose a defect first. AIKit runs its two tiers in discovery order, which is alphabetical by module. That is deterministic but blind: when a change breaks three tests, the failure the operator sees first is the alphabe\n119\t307 [D] Capability-Gated Skips :: The game-testing prototype is deliberately engine- and platform-independent, which forces it to state what a given environment can do — screen capture, input injection, audio capture — instead of assuming. AIKit supports seven host clients and several optional capabilities (git, a browser for media capture, a network for publish\n120\t308 [D] Receipt-Verified Side Effects :: The design sends every command into Studio as a file and requires a result file back, so the orchestrator never treats \"I sent it\" as \"it happened\". AIKit's mutating operations have the same gap in a smaller form: a publish reports success from the command's exit code, a push reports the local view of the ref, and a media captur\n121\t310 [D] Retrieval-Feedback Truncation Retirement :: Boost's rule is small and excellent: when the agent retrieves the full original of the same filtered output three times, **that filter is disabled locally**. The policy does not argue about whether the filter is good in the abstract; it observes that this specific filter, on this machine, keeps hiding something the agent needs, \n122\t311 [D] Turn-Weighted Context Cost Model :: Boost's accounting insight is that a token trimmed from a tool output is not saved once: the conversation is re-sent every turn, so the saving compounds until compaction. \"Saved 2K\" is really 2K per remaining turn. AIKit reasons about context statically — Tier 1 has a 1,750-token budget, `MAX_HANDOFF_CHARS`, report caps — but ne\n123\t312 [D] Immutable Generated Records with Active Pointer :: Holonic Asset never lets a draft become the shipped thing. A draft is mutable, candidates are reviewed, confirmation creates an immutable **Record**, the asset carries a pointer to the active Record, and export reads only that Record. Rollback is a pointer move, and history is a list of Records rather than a pile of overwritten \n124\t313 [D] Scoped Materialised View for Sub-Agents :: Puppyone's File Level Security enforces permissions at the filesystem layer: \"if an agent doesn't have access, the file physically doesn't exist in its environment.\" AIKit's sub-agent model is the opposite: `subagent_delegate.py` hands a child agent the workspace and a role prompt, and PRINCIPLE-008 R6 tells it not to write the \n125\t314 [D] Optimistic Ledger Commit with Base Version :: Puppyone's checkout/commit workflow lets an agent take a copy, work, and commit with conflict detection rather than holding a lock for the whole operation. AIKit holds an exclusive advisory lock for the entire read-modify-write of `task_ledger.json`; that is correct and short today, but it makes long operations (a distillation p\n126\t315 [D] Cost-Ordered Gate Ladder with Dry Run :: Ategnatos orders its pre-flight checks by cost: auditing a dataset is cheap, generating a config is cheaper than a run, validating the environment is cheap, and a 2–5 step dry run is the last cheap thing before hours of GPU time. Each gate must pass before the next runs, and the final gate is a **real but minimal execution** — n\n127\t317 [R] Concept Co-occurrence Map for the Idea Register :: The paper's method builds a network from document terms: tokenise, remove stop words, count co-occurrences within a window, and analyse the resulting graph. Applied to `ideas.json` and the dossiers, the same technique answers questions the register currently cannot: which concept terms appear across many dossiers (possible dupli\n128\t318 [D] Revisioned Registry Entries with Exact `id@revision` Addressing :: PowerContext never edits an artifact in place. It writes a new revision, keeps the artifact id stable, and lets a reader ask for an exact revision or the latest one. `ArtifactStore.revise` refuses the write if the caller's base revision is no longer the latest, which turns a lost update into an explicit conflict. The idea-catche\n129\t319 [D] Bounded Handoff Artifact with Citations, Omissions and Disposition :: A PowerContext handoff is not a transcript summary. It is a bounded artifact with an objective, a `disposition` (`continuable` / `blocked` / `complete`), state statements, next actions, at least one citation, an explicit omission list and a byte budget. Activation happens at a provider-observed Source boundary, the receiver ackn\n130\t322 [D] Composite Truth-Table Conformance Suite :: The source documents each composite's return semantics in a comment and then violates them: `Selector` promises \"Failure if all components failed\" but returns `Running` as soon as one child fails with siblings left. A truth-table suite makes the documented return matrix executable: for every composite and every combination of ch\n131\t325 [D] Tree Definition Validator with Acyclicity and Leaf Contracts :: The source builds trees in code, so a mistyped child, a node used twice where a fresh instance was intended, or a cycle created by assigning a node as its own descendant is only discovered at runtime — often as a stack overflow inside `Behave`. A declaration-time validator checks the tree as data: unique node ids, every referenc\n132\t332 [D] One-Shot Failure Evidence Capture Attached to the Gate Report :: Airtest assertions take `snapshot=True`: on failure they capture the screen once and attach it to the step log, so the report shows the exact state that failed. AIKit's gates report a failure message, but a subagent or dogfood run that fails deep in a sequence leaves the reviewer reconstructing the state from the log. A failure \n133\t336 [D] Lock Lease with Process Incarnation and Dead-Lock Sweep :: The source stores, for each lock: a token, the owner pid, an *incarnation* string derived from the process start time, and the acquisition timestamp. A competing process reclaims a lease only when it can prove the holder is dead (pid gone, or the incarnation changed so the pid was reused) or when the lease is older than `stale_m\n134\t340 [D] Frozen Public-Surface Snapshot for Adapters :: The source ships an `xlua-adapter` tool that compiles a binding layer against a **frozen subset of the public interface** and runs 21 protocol checks. A binding can therefore not silently depend on an internal class, and a public API change breaks the adapter build before it breaks a game. AIKit has several host adapters (Claude\n135\t346 [D] Index-Plus-Topic-Article Projection with a Measured Token Budget :: The idea-catcher `MATRIX.md` already lists every idea for every target, which is useful for an audit and expensive for a consumer that needs one target's open work. codesight's pattern is a two-level projection: a small index that names the topics and one short article per topic, each with its own token budget. For idea-catcher \n136\t347 [D] Detector Registry with AST-First, Regex-Fallback and Confidence :: codesight runs a per-language extractor and then a set of detectors that are mostly language-agnostic. The README is explicit that TypeScript gets AST precision while other languages use \"battle-tested regex detection across the same frameworks\" — a declared degradation, not a hidden one. AIKit's codebase map is Python-AST-first\n137\t348 [D] Impact-Scoped Repair Admission by Blast-Radius Union :: codesight's `analyzeBlastRadius` walks reverse imports up to a depth and returns the affected files; `analyzeMultiFileBlastRadius` unions several files' radii. Aegean Reforge decides whether a repair is automatic or needs review by the *kind* of defect alone. Adding the blast radius makes the decision depend on the code it touch\n138\t349 [D] Test-Impact Map Keyed by Route and Model Identity :: codesight's coverage detector maps each extracted route and schema model to a test file that mentions it, using path heuristics and quoted-path/identifier search, and reports the untested set. Aegean Reforge runs the project's tests after a repair; a test-impact map would let it run *the tests that mention the changed route, mod\n139\t358 [D] Heartbeat-Refreshed Lock with Inactivity-Based Steal :: `file_lock` in `scripts/disruption_recovery.py` protects the ledger with an `O_CREAT|O_EXCL` lockfile, and breaks any lock whose **mtime is older than 30 seconds**. That is an age heuristic, and it is wrong in both directions: a process that legitimately holds the lock for longer (a full knowledge regeneration, a large ledger re\n140\t359 [R] Delegation Contract Enforcement :: VibeGame's `enforce-team-mode.py` intercepts an `Agent` tool call, recognizes a role name, and **denies it**, replying with the command that would have run it correctly: ``` architect agent must be spawned via vibegame lead to get task context. Use: vibegame lead agent --name architect-... --agent-type architect --prompt '...' `"}]},"uuid":"14aa46dd-3f7d-4d11-a20f-0aa7b849a355","timestamp":"2026-10-29T18:48:28.520Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\YUNUS-~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\c--Users-Yunus-Emre-Desktop-portable-agent-kit\\05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787\\scratchpad\\ak_mech.txt","content":"227 [R] Decision records with rationale and rejected alternatives :: A decision is a first-class ledger event: the choice, the rationale and the **rejected alternatives**. At session start, relevant prior decisions are injected into context, which prevents the \"yesterday we chose SQLite, today the agent proposes Postgres\" failure. A comparison table in the README claims this beats MEMORY.md files\n228 [D] Separate history chain from live coordination state :: History (decisions, tasks, verdicts) is append-only and chained. Liveness (claims, heartbeats) is not, because it is live and not historical. Each session writes a small heartbeat snapshot, refreshed as it works and deleted on clean exit. A snapshot that outlives its session means the session died, so peers can see \"stale\", and \n229 [D] Cross-provider review pinned to a commit SHA :: `edda review` has a second provider's agent review a committed branch and records a result with the reviewed SHA, reviewer session, observed model, findings and cost (measured or marked unmeasured). Optional spec and gate evidence qualify the verdict. A related command records a clean-SHA receipt for a local command run.\n231 [D] Deletion tombstones with a recorded reason :: When Edda's garbage collector removes a stored blob, it appends a tombstone with the reason (`retention`, `quota`, `purge_archive` or `manual`). The history then never silently loses data: a missing item can be explained.\n232 [D] Portable ledger and memory interchange export :: The update section names memory interchange formats and portable agent schemas as a growing theme. AIKit keeps its state in `task_ledger.json`, the ADR register and `knowledge/`, readable by every host through scripts. A versioned, documented export (one JSON document: tasks, checkpoints, ADR index, guidelines) would let another\n235 [D] Skill evaluation harness for trigger and outcome :: The benchmarks section lists tools that generate and evaluate skills and measure skill performance on real workflows, plus a guide to testing skills. The idea: treat `.agents/skills/aikit/SKILL.md` as code with tests. A fixture file lists prompts that should and should not trigger the skill (checked against the `description`) an\n236 [D] Third-party skill intake scanner :: \"Treat skills like code\" becomes a gate. Before a third-party skill is added to a skills directory, a scanner reads its `SKILL.md` and bundled scripts for risky patterns: shell execution of downloaded content, network calls to unknown hosts, reads of credential paths, instructions that tell the agent to ignore rules or disable c\n237 [R] Typed source labels in the intake register :: Each list entry starts with a type label (`Library:`, `Blog:`, `Paper:`) and one line of value. Idea Catcher reviews so far cover very different repo kinds (frameworks, lists, tools). A `kind` field on each review record (`framework`, `tool`, `awesome-list`, `survey`) would make the matrix filterable.\n238 [D] Contract-keyed incremental regeneration :: Cached work is reused only when every input of the contract matches: source hashes, ownership, model and prompt version. A prompt-version bump alone does not invalidate everything. If a snapshot is missing or incompatible, the system falls back to doing the work.\n239 [D] Staged candidates with precondition-checked approval :: Generated content can be staged as a candidate instead of being written directly. Approval re-validates, requires that the candidate body is unchanged since it was staged, and checks a recorded precondition on the target (it still looks as it did when the candidate was made). Batch approval records its intent first so an interru\n240 [R] Computed, never-persisted dossier freshness :: Freshness is derived on demand from a snapshot with three orthogonal signals (source-derived status, contradicted, archived) and is never stored, so it cannot go stale itself. For us: each dossier records its analysed commit, and review records hold the date. A pure function over that data classifies each dossier as current, age\n241 [D] Narrow storage interfaces over local-disk state :: Since 1.5.0, behaviour (what changed, whether a candidate can be approved) is separate from persistence. Features use narrow per-feature interfaces, and a local-disk implementation holds file handles, locking, atomic writes, path confinement, symlink handling and crash recovery. The interface does not promise POSIX semantics, an\n242 [D] Read-only persona reviewers with a synthesizer :: Several narrow reviewers run in parallel, each with one lens and no write tools: a naive reader who flags undefined terms, a structural checker, a style checker, an implementation-readiness checker and a design critic. A synthesizer merges and de-duplicates their reports for the primary agent, which applies only approved fixes. \n243 [D] Design-versus-code drift report :: For each design document, the drift skill reads its intent, locates the named files and symbols (asking the author to add links when it cannot), reads the current code and lists divergences: missing features, incomplete models (fields in the design but not the class), logic shortcuts (hard-coded values where dynamic logic was pl\n244 [D] Brief-to-draft design gate with human approval :: EGM separates thinking from writing with explicit artifacts. Brainstorming ends in a saved brief; drafting produces the design section by section and pauses after each for the human; fresh reviewers then challenge it; the human approves before implementation. It is advertised for features that need design decisions and not for s\n245 [D] Ship AIKit as a multi-host plugin :: One repository carries manifests for several hosts (`.claude-plugin`, `.codex-plugin`) plus a shared set of skills and agents, and installs with a single command per host, including Antigravity (`agy plugin install <repo URL>`). A distributions folder and a harness guide cover host differences, including a rule to stop if the ho\n247 [D] Append-only JSONL ledger writes :: CubeLoop's checkpointer writes only new messages, so cost per checkpoint is constant, unlike a full snapshot on every change. For AIKit, `task_ledger.json` is rewritten whole on each checkpoint (temp, fsync, replace). As history grows, each write costs more and a crash during a write touches all history.\n252 [R] allow / deny / ask permission triad with failure fallbacks :: The tools-and-permissions checklist calls for each tool to have a defined `allow`, `deny` or `ask` outcome, plus defined behaviour on interruption, fallback, and failure of a sibling call in a parallel batch. Permissions are one explicit decision function and not scattered conditions.\n253 [R] Source-map line for every extracted idea :: Both books end with a source-map appendix that links claims to the places they come from. Our dossiers name repos but rarely the exact file or section behind each idea, so a later reader cannot quickly verify one.\n254 [D] Approval-gated learning capture on correction :: When the user corrects the agent, it states what went wrong and proposes a one-line rule in a fixed form (`[LEARN] Category: rule`) with a category from a closed list, then waits for approval before saving. Trigger phrases (\"remember this\", \"don't do that again\") start the flow.\n255 [R] Tool-call budgets by task size :: A context-discipline rule sets tool-call budgets per task size (quick fix 20, bug fix 30, feature 50, large feature 80), tells the agent to wrap up at 80% of budget, to read before write, not to re-read unchanged files, to stop after tests pass, and to iterate at most twice on one failure.\n256 [D] File-based STOP switch for autonomous loops :: Touching one file (`STOP`) halts every research loop. Loops check for it at the top of each iteration. It needs no process signalling, works across hosts and operating systems, and is easy to explain.\n258 [D] Touched-surface optional audits :: Some checks only matter when a change touches what they inspect (packaging, release, mirrors). They are not part of the default regression set, but become mandatory when the diff touches their surface. A manifest maps each optional audit to the path globs that trigger it.\n259 [D] Core regression tier for the fast loop :: The \"default regression set\" is deliberately small and capability-based: install, runtime entry, routing, truth artifacts. It protects what must keep working, and avoids wording and layout tests that break on harmless edits.\n260 [R] Host parity matrix enforced by a test :: GSD publishes what each command, workflow, agent role and hook becomes on each host, and a test fails when a new surface is added without a disposition for that host (supported, adapted, or explicitly unsupported with a reason). New features therefore cannot silently skip a host.\n261 [D] Optional capability registry :: Beyond the core, GSD has named, independently maintained capabilities (for example drift, gap analysis, nyquist, broken-windows, markdown linting, numerics steering) that a user opts into, discovered through a registry. The core stays small.\n263 [D] Plan-fits-context fit check per milestone :: The plan step verifies that each plan fits a fresh context window, because quality degrades as context fills (context rot), and each executor then starts clean. The fit check happens before execution, not after degradation shows.\n264 [D] Compile-time lint for agent instruction Markdown :: Playbooks programs are Markdown with a fixed structure, and a compile step parses them to an AST, resolves imports and checks expressions before anything runs. Errors surface at compile time with a location, not as odd model behaviour later.\n271 [D] Strict Declarative Reference Resolution :: Spelltest resolves cross-references inside its YAML in the most forgiving possible way. In `yaml_tests.py`, a user's metric list is built with `[defined_metrics[name] for name in metric_names if name in defined_metrics]`: a typo in a metric name does not fail, it **silently removes that metric from the evaluation**, and the run \n273 [D] Plan Attestation Drift Detector :: AIKit's plan lives in the ledger, and the ledger is protected by a lock and atomic writes — against corruption, not against **the agent's belief diverging from it**. The failure mode is real and unguarded: an agent reads the plan in Phase 2, the context compacts, the agent reconstructs the plan from memory, and it proceeds again\n274 [D] Worker Journal with Shared Tick :: AIKit's rule is that sub-agents never write the ledger (PRINCIPLE-008 R6), and the implementation enforces it with a global lock over one JSON document. That is correct for integrity and wrong for observability: a sub-agent's findings, failures and file touches are invisible to the orchestrator until it summarizes them in its ow\n275 [D] Environment Doctor with Always-Zero Exit :: `plan-doctor.sh` exists because every mechanism in that project can fail **silently and only on some machines**: a path canonicalizer that emits `C:\\` style output makes resolution go dark, a `.planning/` directory can exist while nothing resolves, an attestation file can sit where the hooks do not look. The script runs one pass\n276 [R] Selector Binding Refusal :: planning-with-files documents a bug class it had to fix explicitly: *\"Explicit selectors are bindings, not hints (issue #237). This script WRITES ledger rows into the plan dir it picks, so a legacy cwd fallback after a rejected selector files another plan's run history.\"* In other words, when an operator names a plan and the nam\n280 [D] Untracked Local Overlay Layer :: `headroom learn` mines failed sessions and writes corrections to an instruction file, and its default target is `CLAUDE.local.md` — local and gitignored — with the tracked `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md` as explicit opt-ins. That default encodes a governance judgement worth adopting: **machine- and person-specific tuning must not enter \n281 [D] Signal-Preserving Truncation Contract :: Headroom's demo claim is precise: a 10,144-token log compresses to 1,260 tokens **and the `FATAL` line survives byte for byte**. That is a contract, not a capability — the compressor commits to preserving a declared class of lines, and the benchmark verifies it. AIKit truncates constantly and makes no such promise: gate output i\n282 [R] Plugin Contract Test Harness :: The source gives every server plugin its own test file (auth, caching, compression, rate limiting, logging, metrics, graceful shutdown). That is better than no tests, but it does not prove the plugins share a contract. A single harness defines required hooks (`register`, `startup`, `shutdown`), optional hooks, ordering and isola\n290 [D] Trace-Only Learning Loop with Evidence-Linked Lesson Candidates :: ACE's `TraceAnalyser` learns from pre-recorded traces without re-running the task. For AIKit, the same idea applies to dogfood and subagent transcripts: mine repeated failure patterns offline and produce *lesson candidates* with evidence event ids, rather than mutating rules during a live run. A candidate is reviewed by a human \n292 [D] Implementation-Keyed Persistent Memo :: CocoIndex's memo key is the whole idea: `hash(input) + hash(function source)`. The second half is what most caches get wrong, and it is what makes a cache safe to keep across versions. AIKit has the unsafe half: `_extract_symbols_uncached` is memoized in `_SYMBOL_CACHE` keyed by `(file_path, content, strict)`, but the cache is *\n295 [D] Declared Cadence Before Work :: Trailmark's `cadence-first` picks Tier 1/2/3 per block **before** execution: trivial work gets a plan-only path, complex refactors get the full chain, hot-fixes skip the loop. The justification is economic, not aesthetic — a protocol that taxes a one-line fix gets bypassed entirely, and then nothing is enforced. AIKit has exactl\n296 [D] Pre-Flight Reuse Table with Symbol Proof :: Trailmark's `library-first` step is a table with one row per unit of work: *does something in the repository already do this, and if not, how many lines will it take?* The framing matters — \"writing your own is faster than finding someone else's, so without the table it wins by default\" — and it attacks the most common form of a\n297 [D] Irreversible Action Classifier :: Trailmark's rule for publishing, deploying and deleting is a **stop**, not a warning: \"the skill halts and waits for a human. Not a warning printed as it goes; a stop.\" AIKit encodes the same intent as prose in PRINCIPLE-018 (no force-push, explicit staging, never destructive recovery) and implements it narrowly in a few call si\n298 [D] Claim Falsification Gate :: BMAD's `claims-check` states the principle plainly: the plan is \"the change's own account of itself: testimony, not evidence — a claim repeated in a code comment is still the same claim, not confirmation.\" The reviewer extracts each checkable claim (what the change does, what it preserves, ordering, parity with existing code) an\n299 [D] Deletion Intent Audit :: BMAD's `deletion-check` asks a question almost no automated review asks: for each removed or replaced chunk, *did it carry behaviour or a contract that the change neither re-established nor intentionally retired?* Pure renames and whitespace are e