{"parentUuid":"e6c0c3ce-6e6a-465c-ab2f-ec6d47f75dc1","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"fe3259f3-06b4-4656-9c7c-f91ae09159c7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016ReCeRAphmTADrYc9Cherg","type":"tool_result","content":"211\t510 [D] GitHub-Compatible Heading Anchor Computation with Collision Suffixes :: A generated table of contents is only useful if its links resolve. GitHub derives an anchor by lowercasing the heading, removing punctuation, replacing spaces with hyphens and, crucially, appending `-1`, `-2` to a repeated slug. The source's shell pipeline implements only the middle part, so a heading with an emoji, a `+`, a `/`\n212\t511 [R] Atomic Catalogue Build with Temp-and-Replace and Pre-Publish Validatio :: The source truncates its output file before writing the first byte. Any failure after that point leaves a partial catalogue and destroys the last good build. An atomic build writes to a temporary file in the same directory, validates the *complete* result (anchors resolve, required sections present, entry count above a floor, no\n213\t512 [D] Canonical URL Deduplication Across Categories :: A curated list accumulates duplicates because the same tool fits two categories and the URL arrives with a different case, a trailing slash or a tracking parameter. A canonicaliser — lowercase scheme and host, drop `www.`, strip default ports, remove `utm_*`/`ref`/`fbclid`, collapse trailing slashes, normalise a GitHub repo URL \n214\t513 [R] Entry Retirement Tombstone with Successor Pointer :: When an entry is removed from a curated list, its URL often reappears later because nobody recorded why it went away. A tombstone keeps the fact of removal: `retired_at`, `reason` (`dead`, `acquired`, `no_longer_capable`, `licence_changed`, `duplicate`), `successor` (a new URL or entry id) and `superseded_by`. The entry disappea\n215\t514 [D] Structural Document Validator with Ordering, Minimum Content and Place :: `grep -q \"## 摘要\"` proves only that the string appears somewhere; it passes a document whose section is empty, out of order, or only present in the table of contents. A structural validator parses the heading tree, requires each section exactly once at the declared level and in the declared order, requires a minimum non-placehold\n216\t515 [D] Language-Parameterised Section Schema with Paired Heading Anchors :: The README is bilingual (English and 中文 sections with explicit `<a name>` anchors), but the GDD validator checks only Chinese headings. A contributor writing an English GDD therefore fails a validator that cannot read their document. A language-parameterised schema stores one section id with a heading per language, so the same s\n217\t517 [D] ADR Status and Supersession Graph Validator :: The ADR template already carries a closed status vocabulary (`proposed`, `accepted`, `deprecated`, `superseded by ADR-XXXX`) and dependency fields. A validator turns that into a small graph check: every status is from the vocabulary; `superseded by` names an ADR that exists and is `accepted`; an ADR cannot be superseded by itsel\n218\t521 [D] Typed Settings Schema with Range Validation, Defaults and Migration :: Game behaviour is driven by `resources/settings.json` (board size, piece count, win rules, minimax depth, simple moves) with no schema, no ranges and no migration. A single mistyped key changes gameplay or crashes on the AI turn, and a settings file from an older version loads silently with a missing field. A typed schema valida\n219\t522 [D] Content-Addressed Release Manifest with Length-Prefixed Hashing and LF :: A release hash is only comparable if the algorithm and the canonical form are declared. The source names the algorithm (`sha256-length-prefixed-utf8-v1`), normalises line endings before hashing, sorts and excludes declared directories, validates a manifest's `hash_algorithm` and exact file set, and aggregates with length prefixe\n220\t523 [D] Generated-Output Path Policy with Symlink Escape and Compiler-Reserved :: A generator that writes whatever path an adapter returns is one bug away from writing outside the project. The source validates every generated path before write with named diagnostics: empty, absolute, drive-prefixed, `..`, non-portable characters, a path reserved for the compiler, an escape from the project root, and an ancest\n221\t524 [D] Retired-ID Migration Contract Validated at Release Time :: Stable spec-unit ids are referenced by generated artefacts and by project overlays. Removing one silently breaks every consumer that names it. The source makes retirement a release-time contract: a candidate release lists its unit ids, the validator compares them with the previous release, and any removed id must appear in a mig\n222\t525 [D] Explicit Override Declarations with Migration-Only Project Upgrades :: A shared framework and a project's local rules must diverge without forking the framework. The source allows divergence only through an explicit `overrides` list in the project configuration: a project names the unit it replaces, the compiler records the mapping, and `upgrade` previews every change and applies `project.yaml`, th\n223\t526 [R] Upstream Source Revalidation for Dossiers :: Every dossier in this register opens with three facts about an upstream project: its URL, its licence, and the commit or date analysed. Those facts are the basis on which every idea in the dossier is read — a mechanism copied from an MIT project is a different proposition from one copied from an AGPL project, and \"archived last \n224\t529 [D] Authorised Target Envelope for Outbound Actions :: The engagement template declares four things before any tool runs: who authorised the work, when, under which document, and exactly which targets are in scope — with an explicit out-of-scope list and rules of engagement. That is the correct precondition for acting on systems you do not own, and it is structured data, not prose, \n225\t532 [D] Alternating-Pass Flakiness Detector :: The README admits a suite that passes on alternate runs and does not know why. The pattern is specific and mechanical: **run the suite twice in the same process** (and once in a fresh process), and compare per-test outcomes. A test whose result differs between the second in-process run and the first is order- or state-dependent;\n226\t535 [D] Follow-On Objective on a Completed Task :: The Balatro agent finishes its goal at Ante 8, stops, and can then be restarted against the *same live run* for a second objective (endless mode collection) with a different termination rule. State survives; objectives are sequential; each objective is declared. AIKit's ledger treats completion as terminal. When work genuinely c\n227\t536 [D] Reference-Implementation Differential Suite :: When an optimisation replaces a trusted implementation, the strongest cheap evidence that behaviour is preserved is agreement on a corpus: same input, both implementations, compare the observable result. Where they agree, the optimisation is plausibly safe; where they disagree, one of them is wrong and the corpus localises it. T\n228\t540 [D] Subsystem-Indexed Plan Archive :: A completed task's plan currently exists only inside the ledger, indexed by task id and time. To learn why `scripts/disruption_recovery.py` has its present shape, a reader must know which task touched it and then read the ledger. The source project instead keeps each plan next to the code, so browsing a directory surfaces the de\n229\t544 [D] Declared Verification Posture per Project :: The memory file says what proof this project can produce: manual in-editor/runtime validation plus a successful C++ build, and no dedicated suite. An agent reading it knows to run the build, exercise the feature in the editor, and describe what it observed — instead of writing \"tests pass\" about a suite that does not exist. `IDE\n230\t546 [D] Virtual Time Controller for the Test Harness :: Gua offers deterministic virtual time as a capability of the runtime: time can be advanced under the harness's control, so behaviour that depends on duration is exercised without waiting. That is the missing primitive behind a recurring constraint in this register — AIKit forbids sleeps in unit tests (10 ms budget, no spawns, no\n231\t547 [D] Structural Assertions on Declared Graphs :: The first two categories in the taxonomy assert properties of the *definition*, not its behaviour: existence, acyclicity, task count, and each node's incoming and outgoing edges. They catch a class of regression that behavioural tests structurally cannot — a deleted dependency leaves every remaining test green, because the tests\n232\t548 [D] Expected-Denial Conformance Assertions as First-Class Passes :: Most test suites assert only that the happy path works, so a suite that verifies a *denial* passes silently when the denial stops happening. The blueprint inverts the convention: two of five steps must be **blocked**, and a success there is the failure. AIKit's gates already check that secrets are absent and that paths do not es\n233\t549 [D] Layered-Control Evidence Model with Per-Layer Proof :: A single \"network blocked\" test does not tell you *which* control blocked it. The blueprint keeps two layers apart — FQDN allow-listing at a DNS proxy and L3/L4 allow-listing at the network layer — gives each its own manifest directory and its own expected failure mode, and documents that the two layers have two separate observa\n234\t550 [D] Isolation-Tier Attestation in the Run Receipt :: The same manifest runs on two tiers: gVisor on Standard EKS and runc on Auto Mode. The script picks the right template, but nothing in the artifact says which tier actually executed, so two \"passing\" conformance runs can prove different things. An attestation records the tier, the runtime class, the template name and the detecti\n235\t551 [D] Configuration Precedence Resolution with Source Provenance :: The conformance script resolves the region through a documented chain and records why in a comment: a hard-coded default is a silent wrong-region failure. The pattern generalises to every multi-source setting in a kit — an explicit flag, a project config, an environment variable, a user config, a default — where the danger is no\n236\t552 [D] Read-Once Project Context Artefact with Schema, Invalidation and Prove :: The library solves context repetition with one file every skill reads. That works until the file drifts: nothing records which repository state it was drafted from, when it was last confirmed, or which sections a human actually reviewed. A context artefact with declared sections, a source digest and a review timestamp turns \"the\n237\t553 [R] Connector Credential Custody: No Plaintext Token File in the Work Tree :: A connector skill needs an OAuth refresh token to read an ad account. The source's setup writes the developer token, client secret and refresh token into `.agents/google-ads-credentials.json` in the project, and the skill text tells the agent to create that file. Three failures follow: the path is inside the repository, the form\n238\t554 [D] Distribution Manifest ↔ Tree Parity Validation :: `clawhub.yaml` lists 37 skill paths, the README says 37, the banner in the README markup says 33, and nothing compares any of them to the directory. A parity check is cheap and catches the whole class: every manifest entry exists and has a `SKILL.md`; every skill directory appears in the manifest; declared `version` matches the \n239\t555 [D] Trigger-Phrase Collision and Coverage Audit Across a Skill Library :: In this library the `description` front-matter is not documentation; it is the router. Each one enumerates trigger phrases (\"audit my Google Ads\", \"find wasted spend\", \"why are my ads not converting\") so the host can select the skill. With 37 skills that is a shared namespace: two skills can claim the same phrase, a skill can cl\n240\t556 [D] Tri-State Verification Exit Semantics with Inconclusive Never Passing :: A verification tool has three outcomes, not two: it found a problem, it verified the property, or it could not form a verdict. Collapsing the third into \"pass\" is the most common way a gate lies. The source separates them in the exit code (`0` clean, `1` findings, `2` inconclusive), zeroes the score when inconclusive rather than\n241\t558 [D] Detected-Guardrail Disclosure in the Scan Report :: The source fingerprints the target's defences — Prompt Shield, Llama Guard and similar — and uses that fingerprint to *pick attacks that get around them*. The operator never sees the fingerprint, so a scan that adapted around a specific guardrail looks identical to one that simply failed. Disclosure inverts that: report which gu\n242\t560 [D] Failure-Class to Tool-Class Routing Table for Test Selection :: Tool lists are organised by product category, but a team chooses a tool because of a *failure it keeps having*: locators break on every deploy, triage takes a day, assertions are ambiguous, coverage is a number nobody trusts. A routing table maps each observed failure class to the tool classes that address it, the evidence you w\n243\t561 [D] Category-Specific Evidence Checklist for Vendor Claims :: The list's categories imply what evidence should exist. A self-healing tool must report a heal rate over repeated runs; an LLM-as-judge product must report agreement with human labels; a visual tool must explain baseline management and false-positive handling; a test-generation tool must report mutation-score or coverage deltas \n244\t564 [D] Hermetic Call Fixture Bundle with Strict Decode and Ordered Calls :: A regression test for an agent is only cheap and deterministic if the tool calls and model completions are recorded. Recording them as an *unordered map* loses the information that matters: the same tool called twice with different results, a second call that errored, the specific `tool_call_id` an assistant message referenced. \n245\t565 [R] Fail-to-Pass Promotion Contract for Regression Cases :: A regression test is only worth keeping if it would have caught the bug. The source makes that a promotion condition: a failing trace becomes a case only when the case **fails against the old code and passes against the fix**. Without it, a promoted case often passes everywhere — it encodes the fixed behaviour rather than the fa\n246\t566 [D] Per-Scenario Score-Drop Comparison with Unit-Scale Filtering :: A gate can pass every case while quality slides: each conversation clears its bar and the judge's mean falls from 0.9 to 0.6. The source compares the *distribution* between the last green gate and the current run, but compares **per scenario first and then averages over the scenarios both runs drove**, because pooling scores acr\n247\t567 [D] Incomplete as a Blocking Gate Count, Never Folded :: A gate counts passed, failed and skipped. A case whose required checks could not run — the judge was unavailable, execution errored — belongs in none of those buckets, and folding it into \"passed\" hides a hole while folding it into \"failed\" sends someone debugging the wrong thing. The source adds a distinct, blocking `incomplete\n248\t568 [D] Bug Diary Contract: Reproduction, Root Cause, Fix and Guarding Test :: A changelog says what changed; a bug diary says what was learned. The source requires four fields per failure — how it was reproduced, the actual root cause, the change made, and the regression test that now catches it — plus at least one failure found beyond the supplied cases, and a baseline-versus-final comparison. That struc\n249\t573 [R] Runtime Path Policy for Request-Controlled Paths with Documented Autho :: When a tool accepts a filesystem path from a caller, lexical containment is not enough: `..` is easy, but a symlink inside the root that points outside defeats a string check. The source resolves the existing prefix with `realpath`, canonicalises the would-be path segment by segment, checks containment **twice** (before and afte\n250\t575 [R] Orphaned Sandbox Reaping by Ownership Label :: A long-lived agent that starts containers will eventually leave orphans: a crash between create and record, a cancelled run, a killed worker. The source ships a dedicated reaper module rather than relying on shutdown hooks. The pattern is small but the discipline is the point: every sandbox gets an ownership label (run id, proje\n251\t577 [D] Failure-Classified Fallback Eligibility :: The source's fallback matrix maps a binary to a binary — but the *reason* a binary failed determines whether a substitute helps. A missing tool (`FileNotFoundError`) is fixable by substitution; a permission error is not (the substitute will fail too, or worse, succeed with different semantics); a timeout may need a longer budget\n252\t578 [R] Signed and Encrypted Agent Envelope with a Replay Window :: Inter-agent messages can cross a boundary the orchestrator does not control — a worker container, another host, a queue. The source defines an envelope with a header (sender, receiver, timestamp), an encrypted payload (X25519 key exchange, AES-256-GCM, nonce and salt) and an Ed25519 signature over header and payload. That combin\n253\t579 [D] Persisted Recovery Ledger with Substitution Attribution :: The source keeps `recovery_history` as an in-memory list appended on each substitution. That is enough for a single process and useless for a post-mortem: the run ends, the list dies, and nobody can answer \"how often did this tool get substituted, and did the substitute succeed?\". A persisted ledger keyed by run id records the f\n254\t582 [D] Single-Writer Protocol with Claim-Lost Reporting :: When several workers share a task graph, the dangerous outcome is not a conflict but a *silent* conflict: two workers both believe they claimed an intent, both do the work, and the second write overwrites the first. The source makes the dispatcher the single writer, and a competing claim returns without applying the write; the t\n255\t583 [D] Execution-Mode Declaration with Unsafe-Combination Refusal :: The source has two execution modes with materially different guarantees: container isolation, and local mode where agents run with the user's permissions and **no sandbox**. It documents the rule (\"run the dispatcher directly on the host — not inside Docker — since the agents run with your user's permissions and no sandbox\") and\n256\t584 [D] Declared Run Matrix with Per-Cell Isolation and Identity :: The suite is written once and parameterised by a declaration (`phones: [...]` in YAML). The fixture is `scope=\"session\"` and `params=test_phones`, so pytest creates one instance per cell, and setup failures abort with the cell named. Execution identity (platform, device uuid, package, build parameters, app version) is written in\n257\t585 [D] Resolved-Run Stamp in the Gate Report :: `config.py` writes the platform, app package and build parameters into `allure-results/environment.xml` before the run, and records the installed app version read from the device. A report therefore answers \"which build, on which device, with which parameters\" without archaeology. The transferable part is not Allure: it is that \n258\t586 [D] Boundary-Attached Retry with a Typed Terminal Error :: The repository binds `tenacity` retry behaviour onto locator and device objects once, in the page-object base class, instead of wrapping each test. The harness distinguishes \"the element never appeared\" (a test failure) from \"the device could not be reconnected\" (an infrastructure failure) by which retry wrapper raised. AIKit's \n259\t587 [D] Applied-Constant Stamp on Heuristic Results :: The suite sets `ST.THRESHOLD_STRICT = 0.6` for image matching. That single number decides pass or fail, and it is invisible in the result. When the same test passes locally and fails on a different device, the threshold is the first hypothesis and the last thing recorded. Generalised: any heuristic gate — similarity, fuzzy match\n260\t596 [D] Certifiability as a Gate Outcome with Declared-Risk Obligations :: The gate returns four distinct answers: pass, new failure, **not certifiable**, and **inconclusive**. The distinction is enforced in code and in precedence: an infrastructure error (missing fixture, worker did not start, suite/source/baseline mismatch) outranks a behavioural failure, because \"a suite that only half executed cann\n261\t597 [D] Budgeted 1-Minimal Failure Reduction Preserving the Signature :: Given a failing scenario (a transcript, a rule set, a checklist, a commit range), repeatedly delete one item at a time in a declared dimension order and keep the deletion only if the failure signature is unchanged. The search stops when a full sweep accepts nothing (locally minimal) or when candidate/round budgets are exhausted,\n262\t598 [D] Socket-Layer Egress Denial for Offline Test Runs :: Install a process-level guard that replaces `socket.socket.connect` and `socket.create_connection` with a function that raises a typed `NetworkAccessDenied` naming the destination. Because every practical Python client (`urllib`, `requests`, `httpx`, `aiohttp`, raw `socket`) reaches one of those two functions, the guard cannot b\n263\t600 [D] Authorization Scope Artifact Required Before the First Side Effect :: Before any action with side effects, a scope artifact must exist and contain two facts: that authorization was granted, for which target, and under which network profile; and the case must be initialised from it. A guard command re-reads the artifact and exits `2` with a readable reason when it is incomplete. Crucially, the sour\n264\t602 [D] Twin-Implementation Parity Gate from One JSON Contract :: Routing semantics live in one structured configuration (`skills/config/routing.json`) and are implemented twice (PowerShell, Bash) because the platforms need different entry points. The contract is explicit: the platform \"only changes the execution entry point, not routing semantics\". A regression benchmark of 175 cases runs on \n265\t608 [R] Actionable Diagnostic Contract: Code, Path, Message, Fix :: Every problem is one object: `severity`, `code`, `path`, `message`, `fix`. The `path` is a machine-readable location (a JSON path in the game, a file path in the script case), the `code` is stable enough to search and test, and `fix` is a sentence an agent can act on without understanding the validator. The validator also comput\n266\t611 [D] A Separate \"Finished?\" Review Pass with Area-Tagged Findings :: A review pass distinct from correctness checks: it loads every artefact, refuses to review those that fail validation, and then emits findings tagged by area (`proof`, `feel`, `looks`, `identity` in the source) with a severity of `issue` or `idea`, a location, and a fix. The report is actionable and the pass is advisory unless `\n267\t615 [D] Status Payload Schema Version with Additive-Only Evolution :: The kernel's status endpoint is consumed by a dashboard and by scripts. The scaffold has no schema marker, so a consumer cannot tell an added field from a changed one, and a removed field fails at the consumer. The rule: the status payload carries `schema_version` (a major integer and a minor integer), additive changes bump the \n268\t616 [R] Surface Parity Contract over One Core :: Munk exposes one engine through CLI, local API, MCP and a web UI, and names the reason: otherwise each surface grows its own business logic. The invariant is that a capability exists once in the core and every surface is a thin adapter to it. AIKit has four surfaces with no such invariant. `aikit.py` exposes subcommands; the `sc\n269\t617 [D] Evidence-Driven Guidance Revision in Shadow Mode :: Munk's self-improvement targets the *guidance* that shapes the next plan: failures become \"knowledge candidates and optimization hints\". The distinction from lesson capture matters. `IDEA-209` produces a note for a human; `IDEA-404` counts how often a written rule is broken. Neither proposes a concrete edit to the text that stee\n270\t618 [D] Declared External Toolchain Manifest :: PentestAgent's Docker mode exists because an autonomous security run needs `nmap`, `sqlmap` and friends to *be there*. The container is the declared environment: it carries the toolchain the framework's commands assume, so a command either works or the environment is wrong — never a half-configured host. AIKit runs commands that\n271\t627 [D] Target-Parametric Cases over Declared Adapters :: The documentation's claim is that one engine runs many scenario kinds because each kind is a plugin behind a common execution model. The useful consequence is that a test case expresses *intent and assertions* once, and the mechanism that reaches the system under test is an adapter chosen by declaration. AIKit's behaviour is ver\n272\t628 [D] Skill Applicability Contract with Negative Scope :: The library makes selection machine-readable: `best_for` states the situations a skill serves, `scenarios` gives example invocations, `estimated_time` states cost, and the body states what the skill is *not*. Together those four fields answer the question an agent actually asks — \"is this the right tool, and is it worth it\" — wi"}]},"uuid":"59484baf-55aa-4761-8a27-b860dff57f6a","timestamp":"2026-10-29T18:49:08.002Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\YUNUS-~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\c--Users-Yunus-Emre-Desktop-portable-agent-kit\\05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787\\scratchpad\\ak_mech.txt","content":"510 [D] GitHub-Compatible Heading Anchor Computation with Collision Suffixes :: A generated table of contents is only useful if its links resolve. GitHub derives an anchor by lowercasing the heading, removing punctuation, replacing spaces with hyphens and, crucially, appending `-1`, `-2` to a repeated slug. The source's shell pipeline implements only the middle part, so a heading with an emoji, a `+`, a `/`\n511 [R] Atomic Catalogue Build with Temp-and-Replace and Pre-Publish Validatio :: The source truncates its output file before writing the first byte. Any failure after that point leaves a partial catalogue and destroys the last good build. An atomic build writes to a temporary file in the same directory, validates the *complete* result (anchors resolve, required sections present, entry count above a floor, no\n512 [D] Canonical URL Deduplication Across Categories :: A curated list accumulates duplicates because the same tool fits two categories and the URL arrives with a different case, a trailing slash or a tracking parameter. A canonicaliser — lowercase scheme and host, drop `www.`, strip default ports, remove `utm_*`/`ref`/`fbclid`, collapse trailing slashes, normalise a GitHub repo URL \n513 [R] Entry Retirement Tombstone with Successor Pointer :: When an entry is removed from a curated list, its URL often reappears later because nobody recorded why it went away. A tombstone keeps the fact of removal: `retired_at`, `reason` (`dead`, `acquired`, `no_longer_capable`, `licence_changed`, `duplicate`), `successor` (a new URL or entry id) and `superseded_by`. The entry disappea\n514 [D] Structural Document Validator with Ordering, Minimum Content and Place :: `grep -q \"## 摘要\"` proves only that the string appears somewhere; it passes a document whose section is empty, out of order, or only present in the table of contents. A structural validator parses the heading tree, requires each section exactly once at the declared level and in the declared order, requires a minimum non-placehold\n515 [D] Language-Parameterised Section Schema with Paired Heading Anchors :: The README is bilingual (English and 中文 sections with explicit `<a name>` anchors), but the GDD validator checks only Chinese headings. A contributor writing an English GDD therefore fails a validator that cannot read their document. A language-parameterised schema stores one section id with a heading per language, so the same s\n517 [D] ADR Status and Supersession Graph Validator :: The ADR template already carries a closed status vocabulary (`proposed`, `accepted`, `deprecated`, `superseded by ADR-XXXX`) and dependency fields. A validator turns that into a small graph check: every status is from the vocabulary; `superseded by` names an ADR that exists and is `accepted`; an ADR cannot be superseded by itsel\n521 [D] Typed Settings Schema with Range Validation, Defaults and Migration :: Game behaviour is driven by `resources/settings.json` (board size, piece count, win rules, minimax depth, simple moves) with no schema, no ranges and no migration. A single mistyped key changes gameplay or crashes on the AI turn, and a settings file from an older version loads silently with a missing field. A typed schema valida\n522 [D] Content-Addressed Release Manifest with Length-Prefixed Hashing and LF :: A release hash is only comparable if the algorithm and the canonical form are declared. The source names the algorithm (`sha256-length-prefixed-utf8-v1`), normalises line endings before hashing, sorts and excludes declared directories, validates a manifest's `hash_algorithm` and exact file set, and aggregates with length prefixe\n523 [D] Generated-Output Path Policy with Symlink Escape and Compiler-Reserved :: A generator that writes whatever path an adapter returns is one bug away from writing outside the project. The source validates every generated path before write with named diagnostics: empty, absolute, drive-prefixed, `..`, non-portable characters, a path reserved for the compiler, an escape from the project root, and an ancest\n524 [D] Retired-ID Migration Contract Validated at Release Time :: Stable spec-unit ids are referenced by generated artefacts and by project overlays. Removing one silently breaks every consumer that names it. The source makes retirement a release-time contract: a candidate release lists its unit ids, the validator compares them with the previous release, and any removed id must appear in a mig\n525 [D] Explicit Override Declarations with Migration-Only Project Upgrades :: A shared framework and a project's local rules must diverge without forking the framework. The source allows divergence only through an explicit `overrides` list in the project configuration: a project names the unit it replaces, the compiler records the mapping, and `upgrade` previews every change and applies `project.yaml`, th\n526 [R] Upstream Source Revalidation for Dossiers :: Every dossier in this register opens with three facts about an upstream project: its URL, its licence, and the commit or date analysed. Those facts are the basis on which every idea in the dossier is read — a mechanism copied from an MIT project is a different proposition from one copied from an AGPL project, and \"archived last \n529 [D] Authorised Target Envelope for Outbound Actions :: The engagement template declares four things before any tool runs: who authorised the work, when, under which document, and exactly which targets are in scope — with an explicit out-of-scope list and rules of engagement. That is the correct precondition for acting on systems you do not own, and it is structured data, not prose, \n532 [D] Alternating-Pass Flakiness Detector :: The README admits a suite that passes on alternate runs and does not know why. The pattern is specific and mechanical: **run the suite twice in the same process** (and once in a fresh process), and compare per-test outcomes. A test whose result differs between the second in-process run and the first is order- or state-dependent;\n535 [D] Follow-On Objective on a Completed Task :: The Balatro agent finishes its goal at Ante 8, stops, and can then be restarted against the *same live run* for a second objective (endless mode collection) with a different termination rule. State survives; objectives are sequential; each objective is declared. AIKit's ledger treats completion as terminal. When work genuinely c\n536 [D] Reference-Implementation Differential Suite :: When an optimisation replaces a trusted implementation, the strongest cheap evidence that behaviour is preserved is agreement on a corpus: same input, both implementations, compare the observable result. Where they agree, the optimisation is plausibly safe; where they disagree, one of them is wrong and the corpus localises it. T\n540 [D] Subsystem-Indexed Plan Archive :: A completed task's plan currently exists only inside the ledger, indexed by task id and time. To learn why `scripts/disruption_recovery.py` has its present shape, a reader must know which task touched it and then read the ledger. The source project instead keeps each plan next to the code, so browsing a directory surfaces the de\n544 [D] Declared Verification Posture per Project :: The memory file says what proof this project can produce: manual in-editor/runtime validation plus a successful C++ build, and no dedicated suite. An agent reading it knows to run the build, exercise the feature in the editor, and describe what it observed — instead of writing \"tests pass\" about a suite that does not exist. `IDE\n546 [D] Virtual Time Controller for the Test Harness :: Gua offers deterministic virtual time as a capability of the runtime: time can be advanced under the harness's control, so behaviour that depends on duration is exercised without waiting. That is the missing primitive behind a recurring constraint in this register — AIKit forbids sleeps in unit tests (10 ms budget, no spawns, no\n547 [D] Structural Assertions on Declared Graphs :: The first two categories in the taxonomy assert properties of the *definition*, not its behaviour: existence, acyclicity, task count, and each node's incoming and outgoing edges. They catch a class of regression that behavioural tests structurally cannot — a deleted dependency leaves every remaining test green, because the tests\n548 [D] Expected-Denial Conformance Assertions as First-Class Passes :: Most test suites assert only that the happy path works, so a suite that verifies a *denial* passes silently when the denial stops happening. The blueprint inverts the convention: two of five steps must be **blocked**, and a success there is the failure. AIKit's gates already check that secrets are absent and that paths do not es\n549 [D] Layered-Control Evidence Model with Per-Layer Proof :: A single \"network blocked\" test does not tell you *which* control blocked it. The blueprint keeps two layers apart — FQDN allow-listing at a DNS proxy and L3/L4 allow-listing at the network layer — gives each its own manifest directory and its own expected failure mode, and documents that the two layers have two separate observa\n550 [D] Isolation-Tier Attestation in the Run Receipt :: The same manifest runs on two tiers: gVisor on Standard EKS and runc on Auto Mode. The script picks the right template, but nothing in the artifact says which tier actually executed, so two \"passing\" conformance runs can prove different things. An attestation records the tier, the runtime class, the template name and the detecti\n551 [D] Configuration Precedence Resolution with Source Provenance :: The conformance script resolves the region through a documented chain and records why in a comment: a hard-coded default is a silent wrong-region failure. The pattern generalises to every multi-source setting in a kit — an explicit flag, a project config, an environment variable, a user config, a default — where the danger is no\n552 [D] Read-Once Project Context Artefact with Schema, Invalidation and Prove :: The library solves context repetition with one file every skill reads. That works until the file drifts: nothing records which repository state it was drafted from, when it was last confirmed, or which sections a human actually reviewed. A context artefact with declared sections, a source digest and a review timestamp turns \"the\n553 [R] Connector Credential Custody: No Plaintext Token File in the Work Tree :: A connector skill needs an OAuth refresh token to read an ad account. The source's setup writes the developer token, client secret and refresh token into `.agents/google-ads-credentials.json` in the project, and the skill text tells the agent to create that file. Three failures follow: the path is inside the repository, the form\n554 [D] Distribution Manifest ↔ Tree Parity Validation :: `clawhub.yaml` lists 37 skill paths, the README says 37, the banner in the README markup says 33, and nothing compares any of them to the directory. A parity check is cheap and catches the whole class: every manifest entry exists and has a `SKILL.md`; every skill directory appears in the manifest; declared `version` matches the \n555 [D] Trigger-Phrase Collision and Coverage Audit Across a Skill Library :: In this library the `description` front-matter is not documentation; it is the router. Each one enumerates trigger phrases (\"audit my Google Ads\", \"find wasted spend\", \"why are my ads not converting\") so the host can select the skill. With 37 skills that is a shared namespace: two skills can claim the same phrase, a skill can cl\n556 [D] Tri-State Verification Exit Semantics with Inconclusive Never Passing :: A verification tool has three outcomes, not two: it found a problem, it verified the property, or it could not form a verdict. Collapsing the third into \"pass\" is the most common way a gate lies. The source separates them in the exit code (`0` clean, `1` findings, `2` inconclusive), zeroes the score when inconclusive rather than\n558 [D] Detected-Guardrail Disclosure in the Scan Report :: The source fingerprints the target's defences — Prompt Shield, Llama Guard and similar — and uses that fingerprint to *pick attacks that get around them*. The operator never sees the fingerprint, so a scan that adapted around a specific guardrail looks identical to one that simply failed. Disclosure inverts that: report which gu\n560 [D] Failure-Class to Tool-Class Routing Table for Test Selection :: Tool lists are organised by product category, but a team chooses a tool because of a *failure it keeps having*: locators break on every deploy, triage takes a day, assertions are ambiguous, coverage is a number nobody trusts. A routing table maps each observed failure class to the tool classes that address it, the evidence you w\n561 [D] Category-Specific Evidence Checklist for Vendor Claims :: The list's categories imply what evidence should exist. A self-healing tool must report a heal rate over repeated runs; an LLM-as-judge product must report agreement with human labels; a visual tool must explain baseline management and false-positive handling; a test-generation tool must report mutation-score or coverage deltas \n564 [D] Hermetic Call Fixture Bundle with Strict Decode and Ordered Calls :: A regression test for an agent is only cheap and deterministic if the tool calls and model completions are recorded. Recording them as an *unordered map* loses the information that matters: the same tool called twice with different results, a second call that errored, the specific `tool_call_id` an assistant message referenced. \n565 [R] Fail-to-Pass Promotion Contract for Regression Cases :: A regression test is only worth keeping if it would have caught the bug. The source makes that a promotion condition: a failing trace becomes a case only when the case **fails against the old code and passes against the fix**. Without it, a promoted case often passes everywhere — it encodes the fixed behaviour rather than the fa\n566 [D] Per-Scenario Score-Drop Comparison with Unit-Scale Filtering :: A gate can pass every case while quality slides: each conversation clears its bar and the judge's mean falls from 0.9 to 0.6. The source compares the *distribution* between the last green gate and the current run, but compares **per scenario first and then averages over the scenarios both runs drove**, because pooling scores acr\n567 [D] Incomplete as a Blocking Gate Count, Never Folded :: A gate counts passed, failed and skipped. A case whose required checks could not run — the judge was unavailable, execution errored — belongs in none of those buckets, and folding it into \"passed\" hides a hole while folding it into \"failed\" sends someone debugging the wrong thing. The source adds a distinct, blocking `incomplete\n568 [D] Bug Diary Contract: Reproduction, Root Cause, Fix and Guarding Test :: A changelog says what changed; a bug diary says what was learned. The source requires four fields per failure — how it was reproduced, the actual root cause, the change made, and the regression test that now catches it — plus at least one failure found beyond the supplied cases, and a baseline-versus-final comparison. That struc\n573 [R] Runtime Path Policy for Request-Controlled Paths with Documented Autho :: When a tool accepts a filesystem path from a caller, lexical containment is not enough: `..` is easy, but a symlink inside the root that points outside defeats a string check. The source resolves the existing prefix with `realpath`, canonicalises the would-be path segment by segment, checks containment **twice** (before and afte\n575 [R] Orphaned Sandbox Reaping by Ownership Label :: A long-lived agent that starts containers will eventually leave orphans: a crash between create and record, a cancelled run, a killed worker. The source ships a dedicated reaper module rather than relying on shutdown hooks. The pattern is small but the discipline is the point: every sandbox gets an ownership label (run id, proje\n577 [D] Failure-Classified Fallback Eligibility :: The source's fallback matrix maps a binary to a binary — but the *reason* a binary failed determines whether a substitute helps. A missing tool (`FileNotFoundError`) is fixable by substitution; a permission error is not (the substitute will fail too, or worse, succeed with different semantics); a timeout may need a longer budget\n578 [R] Signed and Encrypted Agent Envelope with a Replay Win