-repo <path> --project <target>  # register delivered IDEA-NNN references and refresh MATRIX.md\n```\n- Reforge in one paragraph: window N is `start + step*(N-1)`, capped by `stop`; a stage inspects only the lines its own commits add and attributes each finding to the commit that introduced it. Five Python rules repair mechanically (`bare-except`, `mutable-default`, `shell-true`, `verify-false`, `unsafe-yaml-load`), each with a generated regression test; a line marked `hygiene: allow <reason>` is honoured, not reported; every other Python rule, every text check and every host finding is reported for a human, or applied only through a validated replacement. A red test or gate restores the exact bytes, and `--commit`/`--push` stage only reviewed paths plus `.aikit/reforge.json`, never forcing (in a project that file is committed memory; at AIKit's own root `.aikit/` is git-ignored tool state, so only reviewed paths are committed). Reforge may repair beside unrelated uncommitted work: those paths are snapshotted, never staged, and a commit is refused only when a finding's own target file was already dirty (`--allow-dirty` overrides). Python files use the opt-in AST detectors (`--rules`) and every other changed text file uses `reforge/textdetect.py`, so only binary files print as `[SKIP] ... not analysed` instead of passing silently. `audit`/`run` write each new commit's own hunks, oldest to newest, to `.aikit/reforge.reviews/` with credential spans redacted; a later hunk may correct an earlier one, so findings must quote the current file. No model is called and no subagent starts. The host agent reads that packet and returns `path`, `line`, `severity`, `kind`, `message`, `evidence`, optional `commit`/`hunk`, optional `reviewed_hunks` coverage and an optional exact `fix`; `fix --findings answer.json` validates the answer against the stage (path, line, commit, hunk, current evidence, exact-once replacement, stage overlap, Python parse) and applies it. Each project has it