e.\n045 [D] Fair-Baseline A/B Arms Eval with Correctness Gate & Median A :: The benchmark compares the *real agent without the skill* against the agent with the skill (plus cheap controls like a one-line YAGNI prompt), on seeded tasks, scoring the files left behind. Correctness is a gate, not a metric; a safety tier runs the produced code against adversarial input; tests wr\n046 [D] Sub-Agent Policy Propagation with Session-Keyed Mode State & :: Session-level instructions reach only the parent thread, so spawned subagents run unaware of the active policy until a `SubagentStart` hook injects it. The mode (`lite|full|ultra`) lives in a flag file, and an opt-in regex scopes which subagent types receive it. NeuroCore's orchestrator must guarant\n050 [D] Self-Consistency Majority Vote with Agreement-Threshold Esca :: Sample several independent answers and take the most common one. The agreement ratio is also a free confidence signal: low agreement means the question is hard or ambiguous. NeuroCore can use it for classification-like decisions (routing, guardrail verdicts) and escalate to a stronger model or a hum\n051 [R] PAL-Style Deterministic Compute Tool (Whitelisted AST Evalua :: Instead of the model computing dates or arithmetic in prose, it emits a small program and an interpreter produces the answer. In the source the generated code is executed directly. For NeuroCore the same benefit comes without `exec`: a `compute` tool that evaluates a restricted expression via a whit\n052 [D] Gated Prompt Chain with Per-Step Schema Validation :: Prompt chaining splits a task into steps where each output feeds the next; the workflows-vs-agents guidance says to prefer such fixed workflows when the path is predictable, and agents only when it is not. Adding a validation gate between steps stops one bad intermediate output from poisoning the re\n061 [D] Pinned Skill/Prompt Integrity Manifest with Explicit Re-Pin  :: Skills and prompts are executable behaviour. The source lets operators view recent skill-file diffs from git history. A stronger form is a manifest of SHA-256 digests for each skill and prompt file that is checked at load time, so a modified file is refused until a human re-pins it.\n062 [D] Behavioral Drift Canary with Protected Baseline Updates :: A fixed set of deterministic questions is asked of the configured model and the answers are compared with a stored baseline by a similarity score; below a threshold means drift (a silent model or provider change). This protects NeuroCore flows that assume stable model behaviour.\n064 [R] Autonomy Budget Guard with Pre-Execution Blocking & 80% Warn :: Agents running multi-turn autonomous loops risk runaway execution, high token costs, or excessive tool calls. Foundry's `BudgetState` tracks per-run and daily limits across tool calls, LLM turns, monetary cost, and irreversible actions. Crucially, it supports `enforce=True`, checking limits *before*\n067 [D] Multi-Granularity Selective Journal Rollback (`RewindEngine` :: When an agent drifts during a multi-step task, simply killing the session discards all progress, while basic conversation reset loses tool outputs. `RewindEngine` offers multi-granularity rollbacks over the execution journal: - `BOTH`: Full rollback of conversation and tool state to target entry. - \n073 [D] Checksum-Validated, Payload-Preserving PII Guard :: Agno's PII guard shows the right shape: per-type detectors, a policy that either rejects the input with a typed trigger or masks the matches, and custom patterns compiled at construction so a bad regex fails immediately. Our target must go further because NeuroCore handles meshed text, attachments a\n074 [D] Execution-Evidence Tool-Call Reliability Eval :: An evaluation that credits the model for **requesting** a tool is trivially satisfiable: a refused, errored or ill-argumented call still counts. Agno grades from executions, excludes history-injected calls, treats only clean (not errored, not paused) executions as passes, and annotates a missing exp\n080 [D] Symmetric Tool Envelope with Actionable `next_steps` and Typ :: When a tool fails, the model's next action is determined almost entirely by what the tool returned. PageIndex shapes every tool result into one envelope: success adds `success: True` plus `next_steps`; failure carries `error`, an optional stable `errorCode`, diagnostic `details` and a `next_steps` o\n083 [D] Non-Destructive JSON Reply Extractor with Typed Non-Parse Ou :: Model replies arrive wrapped in prose, fenced in backticks, or carrying a stray trailing comma. PageIndex's `extract_json` targets exactly that problem, but its repair steps are destructive: it replaces every `None` substring with `null` (corrupting the word inside a legitimate string), collapses al\n089 [D] Semver-Normalized Skill Catalogue Version Contract :: NeuroCore imports skills and versions them with an internal monotonic integer counter, but an imported catalogue entry also carries its own declared version, and nothing validates that string. The source shows the cost: `version` is a **required** frontmatter field, yet the corpus mixes `version: 1.\n094 [D] Measured Token-Budget Catalogue Projection :: NeuroCore loads skills into a model context, and a catalogue large enough to be useful is large enough to blow the window. The source identifies the right problem — \"when all skills are loaded they exceed the model's token limit\" — and demonstrates almost every way to get the fix wrong: a `minimal` \n099 [D] Context Load-Reason Manifest with Eager/Lazy Boundary Enforc :: NeuroCore assembles a system prompt and a set of instruction and skill files. The source documents the harness rule precisely: ancestor instruction files load at startup, descendant files load only when a file in that subtree is touched, siblings never load, and nested skill directories are discover\n100 [D] Fail-Open Event Sink with Redaction, Strict JSONL and Typed  :: An observability hook has two requirements that pull in opposite directions: it must never break the thing it observes, and it must produce a record that is actually usable later. The source gets the first right — every path, including a JSON parse failure and an unexpected exception, exits 0 with a\n101 [D] Declarative Agent Capability Profile with Static Contradicti :: An agent's blast radius is decided before it runs, by a handful of declaration fields: which tools it may call, which are removed, what permission mode applies, how many turns it gets, which model and effort it uses, and whether it is isolated. The failure mode is not a bad prompt; it is a **declara\n106 [D] Effect-Class-Tagged Hash-Chained Capsule Journal with Defaul :: NeuroCore persists session and run history, but a run record does not say *how much of the world it touched*, and it is not tamper-evident. The source's capsule journal is a compact answer to both. Each entry declares one of five ordered **side-effect classes** — `SE0` read-only, `SE1` reversible lo\n109 [R] Bounded Streaming Hook-Input Scanner with Truncation-Fail-Cl :: A lifecycle hook receives an unbounded JSON document on stdin, and the first thing it needs is usually two strings: the event name and the tool name. Reading all of stdin to get them is a memory-exhaustion vector, and a hook that crashes on a huge or malformed payload either takes the agent down wit\n116 [D] Similarity Merge Guarded by Adversarial Blocking Rules :: NeuroCore accumulates memory entries and knowledge nodes that are often near-duplicates, and merging them is how the graph stays usable. Similarity alone is the wrong criterion: in a technical corpus the *distinguishing* token is usually a number or a content word, while the shared boilerplate is lo\n122 [D] Optional-Judge Circuit Breaker with Single Probe and Visible :: NeuroCore uses model calls as *optional gates* — a judgement, a filter, a relevance check — where a failure already degrades to \"keep\". The subtle cost is not correctness but latency compounding: at a 3 s timeout, sixteen passages × 3 s is 48 s, and eighty judgements is minutes, all spent producing \n124 [D] Capability-Gated Tool Injection from Data Presence :: Every tool description an agent can see costs context and invites a wrong call. The source makes the tool surface a function of the **data**, not of the template: four tools that only make sense on a corpus with compiled knowledge products (navigation tree, structure, graph, per-document outline) ar\n130 [D] Online Sequential Rate Monitor with Persisted Threshold-Boun :: NeuroCore wants a cheap running signal for \"is the rejection/escalation rate still within its expected band?\" — re-scanning history per request is O(N) and unusable on a hot path. A Wald sequential probability ratio test gives a bounded-error answer after each observation: accumulate a log-likelihoo\n131 [R] Fail-Closed Bounded Ingestion Buffer with Backpressure Metri :: Observability and audit writes sit in the request path, and the usual implementations put them in an unbounded queue: memory grows without limit under load, and the first symptom is an OOM rather than a measured decision. The source chooses the opposite and documents the reasoning: a bounded deque w\n134 [D] Coverage-Bounded Compaction Checkpoint with Replay Gate and  :: NeuroCore already has `compact_threshold` and `compact_keep_last`: when the context is large, it drops older messages by count. That is exactly the design Maka's Chapter 3 argues against — it reduces what the model sees by removing facts, with no record of what was removed and no way to tell a delib\n137 [D] Session-Scoped Capability Grants Keyed by Exact Tool Contrac :: \"Allow this MCP server\" is the wrong unit of permission: one server exposes many tools with very different power, and a grant that covers the server silently covers the tool added next Tuesday. Maka's grant is keyed by the **exact contract**: session, provider, contract, server, tool, capability and\n143 [D] Kernel Rule Halt and Capped Context Inclusion :: Two ideas in AI Governor's discovery protocol are worth taking literally. First, a **kernel tier with a halt**: some rules are foundational, and if a mandatory kernel rule is absent the agent must stop and report a critical failure instead of continuing with partial governance — fail-closed at start\n148 [D] Two-Axis Verdict with Evaluation-Order Anchoring and Machine :: An agent asked to review its own work anchors on the tests it just wrote: if the plan is met and the tests are green, everything looks fine. GAAI structures the verdict so that cannot happen silently. The sidecar has two independent axes — conformance to the *state of the art* (`state_of_the_art_con\n152 [D] Single-Authority Stuck Classification with TOCTOU-Hardened S :: An autonomous loop that stalls either spins forever or stops with a useless message. GAAI classifies stalls with a closed schema and contains retries behind one owner. Three properties transfer. First, **one authority**: a single helper is \"the only code allowed to name, parse, publish or invalidate\n154 [D] Replay-Aware Rule Injection with History Marker Scan and Sta :: Ponytail ships one ruleset file that is injected into the conversation before every model turn, and the injection is deliberately *idempotent across turns*. `__init__.py` (`_pre_llm_call`, `build_injected_context`, `_filter_skill_body_for_mode`) does three things that our system-prompt node does not\n156 [D] Correlation-ID Mailbox with Per-Call Reply Futures :: Concurrent flows do not share a call stack. aiFlows gives every message a unique id and lets a sender wait on a future keyed by the request that produced it, so two callers invoking the same tool cannot consume each other's reply. The pattern separates *identity* (`message_id`), *conversation* (`cor\n157 [D] Volatile-Key-Excluding Flow Cache with State and Message Res :: aiFlows caches a Flow result under a digest of its config, state and input, while ignoring explicitly named volatile keys (timestamps, request ids, log handles). The cached value contains the outputs, the full post-call state and the messages created during the call, so a cache hit restores the same\n160 [D] Capability-Scoped Observation Projection with Leak Tests :: The game agent must not read the answer key. The source converts the full world state into a smaller observation: approximate position instead of exact coordinates, visible completion flags instead of internal quest state, a capped newest-first event list, and relative landmarks instead of absolute \n161 [D] A2A Capability Card and Delegation Envelope Separate from To :: The source advertises an agent through a card (`name`, `description`, `model`, `url`, `capabilities`) and delegates work with a task envelope (`target_agent`, `task_name`, `payload`), while MCP remains the tool layer. The separation prevents a common conflation in multi-agent systems: a capability i\n165 [R] Manifest-Driven Resumable Asset Generation with Content-Addr :: The source generates every asset from scratch in one long request path; if image 7 fails, the run has already paid for images 1–6 and the user must regenerate the whole plan. A manifest makes the unit of work explicit: each request has a stable digest, each record stores the output path and SHA-256,\n166 [D] Cost Preflight and Actual-Cost Reconciliation Ledger :: The source shows a model picker but no price, no per-run cap and no record of what a run actually cost. A preflight estimate plus a reconciliation ledger makes spend a first-class contract: before a call, estimate units × unit price and refuse a request that would exceed the run cap; after the call,\n167 [D] Deterministic ZIP Export Manifest with Per-Entry Checksums :: The source packages generated files into an in-memory ZIP with default timestamps and directory order, so the same inputs produce different bytes on different runs and no one can tell whether an archive was modified. A deterministic export manifest fixes the order, timestamps and compression setting\n168 [D] Versioned Decision-Point Registry with Shadow/Active/Off Mod :: Instead of embedding policy in `if` statements, every point where the agent must decide is a `DecisionSpec`: an id, a positive integer version, bounded questions, a policy, a deterministic fallback and a mode. `shadow` asks and records without changing behavior; `active` applies the verdict; `off` n\n170 [D] Pre-Model Admission with Passage Injection Screening and Poi :: Before fetched content or a long tool result reaches the model, it passes through two gates: an injection screen that judges each passage for instructions aimed at the AI, and an admission gate that keeps only chunks relevant to the current goal. Withheld passages never enter the prompt; a note mark\n171 [D] Stale-Aware Tool-Result Pruning with Term Relevance and Loss :: Long agent histories are dominated by tool output that is no longer true or no longer relevant. The source scores each result with cheap facts (was the file read again, was the command re-run, does the live conversation still use its terms), combines that with an optional judge verdict, and prunes l\n181 [R] Planted-Bug Oracle Benchmark with Clean Controls :: The source ships seeded scenarios with deliberately planted bugs and clean control runs, then scores each oracle's firing against the expected bug class. This is a benchmark for the *detector*, not for the game: a detector that fires on a clean control is a false positive even if it also catches eve\n184 [R] Typed Blackboard Facts with Provenance, TTL and Optimistic W :: A blackboard is shared mutable state for an agent. The source stores `object` values with an age counter; a consumer cannot tell who wrote a fact, whether it is still valid, or whether it changed since it was read. A typed fact record adds `source`, `written_at`, `ttl_s`, `revision` and an optional \n185 [R] Action Queue with Priority, Expiry, Interrupt and Empty-Queu :: The source's action manager schedules actions, decrements an expiry timer, sorts by priority, and lets an interrupt preempt the active action. The shape is right; the guards are missing. A correct implementation treats the queue as an explicit state machine (`idle`, `running`, `interrupted`), return\n187 [R] Unified FSM/Behavior-Tree Tick with Snapshot and Resume :: The source exposes an `AiComponent` facade over an FSM or a behavior tree, with sensors and a blackboard ticking together. A unified tick contract makes the two decision models interchangeable and, more importantly, resumable: a `running` child returns its path and memory, so the next tick resumes e\n188 [R] Generator-Neutral Asset Spec Schema with Cross-File Validati :: The source separates an asset spec from the model that renders it: `character_spec`, `background_spec`, `object_spec`, `layout_spec` and `style_guide` each have their own schema, and a preset can be swapped without rewriting the prompt. That is the right abstraction for a multi-provider generation p\n189 [R] Layered Style Tokens with Deterministic Precedence and Confl :: The source has several token libraries that are composed into a prompt by convention. A layered merge makes composition explicit: global style, project style, character/background override, then a preset, each with a declared priority. Higher priority wins; an equal-priority conflict is reported, no\n190 [R] Two-Stage Style Distillation with an Approved Guide Artefact :: The source's pipeline is reference images → style guide → image prompt. The missing seam is approval: a guide generated from references should be a reviewable artefact, with the reference hashes it came from, before it can drive paid generation. Once approved, a changed reference invalidates the app\n197 [R] Generation Pipeline State Machine with Resumable Stages :: The source stores a generation pipeline as a row with a status, a current stage, an error stage and a per-stage result (`pending`, `processing`, `completed`, `failed`, `skipped`). A failed expensive stage can be retried without re-running the cheap ones, and a UI can show exactly where a run stopped\n202 [D] Memory Verification Against Source :: Magic Context's dreamer verifies memories against reality: it maps each memory to the files that back it, re-checks paths and patterns, fixes what drifted, and archives what no longer holds. NeuroCore has most of the storage half of this and none of the verification half. `MemoryStore` already carri\n203 [D] Cache-Stable Deferred Compaction :: Magic Context never compacts inline. It computes the fold, queues it, and applies it only at a cache-safe boundary, so the provider-cached prefix survives and the agent never pauses. NeuroCore's agent loop does the opposite: `_truncate_messages` drops the oldest turns greedily when the estimate exce","numLines":65,"startLine":1,"totalLines":195}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"56323c1b-ff63-419a-9629-f7a9cb2d84e5","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main"}
