 both `/art` and `/train` read them automatically. The distinction matters: a preference is a nudge the model may trade off\n320 [D] Terminal Review Inbox with Approved-Head-Only Recall :: Experience and Skill candidates go to a scope-local review inbox. A reviewer approves or rejects, both decisions are terminal, approval writes an immutable revision, and only the approved current head is eligible for recall — pending, rejected and historical revisions are excluded. NeuroCore writes \n321 [D] Evidence-Cited Memory Reconciliation with create/revise/merg :: PowerContext's atomic-memory reconciler asks a model to compare a new proposal with every related item and return exactly one action: `create`, `revise` (combine with exactly one related item), `merge` (combine with one or more), or `noop`. Every action must cite supplied evidence ids; the model may\n323 [D] Node Outcome Envelope with Typed Failure and Degraded Status :: Every node in the source catches `Exception` and returns `Failure`, so a missing dependency, a bad configuration and a genuinely false condition are indistinguishable. A node outcome envelope keeps the three-valued status but adds a `reason_code`, a message, a `degraded` flag and the exception class\n324 [R] Declared Tick-Budget Decorators (Every-N, Wait-N, Cooldown) :: The source's `Counter` runs a child once every N calls and the `Timer` runs it once an injected elapsed-time function passes a threshold. Both are implicit state machines hidden in decorator fields: the counter resets on fire, the timer resets on fire, and neither declares a budget or a maximum numb\n326 [D] Transactional Root-Event Boundary with Full Rollback :: Cobweb treats a root event and every event it emits as one transaction. The scheduler snapshots the registry and state at the root, executes nested emits with a shared depth counter, and on any exception restores state, the timeline and pipeline mutations, then rethrows. This is stronger than per-ha\n327 [D] Patch-and-Timeline Bundle as the Only Projection Input :: After each root event Cobweb emits a bundle: the root event name, a list of state **patches** and the ordered **timeline** of nested events. The presentation layer consumes the bundle and never reads the mutable tree, so a renderer can be replaced, replayed or tested against a recorded bundle. Neuro\n328 [D] Dynamic Hook Binding with Required-Context Validation :: Cobweb lets a game attach a definition's hooks to a runtime object with `bind({key, kind, id, ctx, slot})`. The binding is a descriptor stored in state (`_bindings`), so a snapshot reloads the same bindings; required context keys are validated before registration; the whole bind is rolled back if re\n330 [R] Retry-Until-Timeout Matcher with Typed Absence and Interval  :: `loop_find(query, timeout, threshold, interval, intervalfunc)` repeatedly captures a screen, matches a target and returns the position, or raises `TargetNotFoundError` after the deadline. The `intervalfunc` runs after each failed attempt, which is how the source dismisses popups without coupling tha\n331 [R] Resolution-Relative Target Coordinates :: An Airtest `Template` records the resolution at which it was captured (`resolution`) and a normalised position (`record_pos`). At run time it converts the match box into a coordinate using the current screen size, so the same template works across devices and window sizes. That is a portable locator\n333 [R] Matching-Method Registry with Declared Capability and Cost :: Airtest resolves a matching method name to a class through one registry (`MATCHING_METHODS`) and raises `InvalidMatchingMethodError` for an unknown name. Every method implements the same `find`/`match_in` contract, so `Template` can carry a method name and `loop_find` does not know which algorithm i\n337 [D] Negative-First Correction Detector with Weak-Pattern Directi :: A user correction is the highest-signal learning event in a session, and it is also easy to misclassify. The source runs three pattern classes in order: **negative** patterns suppress even when a positive matches (\"no, that's fine\"), **strong** patterns always trigger (\"that's wrong\", \"stop doing th\n338 [D] Five-Member Module Lifecycle with Stable Order and Reverse S :: The source reduces a module to five members: `Name`, `InitOrder`, `Init(host)`, `Pump()` and `Shutdown()`. Initialization is a stable sort by `InitOrder` (ties keep registration order), `Get<T>()` may only return a module with a strictly smaller order, and shutdown iterates in reverse. That single o\n339 [D] Bounded Per-Module Error Journal with Readiness Query :: `FrameworkDiagnostics` records a module error with the module name, the phase (`Init`, `Pump`, `Shutdown`) and the exception, and the module center keeps running. That is a deliberate availability choice: one broken module must not take down the host. The missing piece is a bound: an erroring `Pump`\n341 [D] Pack Manifest with CRC, Dependency Resolution and Cache Ledg :: The source ships its own asset pack format: a manifest lists files with a version, a CRC32 and dependencies, a resolver orders packs so a dependency loads before its dependent, a cache ledger records which pack version is installed and where, and a download queue resumes a partial transfer. This is \n342 [D] Two-Hit Thread Creation Gate with Ambiguity Margin :: Creating a durable group from a single observation is how memory systems fill with one-item threads. Memind's gate requires two supporting items for the same canonical anchor, a create score above a threshold, and a marker for work/case threads. For an *attach* decision, it requires the top candidat\n345 [D] Coalescing Wake Scheduler for Background Enrichment :: A burst of new memory items should not start one enrichment job per item. Memind's `CoalescingThreadWakeScheduler` debounces wakes per thread key: the first item schedules a wake after a quiet window, later items within the window join the same wake, and the job sees the accumulated set. The idea is\n350 [D] Observer/Observed Peer Representation with Cross-Peer Modell :: Memories are not facts about a user in general; they are what one participant concluded about another. Honcho stores observations in a collection keyed by `(observer, observed)` and derives a representation per pair, so the same session yields a self-model (`alice` about `alice`) and a theory-of-min\n351 [D] Durable Derivation Queue with Idempotent Work Rows and Backf :: Honcho's deriver does not process a message inline. Adding a message writes a pending-work row, a queue manager claims and leases work, a consumer performs the derivation, and a backlog module re-enqueues rows that were left behind; a scope backfill replays a historical range. The contract is that a\n352 [D] Static Low-Latency Representation Snapshot with Freshness Di :: Honcho separates the reasoned representation from the read path: `representation` returns a static snapshot immediately, while background derivation catches up, and the documentation tells callers that a new message may not be reflected yet. That is a better contract than either a slow synchronous r\n353 [R] Due-List Consolidation Scheduler with Idle and Backlog Gates :: Honcho decides *when* to consolidate with a read-only due list. A collection is a candidate only if it has enough new documents, it has been idle for a timeout, it is past a minimum-hours gate, it has no earlier attempt, it has no pending representation work, and the session has the feature enabled.\n354 [D] Breakpoint Inspection with Resumable Run Snapshots :: Haystack lets an operator attach a breakpoint to a component: execution stops, the accumulated inputs and outputs are inspectable and editable, and the run continues. That is a debugger, and it is the tool missing when an agent flow produces a subtly wrong answer at step 6 and the only recourse is t\n355 [D] Contract-Typed Connection Checks at Build Time :: Haystack's components declare typed sockets, and `connect()` fails when an output cannot feed an input — before anything runs. NeuroCore's `FlowRunner.validate(module_manager)` checks three things: nodes referencing disabled or missing modules, connections referencing missing nodes, and system-promp\n356 [R] Schema-Validated Output with Error-Feedback Retry :: Haystack ships a `json_schema` validator component that runs inside the pipeline, so a malformed structured response is a pipeline branch rather than a corrupt value passed downstream. In the common pattern the failure path joins an error message back into a retry prompt, which is dramatically more \n357 [D] Fail-Closed Retrieval Filter Compilation :: Haystack's document-store layer separates a `FilterPolicy` from the queries it can execute: a backend declares which logical operators and comparators it supports, and an unsupported construct is a policy decision made explicitly rather than silently dropped. The failure it prevents is the worst kin\n371 [D] Leaf Retrieval with Parent Expansion :: Artoo indexes small chunks for retrieval precision but **returns the parent block** for context. Small units match better; large units answer better. Keeping the two roles separate lets one tuning parameter (chunk size) stop being a compromise between recall quality and answer quality. NeuroCore's m\n372 [D] Test-Reliability Reported Separately from Result :: WAI Play refuses to fold the harness's health into the product's score. API access, evidence completeness and agent reliability are separate diagnostics, so a reader can tell \"the game scored 2/5\" apart from \"we could barely test the game, and what we measured is unreliable\". Without that separation\n374 [D] Backward Chaining from the Goal Condition :: WAI Play's `completion_backplanner` starts from the win condition — what the game's \"cleared\" state requires — and works backwards to the actions that establish each prerequisite. When a forward run fails, the agent re-plans from observed state using that inverted tree instead of retrying variations\n376 [D] Import Purity Check for Dynamically Loaded Modules :: arcade promises isolated game saves and breaks the promise in the quietest possible way: `os.chdir` runs at module import, so the change applies once, at the wrong moment, and relative paths resolve somewhere else. The bug is not \"cwd is hard\"; it is that **loading a plugin executed process-global s\n379 [R] Egress-Side Secret Redaction on Tool Output :: `output-secret-filter.sh` runs **after** a tool, rewrites the result before the model sees it, and logs that a masking occurred without logging the value. That ordering is the insight: AIKit's `check_secrets.py` is a *commit-time* control — it stops a secret from entering git — but by then the secre\n380 [D] Transient-Failure Resume with Attempt Ledger :: `api-error-auto-resume.sh` restarts an interrupted run after a provider error rather than discarding the task. The failure it addresses is the most common operational one: a long agent run dies on a 429/5xx/connection reset and all prior work is lost because the only recovery is to start over. Neuro\n383 [D] Single Typed Fact Projection per Turn :: Covel's `WorldIR` is a post-turn projection of facts that every consumer reads: quests, inventory, affinity, the codex and relationship plugins all reuse it rather than re-extracting from the narrative. The problem it solves is subtle and expensive — five consumers reading the same text with five pr\n390 [R] Factor Grid Expansion with Symmetry Reduction :: FAIRGAME's permutation expander takes one scenario and produces every combination of personality, prior, language and provider — then removes **symmetric duplicates** when all agents share a model, because swapping identical agents yields the same experiment. That distinction (same-model symmetry is\n392 [R] Placeholder-Preserving Template Localisation :: FAIRGAME's translator is an LLM, but it is constrained: placeholders and formatting must survive translation. Without that constraint, translating a prompt silently breaks substitution — a `{user_name}` becomes `{nombre}`, and every subsequent render either raises or, worse, ships a literal brace to\n396 [R] Candidate Grid with Indexed Selection Receipt :: The skill generates a grid of candidates, renders it as a numbered contact sheet, and selects by index. The numbering is the mechanism: a human or a model says \"4\", and the choice is unambiguous, reviewable and reproducible, instead of \"the good one on the right\". NeuroCore's generators (memory dist\n409 [D] Evidence-Triggered Learning Capture with a Per-Session Ask B :: Asking an agent \"anything to save?\" every N tool calls produces notes about the first busy turn — usually setup. Trail-Code instead looks for *signs that something was learned*: a test or build failed and then passed after an edit; the person corrected the agent and the agent went on to change code;\n411 [D] Three-Verdict Response Classifier with ERROR, Abstain and St :: A guardrail needs to answer \"did the response comply with an injected instruction?\" without pretending a network failure, an empty body and a refusal are the same thing. The source separates the classifier from the request, distinguishes `BYPASS`/`BLOCKED`/`PARTIAL`/`ERROR`, tries a structured verdi\n414 [D] Phase-Aware Multi-Turn Scoring with Per-Phase Verdicts and E :: Social engineering is cumulative: each turn is individually reasonable, and the leak happens at the end. The source scores a multi-turn session phase by phase (rapport, probing, pressure, extraction) and keeps a per-phase result rather than one final label. The reusable contract is a per-turn verdic\n416 [R] Render-Proof Pixel Statistics with Deterministic State and S :: A screenshot of a black canvas looks like a successful capture. The source inspects pixels objectively — sampled alpha coverage, channel range and distinct colour count — and fails a capture that has fewer than 256 alpha pixels or is flat within the threshold. It also requires the game to expose `se\n417 [D] In-Flight Job Recovery with a Duplicate-Submission Guard :: The source's director states the policy plainly: a temporary failure triggers **bounded recovery of the existing job**, not an immediate downgrade or a duplicate paid submission; a genuinely missing key degrades to procedural assets and reports the limitation. For a pipeline that submits expensive g\n419 [R] Per-Stage Perception Latency Budget with p95 Gate and Frame  :: The repository advertises \"sub-10ms input latency\" and \"low latency\" as a single number. A real loop has at least four stages — capture, preprocess, infer, act — and the end-to-end deadline is what matters. A per-stage budget with a measured p95 and a hard frame deadline makes the claim testable and\n420 [R] Dual-Channel Perception with Disagreement Flags :: The brief lists both a vision engine (object detection) and a state tracker (OCR of health, ammo and similar HUD values). Those are two independent readings of the same world, and the usual failure is that one silently overwrites the other. Recording both channels as typed facts with their own confi\n422 [R] Input Actuator Contract with Held-Input Release Guarantee :: A virtual controller that holds a key down must guarantee release on every exit path, including a timeout, an exception and a cancellation. The brief advertises a \"virtual controller and low-latency keyboard/mouse emulation\"; the classic bug is a held key after a crashed action, which then poisons e\n425 [D] Bounded Embedding Projection with Full-Text Authority on Rec :: Embedding a whole document fails on long inputs and loses the beginning. The source truncates text to a limit before embedding to avoid token errors, but always stores the full original in the database and returns it on recall. The contract is a clean split: the embedding is a bounded *projection* f","numLines":65,"startLine":66,"totalLines":195}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7fff666a-7d9a