and the other to `spine`, are welded by the eye but pulled apart by the skeleton. The source detects co-located vertex *clusters*, counts those with mismatched skin weights, and \n506 [D] Machine-Readable Action Contract with Closed-World Precondit :: The environment defines nine actions with documented preconditions and effects, but the contract is prose in the docs plus branching in a 77 KB world file. A machine-readable contract makes the action set inspectable: for each action, the required role, the parameters with types, the preconditions a\n507 [R] Observation-Semantics Declaration: Replace, Merge or Accumul :: The source's `FindServices` documentation contains a sentence most environments never write: a later observation *replaces* the list, so there is no memory of previously open services. That single decision changes what an agent can learn and makes some policies impossible. A first-class observation \n509 [R] Declared Simultaneous-Outcome Tie-Break Policy with Replay V :: The environment resolves a same-step attacker success and defender detection in favour of the attacker. That is a game-design decision with a measurable effect on agent reward, and it is hard-coded in prose. A declared tie-break policy makes the decision explicit (`attacker`, `defender`, `both`, `ra\n516 [R] Economy Faucet/Sink Ledger with Target Bands and Curve Check :: The economy template asks a designer to list faucets (sources), sinks (drains), formulas and balance targets, but nothing checks that the numbers agree. A faucet/sink ledger makes the model executable: each source and drain declares a rate and a currency, a simulation over a declared horizon compute\n518 [R] Make/Unmake Search with a Search Trace and Guaranteed Restor :: Recursive search that mutates one board is the standard way to avoid copying state at every node, but it is safe only if every mutation is undone on every exit path. The source does the make/unmake pair by hand inline, with no `try/finally`, so a raised evaluation function, a `KeyError` in settings,\n519 [R] Evaluation Registry with Declared Win Polarity, Bounds and S :: The source injects `score_method` and `winning_method`, which is a good extension seam, but the contract is implicit: the win function returns `True` for a human win, `False` for an AI win and `None` otherwise, and the search maps that to `-inf`/`+inf`; a new game that returns the opposite polarity \n520 [R] Seeded Tie-Break with a Deterministic Move Receipt :: The source shuffles moves and settings and returns `random.choice` among equally best moves, so the same position yields different moves on different runs. That is reasonable for a game and unacceptable for a test, a bug report or a replay. A seeded tie-break keeps the variety but makes it reproduci\n527 [D] Definition Reload with In-Flight Run Pinning :: behaviac's authoring model only works because the behaviour definition is data the runtime can be handed: the editor exports, the game executes, and the designer attaches to a running instance and inspects it. The consequence that matters for us is that **changing a definition does not require resta\n528 [R] Branch Reachability from Declared Preconditions :: An HTN method fires only when its preconditions hold, and behaviac's designer shows the designer which methods are applicable for a given world state. That view exists because the alternative is discovering a dead branch in production: a method whose precondition can never be satisfied is silently n\n531 [R] Agent State Owned Outside the Execution Engine :: taOS's claim is that the framework is replaceable because the platform owns what the agent knows — history, channel bindings, adapters, files, credentials — and the framework only executes. Swap the engine, keep the agent. NeuroCore is the engine. The loop's `messages` list *is* the agent's state: h\n534 [D] Authority Drift Detection Across a Run :: Authority Drift is the failure where each individual action is permitted but the *sequence* has expanded beyond what the task authorised — an agent asked to \"clean up test data\" that starts by deleting one fixture and ends by deleting a directory; asked to \"check the config\" and ends by editing it. \n537 [D] Environment Evolution Harness for Stale-State Robustness :: OpenART's central manipulation is elegantly simple: the goal stays fixed, the *environment* changes underneath the agent, and the evaluation asks whether the agent noticed. The paper reports that unsafe behaviour \"tends to appear well after the changed state is first consumed, often through stale as\n538 [D] Trace-Level Verdicts over Final Answers :: OpenART judges \"over traces and snapshots\" rather than the final message. The reason is structural: in a long tool-using run the final answer often looks correct while the path that produced it was unsafe — a secret read, a privilege escalation, a destructive write that was later reverted. Judging t\n539 [D] Conjunctive Dual-Judge with Disagreement Bucket :: OpenART's Strict ASR is defined precisely: an attack counts as successful \"only when both the deterministic evaluator and an LLM judge confirm it\". The consequence is conservative in the right direction — a case where the two disagree does not become a success, and it does not silently become a fail\n543 [R] Hysteresis-Bounded Fidelity Downgrade for Idle Sessions :: The SDK's fidelity model is a pure decision function over a cheap state, with two thresholds instead of one and a group-level consistency rule. Translated to a service that runs many agent sessions: a session that is idle or far from user attention could run at reduced fidelity (no per-turn model ca\n545 [D] Independently Defaulted Capability Grants :: Gua's rule is that every capability has its own grant and its own default, and enabling one never enables another: reading the UI tree, reading world objects, performing semantic UI actions, gameplay input, raw pointer/keyboard input, screenshots and logs are all separate. The sentence that carries \n559 [R] Branch-Search Budget with a Declared Stall Criterion :: Tree-of-Attacks-style search branches on promising paths and prunes stalled ones — but \"stalled\" is usually an intuition. A declared stall criterion makes the pruning reproducible: a branch stalls after `k` consecutive turns with no increase in a named signal (score, indicator count, model confidenc\n562 [D] Locator Fallback Ladder with Per-Strategy Success Accounting :: One entry in the list describes a mobile/web/Smart-TV automation framework with \"a locator fallback ladder across XPath, text, OCR and image strategies\" (reported by the list; not independently verified here). The idea is sound independently: element location should try strategies in a declared orde\n572 [D] Pausable Budget Clock with Persisted Snapshot and Accumulate :: A wall-clock budget that keeps running while the run is paused punishes the user for thinking. The source models the pause explicitly: the deadline shifts by exactly the paused duration, the accumulated pause is recorded, `remainingMs` is computed against the pause instant while paused (so a status \n576 [R] Machine-Validated State Transitions for Model-Proposed Fallb :: Letting a model choose the next state is useful — it can recognise that the plan needs replanning rather than retrying — and dangerous, because a model that names a terminal state ends the run. The fix is not to remove the choice but to constrain it: the model may propose a state, and a transition t\n580 [D] Fact/Intent/Hint Separation with Graph-Derived Task Units :: Most multi-agent systems encode work as a role (\"researcher\", \"coder\") and a queue of tasks. The source encodes three different epistemic states instead: a **Fact** is confirmed and objective; an **Intent** is a declared direction that nobody has executed; a **Hint** is human judgement that agents a\n589 [D] Typed Attachment Ingestion with a Byte Ceiling and Skip Verd :: The source keeps one ingestion module per document kind (`convert.ts` for generic text, `csv.ts`, `docx.ts`), plus a separate local-embedding step. The chat layer does not sniff bytes itself; it dispatches on the declared type, and a type it cannot handle has a defined outcome rather than a decode e\n590 [D] Versioned Operation Template Recorded with Each Result :: The repository keeps two prompt builders side by side (`build-prompt.ts` and `build-prompt-v2.ts`) and a per-tool prompt module for each plugin. In practice a prompt is edited over time while old conversations and outputs persist; without a version stamp, an analysis cannot tell whether a difference\n592 [D] Prioritized Intercept Policy with Durable Ask and Timeout :: A single ordered rule list is evaluated on every tool call. The action is three-valued rather than two: `allow` stops evaluation and permits, `deny` blocks and hands the model an explanation, and `ask` blocks until a human decides. The `ask` path is durable — a pending record is created, an activity\n595 [D] Snapshot-Bound Side Question with Independent Budget and Can :: A side question captures the main agent's context at submission time as an immutable snapshot (system prompt, model identity, recent turns, tool definitions), then answers from that snapshot plus the question. It has its own history, its own output budget, its own cancellation token and a hard timeo\n599 [D] Fail-Closed Simulated Tool Gateway with Fixture Ownership :: During evaluation, every tool definition is checked for a live handler; if one is attached, the specification is refused. The call is then matched against a scenario allow-list and a fixture that owns the invocation. Inputs are validated against the declared schema with an offline validator, budgets\n604 [D] Ephemeral Sandbox Lease with Per-Session Credentials and Gua :: A session acquires a sandbox: an environment record, a credential set scoped to that session, and an endpoint the agent can reach. The session record is the unit of ownership; the environment is disposable. Teardown is not best-effort cleanup at the end of a happy path — it is a lease expiry plus a \n605 [D] Prompt-Keyed Derivation Cache with TTL and Single-Flight :: Expensive derived content (instruction variants, summaries, tag sets) is cached under a digest of the full source prompt, with an explicit expiry. Concurrent requests for the same key share one in-flight generation instead of each starting their own; a failed generation does not poison the cache. Th\n607 [R] Recorded Seed on Derived Content for Regeneration :: Instruction variants are generated from a pool prompt, with the intent of producing several phrasings. Variant generation is either random or model-sampled; in both cases the selection is not reproducible, so a good variant cannot be re-derived and a bad one cannot be explained. Recording the seed (\n609 [D] One-Way Presentation Invariant Enforced by Test :: The engine declares that everything visual or audible reads from the world and never writes to it, and backs the claim with a test: adding art, effects or a renderer cannot change what the simulation does, because the dependency is structurally one-way. In practice the design is enforced by passing \n610 [D] Time-Anchored Assertion Grammar over Declared Fields :: Assertions are strings of the form `<subject> <op> <value> [at <time|end>]`, where the subject is a declared world field (`score`, `won`, `deaths`), an entity field (`player.grounded`), a custom event count (`events.jump`), or entity state (`door.state.open`). The parser rejects anything outside the\n612 [D] Pipeline Run Record with Stage-Level Failure Attribution and :: A pipeline run is an object, not a call stack: it has a run id, the request that started it, an ordered list of stages, and for each stage its status (`pending`, `running`, `completed`, `skipped`, `error`), its input, its output, its error and its start/finish timestamps. A pipeline-wide deadline is\n613 [D] Immutable Stage Handoff :: The kernel threads `output_data` of one stage into `input_data` of the next. The scaffold does this with ordinary dictionaries, which means a later stage can mutate a dict the earlier stage still references, and a run record can end up describing inputs that no longer match the state that produced t\n614 [D] Per-Agent Execution Metrics in the Status Payload :: The kernel keeps, per registered agent, a status, the last run timestamp, the last duration, the last error string and a run count, and exposes all of it through one status endpoint. That is exactly enough to answer \"is this agent healthy and how slow is it?\" without a tracing stack. NeuroCore's mod\n621 [D] Provenance Label Survival Across Output Boundaries :: A label is easy to add and easy to lose. It survives the database write, then disappears when a serializer builds a DTO by hand, when a cache stores the payload from an older schema, when a notification preview is formatted with a subset of fields, or when an export writer picks three columns. Each \n622 [R] Prompt-Extraction Guard on Model Output :: A model can be induced to reproduce its own instructions. When it does, everything in the system prompt becomes user-visible: the operating rules, the tool list, internal endpoints, occasionally an example credential left in a template. Detection is tractable because the protected text is *known* — \n623 [D] Phase-Scoped Context Assembly :: Multi-phase engagements have a contamination problem: reconnaissance material, exploitation payloads and report evidence are different kinds of content, and mixing them lets a payload from one phase be quoted as evidence in another. CyberStrike's guard keeps the agent \"focused on the current test ph\n626 [R] Cross-Backend Numerical Parity with Declared Tolerance :: When one computation has several implementations — a CPU path and an accelerated path, an exact integer routine and a floating approximation, a naive reference and a vectorised version — the only assurance that they agree is a test that runs both and compares, with a tolerance that is itself declare\n630 [D] Four-Phase Epistemic Flow Pattern with Distinct Phase Templa :: `IDEA-002` proposed a `Root -> Branch -> Flower -> Seed` chain with an inverted convergence temperature (low, moderate, high, then low again). Checked against `neurocore`: `modules/autonomous_agent/core/flow_creator.py` has `FlowCreator._match_pattern` (keyword matching), `_build_from_pattern`, `NOD","numLines":65,"startLine":131,"totalLines":195}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"278e04ce-ac35-4b8f-8672-cb3942226e4d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main"}
