{"parentUuid":"93105f6f-e97f-4ec5-9d61-b6bb31e93541","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4ce50c82-a8ed-4889-ac0c-4fb42e7bef08","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XASg6tCLMHYykXEooGV6mg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t711 High/Med Context Governor with Measured Growth and Targeted Compaction :: The governor separates **accepted history** (what the provider has already seen) from the **unsent delta**. Before a request, it measures the full prompt against a local input budget. If the request exceeds the budget, it first summarizes history that the model has already received; if the new tool results still do not fit, it replaces only the necessary unread text results with a budget error that asks the model to read a smaller range. \n2\t712 High/Med Two-Stage Memory Consolidation with Versioned Files :: nanobot has two memory stages. The **Consolidator** turns a session's transcript into a compact summary when the session is compacted. **Dream** processes unprocessed history entries since a cursor, builds a prompt, edits the durable memory files (`MEMORY.md`, `SOUL.md`, `USER.md`), and commits the diff through a git-backed store. Raw history is archived; the cursor records what has been processed; a failure leaves the cursor unchanged so the work \n3\t713 High/Med Subagent Delegation with Isolated Sessions and Ownership :: A subagent gets its own task ID, session, workspace, status and transcript. The parent can spawn, send a message, check status, read the session, cancel, and receive a final outcome. Ownership checks prevent one session from reading or cancelling another session's subagent. `recover_interrupted()` handles tasks left behind by a crash; `_save_transcript()` preserves the subagent's evidence. NeuroCore's visual flows and multi-agent delegation ne\n4\t714 Med-High/Med Hook Pipeline for Lifecycle, Streaming and Capture :: The hook pipeline exposes before/after run, before/after iteration, before/after tool execution, stream deltas, reasoning, error, and finalization. `CompositeHook` fans out to multiple hooks and isolates a failing hook so one observer cannot break the run. `SDKCaptureHook` records what an SDK caller needs without changing the core loop. NeuroCore's UI, metrics, approvals, tracing and budget accounting are cross-cutting concerns. Without hooks, e\n5\t715 High/High Sandboxed Tool Execution with Path Containment and Process-Tre :: Tool execution is sandboxed at the OS level where available: bubblewrap on Linux and seatbelt on macOS, with explicit bind roots. The workspace path resolver canonicalizes paths and refuses escape. The shell tool extracts absolute paths, guards dangerous commands, and owns the whole process tree so a timeout kills children, not just the parent; on Windows it uses a job object. NeuroCore runs shell, file and web tools for users. A prompt\n6\t756 High/Med Unified Sandbox Adapter Contract with Provider Capabilities :: FastGPT's sandbox SDK defines one `ISandbox` contract composed of four capabilities: lifecycle, command execution, filesystem, and health. A provider declares `capabilities` (for example whether command streaming is supported) and implements a provider-specific stop policy. The caller can `connectRunning()` to an existing sandbox, `ensureRunning()` to create or resume, execute foreground/streaming/background commands, interrupt a session, a\n7\t757 High/Med Stream Resume with TTL, Memory Guard and Stale-Generation Clea :: FastGPT mirrors a streaming chat into Redis so a client can reconnect and resume. The mirror has a TTL, a post-complete TTL, an active/stale state, and a memory-pressure guard that disables new mirrors when Redis is near its maxmemory ratio. A cleanup job marks stuck `generating` chats as `done` when the stream has been inactive for two minutes, or falls back to a 30-minute `updateTime` threshold if Redis is unavailable. NeuroCore's UI i\n8\t758 High/Med Persistent Active Plan with a Tool Provider :: The agent loop persists an **active plan** in the chat history. On the next turn, `readAgentLoopCoreActivePlan()` walks backward through AI messages, skips display-only values, parses the plan, and returns it only if it still has unfinished items; a `null` plan explicitly clears it. The tool provider builds the runtime tool list, resolves tool info, executes tools, and has a separate path for interactive tools. The loop pauses into an `interactive` status \n9\t759 High/Med RAG Search with Citation Template and Quote Budget :: Dataset search is a workflow node, not a global side effect. It resolves which collections the user may read, loads the selected datasets and models, optionally extends the query, reranks, and returns a `quoteQA` list. The citation builder formats each quote with a stable index, source, timestamp and content, then trims the list to the model's `quoteMaxToken` budget. The quote can be injected into a user or system prompt depending on the template. N\n10\t760 Med-High/Med Interactive Form Nodes with Dynamic Options and File Limits :: An interactive node pauses the flow and returns a form specification: description, fields, dynamic options, file limits. When the user submits, the runtime validates the values, registers uploaded files, applies the per-module file limit, and resumes the flow with the form result. The node can be an entry node or a mid-flow checkpoint. NeuroCore's visual flows need human-in-the-loop steps: approve a quote, choose a dataset, fill a missi\n11\t776 High/Med Bi-Temporal Fact Edges with Reference-Time Normalization :: Graphiti stores facts as edges with two time axes: when the fact was **valid in the world** (`valid_at`/`invalid_at`) and when the episode that produced it was **observed** (`reference_time`). When a new fact contradicts an old one, the old edge is invalidated, not deleted. Future-tense statements are normalised so a plan or promise is anchored to the reference time, while a soft deadline (\"will finish by Friday\") is not treated as an invalida\n12\t777 High/Med Deterministic-to-LLM Entity Resolution Cascade :: Graphiti resolves extracted entities in tiers. Exact normalised names are matched first for every candidate. Names that are short or low-entropy are sent to the LLM because fuzzy similarity is unreliable for them. The remaining names go through MinHash signatures and LSH bands to find a small candidate set, then Jaccard similarity on 3-gram shingles; a score at or above 0.9 resolves deterministically. Only unresolved or ambiguous names reach the LLM. Re\n13\t778 High/Low Episode-Anchored Fact Provenance with Multi-Episode Attributio :: Graphiti stores every source document as an `EpisodicNode` and creates `MENTIONED_IN` edges from each extracted entity to the episodes that mention it. When several episodes are extracted together, the model returns `episode_indices` per fact, and the fact edge keeps the exact episode UUIDs that support it. A fact can therefore be traced back to one or more source passages, and a later retrieval can cite the passage rather than only the \n14\t779 Med-High/Med Chunked Bulk Ingestion with Alias-Chain Collapse :: Graphiti's bulk path accepts a list of episodes, chunks them (default 10), extracts entities and edges for a chunk in one bounded operation, resolves duplicates across the whole batch, and writes all episodes, entities and edges in a single graph transaction. Alias chains discovered during resolution are collapsed with a **directed union-find**: if A resolves to B and B resolves to C, every reference to A ends at C, regardless of UUID ordering. Th\n15\t780 Med-High/Low Dangling Endpoint Materialization with Orphan Pruning :: Graphiti's LLM sometimes emits an edge whose source or target is not in the extracted entity list. Instead of dropping the fact, Graphiti **materialises** a provisional `EntityNode` for the missing name, then prunes any materialised node that no kept edge references. The provisional node goes through the normal dedup path on the next episode, so a later extraction can merge it into the canonical entity. The fact survives; the graph stays refe\n16\t838 High/High Channel Binding Router with Specificity Tiers and Explicit Unb :: OpenClaw routes every inbound message through a binding list ordered by specificity: exact peer, parent peer, peer wildcard, guild+roles, guild, team, account, channel, then an explicit fallback owner. All fields in one binding must match (`AND`), multiple matches in the same tier resolve by configuration order, and a message that matches nothing is **not** handed to an arbitrary first agent. If a configured owner exists it receives the\n17\t839 High/Med Sender Pairing, Two-Layer Allowlist, and Context-Visibility Sp :: OpenClaw separates three questions that NeuroCore currently collapses: **who may trigger the agent**, **what supplemental context the model may see**, and **which commands are authorized**. Every DM-capable channel has a `dmPolicy`: `pairing` (default; unknown senders get a short code and the bot ignores them until approved), `allowlist` (block without a handshake), `open` (requires an explicit `*` allowlist entry), or `disabled`. Pairin\n18\t840 High/Med Per-Agent Execution Profile with Global-Ceiling Intersection :: OpenClaw treats an agent as a **brain with its own workspace, model, session store, skills and execution profile**, not just a prompt. Each agent can have `sandbox.mode` (`off`, `non-main`, `all`), `sandbox.scope` (`agent`, `session`, `shared`), a backend, a tool allow/deny list, and a skills allowlist. The global configuration is a ceiling: a per-agent gate can only further restrict it, never widen it. `tools.elevated` is both globally an\n19\t841 Med-High/High Parameter-Derived Operator Scope Matrix with Deny-Unclassified :: OpenClaw does not authorize by URL alone. It classifies every control-plane method with one or more **operator scopes** (`read`, `write`, `admin`, plus narrower pairing, approvals, questions and talk scopes), and it derives scopes from the **actual request parameters before dispatch**. A method that takes a privileged parameter therefore needs the privileged scope even if its path looks ordinary. Methods with no scope classification\n20\t842 High/Med Bound Approval Receipt over Command, CWD, Environment, and Ope :: OpenClaw's exec approval is not a boolean \"user said yes\". It binds an approved run to its **exact command, working directory, environment hash, and content-hashed file operands**. Any drift after approval is a denial. Shell forms or interpreter invocations for which the runtime cannot establish the required execution and file bindings are refused, even if a generic approval exists. When no approval UI is reachable, the answer is deny by\n21\t843 Med-High/High Versioned State Contract with Migration Receipts and Crash-Rec :: OpenClaw makes runtime state **database-first** and gives it a written contract: one global SQLite store, one per agent, and runtime code never treats JSON sidecars as active state. Schemas carry a two-place version; a build refuses to open a database newer than itself; `openclaw update` refuses a target whose declared schema support is older than the on-disk databases. `doctor --fix` is the single owner of file-to-SQLite migrations\n22\t844 High/Med Memory Provenance Lattice with Session-Kind Promotion Ban :: OpenClaw's memory model separates **instructions** (human-written, always injected), **curated core** (`MEMORY.md`, `USER.md`, written only by gated consolidation), **episodic** (daily notes and transcripts, recalled on demand), **prospective** (standing intents and cron), and **review** (dream reports, never injected). The key security property is provenance: every indexed entry carries an origin class from a closed set (`owner`, `agent`, `u\n23\t845 High/Med Standing Intent Ledger with Trigger Prefilter and Anti-Nag Bud :: OpenClaw treats \"remember to act\" as a different faculty from \"remember a fact\", and compiles it out of the model. Time-based intentions (\"remind me Friday\") become cron jobs at the moment they are uttered. Event-based intentions (\"when the release comes up, mention the changelog\") go into a per-agent SQLite table with machine-checkable trigger fields: keywords, an optional trigger embedding, channel and sender scope, expiry, fire budget\n24\t846 High/Med Durable Delivery Obligation Ledger with Ambiguity-Aware Redeli :: Hermes records a **delivery obligation** before it attempts to send a final response. The row moves through `pending` (recorded, never started), `attempting` (the send is in flight), `delivered` (only on a confirmed success), and `failed` (a definitive rejection). The crash semantics are the valuable part: a `pending` row is safe to redeliver plainly because the platform never saw it; an `attempting` row may have been delivered, so a red\n25\t847 High/High Occurrence-Keyed Scheduler with Tick Ownership, Liveness Watch :: Hermes gives every scheduled run an **occurrence identity**: `(job_id, scheduled_instant)` canonicalized to UTC. Before a due job fires, the scheduler checks whether that exact occurrence already completed; if it did, the slot is consumed without a new run. A fire claim allows a small clock skew, and a skewed early fire still counts as completing its slot. A stale-schedule catch-up counter prevents a restart from replaying a backlog of \n26\t848 High/High Event-Triggered SOP Runtime with Typed Decision Gate and Resum :: A ZeroClaw SOP is a directory with a manifest (`SOP.toml`) and an optional human-readable procedure (`SOP.md`). The manifest declares triggers, steps, dependencies, conditions, approval requirements, and an execution mode. Triggers are a fan-in: an authenticated webhook, an MQTT topic, a filesystem event, an AMQP message, a periodic cron tick, or the in-agent `sop_execute` tool. A run is started by dispatch, then advances through `sop_s\n27\t849 High/Med Pre-Activation Skill Package Scanner with Policy-Scoped Rules  :: QwenPaw's scanner treats a skill as a **package**, not a single Markdown file. It discovers every scannable file under the skill directory, skips inert and archive extensions, caps the number of files and the size of each file, refuses symlinks and any real path that escapes the skill root, and then runs a list of analyzers. The default analyzer reads YAML signature rules for prompt injection, command injection, data exfiltration, hardco\n28\t850 Med-High/Med Bidirectional ACP Bridge for External Agent Delegation :: Octop separates the two directions of ACP. **Inbound** is a standalone server: `octop acp --agent main` reads `~/.octop`, boots the agent, speaks ACP on stdin/stdout, and maps an editor thread to a session; it does not require the main server to be running. **Outbound** is a tool: the `acp_runner` tool exposes `action=list|start|message|respond|status|close`. `start` takes a runner and a message (plus an optional cwd), `message` continues th\n29\t851 Med/Med Agent-Curated Topic Wiki with Link Graph and Stale-Topic Revie :: CowAgent's knowledge base is a directory of interlinked Markdown pages. `index.md` is the entry point and is regenerated from the page set; `log.md` records every write; `concepts/`, `entities/` and `sources/` are default categories but the agent may create others. Each page is a refined, structured note with cross-references to related pages. The agent writes a page when a user shares an article/document, when a conversation produces a v\n30\t887 High/Med Deployment Pool with Health-Aware Fallback and Cooldowns :: A LiteLLM model group is backed by several deployments. The router tracks health per deployment, allows a configurable number of failures before putting a deployment into cooldown, and routes around a cooled-down deployment. If the whole group fails, it can fall back to another model group. The fallback is a first-class policy (`fallbacks`), not an ad-hoc `try/except` at every call site. NeuroCore's `LLMBridge` points at one base URL and one m\n31\t888 High/Med Cost/Latency-Aware Routing with Per-Request Cost Ledger :: LiteLLM can choose a deployment by lowest cost, lowest latency, least busy or usage. The cost calculator turns prompt and completion tokens into input and output costs using a model cost map, and the router keeps per-deployment usage windows so a cheap but saturated deployment is not always chosen. The choice is a policy, not a hard-coded endpoint. NeuroCore's `ExecutionBudget` tracks tokens, calls, nodes and wall time, but it does not choose b\n32\t889 Med-High/Med Model Access Groups with Wildcard Provider Routes :: LiteLLM's proxy does not grant access to a flat model list only. It supports model access groups (a named set of models), wildcard provider routes such as `anthropic/*`, and sentinels such as `all-team-models` and `all-proxy-models`. A key or team can be granted a group, and the effective model list is resolved by combining key, team, access-group and proxy lists. The wildcard route is resolved only for known provider models. NeuroCore is self-ho\n33\t890 High/Low Context-Window Fallback and Model Aliasing :: LiteLLM lets a caller use a stable alias (`gpt-4`) that resolves to a model group. If a prompt exceeds the selected deployment's context window, `context_window_fallbacks` routes the request to a model with a larger window instead of failing. Pre-call checks filter out deployments that cannot handle the prompt before a request is sent. NeuroCore uses a single `default_model` and a per-call model name. When a long conversation or a large retrieved context ex\n34\t891 Med-High/Low Provider Exception Taxonomy with Retry-After Preservation :: LiteLLM maps provider-specific failures into a stable exception taxonomy. A 429 becomes `RateLimitError`, a too-long prompt becomes `ContextWindowExceededError`, a 401 becomes `AuthenticationError`, and a 503 becomes `ServiceUnavailableError`. The rate-limit error preserves the `retry-after` header and reset metadata so the caller can wait the right amount instead of guessing. The mapping is centralized, so every provider benefits from th\n35\t913 High/High Virtual Context Paging with Self-Directed Memory Edits :: Keep a bounded working set; let the agent call `page_in`, `page_out` and `note`; track dirty pages and evict least-recently-used memory with a provenance record. NeuroCore's context window is the scarce resource; paging makes eviction explicit and testable instead of silent truncation.\n36\t914 High/Med Experience-Stage Memory Promotion :: Store observations as `candidate`; promote to `validated` after repetition or an external check; only `validated` memories are retrievable as facts. One-off model claims never become durable memory. NeuroCore's memory is only as good as its promotion rule; staging prevents hallucinated self-facts from persisting.\n37\t915 High/Med Governed Memory Consolidation with Identity-Filtered Retrieval :: Periodically summarise episodes into facts, but filter by tenant/user identity before retrieval, redact sensitive fields, and keep the source episode links for audit. Multi-user NeuroCore deployments need memory that does not leak across identities when summaries lose their source.\n38\t916 Med-High/Med Associative Graph Recall with Personalized PageRank :: Build a lightweight entity/relationship graph over memories; seed it with query entities; rank with personalized PageRank; cap expansion and diversify the final set. Embedding-only retrieval misses multi-hop associations that a graph can surface.\n39\t917 Med-High/Low Position-Aware Context Assembly :: Assemble context with the strongest evidence first and last, a bounded middle, and a declared ordering policy; measure answer accuracy by position in a replay test. NeuroCore currently concatenates retrieval results; a deliberate order is a cheap accuracy win.\n40\t945 Med-High/Med Weighted Virtual-Token Fair Share for Concurrent Agents :: Add a model-call arbiter that charges each agent or subagent a virtual counter for prompt plus completion tokens actually consumed, then admits the backlogged agent with the lowest weighted counter. Weights encode operator shares; a dominant-resource-fairness layer combines tokens, in-flight tool calls and wall-clock so a long-context agent cannot monopolize every resource. Idle capacity is borrowed and reconciled at turn end. NeuroCore run\n41\t946 High/High Dependency-Aware Parallel Tool Plan with Sequential Fallback :: On each tool-use turn, a planner pass emits a DAG of calls with placeholders for dependent arguments. The runtime dispatches every ready node concurrently under per-tool limits, substitutes results into dependents, and falls back to the existing sequential loop if the plan fails schema validation. Record the plan and per-node timings for replay. Independent retrieval, search and tool calls in NeuroCore flows are serialized today; this cut\n42\t958 High/Med Claim-Evidence Ledger with Offline Entailment Gate :: Split each answer into atomic claims. Require every claim to bind to a retrieved chunk with exact character offsets, stored as `claims(answer_id, text, chunk_id, span_start, span_end, verdict, score)` in SQLite. An offline ONNX cross-encoder NLI model scores `(cited span, claim)` with thresholds frozen against the model hash. Unsupported claims are dropped, re-retrieved once, or replaced with \"insufficient evidence\"; the answer is classified `ground"}]},"uuid":"e610da8b-43b4-46d9-a5d1-d94c7a20f015","timestamp":"2026-10-30T12:30:07.258Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\YUNUS-~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\c--Users-Yunus-Emre-Desktop-portable-agent-kit\\05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787\\scratchpad\\new_neurocore.txt","content":"711 High/Med Context Governor with Measured Growth and Targeted Compaction :: The governor separates **accepted history** (what the provider has already seen) from the **unsent delta**. Before a request, it measures the