order, and a message that matches nothing is **not** handed to an arbitrary first agent. If a configured owner exists it receives the\n839 High/Med Sender Pairing, Two-Layer Allowlist, and Context-Visibility Sp :: OpenClaw separates three questions that NeuroCore currently collapses: **who may trigger the agent**, **what supplemental context the model may see**, and **which commands are authorized**. Every DM-capable channel has a `dmPolicy`: `pairing` (default; unknown senders get a short code and the bot ignores them until approved), `allowlist` (block without a handshake), `open` (requires an explicit `*` allowlist entry), or `disabled`. Pairin\n840 High/Med Per-Agent Execution Profile with Global-Ceiling Intersection :: OpenClaw treats an agent as a **brain with its own workspace, model, session store, skills and execution profile**, not just a prompt. Each agent can have `sandbox.mode` (`off`, `non-main`, `all`), `sandbox.scope` (`agent`, `session`, `shared`), a backend, a tool allow/deny list, and a skills allowlist. The global configuration is a ceiling: a per-agent gate can only further restrict it, never widen it. `tools.elevated` is both globally an\n841 Med-High/High Parameter-Derived Operator Scope Matrix with Deny-Unclassified :: OpenClaw does not authorize by URL alone. It classifies every control-plane method with one or more **operator scopes** (`read`, `write`, `admin`, plus narrower pairing, approvals, questions and talk scopes), and it derives scopes from the **actual request parameters before dispatch**. A method that takes a privileged parameter therefore needs the privileged scope even if its path looks ordinary. Methods with no scope classification\n842 High/Med Bound Approval Receipt over Command, CWD, Environment, and Ope :: OpenClaw's exec approval is not a boolean \"user said yes\". It binds an approved run to its **exact command, working directory, environment hash, and content-hashed file operands**. Any drift after approval is a denial. Shell forms or interpreter invocations for which the runtime cannot establish the required execution and file bindings are refused, even if a generic approval exists. When no approval UI is reachable, the answer is deny by\n843 Med-High/High Versioned State Contract with Migration Receipts and Crash-Rec :: OpenClaw makes runtime state **database-first** and gives it a written contract: one global SQLite store, one per agent, and runtime code never treats JSON sidecars as active state. Schemas carry a two-place version; a build refuses to open a database newer than itself; `openclaw update` refuses a target whose declared schema support is older than the on-disk databases. `doctor --fix` is the single owner of file-to-SQLite migrations\n844 High/Med Memory Provenance Lattice with Session-Kind Promotion Ban :: OpenClaw's memory model separates **instructions** (human-written, always injected), **curated core** (`MEMORY.md`, `USER.md`, written only by gated consolidation), **episodic** (daily notes and transcripts, recalled on demand), **prospective** (standing intents and cron), and **review** (dream reports, never injected). The key security property is provenance: every indexed entry carries an origin class from a closed set (`owner`, `agent`, `u\n845 High/Med Standing Intent Ledger with Trigger Prefilter and Anti-Nag Bud :: OpenClaw treats \"remember to act\" as a different faculty from \"remember a fact\", and compiles it out of the model. Time-based intentions (\"remind me Friday\") become cron jobs at the moment they are uttered. Event-based intentions (\"when the release comes up, mention the changelog\") go into a per-agent SQLite table with machine-checkable trigger fields: keywords, an optional trigger embedding, channel and sender scope, expiry, fire budget\n846 High/Med Durable Delivery Obligation Ledger with Ambiguity-Aware Redeli :: Hermes records a **delivery obligation** before it attempts to send a final response. The row moves through `pending` (recorded, never started), `attempting` (the send is in flight), `delivered` (only on a confirmed success), and `failed` (a definitive rejection). The crash semantics are the valuable part: a `pending` row is safe to redeliver plainly because the platform never saw it; an `attempting` row may have been delivered, so a red\n847 High/High Occurrence-Keyed Scheduler with Tick Ownership, Liveness Watch :: Hermes gives every scheduled run an **occurrence identity**: `(job_id, scheduled_instant)` canonicalized to UTC. Before a due job fires, the scheduler checks whether that exact occurrence already completed; if it did, the slot is consumed without a new run. A fire claim allows a small clock skew, and a skewed early fire still counts as completing its slot. A stale-schedule catch-up counter prevents a restart from replaying a backlog of \n848 High/High Event-Triggered SOP Runtime with Typed Decision Gate and Resum :: A ZeroClaw SOP is a directory with a manifest (`SOP.toml`) and an optional human-readable procedure (`SOP.md`). The manifest declares triggers, steps, dependencies, conditions, approval requirements, and an execution mode. Triggers are a fan-in: an authenticated webhook, an MQTT topic, a filesystem event, an AMQP message, a periodic cron tick, or the in-agent `sop_execute` tool. A run is started by dispatch, then advances through `sop_s\n849 High/Med Pre-Activation Skill Package Scanner with Policy-Scoped Rules  :: QwenPaw's scanner treats a skill as a **package**, not a single Markdown file. It discovers every scannable file under the skill directory, skips inert and archive extensions, caps the number of files and the size of each file, refuses symlinks and any real path that escapes the skill root, and then runs a list of analyzers. The default analyzer reads YAML signature rules for prompt injection, command injection, data exfiltration, hardco\n850 Med-High/Med Bidirectional ACP Bridge for External Agent Delegation :: Octop separates the two directions of ACP. **Inbound** is a standalone server: `octop acp --agent main` reads `~/.octop`, boots the agent, speaks ACP on stdin/stdout, and maps an editor thread to a session; it does not require the main server to be running. **Outbound** is a tool: the `acp_runner` tool exposes `action=list|start|message|respond|status|close`. `start` takes a runner and a message (plus an optional cwd), `message` continues th\n851 Med/Med Agent-Curated Topic Wiki with Link Graph and Stale-Topic Revie :: CowAgent's knowledge base is a directory of interlinked Markdown pages. `index.md` is the entry point and is regenerated from the page set; `log.md` records every write; `concepts/`, `entities/` and `sources/` are default categories but the agent may create others. Each page is a refined, structured note with cross-references to related pages. The agent writes a page when a user shares an article/document, when a conversation produces a v\n887 High/Med Deployment Pool with Health-Aware Fallback and Cooldowns :: A LiteLLM model group is backed by several deployments. The router tracks health per deployment, allows a configurable number of failures before putting a deployment into cooldown, and routes around a cooled-down deployment. If the whole group fails, it can fall back to another model group. The fallback is a first-class policy (`fallbacks`), not an ad-hoc `try/except` at every call site. NeuroCore's `LLMBridge` points at one base URL and one m\n888 High/Med Cost/Latency-Aware Routing with Per-Request Cost Ledger :: LiteLLM can choose a deployment by lowest cost, lowest latency, least busy or usage. The cost calculator turns prompt and completion tokens into input and output costs using a model cost map, and the router keeps per-deployment usage windows so a cheap but saturated deployment is not always chosen. The choice is a policy, not a hard-coded endpoint. NeuroCore's `ExecutionBudget` tracks tokens, calls, nodes and wall time, but it does not choose b\n889 Med-High/Med Model Access Groups with Wildcard Provider Routes :: LiteLLM's proxy does not grant access to a flat model list only. It supports model access groups (a named set of models), wildcard provider routes such as `anthropic/*`, and sentinels such as `all-team-models` and `all-proxy-models`. A key or team can be granted a group, and the effective model list is resolved by combining key, team, access-group and proxy lists. The wildcard route is resolved only for known provider models. NeuroCore is self-ho\n890 High/Low Context-Window Fallback and Model Aliasing :: LiteLLM lets a caller use a stable alias (`gpt-4`) that resolves to a model group. If a prompt exceeds the selected deployment's context window, `context_window_fallbacks` routes the request to a model with a larger window instead of failing. Pre-call checks filter out deployments that cannot handle the prompt before a request is sent. NeuroCore uses a single `default_model` and a per-call model name. When a long conversation or a large retrieved context ex\n891 Med-High/Low Provider Exception Taxonomy with Retry-After Preservation :: LiteLLM maps provider-specific failures into a stable exception taxonomy. A 429 becomes `RateLimitError`, a too-long prompt becomes `ContextWindowExceededError`, a 401 becomes `AuthenticationError`, and a 503 becomes `ServiceUnavailableError`. The rate-limit error preserves the `retry-after` header and reset metadata so the caller can wait the right amount instead of guessing. The mapping is centralized, so every provider benefits from th\n913 High/High Virtual Context Paging with Self-Directed Memory Edits :: Keep a bounded working set; let the agent call `page_in`, `page_out` and `note`; track dirty pages and evict least-recently-used memory with a provenance record. NeuroCore's context window is the scarce resource; paging makes eviction explicit and testable instead of silent truncation.\n914 High/Med Experience-Stage Memory Promotion :: Store observations as `candidate`; promote to `validated` after repetition or an external check; only `validated` memories are retrievable as facts. One-off model claims never become durable memory. NeuroCore's memory is only as good as its promotion rule; staging prevents hallucinated self-facts from persisting.\n915 High/Med Governed Memory Consolidation with Identity-Filtered Retrieval :: Periodically summarise episodes into facts, but filter by tenant/user identity before retrieval, redact sensitive fields, and keep the source episode links for audit. Multi-user NeuroCore deployments need memory that does not leak across identities when summaries lose their source.\n916 Med-High/Med Associative Graph Recall with Personalized PageRank :: Build a lightweight entity/relationship graph over memories; seed it with query entities; rank with personalized PageRank; cap expansion and diversify the final set. Embedding-only retrieval misses multi-hop associations that a graph can surface.\n917 Med-High/Low Position-Aware Context Assembly :: Assemble context with the strongest evidence first and last, a bounded middle, and a declared ordering policy; measure answer accuracy by position in a replay test. NeuroCore currently concatenates retrieval results; a deliberate order is a cheap accuracy win.\n945 Med-High/Med Weighted Virtual-Token Fair Share for Concurrent Agents :: Add a model-call arbiter that charges each agent or subagent a virtual counter for prompt plus completion tokens actually consumed, then admits the backlogged agent with the lowest weighted counter. Weights encode operator shares; a dominant-resource-fairness layer combines tokens, in-flight tool calls and wall-clock so a long-context agent cannot monopolize every resource. Idle capacity is borrowed and reconciled at turn end. NeuroCore run\n946 High/High Dependency-Aware Parallel Tool Plan with Sequential Fallback :: On each tool-use turn, a planner pass emits a DAG of calls with placeholders for dependent arguments. The runtime dispatches every ready node concurrently under per-tool limits, substitutes results into dependents, and falls back to the existing sequential loop if the plan fails schema validation. Record the plan and per-node timings for replay. Independent retrieval, search and tool calls in NeuroCore flows are serialized today; this cut\n958 High/Med Claim-Evidence Ledger with Offline Entailment Gate :: Split each answer into atomic claims. Require every claim to bind to a retrieved chunk with exact character offsets, stored as `claims(answer_id, text, chunk_id, span_start, span_end, verdict, score)` in SQLite. An offline ONNX cross-encoder NLI model scores `(cited span, claim)` with thresholds frozen against the model hash. Unsupported claims are dropped, re-retrieved once, or replaced with \"insufficient evidence\"; the answer is classified `ground","numLines":42,"startLine":1,"totalLines":42}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"93105f6f-e97f-4ec5-9d61-b6bb31e93541","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main"}
