{"parentUuid":"2a4a9e07-2714-4dfd-8858-3bb670e0217a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4ce50c82-a8ed-4889-ac0c-4fb42e7bef08","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NZyWvitwfCipz3aXZY3Tf7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t705 High/Med Deterministic Pre-Execution Tool Governance with Approval Esca :: `govern(my_tool, policy=\"policy.yaml\")` returns a wrapped callable. Every call is evaluated by a policy before the underlying function runs. The decision is allow, deny, or escalate to an approval handler; a denial never reaches the tool. The wrapper also infers a resource type (subprocess, network, filesystem, tool execution), writes an audit entry, and shares a breach detector per `(agent_id, session_id)` so a rogue agent cannot reset \n2\t706 High/Med Hash-Chained Audit Log with HMAC and Merkle Checkpoints :: Each audit entry carries the previous entry hash and an HMAC signature; the log can produce a Merkle root over a batch. Verification walks the chain and checks the signatures, so a deleted, reordered, or edited entry is detected. The key must come from the caller or an environment variable; the code explicitly refuses to generate a random per-instance key because that would make past entries unverifiable. AIKit's task ledger and ADRs are plain \n3\t707 Med-High/Med Agent Identity with DID, Sponsor, Capabilities and Delegation  :: An agent identity is a DID plus an Ed25519 key, a human sponsor, a capability list, a status, and a delegation lineage. A child agent cannot start with more trust than its parent allowed (`max_initial_trust_score`), which is a Sybil-resistance invariant. The identity is revocable and rotatable. AIKit's subagents and host sessions are anonymous. The task ledger records `--agent <name>` as a string, but there is no key, no sponsor, no \n4\t708 High/Med Runtime Integrity Manifest for Rules and Critical Functions :: The integrity verifier hashes every governance module file and every critical function's compiled code object. The bytecode hash covers `co_code`, constants, names, free variables, nested code objects, and every other attribute `marshal` serializes; the source comment explicitly notes that hashing only `co_code` and `str(co_consts)` would miss renamed name lookups and nested closures. A manifest is generated once and verified at startup; a \n5\t709 Med-High/Med Policy Lint, Fixture Replay and Signed Attestation :: AGT lints policy documents for schema and semantic problems, replays a set of fixtures against the policy engine, and produces an attestation with controls, evidence, and a coverage grade. The lint is fast and local; the replay is the behavioural test; the attestation is the signed result. AIKit's `GUIDELINES.md` is prose with numbered principles and rules. `sync_principles.py --check` verifies freshness and citations, but it does not test wheth\n6\t710 Med/Low Host Adapter Conformance Matrix :: AGT documents exactly what each SDK and host adapter supports: generic model/tool/run, approval, LangChain, OpenAI Agents, Anthropic, AutoGen, Semantic Kernel, CrewAI, LiteLLM, MCP, Copilot, OpenClaw, Rig. A cell marked `base` means the generic wrapper works but no dedicated adapter exists; `n/a` means the framework has no first-party package in that language. The matrix prevents a false parity claim. AIKit syncs its principles to AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md, Curso\n7\t751 High/Med Op Trail JSONL with Governance Context Hash and Evidence Eligi :: Every governed invocation writes a JSONL file before the executor returns: a `started` event with a ULID, profile, action, actor, mode of work, `governance_context_hash`, availability flag, and timestamp; a `completed` event with outcome (`done`/`failed`/`abandoned`), closer (`agent`/`doctor_sweep`), and optional evidence reference. The trail is mandatory even without SaaS; optional tiers add artifact and commit links. A lifecycle reader\n8\t752 High/Med Work-Package Lane State Machine with Acceptable-Ending Provena :: Spec Kitty separates three decisions that are easy to conflate: **terminality** (`done`, `canceled`), **acceptability** (`approved`, `done`, and `canceled` only with operator provenance), and **provenance** (`reason_source == \"operator\"`). A mission is not ready to merge unless every declared work package is at an acceptable ending; a missing work-package snapshot fails closed rather than being treated as ready. AIKit's task ledger has p\n9\t753 High/Med Owned Checkout and Worktree Isolation with Refusal Codes :: `OwnedCheckout` is a validated ownership fact, minted once per command by a private token and threaded immutably. It proves the checkout is not the repository root, is not a mission worktree, has the right mission directory and branch, and that every requested path is inside the mission directory. Refusals use a typed `OwnedRefusalCode` (`WORKTREE_INVOCATION_REFUSED`, `OWNERSHIP_NESTED`, `OWNERSHIP_FOREIGN`, `OWNED_MISSION_PATH_REFUSED`, etc.)\n10\t754 High/Med Token-Budgeted Governance Context with Fetch Substitution :: The governance context is assembled from required and suggested doctrine. `requires` edges are delivered inline (the eager closure); `suggests` edges are delivered as links with a `when` clause. If the rendered payload exceeds a character budget, the longest substitutable sections are replaced with a canonical fetch command until the payload fits. Core authority paths stay inline; every replaced section is named and fetchable, so the union of\n11\t755 Med/Low Bounded Acceptance History with Commit and Artifact Links :: Acceptance is recorded as an event with the actor, mode, source commit and acceptance commit, and a bounded history. Discarding a mission does not delete its artifacts; it stamps `discarded_at` so the audit trail survives. The invocation trail can link artifacts and commits to the invocation. AIKit's commit workflow records ADRs and task checkpoints, but acceptance is implicit in \"tests passed\" and a commit message. A bounded acceptance record\n12\t771 High/Med Speculative Attempt Journaling with No-Trace Rejection :: Temporal normally persists a `WorkflowTaskScheduled`/`Started` pair and a failure event on every attempt. A **transient** task suppresses repeated failure events and only writes the pair when the task finally succeeds. A **speculative** task goes further: its events are attached to the worker response but never written to the database. If the worker rejects the update, or returns no commands, the server discards the task as if it never existed; \n13\t772 Med-High/Med Event-Level Reset and Deterministic Replay to a Checkpoint :: Temporal can **reset** a workflow to a chosen history event, discard the events after it, and let the worker replay from that point with a new set of commands. The old history is not edited in place; the reset creates a new run that points at the base event, so the original trail remains auditable. Replay is deterministic because the event sequence, not the worker's memory, defines state. AIKit's `--reset` resets the **whole active task*\n14\t773 High/Med Idempotent Request Admission with an Abort-Reason Matrix :: A Temporal Update is a request that can be admitted, accepted, completed, rejected or aborted. Every request carries an `UpdateId`; the registry's `FindOrCreate` returns the existing update for a duplicate ID instead of running it twice. Rejections leave no history event. Every abort path has a named reason (`RegistryCleared`, `WorkflowCompleted`, `WorkflowContinuing`, `WorkflowTaskFailed`), and the document publishes the full state × reason m\n15\t774 Med-High/Med Atomic Multi-Record Checkpoint Transaction :: Temporal does not write the history event, the mutable-state row and the follow-up task independently. A single persistence update carries the whole transition: append the events, update the projection, enqueue the next task. A crash either leaves the old state or the complete new state; there is no window where the history says \"activity scheduled\" but the task queue never learns about it. AIKit's Phase 4 delivery already writes several artefacts in se\n16\t775 Med-High/Low Per-Destination Circuit Breaker for Tool Calls :: Temporal's outbound queue calls systems it does not control. A dead destination can occupy scheduler goroutines and starve healthy destinations, so the queue wraps each task in a per-destination breaker. The breaker is **two-step**: `Allow()` returns either a fast rejection or a `done` callback; the caller reports success or failure after the work. The failure signal is deliberately narrow — only a `DestinationDownError` counts, not an application-l\n17\t813 High/Med Constitution and Phase Command Pipeline :: Spec Kit treats a coding task as a sequence of explicit phases, each with a template and a command. The **constitution** is a project-level set of non-negotiables; `specify` captures intent; `clarify` resolves ambiguity; `plan` records architecture; `tasks` decomposes; `analyze`/`checklist` gate; `implement` executes; `converge` checks that the result matches the plan. AIKit already has principles, phases and gates, but its task ledger does not enforce a phase\n18\t814 High/Med Layered Artifact Resolution with Project-Local Precedence :: An artifact (command, template, script) can come from the core, a preset, an extension, or a project-local override. The resolver picks the highest-precedence layer and can report provenance: which layer supplied the file. The project-local override wins, but the inventory shows that it did. AIKit syncs rules to many hosts and has `GUIDELINES.md`, project `domain.md`, and host files. There is no single resolver that says which rule wins when \n19\t815 Med/Med Agent Integration Adapter Packages :: Each supported agent has an integration package that knows where to write its commands/skills and how to tear them down. The core does not hard-code host paths; it calls the adapter. A new agent is a new package, not a chain of `if host == ...`. AIKit already ships host rules for AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md, Cursor, Windsurf, Cline and Copilot, but the sync logic is centralized. An adapter package per host would isolate path conventions, file formats, setup/tear\n20\t816 High/Med Runtime Event Hooks for Extension Commands :: Extensions can register handlers for canonical runtime events. Resolution is layered: CLI flag → YAML override → extension-declared → built-in. A hook dispatcher runs the handler with a path-confinement check and a timeout buffer, so a hook cannot escape the project or orphan a child process. AIKit's gates are mostly command-invoked, not lifecycle-driven. A host agent can start a session, call a tool, or finish a task without triggering a rule check. Runtim\n21\t817 Med-High/Med Private Catalog and Allowlist for Components :: Spec Kit components come from catalogs. A catalog can be public or private; authentication resolves a token; a version is resolved to a concrete artifact; an install plan is validated. A private catalog lets an organization approve a set of extensions/presets/workflows and host it internally. AIKit ships skills and host files but has no signed/approved component catalog. A private catalog would let an organization publish approved skills/templates, pi\n22\t877 High/Med Decision Log Receipt with Policy Revision, Input Digest and Fi :: Every OPA decision can produce a receipt: a unique `decision_id`, the policy path, the input, the result, the bundle revision that produced it, performance metrics, the rule IDs that fired, and the non-deterministic builtin cache needed for replay. Before the event leaves the process, a masking policy returns JSON Pointers; the engine erases those fields and records the pointers in `erased`. If the remote log sink fails, the last chunk i\n23\t878 Med-High/Med Partial Evaluation for Precomputed Gate Residuals :: OPA partial evaluation separates the parts of a policy that depend on unknown input from the parts that are already known. Known values are dereferenced and false branches are discarded; what remains is a residual policy that only mentions the unknowns. This turns a large policy into a smaller runtime check and makes the remaining conditions explicit. AIKit re-evaluates the same static context on every gate run: unchanged files, unchanged rules, \n24\t879 High/Med Signed Rule Bundle with Roots, Activation and Delta Updates :: An OPA bundle is a tarball with policy, data and a `.manifest` that declares a revision and the data roots the bundle owns. A signed bundle adds `.signatures.json`; OPA verifies it against a key configured out of band and only activates the bundle if verification succeeds. If activation fails, the previous bundle stays active and the failure is reported through the status API. Delta bundles update only changed files, and root overlap is val\n25\t880 High/Low Strict Built-in Errors and Undefined-Outcome Refusal :: In Rego, a rule body that never becomes true produces **undefined**, not `false`. A built-in error also produces undefined unless strict built-in errors are enabled. This distinction matters: `false` means the policy evaluated and said no; `undefined` means the policy could not decide. OPA lets a caller opt into strict errors so a malformed input fails loudly instead of silently becoming \"no\". AIKit's gates often return a boolean, and a missing fi\n26\t881 Med-High/Low Rule Coverage Report with Skip Reasons and Conflict Refusal :: OPA's test runner reports which policy lines were evaluated and which were not, including a reason when a path was skipped by rule indexing rather than left untested. It also refuses conflicting rule definitions and overlapping bundle roots. Coverage is not just a percentage; the skip reason tells a reviewer whether a gap is a real test hole or an optimization. AIKit's tests cover the kit, but there is no report that says which principl\n27\t908 High/Med Capability-Typed Data Flow for Tool Calls :: Represent tool arguments as capability-typed values (`user`, `retrieved`, `tool_output`) and reject a call when an effectful sink receives a value whose type the task grant does not permit. AIKit governs agent actions; a type checker for tool arguments is a deterministic gate that does not depend on the model's cooperation.\n28\t909 High/Low Structured Instruction/Data Separation for Untrusted Content :: Wrap retrieved or tool-produced text in a typed data channel with a fixed delimiter and a declaration that its contents are data, then lint prompt builders for interpolation into the instruction channel. AIKit's own prompts mix rules, file content and tool output; a structural separation is testable and reduces injection surface.\n29\t910 High/Med Layered Attack-Surface Coverage Gate :: A manifest lists every untrusted input, tool, network egress and persistence sink; the gate fails when a surface has neither a control nor a reviewed `accepted_risk` record. AIKit already has risk/benefit checkpoints; this turns them into a coverage matrix that cannot silently omit a surface.\n30\t911 Med-High/Med Prompt-Injection Regression Suite with Utility/Security Metric :: Pin a small suite of benign tasks with injected payloads; report attack success rate and utility on the same run, and fail when security improves only by refusing everything. AIKit's gates need a security regression signal that is not a single hand-written prompt.\n31\t912 Med-High/Med Protocol-Level Tool Boundary Audit :: Enumerate every tool server, its transport, auth mode and data retention; fail on unauthenticated transport, wildcard scopes or sensitive payload logging. AIKit's tool integrations cross a process boundary where prompt-level rules do not apply.\n32\t943 High/Med Mutation-Adequacy Gate for Governance Tests :: Generate a bounded set of semantic mutants of gate predicates - flip comparisons, swap and/or, remove a check, alter a threshold - and run the existing gate tests against them. Require a declared kill rate per changed gate module; surviving mutants become named test obligations. Use diff-based mutation so cost tracks changed gate logic, not the whole repository. AIKit's product is its gates, so a green suite is the trust anchor. Mutation adequacy turns 'th\n33\t944 High/Med Metamorphic Invariance Gate for Deterministic Verdicts :: Declare metamorphic relations for gate commands: renaming a temporary directory or repository path, reordering independent files or rules, changing CRLF/LF, adding comments or whitespace, or shuffling JSON keys must leave the normalized verdict and findings identical. Run source/follow-up pairs and fail on any difference not explained by the declared relation. AIKit claims deterministic tooling; metamorphic relations test that claim directly wit\n34\t955 Med-High/Med Rule Canary Gate :: Each machine-checkable rule gets a canary entry: a positive probe that should pass, a negative probe against a deliberately broken fixture that must fail with a specific exit code, and SHA-256 hashes of the files the rule talks about. Phase 4 runs the probes and records the result. If a referenced artifact changes without a `rule_change` ADR or ledger record naming the rule, the gate fails. A lint also checks that meta/safety rules sit at the start or end of Tier 1 rather than i\n35\t969 Med-High/Low Rule Context-Edge Budget Linter :: Lint the Tier 1 rule file for critical-rule position and total size. Fail when a safety/meta rule drifts into the middle, when the file exceeds its token budget, or when a rule has no `critical`/`advisory` class. AIKit controls the exact text an agent reads; position and length are cheap, testable levers that the literature says matter."}]},"uuid":"51ce46ab-3696-4a66-988c-a8835cb3eb06","timestamp":"2026-10-30T12:32:21.152Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\YUNUS-~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\c--Users-Yunus-Emre-Desktop-portable-agent-kit\\05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787\\scratchpad\\new_aikit.txt","content":"705 High/Med Deterministic Pre-Execution Tool Governance with Approval Esca :: `govern(my_tool, policy=\"policy.yaml\")` returns a wrapped callable. Every call is evaluated by a policy before the underlying function runs. The decision is allow, deny, or escalate to an approval handler; a denial never reaches the tool. The wrapper also infers a resource type (subprocess, network, filesystem, tool execution), writes an audit entry, and shares a breach detector per `(agent_id, session_id)` so a rogue agent cannot reset \n706 High/Med Hash-Chained Audit Log with HMAC and Merkle Checkpoints :: Each audit entry carries the previous entry hash and an HMAC signature; the log can produce a Merkle root over a batch. Verification walks the chain and checks the signatures, so a deleted, reordered, or edited entry is detected. The key must come from the caller or an environment variable; the code explicitly refuses to generate a random per-instance key because that would make past entries unverifiable. AIKit's task ledger and ADRs are plain \n707 Med-High/Med Agent Identity with DID, Sponsor, Capabilities and Delegation  :: An agent identity is a DID plus an Ed25519 key, a human sponsor, a capability list, a status, and a delegation lineage. A child agent cannot start with more trust than its parent allowed (`max_initial_trust_score`), which is a Sybil-resistance invariant. The identity is revocable and rotatable. AIKit's subagents and host sessions are anonymous. The task ledger records `--agent <name>` as a string, but there is no key, no sponsor, no \n708 High/Med Runtime Integrity Manifest for Rules and Critical Functions :: The integrity verifier hashes every governance module file and every critical function's compiled code object. The bytecode hash covers `co_code`, constants, names, free variables, nested code objects, and every other attribute `marshal` serializes; the source comment explicitly notes that hashing only `co_code` and `str(co_consts)` would miss renamed name lookups and nested closures. A manifest is generated once and verified at startup; a \n709 Med-High/Med Policy Lint, Fixture Replay and Signed Attestation :: AGT lints policy documents for schema and semantic problems, replays a set of fixtures against the policy engine, and produces an attestation with controls, evidence, and a coverage grade. The lint is fast and local; the replay is the behavioural test; the attestation is the signed result. AIKit's `GUIDELINES.md` is prose with numbered principles and rules. `sync_principles.py --check` verifies freshness and citations, but it does not test wheth\n710 Med/Low Host Adapter Conformance Matrix :: AGT documents exactly what each SDK and host adapter supports: generic model/tool/run, approval, LangChain, OpenAI Agents, Anthropic, AutoGen, Semantic Kernel, CrewAI, LiteLLM, MCP, Copilot, OpenClaw, Rig. A cell marked `base` means the generic wrapper works but no dedicated adapter exists; `n/a` means the framework has no first-party package in that language. The matrix prevents a false parity claim. AIKit syncs its principles to AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md, Curso\n751 High/Med Op Trail JSONL with Governance Context Hash and Evidence Eligi :: Every governed invocation writes a JSONL file before the executor returns: a `started` event with a ULID, profile, action, actor, mode of work, `governance_context_hash`, availability flag, and timestamp; a `completed` event with outcome (`done`/`failed`/`abandoned`), closer (`agent`/`doctor_sweep`), and optional evidence reference. The trail is mandatory even without SaaS; optional tiers add artifact and commit links. A lifecycle reader\n752 High/Med Work-Package Lane State Machine with Acceptable-Ending Provena :: Spec Kitty separates three decisions that are easy to conflate: **terminality** (`done`, `canceled`), **acceptability** (`approved`, `done`, and `canceled` only with operator provenance), and **provenance** (`reason_source == \"operator\"`). A mission is not ready to merge unless every declared work package is at an acceptable ending; a missing work-package snapshot fails closed rather than being treated as ready. AIKit's task ledger has p\n753 High/Med Owned Checkout and Worktree Isolation with Refusal Codes :: `OwnedCheckout` is a validated ownership fact, minted once per command by a private token and threaded immutably. It proves the checkout is not the repository root, is not a mission worktree, has the right mission directory and branch, and that every requested path is inside the mission directory. Refusals use a typed `OwnedRefusalCode` (`WORKTREE_INVOCATION_REFUSED`, `OWNERSHIP_NESTED`, `OWNERSHIP_FOREIGN`, `OWNED_MISSION_PATH_REFUSED`, etc.)\n754 High/Med Token-Budgeted Governance Context with Fetch Substitution :: The governance context is assembled from required and suggested doctrine. `requires` edges are delivered inline (the eager closure); `suggests` edges are delivered as links with a `when` clause. If the rendered payload exceeds a character budget, the longest substitutable sections are replaced with a canonical fetch command until the payload fits. Core authority paths stay inline; every replaced section is named and fetchable, so the union of\n755 Med/Low Bounded Acceptance History with Commit and Artifact Links :: Acceptance is recorded as an event with the actor, mode, source commit and acceptance commit, and a bounded history. Discarding a mission does not delete its artifacts; it stamps `discarded_at` so the audit trail survives. The invocation trail can link artifacts and commits to the invocation. AIKit's commit workflow records ADRs and task checkpoints, but acceptance is implicit in \"tests passed\" and a commit message. A bounded acceptance record\n771 High/Med Speculative Attempt Journaling with No-Trace Rejection :: Temporal normally persists a `WorkflowTaskScheduled`/`Started` pair and a failure event on every attempt. A **transient** task suppresses repeated failure events and only writes the pair when the task finally succeeds. A **speculative** task goes further: its events are attached to the worker response but never written to the database. If the worker rejects the update, or returns no commands, the server discards the task as if it never existed; \n772 Med-High/Med Event-Level Reset and Deterministic Replay to a Checkpoint :: Temporal can **reset** a workflow to a chosen history event, discard the events after it, and let the worker replay from that point with a new set of commands. The old history is not edited in place; the reset creates a new run that points at the base event, so the original trail remains auditable. Replay is deterministic because the event sequence, not the worker's memory, defines state. AIKit's `--reset` resets the **whole active task*\n773 High/Med Idempotent Request Admission with an Abort-Reason Matrix :: A Temporal Update is a request that can be admitted, accepted, completed, rejected or aborted. Every request carries an `UpdateId`; the registry's `FindOrCreate` returns the existing update for a duplicate ID instead of running it twice. Rejections leave no history event. Every abort path has a named reason (`RegistryCleared`, `WorkflowCompleted`, `WorkflowContinuing`, `WorkflowTaskFailed`), and the document publishes the full state × reason m\n774 Med-High/Med Atomic Multi-Record Checkpoint Transaction :: Temporal does not write the history event, the mutable-state row and the follow-up task independently. A single persistence update carries the whole transition: append the events, update the projection, enqueue the next task. A crash either leaves the old state or the complete new state; there is no window where the history says \"activity scheduled\" but the task queue never learns about it. AIKit's Phase 4 delivery already writes several artefacts in se\n775 Med-High/Low Per-Destination Circuit Breaker for Tool Calls :: Temporal's outbound queue calls systems it does not control. A dead destination can occupy scheduler goroutines and starve healthy destinations, so the queue wraps each task in a per-destination breaker. The breaker is **two-step**: `Allow()` returns either a fast rejection or a `done` callback; the caller reports success or failure after the work. The failure signal is deliberately narrow — only a `DestinationDownError` counts, not an application-l\n813 High/Med Constitution and Phase Command Pipeline :: Spec Kit treats a coding task as a sequence of explicit phases, each with a template and a command. The **constitution** is a project-level set of non-negotiables; `specify` captures intent; `clarify` resolves ambiguity; `plan` records architecture; `tasks` decomposes; `analyze`/`checklist` gate; `implement` executes; `converge` checks that the result matches the plan. AIKit already has principles, phases and gates, but its task ledger does not enforce a phase\n814 High/Med Layered Artifact Resolution with Project-Local Precedence :: An artifact (command, template, script) can come from the core, a preset, an extension, or a project-local override. The resolver picks the highest-precedence layer and can report provenance: which layer supplied the file. The project-local override wins, but the inventory shows that it did. AIKit syncs rules to many hosts and has `GUIDELINES.md`, project `domain.md`, and host files. There is no single resolver that says which rule wins when \n815 Med/Med Agent Integration Adapter Packages :: Each supported agent has an integration package that knows where to write its commands/skills and how to tear them down. The core does not hard-code host paths; it calls the adapter. A new agent is a new package, not a chain of `if host == ...`. AIKit already ships host rules for AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md, Cursor, Windsurf, Cline and Copilot, but the sync logic is centralized. An adapter package per host would isolate path conventions, file formats, setup/tear\n816 High/Med Runtime Event Hooks for Extens