ble_agent_kit\\projects\\research-engine","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"73dbd771-969d-4482-91bd-fee38b8f6e96","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4ce50c82-a8ed-4889-ac0c-4fb42e7bef08","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XTokXnfXLa8oWKC9wbe78k","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t694 High/Med Immutable Evidence Versions with Availability Cutoff :: Every source is an immutable `MaterialVersion`. The version ID is distinct from the URL; the record carries the exact retrieval time, publication time, availability time and basis, issuer, and content. A `Span` binds a quote to a version and character offsets. `SnapshotPoolProvider` excludes versions that were not available at the claim's `as_of` cutoff, and `_validate_evidence()` rejects future, stale, or unquoted evidence before it enters the gr\n2\t695 High/Med Lineage-Aware Independence via Union-Find :: FactCircuit does not count URLs as independent evidence. It builds groups by linking any two evidence items that share a publisher group, an origin ID, a canonical URL, or a content hash. The linkage is transitive: a wire story, its syndicated copies, and a repost with the same content collapse into one group. The decision then counts groups, not documents. A group that contains both support and contradiction is marked conflicting and counted on both sides. \n3\t696 High/Med Evidence Verdict vs. World Verdict :: FactCircuit separates two questions that are easy to conflate: **does the supplied evidence support the claim?** and **did the event actually happen in the world?** `VerificationResult` can carry an `evidence_verdict` and a `world_verdict` with separate bases and rationales. A document can strongly support a claim while the underlying event is still unverified, or the world evidence can contradict a widely reported claim. Research Engine's verdicts need this distin\n4\t697 High/Med Gap-Driven Bounded Loop with Exact Tasks :: Uncertainty is represented as a `Gap`, not a vague \"need more research\". A gap has a question, a stage (provenance or verification), a dimension, whether it blocks a verdict, its decision impact, an action (`search`, `fetch`, `reanalyze`), a locator, and a probe ID. The loop selects a source for the gap, records the task IDs that selected it, and carries open gaps forward until they are resolved or the budget runs out. `CallBudgetTransport` counts every model\n5\t698 Med-High/Med Fixed-Target Evaluation with Cutoffs and Event-Cluster Bootstr :: The evaluation harness is fixed-target: gold labels are supplied separately and adjudicated; predictions must cover exactly the same case set; the prediction cutoff must equal the gold cutoff; provenance roots must be reachable through annotated edges; unknown evidence IDs are rejected rather than silently treated as true. Metrics include a four-state confusion matrix, source correctness, high-risk false acceptance, and calibration, \n6\t738 High/Med Typed Logical vs. Dialectical Relation Model :: Argdown does not use one generic \"edge\". It separates: - **Logical relations** between statements: `entails`, `contrary`, `contradictory`. - **Dialectical relations**: `attack` and `support` from an argument or statement to an argument. - **Undercut**: a relation from an argument or statement to an inference, attacking the inference rather than its conclusion. - **Equivalence classes**: statements that are logically equivalent, modeled separately from rel\n7\t739 High/Med Implicit Relation Derivation from Premise-Conclusion Structure :: If a statement is used as a premise in an argument, the statement supports the argument. If it is the main conclusion, the argument supports the statement. If an equivalence class attacks another argument and is used as a premise, the containing argument inherits an attack. Argdown derives these implicit relations from the premise-conclusion structure instead of asking the author to duplicate every edge. Research Engine's claim graph has\n8\t740 Med-High/Med Plugin/Processor Pipeline with Error Isolation :: Argdown's application is a chain of processors, each holding plugins. For each processor, plugins run `prepare`, then AST listeners, then `run`, in registration order. A plugin error cancels the current processor and is collected; the application can continue to the next processor. Plugins are stateless; the request and response objects carry the data. Research Engine's pipeline has stages: parse a claim, build the graph, derive relations, score evi\n9\t741 High/Low Equivalence Classes for Duplicate Claims :: An equivalence class groups statements that mean the same thing. It is not an identity merge: the members remain distinct statements with their own source locations and provenance, but the model treats them as one logical unit for relation derivation and display. This is the right shape for duplicate claims found in different sources. Research Engine will encounter the same claim phrased differently across papers, reports and datasets. If it creates a new nod\n10\t742 Med/Low Stable Intermediate Model with Multi-Format Export :: Argdown builds one response model (statements, arguments, relations, sections, maps) and exports it to JSON, GraphML, DOT, HTML, web components and Pandoc. Exporters read the model; they do not re-parse the source or invent their own graph. The intermediate model is the contract. Research Engine needs the same separation. A report, a graph visualization, a JSON API and a reproducibility bundle should all come from one canonical graph, not four ad-hoc\n11\t808 High/Med Evidence-Budgeted Retrieval with Relevance Cutoff and Source C :: PaperQA treats evidence retrieval as a budget, not an open-ended search. `evidence_k` caps how many texts are retrieved, `evidence_relevance_score_cutoff` drops contexts below a relevance score, and `answer_max_sources` caps how many distinct documents can support an answer. Contexts are de-duplicated by identity before they enter the session. The result is a small, scored, source-diverse evidence set instead of a pile of loosely related\n12\t809 High/Med Typed Evidence Context with Summary, Score and Citation Bindin :: PaperQA's unit of evidence is a typed `Context`, not a raw chunk. It carries the source text, the question it was evaluated against, a bounded summary, a 0–10 relevance score, and a citation string. The summary is generated with the citation already embedded, so the answer model sees a grounded, compact evidence item. Contexts are compared and de-duplicated as objects, and the final answer records which contexts it actually used (`used_c\n13\t810 High/Low Explicit Abstention and Answer-Status Contract :: PaperQA distinguishes four outcomes: a supported answer (`SUCCESS`), an answer the agent is unsure about (`UNSURE`), a run that was cut short by a budget (`TRUNCATED`), and a failed run (`FAIL`). When there is no usable context, `aquery` does not invent an answer; it returns an explicit cannot-answer phrase. The `Complete` tool forces the agent to state certainty, and the runner downgrades a completion with `has_successful_answer is False` to `UNSURE`. \n14\t811 Med-High/Low Deterministic Fake Agent for Pipeline Testing :: PaperQA ships a fake agent that is not an LLM. It invokes the same tools in a deterministic order, so the environment, tool contracts, status line and answer assembly can be tested end to end without model calls. The real agent and the fake agent share the same `EnvironmentState` and tool implementations; only the policy that chooses tools differs. Research Engine's gap-driven loop is LLM-driven, which makes end-to-end tests slow, flaky and expensive\n15\t812 Med-High/Low Status Line with Evidence Counters and Hard Step/Cost Budget :: After every tool call, PaperQA returns a compact status line: paper count, relevant paper count, current evidence count and current cost. The agent sees this line in its observation and can decide whether to search more, gather more evidence or answer. `max_timesteps`, `max_answer_attempts` and a timeout bound the loop; when the timeout hits, the runner answers with `TRUNCATED` rather than pretending the run completed. Research Engine'\n16\t818 High/Low Three-Valued Verdict Labels: In, Out, Undecided :: Every statement and argument node gets a value: `1.0` is `in`, `0.0` is `out`, anything else is `undecided`. The label is a first-class output, not a fallback. `node-standard-label` maps the numeric value to the symbol. Research Engine's verdicts should not force a binary true/false. A claim with insufficient evidence is `undecided`, not false. A claim with conflicting evidence is `undecided` or `disputed`, not a coin flip. A three-valued label makes t\n17\t819 High/Med Pluggable Proof Standards per Claim/Domain :: A proof standard decides how pro and con arguments combine. Carneades supports several standards; the policy analysis tool lets the analyst choose one and compare the outcome. `evaluate-policy` applies the selected policy to the case; `give-acceptability?` decides whether the main issue is acceptable. Research Engine should not use one universal threshold. A medical claim, a legal claim and a product claim have different evidence standards. A proof standard\n18\t820 High/High Multiple Argumentation Semantics Behind One Evaluator :: Carneades separates the **argument graph** from the **evaluation semantics**. Dung's grounded labelling, ASPIC's structured argumentation, and CAES are different evaluators over the same graph. The protocol lets an evaluator assign values and labels; a skeptical or credulous variation maps multiple extensions to one label. Research Engine can use this to avoid baking one semantics into the graph. A simple claim might use grounded semantics; a le\n19\t821 High/Med Argument Construction with Assumptions and Undercutters :: Carneades distinguishes a statement's truth from an argument's support and allows an argument to be **undercut** by attacking an assumption or an inference. The graph is constructed from premises, conclusions, assumptions and exceptions, not just support/attack edges. Research Engine's kill tests should target assumptions and inference steps, not only claims. An argument can be valid on its premises but undercut by a violated assumption; a clai\n20\t822 Med/Med Scenario Separation and Evaluation Trace :: Carneades separates a **case** (facts, statements, arguments) from an **evaluation** (a policy/standard applied to the case). The same case can be evaluated under multiple policies, and the results can be compared. The case is shareable; an evaluation is a derived artifact. Research Engine should separate the claim graph from the evaluation run. A graph can be evaluated under different proof standards, semantics, or evidence cutoffs; each evaluation is a trace\n21\t918 High/Med GRADE-Style Certainty Profile for Claims :: Each claim carries a certainty profile with the five downgrade domains and an explicit starting level; the verdict reports both the raw confidence and the certainty ceiling. Research Engine already has verdicts but no structured way to say *why* confidence is capped.\n22\t919 High/Med PICO Applicability Matrix for Questions and Evidence :: Store PICO fields on questions and evidence; build an applicability matrix and flag evidence that differs on a dimension (e.g., different population) instead of treating it as direct support. Indirect evidence is a common source of overconfident verdicts.\n23\t920 Med-High/Low Publication-Bias and File-Drawer Audit :: Require a search log with databases, queries, dates and counts; record unpublished/negative evidence explicitly; run a trim-and-fill-style sensitivity check and downgrade when the file-drawer risk is high. A claim graph built only from positive citations will look stronger than the literature.\n24\t921 Med-High/Med Replication-Aware Effect Synthesis with Shrinkage :: When multiple studies support a claim, record effect size and sample size; synthesise with a random-effects model, report heterogeneity, and shrink overconfident small-sample effects toward the pooled estimate. Research Engine currently counts support edges; a synthesis model would distinguish one large study from five tiny ones.\n25\t922 High/Med Source-Level Risk-of-Bias Assessment Record :: Store a per-source, per-result risk-of-bias record with the five RoB 2 domains, a judgment (`low`/`some`/`high`) and a support sentence; propagate the judgment into the certainty profile. A source can be reliable for one result and biased for another; a paper-level flag loses that distinction.\n26\t951 High/High Provenance-Polynomial Support and Defeat Certificates :: Annotate each claim/evidence edge with a semiring value and propagate it through derivations. Every verdict receives a polynomial whose terms are alternative evidence combinations; simplify it to a minimal support certificate and a minimal defeat/contradiction certificate. Retracting a source updates affected certificates exactly, and two claims backed by the same underlying source become visibly non-independent. The engine's promise is provenan\n27\t952 High/Med Equivalence-Bounded Negative Claims (TOST Verdict Gate) :: Before a claim can become evidence of absence or no effect, require a pre-declared smallest effect size of interest and equivalence bounds. Run two one-sided tests, or require the 90% confidence interval to lie wholly within the bounds; a non-significant difference test remains UNKNOWN, not FALSIFIED or PROVEN. Underpowered tests cannot become negative claims. Falsification-first verdicts need a rule for negative claims; this prevents the engin\n28\t964 High/Med Pre-Registered Kill-Test Ledger with Calibration :: Before any evidence exists, write a hash-chained forecast row: `{claim_id, p_support (quantised to 0.01), kill_test_spec, survival_region, stopping_rule, prev_hash, row_hash = sha256(canonical_json || prev_hash)}`. Edits are new `supersedes` rows; history is never mutated. A test is *severe* only if its survival region is a non-empty proper subset of the declared outcome space; a non-severe test forces `UNFALSIFIABLE`. `SUPPORTED` also requires two ru\n29\t971 High/Med Immutable Analysis Records with Re-Analysis :: A statistical comparison becomes a first-class immutable record: claim, method, seed/resamples, inputs digest, protocol/instrument identity, result JSON, and the analysis it supersedes. Re-running under a new method appends a new row; the old row is never edited. A verdict can cite the analysis, and the engine refuses to treat it as current when the observation set has changed since. The engine already binds kill-test runs to command, code and inputs. Obse\n30\t972 High/Med Random-Effects Meta-Analysis with Forest Plot :: Pool the same outcome across several claims or studies: Hedges' g with small-sample correction, inverse-variance fixed effects, DerSimonian-Laird random effects, Cochran's Q with a chi-square p-value, I-squared, tau-squared, a pooled confidence interval, study weights, and an ASCII forest plot with a summary diamond. The engine can compare two observation groups inside one claim, but it cannot answer \"what is the pooled effect across these five claims?\".\n31\t973 High/Med Semantic Snapshot Verification :: `export --check` currently recomputes the export digest. A file with a recomputed digest and nonsense records would pass. Semantic verification checks unique `(type, id)`, known record types, edge endpoints and same-program scope, closed vocabularies, existing parents, acyclic supersession, finite numeric fields and parseable timestamps. This makes a snapshot portable and reviewable: a third party can trust the graph, not just the bytes.\n32\t974 High/Low Mass-Missing Evidence Volume Guard :: If a large fraction of tracked input files vanish in one pass, treat it as an unavailable volume, not mass deletion. Remove nothing and require explicit acceptance. The engine already tags missing inputs in `inputs_sha256`, but a moved data folder makes every NUMERIC claim emit `EVID-005` \"stale evidence\" at once. The reviewer sees a wall of stale-evidence errors instead of the one fact that matters: the evidence volume is missing.\n33\t975 High/Low Preview-Bound Confirmation Digest :: A destructive command computes a digest of exactly what it will change. Execution requires that digest, so a stale preview is refused. `cascade preview` shows the affected claims but does not bind the plan. An agent can preview, the graph changes, and then execute a different cascade than the one reviewed. The same risk applies to `claim merge`, `source retract` and `observation void`.\n34\t976 High/Low Offline Bibliography Import :: Parse local BibTeX, RIS and CSL-JSON files into source records. This is explicit registration, not literature crawling: the engine still makes no network calls and does not parse PDFs. It removes the manual typing that makes a bibliography tedious.\n35\t977 Med/Low Evidentiary Support-Cycle Check :: A claim cannot corroborate itself through a loop. The engine already checks dependency cycles (`GRAPH-001`), but `supports` and `extends` edges can form a cycle that makes two claims look independently supported.\n36\t978 Med-High/Low Coverage-Aware Consensus :: A consensus score is always paired with the fraction of intended evidence that actually contributed. Missing methods are renormalized, never imputed; an untested dimension cannot pass. The engine's current consensus is a confidence-weighted support-minus-contradiction score with no coverage or single-source warning.\n37\t979 High/Med Executable Test Identity :: A kill test is evidence only if the engine can say exactly what it executed. Today `binding()` covers the command string, the negative control, `engine/**.py` and declared inputs. The test script is usually `scripts/kill.py`, which is not bound; `expect_exit` and `timeout_seconds` are stored on the test row but excluded from the binding; `cwd`, shell and interpreter are fixed but not recorded. A researcher can edit the script, the expected exit code or the timeout and keep t\n38\t980 High/Med Source Work Identity and Terminal Retraction :: A paper is one work, not one row. Normalize DOI, resolve identity at write time, and make retraction cover every row that denotes the same work. A reinstatement creates a new source row linked to the old one; old claims stay retired and must be re-cited. This is the difference between a retraction that means something and a row-level flag that a twin bypasses. The current engine always inserts, has no unique DOI, and accepts `retracted -> active`. If a pa\n39\t981 High/Med Anchored Evidence Ledger :: Append every consequential record to a per-program ledger: sequence, kind, subject, payload digest, previous hash, hash. A high-water counter makes a deleted tail unreusable. `ledger anchor --out FILE` commits `(seq, head)` to an external journal; `ledger verify --anchors` walks the chain and detects an edit, a gap or a truncation. The trust boundary is the published anchor, not the local file. The engine's append-only tables are ordinary rows. A probe deleted the newest eve\n40\t982 High/Low Cascade Volume Guard :: Before `claim invalidate` or `source retract` writes, compute the whole affected set. If it is implausibly large both absolutely and proportionally, refuse the entire transaction and report a held-back event. A mistaken mass collapse is terminal for promotion and can only be repaired by revisions; the guard turns a one-command catastrophe into a reviewable decision. The engine already computes the transitive set; it simply applies it. A 30-claim program can lose 25 claims becaus\n41\t983 High/Med Whole-State Fingerprint :: Hash schema version, counters, schema metadata and every table in canonical order. The fingerprint becomes a witness of completeness: if it does not change, no logical state changed. Use it to prove that `cascade preview`, `analysis`, `outline`, `brief`, `report` (without `--out`) and `export --check` are read-only, and that a backup is logically identical to the source. `db integrity` currently runs SQLite PRAGMAs only; it cannot tell a read-only command from a writer, and a\n42\t984 Med-High/Low Finding-Code Registry and Capabilities Manifest :: Every finding code is one registry record: code, family, default severity, summary. A scanner finds code literals in `engine/*.py`; tests fail when a literal is unregistered, a registry entry is missing from the README, or a code appears in one but not the other. A `capabilities` command derives commands, exit codes, vocabularies and limits from live constants. The engine emits 20+ codes across `integrity.py`, but `test_docs.py` checks only 8 and t\n43\t985 High/Med Review Provenance and Inter-Rater Agreement :: Record who decided, under which criteria version, and with what independent second opinion. Screening becomes append-only decisions rather than one mutable row. Two reviewers can screen the same source; the engine computes Cohen's kappa for two raters or Krippendorff's alpha for missing/multiple raters, and a conflict requires an explicit resolution. Verdicts and observations also carry the actor. The engine currently defaults the actor to `\"agent\"`, has n\n44\t986 High/Med Pre-Registered Analysis Plan :: Register claim, baseline label, treatment label, direction and expected effect before the groups have data. A plan registered after data exists is accepted but marked `post_data`. `analysis stats` matches a plan, stores the plan id and observation digest, and labels an unmatched run exploratory. A `NUMERIC` promotion whose latest stats run is exploratory warns. Hypothesis pre-registration stops one step too early: the researcher can still choose the comparison after seei\n45\t987 Med-High/Low Protocol Document Recompute :: When a protocol names a document, hash the document, not a string the operator typed. `protocol add --doc PATH` computes the digest; `protocol verify` re-reads and compares; an observation stores the protocol digest it used. A changed document is an error, a missing document a warning. The current `--doc-digest` accepts any 64-hex string and is never recomputed. A probe registered a protocol with `a`*64; `check` accepted it.\n46\t988 Med/Low Robust Observation Summary :: Add median, quartiles, IQR, MAD and a Tukey outlier flag to the observation summary. Scale values by their largest magnitude before squaring so extreme finite inputs do not overflow. Report an outlier count as a warning; never trim or void automatically. `observation_summary` currently prints only n, min, max and mean. One corrupt value can dominate Welch/Cohen's d with no visible warning, and `statistics.variance` can raise on `[1e308, -1e308]`.\n47\t989 Med/Low Polarity-Aware Contradiction Proposals :: Keep negation tokens out of the content set and record a polarity bit. High-overlap pairs with opposite polarity are contradiction candidates; high-overlap same-polarity pairs are paraphrases/duplicates. Rank opposite pairs first and include the shared terms in the reason. Advisory only; never auto-link. `analysis.keywords` currently treats `not`/`no` as stopwords, so it erases polarity. `unlinked_contradictions` scores pure Jaccard and can rank a paraphrase abo\n48\t990 High/Med Comparison Family with Holm Correction :: A p-value is only interpretable inside the family of tests that were actually run. Declare a family label, record each member's raw p-value and test kind, and report the Holm-adjusted p for every member. Holm controls the family-wise error rate under arbitrary dependence and is simple enough to keep the engine standard-library only. `analysis.stats` currently warns that a single pairwise test is uncorrected when more than two groups exist, but the warning disap\n49\t991 High/Med Verified Restore with Quarantine :: `db restore BACKUP [--force]` verifies the backup's SQLite integrity and schema first, then moves the live database and its `-wal`/`-shm` sidecars into a timestamped `.corrupt_<utc>` quarantine, copies the backup through a flushed temporary file and `os.replace`, and re-verifies the result. A failed restore puts the quarantine back. `db backup` gains a sidecar with schema, counts, SHA-256 and engine version. A backup without a tested restore path is only a copy. A re\n50\t992 Med/Low Aggregate Context Pack :: `context RESEARCH --budget N` assembles one orientation pack from the existing line builders: header and counts, questions, strongest claims, open findings and measurement summaries. Allocate a fixed share to each section, pack each share with the existing `context.pack`, and assert the final `estimate_tokens(text) <= N`. Report per-section omitted counts and the total. Today `outline`, `brief` and `claim context` each take an independent budget; an agent that calls all three c\n51\t993 High/Med Pre-Registration Amendment Ledger :: An amendment is an append-only row: subject kind and id, field, old value, new value, reason, actor and time. `hypothesis amend` and `protocol amend` write it; `hypothesis card` and `protocol list` show the chain. The original registration stays visible, and calibration scores the original confidence unless a new hypothesis is created. A statement or criterion change is not a cosmetic edit: it changes what the evidence was supposed to test. Without a ledger, a post-"}]},"uuid":"d3249373-a356-463e-9891-661455e9977f","timestamp":"2026-10-30T12:33:50.024Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\YUNUS-~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\c--Users-Yunus-Emre-Desktop-portable-agent-kit\\05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787\\scratchpad\\new_research-engine.txt","content":"694 High/Med Immutable Evidence Versions with Availability Cutoff :: Every source is an immutable `MaterialVersion`. The version ID is distinct from the URL; the record carries the exact retrieval time, publication time, availability time and basis, issuer, and content. A `Span` binds a quote to a version and character offsets. `SnapshotPoolProvider` excludes versions that were not available at the claim's `as_of` cutoff, and `_validate_evidence()` rejects future, stale, or unquoted evidence before it enters the gr\n695 High/Med Lineage-Aware Independence via Union-Find :: FactCircuit does not count URLs as independent evidence. It builds groups by linking any two evidence items that share a publisher group, an origin ID, a canonical URL, or a content hash. The linkage is transitive: a wire story, its syndicated copies, and a repost with the same content collapse into one group. The decision then counts groups, not documents. A group that contains both support and contradiction is marked conflicting and counted on both sides. \n696 High/Med Evidence Verdict vs. World Verdict :: FactCircuit separates two questions that are easy to conflate: **does the supplied evidence support the claim?** and **did the event actually happen in the world?** `VerificationResult` can carry an `evidence_verdict` and a `world_verdict` with separate bases and rationales. A document can strongly support a claim while the underlying event is still unverified, or the world evidence can contradict a widely reported claim. Research Engine's verdicts need this distin\n697 High/Med Gap-Driven Bounded Loop with Exact Tasks :: Uncertainty is represented as a `Gap`, not a vague \"need more research\". A gap has a question, a stage (provenance or verification), a dimension, whether it blocks a verdict, its decision impact, an action (`search`, `fetch`, `reanalyze`), a locator, and a probe ID. The loop selects a source for the gap, records the task IDs that selected it, and carries open gaps forward until they are resolved or the budget runs out. `CallBudgetTransport` counts every model\n698 Med-High/Med Fixed-Target Evaluation with Cutoffs and Event-Cluster Bootstr :: The evaluation harness is fixed-target: gold labels are supplied separately and adjudicated; predictions must cover exactly the same case set; the prediction cutoff must equal the gold cutoff; provenance roots must be reachable through annotated edges; unknown evidence IDs are rejected rather than silently treated as true. Metrics include a four-state confusion matrix, source correctness, high-risk false acceptance, and calibration, \n738 High/Med Typed Logical vs. Dialectical Relation Model :: Argdown does not use one generic \"edge\". It separates: - **Logical relations** between statements: `entails`, `contrary`, `contradictory`. - **Dialectical relations**: `attack` and `support` from an argument or statement to an argument. - **Undercut**: a relation from an argument or statement to an inference, attacking the inference rather than its conclusion. - **Equivalence classes**: statements that are logically equivalent, modeled separately from rel\n739 High/Med Implicit Relation Derivation from Premise-Conclusion Structure :: If a statement is used as a premise in an argument, the statement supports the argument. If it is the main conclusion, the argument supports the statement. If an equivalence class attacks another argument and is used as a premise, the containing argument inherits an attack. Argdown derives these implicit relations from the premise-conclusion structure instead of asking the author to duplicate every edge. Research Engine's claim graph has\n740 Med-High/Med Plugin/Processor Pipeline with Error Isolation :: Argdown's application is a chain of processors, each holding plugins. For each processor, plugins run `prepare`, then AST listeners, then `run`, in registration order. A plugin error cancels the current processor and is collected; the application can continue to the next processor. Plugins are stateless; the request and response objects carry the data. Research Engine's pipeline has stages: parse a claim, build the graph, derive relations, score evi\n741 High/Low Equivalence Classes for Duplicate Claims :: An equivalence class groups statements that mean the same thing. It is not an identity merge: the members remain distinct statements with their own source locations and provenance, but the model treats them as one logical unit for relation derivation and display. This is the right shape for duplicate claims found in different sources. Research Engine will encounter the same claim phrased differently across papers, reports and datasets. If it creates a new nod\n742 Med/Low Stable Intermediate Model with Multi-Format Export :: Argdown builds one response model (statements, arguments, relations, sections, maps) and exports it to JSON, GraphML, DOT, HTML, web components and Pandoc. Exporters read the model; they do not re-parse the source or invent their own graph. The intermediate model is the contract. Research Engine needs the same separation. A report, a graph visualization, a JSON API and a reproducibility bundle should all come from one canonical graph, not four ad-hoc\n808 High/Med Evidence-Budgeted Retrieval with Relevance Cutoff and Source C :: PaperQA treats evidence retrieval as a budget, not an open-ended search. `evidence_k` caps how many texts are retrieved, `evidence_relevance_score_cutoff` drops contexts below a relevance score, and `answer_max_sources` caps how many distinct documents can support an answer. Contexts are de-duplicated by identity before they enter the session. The result is a small, scored, source-diverse evidence set instead of a pile of loosely related\n809 High/Med Typed Evidence Context with Summary, Score and Citation Bindin :: PaperQA's unit of evidence is a typed `Context`, not a raw chunk. It carries the source text, the question it was evaluated against, a bounded summary, a 0–10 relevance score, and a citation string. The summary is generated with the citation already embedded, so the answer model sees a grounded, compact evidence item. Contexts are compared and de-duplicated as objects, and the final answer records which contexts it actually used (`used_c\n810 High/Low Explicit Abstention and Answer-Status Contract :: PaperQA distinguishes four outcomes: a supported answer (`SUCCESS`), an answer the agent is unsure about (`UNSURE`), a run that was cut short by a budget (`TRUNCATED`), and a failed run (`FAIL`). When there is no usable context, `aquery` does not invent an answer; it returns an explicit cannot-answer phrase. The `Complete` tool forces the agent to state certainty, and the runner downgrades a completion with `has_successful_answer is False` to `UNSURE`. \n811 Med-High/Low Deterministic Fake Agent for Pipeline Testing :: PaperQA ships a fake agent that is not an LLM. It invokes the same tools in a deterministic order, so the environment, tool contracts, status line and answer assembly can be tested end to end without model calls. The real agent and the fake agent share the same `EnvironmentState` and tool implementations; only the policy that chooses tools differs. Research Engine's gap-driven loop is LLM-driven, which makes end-to-end tests slow, flaky and expensive\n812 Med-High/Low Status Line with Evidence Counters and Hard Step/Cost Budget :: After every tool call, PaperQA returns a compact status line: paper count, relevant paper count, current evidence count and current cost. The agent sees this line in its observation and can decide whether to search more, gather more evidence or answer. `max_timesteps`, `max_answer_attempts` and a timeout bound the loop; when the timeout hits, the runner answers with `TRUNCATED` rather than pretending the run completed. Research Engine'\n818 High/Low Three-Valued Verdict Labels: In, Out, Undecided :: Every statement and argument node gets a value: `1.0` is `in`, `0.0` is `out`, anything else is `undecided`. The label is a first-class output, not a fallback. `node-standard-label` maps the numeric value to the symbol. Research Engine's verdicts should not force a binary true/false. A claim with insufficient evidence is `undecided`, not false. A claim with conflicting evidence is `undecided` or `disputed`, not a coin flip. A three-valued label makes t\n819 High/Med Pluggable Proof Standards per Claim/Domain :: A proof standard decides how pro and con arguments combine. Carneades supports several standards; the policy analysis tool lets the analyst choose one and compare the outcome. `evaluate-policy` applies the selected policy to the case; `give-acceptability?` decides whether the main issue is acceptable. Research Engine should not use one universal threshold. A medical claim, a legal claim and a product claim have different evidence standards. A proof standard\n820 High/High Multiple Argumentation Semantics Behind One Evaluator :: Carneades separates the **argument graph** from the **evaluation semantics**. Dung's grounded labelling, ASPIC's structured argumentation, and CAES are different evaluators over the same graph. The protocol lets an evaluator assign values and labels; a skeptical or credulous variation maps multiple extensions to one label. Research Engine can use this to avoid baking one semantics into the graph. A simple claim might use grounded semantics; a le\n821 High/Med Argument Construction with Assumptions and Undercutters :: Carneades distinguishes a statement's truth from an argument's support and allows an argument to be **undercut** by attacking an assumption or an inference. The graph is constructed from premises, conclusions, assumptions and exceptions, not just support/attack edges. Research Engine's kill tests should target assumptions and inference steps, not only claims. An argument can be valid on its premises but undercut by a violated assumption; a clai\n822 Med/Med Scenario Separation and Evaluation Trace :: Carneades separates a **case** (facts, statements, arguments) from an **evaluation** (a policy/standard applied to the case). The same case can be evaluated under multiple policies, and the results can be compared. The case is shareable; an evaluation is a derived artifact. Research Engine should separate the claim graph from the evaluation run. A graph can be evaluated under different proof standards, semantics, or evidence cutoffs; each evaluation is a trace\n918 High/Med GRADE-Style Certainty Profile for Claims :: Each claim carries a certainty profile with the five downgrade domains and an explicit starting level; the verdict reports both the raw confidence and the certainty ceiling. Research Engine already has verdicts but no structured way to say *why* confidence is capped.\n919 High/Med PICO Applicability Matrix for Questions and Evidence :: Store PICO fields on questions and evidence; build an applicability matrix and flag evidence that differs on a dimension (e.g., different population) instead of treating it as direct support. Indirect evidence is a common source of overconfident verdicts.\n920 Med-High/Low Publication-Bias and File-Drawer Audit :: Require a search log with databases, queries, dates and counts; record unpublished/negative evidence explicitly; run a trim-and-fill-style sensitivity check and downgrade when the file-drawer risk is high. A claim graph built only from positive citations will look stronger than the literature.\n921 Med-High/Med Replication-Aware Effect Synthesis with Shrinkage :: When multiple studies support a claim, record effect size and sample size; synthesise with a random-effects model, report heterogeneity, and shrink overconfident small-sample effects toward the pooled estimate. Research Engine currently counts support edges; a synthesis model would distinguish one large study from five tiny ones.\n922 High/Med Source-Level Risk-of-Bias Assessment Record :: Store a per-source, per-result risk-of-bias record with the five RoB 2 domains, a judgment (`low`/`some`/`high`) and a support sentence; propagate the judgment into the certainty profile. A source can be reliable for one result and biased for another; a paper-level flag loses that distinction.\n951 High/High Provenance-Polynomial Support and Defeat Certificates :: Annotate each claim/eviden