Med Random-Effects Meta-Analysis with Forest Plot :: Pool the same outcome across several claims or studies: Hedges' g with small-sample correction, inverse-variance fixed effects, DerSimonian-Laird random effects, Cochran's Q with a chi-square p-value, I-squared, tau-squared, a pooled confidence interval, study weights, and an ASCII forest plot with a summary diamond. The engine can compare two observation groups inside one claim, but it cannot answer \"what is the pooled effect across these five claims?\".\n973 High/Med Semantic Snapshot Verification :: `export --check` currently recomputes the export digest. A file with a recomputed digest and nonsense records would pass. Semantic verification checks unique `(type, id)`, known record types, edge endpoints and same-program scope, closed vocabularies, existing parents, acyclic supersession, finite numeric fields and parseable timestamps. This makes a snapshot portable and reviewable: a third party can trust the graph, not just the bytes.\n974 High/Low Mass-Missing Evidence Volume Guard :: If a large fraction of tracked input files vanish in one pass, treat it as an unavailable volume, not mass deletion. Remove nothing and require explicit acceptance. The engine already tags missing inputs in `inputs_sha256`, but a moved data folder makes every NUMERIC claim emit `EVID-005` \"stale evidence\" at once. The reviewer sees a wall of stale-evidence errors instead of the one fact that matters: the evidence volume is missing.\n975 High/Low Preview-Bound Confirmation Digest :: A destructive command computes a digest of exactly what it will change. Execution requires that digest, so a stale preview is refused. `cascade preview` shows the affected claims but does not bind the plan. An agent can preview, the graph changes, and then execute a different cascade than the one reviewed. The same risk applies to `claim merge`, `source retract` and `observation void`.\n976 High/Low Offline Bibliography Import :: Parse local BibTeX, R