{"parentUuid":"7d1d6695-a605-4eec-85ee-4f251405fd93","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4ce50c82-a8ed-4889-ac0c-4fb42e7bef08","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WVGBShX4BqYATRtsGFKQTu","type":"tool_result","content":"aegean-licensing-ops/aegean_licensing_ops: __init__.py __main__.py __pycache__ auditor.py cli.py database.py issuer.py markdown.py operations.py register_writer.py sanitize.py storage.py templates.py ui.html vocabulary.py webapp.py  aegean-licensing/aegean_licensing: __init__.py __main__.py __pycache__ bounded_json.py bundler.py cert.py cli.py fingerprint.py license_terms.py metadata.py register.py verifier.py \n682 High/Med Canonical Leaf Construction with Timestamp-Bound Identity :: A CT leaf is not \"the certificate bytes\". It is a `MerkleTreeLeaf` containing a timestamped entry: the log entry type, the X.509 entry (or precertificate entry with issuer key hash), the SCT timestamp, and extensions. `ctutil.LeafHash()` hashes that canonical structure. The same certificate submitted at two different times produces two different leaves b\n683 High/Med Inclusion and Consistency Proofs for Public Receipts :: CT separates two questions. **Inclusion** proves that a leaf is in a tree with a given root: the client hashes the leaf, follows the audit path with the leaf index and tree size, and compares the result to the signed root. **Consistency** proves that a later tree extends an earlier one: two roots and a consistency path show that no earlier leaf was removed or\n684 High/Low Signed Checkpoints with Frozen STH and Key Pinning :: A Signed Tree Head is a small statement: tree size, timestamp, root hash, and a signature over those bytes. `GetSTH()` verifies the signature before returning the STH. A frozen STH does the same for a pinned checkpoint at startup: if the configured root/size does not match the signature, the log refuses to start. The log ID is the SHA-256 hash of the public key\n685 Med-High/Med Trusted Register List with Lifecycle States and MMD :: CT clients do not trust an arbitrary log URL. They consume a signed log list that names operators, log URLs, public keys, maximum merge delay (MMD), lifecycle state, and the temporal interval a log accepts. A read-only log publishes a final tree head that must never change. A retired or rejected log stays in the list for historical verification, but is not\n686 Med-High/Med Monitor/Scanner for Unauthorized or Broken Entries :: A CT monitor scans every entry in a log and applies matchers. It can find a specific serial number, a subject or issuer pattern, a timestamp, a certificate that fails to parse, or a certificate that fails chain verification. The scanner exposes callbacks for matching certificates and precertificates, so a monitor can alert without holding the log's private \n687 Med/High Temporal Sharding for a Long-Lived Register :: A temporal log splits one logical log into shards by certificate `NotAfter` (or another time boundary). Each shard has its own URL, key, and interval. `NewTemporalLogClient()` validates that the intervals are contiguous and chronological; `IndexByDate()` routes a certificate to the shard whose interval contains its date. The client can fetch roots from all shards and \n728 High/Med Pluggable Entry Type Registry with Kind and Version :: Rekor entries are registered by `(kind, version)`. Each type implements the same `EntryImpl` contract: it can be created from artifact properties, unmarshaled from a proposed entry, canonicalized, indexed, and asked for its verifiers and artifact hash. A factory creates the right implementation; an allowlist controls which kinds a server accepts. The log itsel\n729 High/Low JCS Canonicalization and Signed Entry Envelope :: Before signing, Rekor marshals the entry, canonicalizes it with JCS, and signs the canonical bytes. JCS fixes object-key ordering, number formatting, string escaping and whitespace, so two semantically identical JSON documents produce the same bytes. Without it, a signature can fail after a harmless re-serialization, or two different byte strings can represent the \n730 Med-High/High Virtual-Index Sharding with Active and Inactive Ranges :: Rekor can run multiple Trillian trees as shards. A **virtual index** maps a global entry position to `(tree_id, leaf_index)`, so a client sees one continuous log even though the data lives in several trees. Inactive shards remain readable; their ranges and public keys are served alongside the active shard. An entry ID encodes the tree ID, so a lookup c\n731 Med-High/Med Bounded Index Query with Set Operators :: Rekor's search endpoint accepts a bounded set of lookup keys (hash, public key, email, subject) and combines results with `or` or `and` set operations. Entries declare their own `IndexKeys()`, so the index is built from the canonical entry, not from a separate hand-maintained table. The `maxSearchQueries` cap prevents an unbounded query. Aegean-Licensing's public regis\n732 High/Med Pluggable PKI and Signature Verifier Registry :: Rekor does not hard-code one signature format. An `ArtifactFactory` creates a public-key parser for a declared format; the entry type asks for its verifiers and validates the signature with the matching implementation. X.509, PGP, SSH, minisign, PKCS#7 and TUF are separate packages behind the same interface. Aegean-Licensing must verify certificates from different i\n761 High/Med Provisioner Registry for Pluggable Issuance Authorization :: A provisioner is an authorization method, not a certificate type. It receives a one-time token or identity assertion, authenticates it, and returns a set of `SignOption`s that constrain the certificate. JWK uses a pre-shared key, OIDC uses an identity token, ACME uses a challenge, X5C uses a trusted client certificate, cloud uses an instance identity doc\n762 High/Med Short-Lived Certificates with Passive Revocation :: A short-lived certificate expires quickly, so a revocation list is less critical: an unexpired certificate is the main trust decision, and renewal is the normal path. `step-ca` supports `Renew()` and `Rekey()` under a provisioner's `AuthorizeRenew()` policy, and still supports explicit revocation. Aegean-Licensing can issue license certificates with a short valid\n763 High/Med Name/Identity Policy Engine for Certificate Constraints :: The policy engine checks a certificate or CSR against permitted and excluded names: common name, DNS, IP range, email, URI domain, and principal. It supports literal wildcard rules and returns a typed `NamePolicyError`. The authority calls it before signing, so a provisioner cannot issue a certificate outside the configured namespace. Aegean-Licensing need\n764 Med-High/Med Provisioner-Scoped Certificate Templates :: A provisioner can carry a certificate template and a set of X.509 options: allowed/denied names, wildcard policy, key usage, lifetime. `TemplateOptions()` renders the template with request data; `signX509()` applies it before the authority signs. The template is scoped to the provisioner, so different issuance channels can produce different certificate shapes. Aegean\n765 High/Med Authorizing and Enriching Webhooks Before Issuance :: Before signing, the authority can call **enriching** webhooks (add data to the certificate request) and **authorizing** webhooks (approve or deny the certificate). The webhook is configured per provisioner; the admin API validates HTTPS, no embedded credentials, and a known webhook kind. The call happens in the signing path, so a denial prevents issuance. Aegea\n796 High/Med Signed Layout as an Issuance Policy :: A layout is a signed policy, not a config file. It names the steps, the functionary keys authorized for each step, the artifact rules, the inspections, and an expiration. The verifier rejects an unsigned, wrongly signed or expired layout before looking at any evidence. Aegean-Licensing can publish a signed **issuance policy**: which issuers may create which certificate types,\n797 High/Med Link Metadata as Signed Issuance Evidence :: A link is a signed record of one step: what command ran, which materials went in, which products came out, and any byproducts. The verifier loads the links, checks the signatures and the authorized functionaries, then applies the layout's artifact rules. Aegean-Licensing can use a **signed issuance link** per certificate: the application/terms/customer data are material\n798 High/Med Artifact Rules for Certificate Field Authorization :: Artifact rules are a small policy language: `CREATE`, `DELETE`, `MODIFY`, `ALLOW`, `DISALLOW`, `REQUIRE`, `MATCH ... FROM <step>`. They authorize what a step may create, delete or change, and chain a step's products to a later step's materials. `DISALLOW *` is the recommended final rule. Aegean-Licensing can apply the same language to certificate fields: an iss\n799 High/Med Layout Expiration and Threshold Verification :: A layout has an expiration; an expired layout is rejected before evidence is checked. A step has a threshold; the verifier requires at least that many authorized functionary links and checks that their materials/products agree. Sublayouts allow delegation. Aegean-Licensing needs both: an issuance policy that expires (so an old policy cannot be used forever) and a thr\n800 Med-High/Med Inspections as Post-Issuance Verification Hooks :: An inspection is a command that runs during verification, not during the supply-chain step. It can check the final artifacts against the layout's expectations (for example, run a test, verify a signature, inspect a package) and produce a link. Inspections are the verifier's own checks, not the producer's claims. Aegean-Licensing can use inspections for post-is\n933 High/Med Merkle Inclusion and Consistency Proofs for the Public Registe :: Hash leaves as `SHA-256(0x00 || record)` and nodes as `SHA-256(0x01 || L || R)`; publish signed checkpoints and O(log n) inclusion/consistency proofs that an offline client can verify. A public register must prove that a licence was included and that history was not rewritten.\r\n934 Med-High/Med Witness-Cosigned Register Head :: Collect k-of-n witness signatures over each checkpoint; a client rejects a head without a quorum and gossips conflicting heads. A single register operator can otherwise show different histories to different verifiers.\r\n935 High/Med Signed Revocation Status List with Freshness Window :: Publish a signed `revocation_set` with a monotone version, `thisUpdate`, `nextUpdate` and a delta; clients fail closed when the window has expired. A stale or unsigned revocation list is worse than none because it looks authoritative.\r\n936 High/Low Machine-Readable Relying-Party Verification Policy :: A signed JSON policy declares accepted algorithms, key-table root, revocation freshness, required claims and clock skew; the verifier loads it and reports every rejection reason. Different relying parties need different trust rules without forking the verifier.\r\n937 Med/Med Register Transparency Monitor with Anomaly Receipts :: A monitor fetches checkpoints on a schedule, compares them, and emits a signed anomaly receipt for a split view, a size rollback or a root mismatch; verifiers can publish receipts. Proofs only help if someone actually checks the head over time.\r\n947 Med-High/Med Claim-Granular Disclosure Envelope with Salted Commitments :: Add a presentation envelope in which every certificate claim is salted and hashed into a Merkle tree, with the root stored in the public register alongside the certificate digest. A licensee presents only the claims a relying party needs - license type, project scope, expiry, allowances - plus sibling hashes; the verifier recomputes the root, matche\n948 Med-High/Low Typed Revocation Reasons with Retroactive Invalidity :: Replace the register's free-text reason with a closed reason code from RFC 5280 plus an optional invalidity date. The verifier evaluates the certificate as of the refe