{"parentUuid":"9b7e2598-f454-4f79-b7c3-fecae6146a6b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4ce50c82-a8ed-4889-ac0c-4fb42e7bef08","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DUEFSwE1W6qcvn4aoANYJ1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t1005 High/Med Chain-State Writer Lock :: Merklock now locks the keystore leaf counter (ADR-050) but still writes `chain.json` through an unlocked read-modify-write. Two `send`, `mine` or `sync` processes can load the same tip, each mutate its own copy, and let the last writer erase the other's mempool entry or block. The chain file is the other half of the lost-update problem; the leaf is safe, but the transfer that burned it can disappear. The fix reuses `storage.file_lock` from ADR-050. Hold it across the load → \n2\t1006 High/Med Peer Dial Address Policy :: `PeerLink.__enter__` calls `socket.create_connection((host, port))`; the name is resolved at connect time and no address class is checked. PEX and peer-book hosts are peer-supplied and only length/type-checked, so a hostile peer can point a node at loopback, RFC1918, link-local or multicast services, or use DNS rebinding to pass a future hostname check. A public chain should not dial private space because a peer asked it to. Resolve the host once, validate every numeric res\n3\t1007 Med-High/Low Ordered Shutdown with Final Persist :: `PeerService.stop` closes the accept socket and relay, then joins the accept thread for two seconds, but connection handlers are daemon threads and `_serve_bounded` never checks a stopping flag. A block accepted just before shutdown can be dropped before `persist` runs; `_run_serve` returns immediately after `stop()` with no final save. On a small testnet that is a lost block, not a theoretical race. Add a `threading.Event` stopping flag, check it at the top \n4\t1008 Med-High/Med Transaction Version Fail-Closed :: `Transaction.body()` always emits `TRANSACTION_VERSION`, but `Transaction.from_dict` never reads the field and silently drops unknown keys. A document labelled version 2 is reinterpreted as version 1, and an unauthenticated extra field disappears without a finding. Today only v1 exists, so the risk is a future upgrade hazard rather than a live exploit; the same shape appears in block and chain loaders. Make admission fail closed: require the exact supported versi\n5\t1009 Med/Low Integer Digit Cap in Strict JSON :: `hardening.strict_loads` rejects floats, NaN, duplicate keys and deep nesting, but passes integers straight to `json.loads`. Python 3.11 added a 4,300-digit conversion limit; 3.10.0-3.10.6, which Merklock still supports, did not. A 1 MiB frame of digits can therefore burn CPU and memory before the parser rejects it, inside the 1 MiB frame bound. A `parse_int` hook with a small digit cap closes the slow path without changing any valid protocol value.\n6\t1010 Med-High/Low Canonical Merkle Proof Length :: `hashing.merkle_verify` walks siblings until the proof ends and accepts trailing empty elements: a valid proof can be padded with arbitrary empty siblings and still verify. That is not a forgery, but it makes the encoding malleable, so two peers can hold different bytes for the same proof and the wire format has no canonical form. A strict length check derived from the leaf count and index removes the ambiguity and bounds the work.\n7\t1011 Med/Low Tri-State CLI Verification :: `verify-account --confirmations 6` with no local chain prints `Confirmations: 0` and exits 0. A requested check that could not run reads as a pass, which is the wrong default for an operator or a script. The verifier should say `UNAVAILABLE`, explain that no chain or light state is present, and exit non-zero when a positive confirmation count was requested.\n8\t1012 Med/Med Stable Diagnostic Codes for Doctor and Verify :: `doctor --json` emits ad-hoc `{name, ok, detail}` objects and `verify` prints prose. CI can assert only the check name, not the specific failure, and rewording a message breaks tooling. A small registry of stable codes (`storage.backup_missing`, `keystore.rolled_back`, `chain.bad_state_root`) lets every failure carry a code, a location and a fix while keeping the human prose.\n9\t1013 Med/Low Claims Bound to Live Test Guards :: SECURITY.md, WHITEPAPER.md and README make load-bearing claims: fixed supply, a leaf is never reused, no governance, no dependencies. A test that parses each claim's guard file and asserts the named class/method exists turns document rot into a suite failure without changing consensus.\n10\t1014 Med/Med Generated Capability Manifest :: Consensus and wire limits are scattered across `ChainParams`, `netwire` and `hardening`, and there is no single discovery surface for an operator or an agent. A `capabilities --json` command can publish the chain id/genesis/supply/suite activation, protocol/frame/sync caps, CLI commands and exit codes, assembled from the same constants the code uses. Descriptive only; no governance.\n11\t632 High/Med Account-key compact filters for light clients :: Commit a per-block probabilistic filter over touched account identifiers and state keys, anchor its header chain to block headers, and let a light client download headers plus filters and request only matching blocks and proofs. Merklock's light client currently has no private relevance test; filters would reduce what a server learns about a wallet and fit the existing state-root proof path.\n12\t633 Med/Med Committed-header sampling for bounded-memory sync :: Store random-offset commitments to header hashes while catching up, then redownload and fully validate the header range once a work threshold is reached. A Python light client has limited memory; this bounds initial header state without weakening final validation.\n13\t634 Med-High/Med Source-diverse bucketed peer manager :: Maintain new and tried peer buckets keyed by hashed network group, cap slots per bucket, evict low-quality entries and occasionally test unreachable peers to refresh the tried set. Merklock already caps discovered peers per range but has no persistent new/tried structure; this raises the cost of address poisoning and single-range eclipses.\n14\t635 Med/Low Damped per-block difficulty controller :: Replace interval retargeting with a per-block controller that damps each deviation by a configurable factor and clamps the adjustment, so a low-hashrate chain recovers from spikes without oscillating. Merklock targets a small testnet hash market; a damped controller can stabilize block time without touching emission or supply.\n15\t636 High/Med Recovery phrase with on-chain leaf watermark :: Encode the seed, tree height and suite with a checksum; on restore rebuild the leaves, scan the chain from a birthday height for the address's transactions, read the public nonce (leaf index), and set next_leaf to the maximum seen plus one. Refuse to sign until the scan reaches the tip. A stale keystore counter is the one recovery bug that causes catastrophic Winternitz reuse; Merklock already exposes the leaf index in every transfer.\n16\t637 Med/Med Template-first miner handshake :: A miner requests a block template from a full node, validates prev-hash, height and target, hashes it, sends shares to the job issuer and block solutions to the node, then refreshes. Merklock's current external backend is one-shot; a template-first session lets a backend prove work against the real tip without trusting a pool.\n17\t638 Low/Low Deterministic benchmark transcript :: A fixed-seed hash batch produces an order-independent checksum and an expected-value table by algorithm, size and thread count; a verifier detects skipped work or fake hashrate using a keyed chain rather than XOR. Merklock has CPU/external benchmark commands but no verifiable transcript for reported hashrate.\n18\t639 Med-Low/Low Operator API safety envelope :: Loopback-first binding, optional bearer token, read-only mode blocking non-GET, JSON content-type enforcement, constant-time token comparison and a per-token request budget. Any future node dashboard or admin API should be safe by default; Merklock currently has no HTTP surface.\n19\t640 Med/Low Feature bits with dependency closure :: Advertise an optional/required capability vector where setting a bit transitively requires its dependencies; unknown required bits reject the connection, and compiled-in bits cannot be unset. Merklock is adding header long-poll, snapshots and verifier seams; negotiated capabilities let old peers interoperate without silent fallback.\n20\t641 Med/Low Bounded priority egress with RTT liveness :: Two outbound classes (control before bulk), caps on message count and retained bytes, fixed per-message overhead, disconnect on overflow, and periodic ping with timeout and measured RTT. Merklock's relay queue is bounded but has no priority classes or RTT-based stale-peer detection.\n21\t642 Med/Low Encrypted versioned backup with atomic swap :: Seal a checkpoint bundle in a versioned envelope with a checksum, write it to a temporary file, flush and rename over the old file, retaining one archive generation; restore with a seed-derived key and replay. Merklock's light state and peer book need portable encrypted backups without exposing keys.\n22\t643 Med/Med Out-of-process verifier with pinned key registry :: Invoke a configured verifier binary with a versioned envelope (scheme, curve, transcript, circuit id, verifying-key hash, proof, public inputs); enforce timeout and output caps; reject unknown schemes or key-hash mismatches, and pin keys by hash in genesis/state. A future zk or ML-DSA verifier can be optional without linking heavy C++/CUDA into consensus; pinning prevents silent circuit substitution.\n23\t644 High/Med Witnessed header sync with divergence evidence :: Pin a checkpoint; fetch headers from a primary and several witnesses; on mismatch, walk back to the bifurcation, verify both branches, persist conflicting headers plus peer identities as an evidence bundle, and drop the divergent peer. Merklock's light client currently trusts one peer's page; witness comparison and a portable evidence bundle raise the cost of split-view attacks.\n24\t645 Med/Low Evidence inbox with expiry and rebroadcast :: Verify evidence on receipt, deduplicate by digest, index by height and wall time, expire after configurable limits, cap message size and rebroadcast pending items on an interval. Merklock has no place to retain proof of one-time-signature reuse or conflicting headers before it is forgotten.\n25\t646 High/Med Anchored snapshot manifest :: Define a manifest with height, format version, chunk count, the trusted header's state root and one hash per chunk; fetch chunks from several peers, verify immediately, keep partial progress, resume after disconnect, and accept only when the recomputed state root matches. Merklock's sync replays from genesis and has no snapshot bootstrap; an anchored manifest would speed a new node without trusting one peer.\n26\t647 Med/Med Expiring scoped session grants :: The account root key signs a grant naming a delegate key, allowed transaction kinds, a spend cap and an expiry height/time; the grant lives in state with a time-ordered expiry index and can be revoked by the root. Winternitz keys are single-use; hot or light clients should delegate narrow authority instead of reusing the root key.\n27\t648 Med/Low Quality-weighted sync scheduler :: Order block/header sources by per-peer success/failure history, cap in-flight requests per peer, demote peers that stall or serve invalid data, and retry from the next-best source while fully validating every block. Merklock's peer book records counters but sync selection does not yet use them for source ordering.\n28\t649 Med-Low/Low Cover-set account proof requests :: When a light client requests state proofs, ask for a shuffled batch of k addresses: the real one plus deterministic decoys derived from a rotating local seed and the chain tip, then discard the decoys. Merklock's address-keyed proof requests leak wallet links; this is k-anonymity at a bandwidth cost, not cryptographic privacy.\n29\t650 Med-Low/Low Clock-skew-aware signed operator envelope :: HMAC over sorted params with timestamp plus a short validity window, periodic server-time calibration and a nonce/replay cache for privileged operator commands. A future Merklock admin/RPC surface needs replay-resistant signed commands and explicit clock handling.\n30\t651 Med/Low Bounded reconnect with backpressure :: Capped attempts, full-jitter exponential backoff, bounded queue, explicit terminal error and a give-up state for peer and light-client connections. Merklock's long-poll/watch loop reconnects every round without a shared backoff policy; a bounded reconnect helper would avoid tight loops.\n31\t652 Med/High Horizon-pruned archival profile :: An opt-in pruned node keeps the last H blocks plus all headers and state roots; older bodies and Winternitz signatures become a per-height digest, and new nodes bootstrap from a pinned state-root checkpoint while archival nodes keep everything. Winternitz signatures and authentication paths dominate storage; the state root already commits to balances. This is a trust-boundary profile, never default.\n32\t653 High/Med Mempool-delta compact block relay :: Announce a header plus per-peer-salted short transaction IDs; peers reconstruct from the mempool, request only missing bodies, and fall back to a full block after one timeout. Multi-kilobyte Winternitz signatures make block propagation the sync bottleneck.\n33\t654 High/Low Transaction Block-to-Live Expiry :: A transaction may carry a block-to-live (BTL) height. When the field is non-zero, `tx_val()` rejects it unless `bnum <= btl <= bnum + 0x100`: the transaction is not valid before the current height and cannot live more than 256 blocks into the future. A zero field means no deadline, which keeps old transactions valid. Merklock has no transaction expiry. A signed transfer can sit in a mempool indefinitely, be replayed months later, or remain a pending conflict for a on\n34\t655 High/Med Gap-Free, Source-Ordered Block Inclusion :: Mochimo enforces a canonical block order: every transaction's source address must be strictly greater than the previous one, and no source may appear twice in the same block. This is not cosmetic. With one-time signatures, two transactions from the same account can reveal overlapping chain material; a canonical order plus deduplication makes the block's state transition deterministic and eliminates a class of intra-block double-spend ambiguity before signatur\n35\t656 Med-High/Med Orphaned-Block Archive with Split Metadata :: When Mochimo accepts a block at a height that already has a different block, it does not delete the old one. It renames the old block to a `.sp` split file, preserving the losing candidate alongside the winner. The split file is a local forensic record: it shows what the node saw, which branch lost, and when. Merklock's `Chain.adopt()` replaces `self.blocks` wholesale with the heavier candidate and keeps only the surviving mempool. The orphaned branch d\n36\t657 Med/Low Capability-Bit Handshake Negotiation :: Mochimo carries an 8-bit capability field in the transaction/version header. A node advertises what it can do (push candidate blocks, opt in to network activity, logging, miner-fee signalling, sanctuary protocol), and peers can avoid asking for operations the other side does not support. Merklock's handshake authenticates the peer and exchanges height/work, but it has no feature negotiation. Today every peer is assumed to speak the same protocol. As soon as Merklo\n37\t659 High/Med Bounded OTS Usage Bitmap with Overflow Counter :: QRL tracks one-time-signature usage in two tiers. For indices below `max_ots_tracking_index` (8,192), it stores a bit per leaf and can answer \"was this exact leaf used?\" For indices at or above the threshold, it stores a single high-water `ots_counter`; any index at or below the counter is considered used. `get_unused_ots_index()` first scans the bitmap for a zero bit, then falls back to `counter + 1`; it returns `None` when the tree is exhausted. Merkl\n38\t660 High/Med Burn-on-First-Sight Leaf Tombstones Across Reorgs :: QRL's OTS machinery has both a set and an unset path. On rollback, `unset_ots_key()` clears the bit for a tracked index, or recomputes `ots_counter` from the remaining transaction hashes. The intent is to let a reorged transaction be re-included. The security problem is that the signature was already public: if the same leaf is later used for a different message, the WOTS/XMSS chain material overlaps and the key can be forged. Unsetting a leaf on reo\n39\t661 High/Med Bounded Reorg Limit with Published Finality Depth :: QRL maintains a moving re-org limit: when the chain reaches height `h`, the limit becomes `h - reorg_limit` (if that is greater than the stored value). A block at or below the limit is ignored; a fork whose fork point is below the limit is refused. The limit is persisted in the state DB, so a restart does not reopen deep history. Merklock's `Chain.adopt()` accepts any heavier, compatible chain, as long as genesis and parameters match. There is no fin\n40\t662 High/High Suite Migration Transaction Before Retirement :: QRL's lattice transaction is a migration primitive. The owner of an existing XMSS address signs a transaction that carries new-suite public keys (Kyber for KEM, Dilithium for signatures, ECDSA as a compatibility key). The transaction pays a fee, is validated under a hard-fork gate, and stores the new key material against the existing address. It does not move the balance; it binds a future key to the account. A later spend can use the new scheme. Merklo\n41\t663 High/High Weighted Multisig with Expiring Spend Proposals :: QRL's multisig is a policy account, not a script. `MultiSigCreate` registers a set of signatory addresses with weights and a threshold; the multisig address is derived from the creation transaction hash. A `MultiSigSpend` proposal commits to recipients, amounts, and an expiry height. Signatories vote with `MultiSigVote`; when the accumulated weight reaches the threshold, the spend executes. Votes can be withdrawn before execution, and an expired propo\n42\t664 High/High BDS Traversal State in the Keystore :: BDS (Buchmann–Dahmen–Szydlo) keeps the authentication path for the next leaf and updates it incrementally. The state contains the current `auth` path, a `keep` cache, a set of `treehash` instances that compute future nodes, a stack, a `retain` area for the lower tree levels, and `next_leaf`. On each signature, `xmss_core_sign()` copies the already-computed `auth` path, signs, then calls `bds_round()` and `bds_treehash_update()` to prepare the next path. The expen\n43\t665 High/High Hypertree Capacity for Long-Lived Addresses :: XMSS^MT splits a total height `full_height` into `d` layers of `tree_height = full_height / d`. A signature carries one WOTS+ signature and one authentication path per layer, and the signer advances the leaf index across the layers. Key generation and signing scale with the subtree height, not the full height, so a total height of 20 or 40 is practical. Merklock's single tree has `MAX_HEIGHT = 16`, i.e. 65,536 signatures per address, and even that is impr\n44\t666 Med-High/Med Parameter-Set OID in Keys and Witnesses :: XMSS keys and signatures start with a 32-bit OID. The OID selects the hash function, `n`, Winternitz `w`, tree height, number of layers, and BDS trade-off. `xmss_str_to_oid(\"XMSS-SHA2_10_256\")` returns a stable identifier; `xmss_parse_oid()` populates a `xmss_params` struct that drives every size and validation decision. The implementation never guesses a parameter from context. Merklock's suite prefix identifies the algorithm family (`mkl1`), and the tran\n45\t667 Med-High/Med Versioned Keystore State Blob with Forward Migration :: XMSS does not promise a fixed secret-key layout. The traversal choice changes the state, and the serialized key includes the BDS state. The reference implementation exposes `xmss_xmssmt_core_sk_bytes()` so callers allocate the right size, and its README explicitly warns that the BDS state format may change without changing the public API or output. Merklock's keystore is version 1 with a fixed set of fields: `version`, `kind`, `address`, `suit\n46\t668 Med/Low Hard-Stop Exhaustion and Secret Zeroization :: XMSS treats exhaustion as a hard stop. When the last leaf is reached, `xmss_core_sign()` zeroizes the secret key in memory and returns an error. The test signs exactly `2^h` times, then expects every further attempt to fail. The comment is explicit: production code must make the deletion durable on disk, and the last index may be reserved to avoid an ambiguous \"finished\" state. Merklock's `Wallet.reserve_leaf()` already refuses at capacity with `WalletError\n47\t669 Med-High/Med Generation-fenced solver leases :: Bind every external mining assignment to a lease tuple of tip hash, monotonically increasing generation, salt range and deadline. A new tip increments the generation, drops staged and in-flight work, clears salts and broadcasts a cancel; results carry the generation and are discarded before validation if stale. Give each spawned solver a fresh session token so a restarted process cannot inherit an old lease. Merklock's external backend is one-shot today; fencing m\n48\t670 Med/Med Inclusion-state submission verifier :: After a block or share submission is included, read the canonical slot for that height (the accepted block producer or equivalent state entry) instead of trusting the transaction status. Classify the outcome as accepted, lost race (another producer won the same height) or rejected (no entry written); treat a lost race as stale-round, not invalid work, and do not use a head-state dry run to explain it. A miner integration that reports every included-but-not-winning \n49\t671 Med/Low Tip-readiness gate for miner startup :: Before funding or starting an external miner, poll the node's sync state: accept the first clear answer if no sync was ever observed, require two consecutive clear polls after a sync, keep waiting without a fixed ceiling while block progress is reported, and give up after a bounded unreachable grace. Warn when the node expects peers but has none. Starting a miner against a node that is still importing history wastes work on a stale tip and produces avoidable stale\n50\t672 High/Med Secure-Store Callback Before Signature Release :: liboqs does not write a stateful secret key itself. The application supplies `secure_store_sk`, which receives the serialized key bytes and a context, and the stateful signer calls it before the signature is considered complete. The callback is the storage contract: a deployment can use a file, an HSM, a secure element or an encrypted database without the algorithm knowing. Merklock's `Wallet.save()` is already atomic (temp, fsync, `.bak`, replace), and\n51\t673 Med-High/Low Lock/Unlock/Mutex Triple for Stateful Signing :: A stateful one-time signature is only safe if two signers cannot reserve the same leaf. liboqs makes the application provide all three of a lock function, an unlock function and a mutex. If only part of the triple is set, the lock operation fails rather than silently serializing nothing. Merklock's `Wallet` is a single-process object. The CLI is single-writer, but a daemon, a test harness or two concurrent CLI processes can load the same keystore and\n52\t674 Med-High/Low Suite Support Tier and Upstream Evidence :: liboqs separates three facts that are easy to conflate: the algorithm identifier is known, the algorithm is compiled in, and the project makes a support commitment to it. `ALGORITHMS.md` records upstream maintenance, OQS tier, NIST level, constant-time status, formal verification and optimization targets. `OQS_SIG_alg_is_enabled()` answers the runtime availability question separately from the name list. Merklock's `suites.py` has `suite_id`, `prefix`, `al\n53\t675 High/Med KAT/ACVP Vector Gate for Signature Primitives :: liboqs pins primitive outputs to reviewed known-answer vectors and to NIST ACVP vectors. A KAT failure means the implementation changed its output, not that a test was inconvenient. The stateful KAT is especially relevant: it reads a pre-generated key because key generation can take hours. Merklock has a deterministic WOTS/Merkle construction. A refactor of the hash domain, the Winternitz chain order or the auth-path ordering can change a signature while\n54\t676 Med-High/Low Malformed-Signature Bitflip Corpus :: liboqs does not only test a valid signature. It flips message and signature bits and asserts that verification fails, and it surrounds buffers with magic canaries so an out-of-bounds read or write is visible. The Wycheproof harness adds an external corpus of malformed inputs. Merklock's `verify_signature()` is the consensus boundary. A malformed signature must return false with a typed error, never raise an unexpected exception, read past the buffer or accept a\n55\t677 Med-High/Med Namespace-Scoped State Proof with Completeness :: NMT orders leaves by namespace and proves a complete namespace range: the verifier supplies the leaves, gets their namespaced leaf hashes, and checks that no leaf of that namespace exists outside `[proof.start, proof.end)`. `VerifyNamespace` is therefore stronger than a single inclusion proof. Merklock's account state is sorted by address and committed as a single Merkle root. A light client can prove one account, but cannot prove \"all accounts for \n56\t678 Med-High/Med Absence Proof for Non-Membership :: NMT distinguishes an empty proof, an absence proof with a boundary leaf hash, and a normal inclusion proof. An absence proof shows that the namespace falls inside the covered range but no leaf with that namespace exists; an empty proof covers a namespace outside the root's range. Merklock's `verify_account` can return a zero-valued account record for an unknown address, but that is an assertion by the prover, not a proof of absence. A light client cannot distingu\n57\t679 High/Low Inclusion vs Completeness as Separate Verify Verbs :: NMT makes the difference explicit in the API: `VerifyInclusion` proves that the supplied leaves are in the tree, but it does not prove that no other leaves of the namespace exist. `VerifyNamespace` adds the completeness check. A caller cannot accidentally get \"complete\" from the weaker verb. Merklock's `verify_account` and `verify_inclusion` already return a height/confirmation count, but the names do not say whether the result is a single fact or a\n58\t680 Med-High/Low Proof Envelope Carries Tree Options :: An NMT proof is only meaningful under the tree options used to build it: namespace ID size, the `IgnoreMaxNamespace` policy and the hash function. NMT carries `isMaxNamespaceIDIgnored` in the proof and derives the namespace size from the queried ID; a mismatch with the verifier's tree fails. This is the difference between \"the proof is valid\" and \"the proof is valid for the tree I think I am checking\". Merklock proof documents carry `version`, `chain_id`, `hei\n59\t681 High/Low Proof Structure Validation Before Slicing :: NMT validates a proof's shape before it slices or hashes: the range must be `start < end`, every node must have the hasher's expected length, the root format must be checked before reading its namespace bounds, and a short root must return false rather than panic. Merklock's `verify_proof` and `verify_account_proof` already parse attacker-controlled JSON. They should never raise a raw `IndexError`, `ValueError` or `TypeError` on malformed input. A proof that\n60\t699 Med/Low Batched leaf reservation watermark :: On top of the secure-store contract, persist a reservation watermark for a batch of leaves before signing any of them; sign only below the watermark, and on startup resume at the watermark, burning unused leaves. Choose the batch size to amortize durable writes, and never reserve past tree capacity or across a suite migration. Per-signature fsync is expensive; batching reduces writes without reopening the crash-reuse window, provided the watermark is committed befor\n61\t700 High/Med Peer Watermarks with Grace Period and Protected Peers :: `BasicConnMgr` keeps connections between a low and a high watermark. When the count exceeds the high watermark, it trims down to the low watermark, but it skips new peers still inside a grace period and skips peers with a protected tag. A silence period prevents trim thrash, and a protected peer is only closed when there is no unprotected candidate left. Merklock's `PeerBook` has a hard `MAX_PEERS = 256`; once full, `add()` raises. That rejects a\n62\t701 High/Med Scoped Resource Budgets per Peer and Connection :: The resource manager accounts for resources at nested scopes: a stream belongs to a connection, which belongs to a peer; usage rolls up. Limits can be set at the system, peer, connection, stream, protocol or service level, and an autoscaler derives concrete values from system memory. A limit failure returns a typed error; the caller decides whether to degrade or close. Merklock has global wire limits (`MAX_FRAME_BYTES`, `MAX_MESSAGES_PER_CONNECTION`, `"}]},"uuid":"2db38412-a079-44a2-bfc5-083289250997","timestamp":"2026-10-30T12:37:52.816Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\YUNUS-~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\c--Users-Yunus-Emre-Desktop-portable-agent-kit\\05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787\\scratchpad\\new_merklock.txt","content":"1005 High/Med Chain-State Writer Lock :: Merklock now locks the keystore leaf counter (ADR-050) but still writes `chain.json` through an unlocked read-modify-write. Two `send`, `mine` or `sync` processes can load the same tip, each mutate its own copy, and let the last writer erase the other's mempool entry or block. The chain file is the other half of the lost-update problem; the leaf is safe, but the transfer that burned it can disappear. The fix reuses `storage.file_lock` from ADR-050. Hold it across the load → \n1006 High/Med Peer Dial Address Policy :: `PeerLink.__enter__` calls `socket.create_connection((host, port))`; the name is resolved at connect time and no address class is checked. PEX and peer-book hosts are peer-supplied and only length/type-checked, so a hostile peer can point a node at loopback, RFC1918, link-local or multicast services, or use DNS rebinding to pass a future hostname check. A public chain should not dial private space because a peer asked it to. Resolve the host once, validate every numeric res\n1007 Med-High/Low Ordered Shutdown with Final Persist :: `PeerService.stop` closes the accept socket and relay, then joins the accept thread for two seconds, but connection handlers are daemon threads and `_serve_bounded` never checks a stopping flag. A block accepted just before shutdown can be dropped before `persist` runs; `_run_serve` returns immediately after `stop()` with no final save. On a small testnet that is a lost block, not a theoretical race. Add a `threading.Event` stopping flag, check it at the top \n1008 Med-High/Med Transaction Version Fail-Closed :: `Transaction.body()` always emits `TRANSACTION_VERSION`, but `Transaction.from_dict` never reads the field and silently drops unknown keys. A document labelled version 2 is reinterpreted as version 1, and an unauthenticated extra field disappears without a finding. Today only v1 exists, so the risk is a future upgrade hazard rather than a live exploit; the same shape appears in block and chain loaders. Make admission fail closed: require the exact supported versi\n1009 Med/Low Integer Digit Cap in Strict JSON :: `hardening.strict_loads` rejects floats, NaN, duplicate keys and deep nesting, but passes integers straight to `json.loads`. Python 3.11 added a 4,300-digit conversion limit; 3.10.0-3.10.6, which Merklock still supports, did not. A 1 MiB frame of digits can therefore burn CPU and memory before the parser rejects it, inside the 1 MiB frame bound. A `parse_int` hook with a small digit cap closes the slow path without changing any valid protocol value.\n1010 Med-High/Low Canonical Merkle Proof Length :: `hashing.merkle_verify` walks siblings until the proof ends and accepts trailing empty elements: a valid proof can be padded with arbitrary empty siblings and still verify. That is not a forgery, but it makes the encoding malleable, so two peers can hold different bytes for the same proof and the wire format has no canonical form. A strict length check derived from the leaf count and index removes the ambiguity and bounds the work.\n1011 Med/Low Tri-State CLI Verification :: `verify-account --confirmations 6` with no local chain prints `Confirmations: 0` and exits 0. A requested check that could not run reads as a pass, which is the wrong default for an operator or a script. The verifier should say `UNAVAILABLE`, explain that no chain or light state is present, and exit non-zero when a positive confirmation count was requested.\n1012 Med/Med Stable Diagnostic Codes for Doctor and Verify :: `doctor --json` emits ad-hoc `{name, ok, detail}` objects and `verify` prints prose. CI can assert only the check name, not the specific failure, and rewording a message breaks tooling. A small registry of stable codes (`storage.backup_missing`, `keystore.rolled_back`, `chain.bad_state_root`) lets every failure carry a code, a location and a fix while keeping the human prose.\n1013 Med/Low Claims Bound to Live Test Guards :: SECURITY.md, WHITEPAPER.md and README make load-bearing claims: fixed supply, a leaf is never reused, no governance, no dependencies. A test that parses each claim's guard file and asserts the named class/method exists turns document rot into a suite failure without changing consensus.\n1014 Med/Med Generated Capability Manifest :: Consensus and wire limits are scattered across `ChainParams`, `netwire` and `hardening`, and there is no single discovery surface for an operator or an agent. A `capabilities --json` command can publish the chain id/genesis/supply/suite activation, protocol/frame/sync caps, CLI commands and exit codes, assembled from the same constants the code uses. Descriptive only; no governance.\n632 High/Med Account-key compact filters for light clients :: Commit a per-block probabilistic filter over touched account identifiers and state keys, anchor its header chain to block headers, and let a light client download headers plus filters and request only matching blocks and proofs. Merklock's light client currently has no private relevance test; filters would reduce what a server learns about a wallet and fit the existing state-root proof path.\n633 Med/Med Committed-header sampling for bounded-memory sync :: Store random-offset commitments to header hashes while catching up, then redownload and fully validate the header range once a work threshold is reached. A Python light client has limited memory; this bounds initial header state without weakening final validation.\n634 Med-High/Med Source-diverse bucketed peer manager :: Maintain new and tried peer buckets keyed by hashed network group, cap slots per bucket, evict low-quality entries and occasionally test unreachable peers to refresh the tried set. Merklock already caps discovered peers per range but has no persistent new/tried structure; this raises the cost of address poisoning and single-range eclipses.\n635 Med/Low Damped per-block difficulty controller :: Replace interval retargeting with a per-block controller that damps each deviation by a configurable factor and clamps the adjustment, so a low-hashrate chain recovers from spikes without oscillating. Merklock targets a small testnet hash market; a damped controller can stabilize block time without touching emission or supply.\n636 High/Med Recovery phrase with on-chain leaf watermark :: Encode the seed, tree height and suite with a checksum; on restore rebuild the leaves, scan the chain from a birthday height for the address's transactions, read the public nonce (leaf index), and set next_leaf to the maximum seen plus one. Refuse to sign until the scan reaches the tip. A stale keystore counter is the one recovery bug that causes catastrophic Winternitz reuse; Merklock already exposes the leaf index in every transfer.\n637 Med/Med Template-first miner handshake :: A miner requests a block template from a full node, validates prev-hash, height and target, hashes it, sends shares to the job issuer and block solutions to the node, then refreshes. Merklock's current external backend is one-shot; a template-first session lets a backend prove work against the real tip without trusting a pool.\n638 Low/Low Deterministic benchmark transcript :: A fixed-seed hash batch produces an order-independent checksum and an expected-value table by algorithm, size and thread count; a verifier detects skipped work or fake hashrate using a keyed chain rather than XOR. Merklock has CPU/external benchmark commands but no verifiable transcript for reported hashrate.\n639 Med-Low/Low Operator API safety envelope :: Loopback-first binding, optional bearer token, read-only mode blocking non-GET, JSON content-type enforcement, constant-time token comparison and a per-token request budget. Any future node dashboard or admin API should be safe by default; Merklock currently has no HTTP surface.\n640 Med/Low Feature bits with dependency closure :: Advertise an optional/required capability vector where setting a bit transitively requires its dependencies; unknown required bits reject the connection, and compiled-in bits cannot be unset. Merklock is adding header long-poll, snapshots and verifier seams; negotiated capabilities let old peers interoperate without silent fallback.\n641 Med/Low Bounded priority egress with RTT liveness :: Two outbound classes (control before bulk), caps on message count and retained bytes, fixed per-message overhead, disconnect on overflow, and periodic ping with timeout and measured RTT. Merklock's relay queue is bounded but has no priority classes or RTT-based stale-peer detection.\n642 Med/Low Encrypted versioned backup with atomic swap :: Seal a checkpoint bundle in a versioned envelope with a checksum, write it to a temporary file, flush and rename over the old file, retaining one archive generation; restore with a seed-derived key and replay. Merklock's light state and peer book need portable encrypted backups without exposing keys.\n643 Med/Med Out-of-process verifier with pinned key registry :: Invoke a configured verifier binary with a versioned envelope (scheme, curve, transcript, circuit id, verifying-key hash, proof, public inputs); enforce timeout and output caps; reject unknown schemes or key-hash mismatches, and pin keys by hash in genesis/state. A future zk or ML-DSA verifier can be optional without linking heavy C++/CUDA into consensus; pinning prevents silent circuit substitution.\n644 High/Med Witnessed header sync with divergence evidence :: Pin a checkpoint; fetch headers from a primary and several witnesses; on mismatch, walk back to the bifurcation, verify both branches, persist conflicting headers plus peer identities as an evidence bundle, and drop the divergent peer. Merklock's light client currently trusts one peer's page; witness comparison and a portable evidence bundle raise the cost of split-view attacks.\n645 Med/Low Evidence inbox with expiry and rebroadcast :: Verify evidence on receipt, deduplicate by digest, index by height and wall time, expire after configurable limits, cap message size and rebroadcast pending items on an interval. Merklock has no place to retain proof of one-time-signature reuse or conflicting headers before it is forgotten.\n646 High/Med Anchored snapshot manifest :: Define a manifest with height, format version, chunk count, the trusted header's state root and one hash per chunk; fetch chunks from several peers, verify immediately, keep partial progress, resume after disconnect, and accept only when the recomputed state root matches. Merklock's sync replays from genesis and has no snapshot bootstrap; an anchored manifest would speed a new node without trusting one peer.\n647 Med/Med Expiring scoped session grants :: The account root key signs a grant naming a delegate key, allowed transaction kinds, a spend cap and an expiry height/time; the grant lives in state with a time-ordered expiry index and can be revoked by the root. Winternitz keys are single-use; hot or light clients should delegate narrow authority instead of reusing the root key.\n648 Med/Low Quality-weighted sync scheduler :: Order block/header sources by per-peer success/failure history, cap in-flight requests per peer, demote peers that stall or serve invalid data, and retry from the next-best source while fully validating every block. Merklock's peer book records counters but sync selection does not yet use them for source ordering.\n649 Med-Low/Low Cover-set account proof requests :: When a light client requests state proofs, ask for a shuffled batch of k addresses: the real one plus deterministic decoys derived from a rotating local seed and the chain tip, then discard the decoys. Merklock's address-keyed proof requests leak wallet links; this is k-anonymity at a bandwidth cost, not cryptographic privacy.\n650 Med-Low/Low Clock-skew-aware signed operator envelope :: HMAC over sorted params with timestamp plus a short validity window, periodic server-time calibration and a nonce/replay cache for privileged operator commands. A future Merklock admin/RPC surface needs replay-resistant signed commands and explicit clock handling.\n651 Med/Low Bounded reconnect with backpressure :: Capped attempts, full-jitter exponential backoff, bounded queue, explicit terminal error and a give-up state for peer and light-client connections. Merklock's long-poll/watch loop reconnects every round without a shared backoff policy; a bounded reconnect helper would avoid tight loops.\n652 Med/High Horizon-pruned archival profile :: An opt-in pruned node keeps the last H blocks plus all headers and state roots; older bodies and Winternitz signatures become a per-height digest, and new nodes bootstrap from a pinned state-root checkpoint while archival nodes keep everything. Winternitz signatures and authentication paths dominate storage; the state root already commits to balances. This is a trust-boundary profile, never default.\n653 High/Med Mempool-delta compact block relay :: Announce a header plus per-peer-salted short transaction IDs; peers reconstruct from the mempool, request only missing bodies, and fall back to a full block after one timeout. Multi-kilobyte Winternitz signatures make block propagation the sync bottleneck.\n654 High/Low Transaction Block-to-Live Expiry :: A transaction may carry a block-to-live (BTL) height. When the field is non-zero, `tx_val()` rejects it unless `bnum <= btl <= bnum + 0x100`: the transaction is not valid before the current height and cannot live more than 256 blocks into the future. A zero field means no deadline, which keeps old transactions valid. Merklock has no transaction expiry. A signed transfer can sit in a mempool indefinitely, be replayed months later, or remain a pending conflict for a on\n655 High/Med Gap-Free, Source-Ordered Block Inclusion :: Mochimo enforces a canonical block order: every transaction's source address must be strictly greater than the previous one, and no source may appear twice in the same block. This is not cosmetic. With one-time signatures, two transactions from the same account can reveal overlapping chain material; a canonical order plus deduplication makes the block's state transition deterministic and eliminates a class of intra-block double-spend ambiguity before signatur\n656 Med-High/Med Orphaned-Block Archive with Split Metadata :: When Mochimo accepts a block at a height that already has a different block, it does not delete the old one. It renames the old block to a `.sp` split file, preserving the losing candidate alongside the winner. The split file is a local forensic record: it shows what the node saw, which branch lost, and when. Merklock's `Chain.adopt()` replaces `self.blocks` wholesale with the heavier candidate and keeps only the surviving mempool. The orphaned branch d\n657 Med/Low Capability-Bit Handshake Negotiation :: Mochimo carries an 8-bit capability field in the transaction/version header. A node advertises what it can do (push candidate blocks, opt in to network activity, logging, miner-fee signalling, sanctuary protocol), and peers can avoid asking for operations the other side does not support. Merklock's handshake authenticates the peer and exchanges height/work, but it has no feature negotiation. Today every peer is assumed to speak the same protocol. As soon as Merklo\n659 High/Med Bounded OTS Usage Bitmap with Overflow Counter :: QRL tracks one-time-signature usage in two tiers. For indices below `max_ots_tracking_index` (8,192), it stores a bit per leaf and can answer \"was this exact leaf used?\" For indices at or above the threshold, it stores a single high-water `ots_counter`; any index at or below the counter is considered used. `get_unused_ots_index()` first scans the bitmap for a zero bit, then falls back to `counter + 1`; it returns `None` when the tree is exhausted. Merkl\n660 High/Med Burn-on-First-Sight Leaf Tombstones Across Reorgs :: QRL's OTS machinery has both a set and an unset path. On rollback, `unset_ots_key()` clears the bit for a tracked index, or recomputes `ots_counter` from the remaining transaction hashes. The intent is to let a reorged transaction be re-included. The security problem is that the signature was already public: if the same leaf is later used for a different message, the WOTS/XMSS chain material overlaps and the key can be forged. Unsetting a leaf on reo\n661 High/Med Bounded Reorg Limit with Published Finality Depth :: QRL maintains a moving re-org limit: when the chain reaches height `h`, the limit becomes `h - reorg_limit` (if that is greater than the stored value). A block at or below the limit is ignored; a fork whose fork point is below the limit is refused. The limit is persisted in the state DB, so a restart does not reopen deep history. Merklock's `Chain.adopt()` accepts any heavier, compatible chain, as long as genesis and parameters match. There is no fin\n662 High/High Suite Migration Transaction Before Retirement :: QRL's lattice transaction is a migration primitive. The owner of an existing XMSS address signs a transaction that carries new-suite public keys (Kyber for KEM, Dilithium for signatures, ECDSA as a compatibility key). The transaction pays a fee, is validated under a hard-fork gate, and stores the new key material against the existing address. It does not move the balance; it binds a future key to the account. A later spend can use the new scheme. Merklo\n663 High/High Weighted Multisig with Expiring Spend Proposals :: QRL's multisig is a policy account, not a script. `MultiSigCreate` registers a set of signatory addresses with weights and a threshold; the multisig address is derived from the creation transaction hash. A `MultiSigSpend` proposal commits to recipients, amounts, and an expiry height. Signatories vote with `MultiSigVote`; when the accumulated weight reaches the threshold, the spend executes. Votes can be withdrawn before execution, and an expired propo\n664 High/High BDS Traversal State in the Keystore :: BDS (Buchmann–Dahmen–Szydlo) keeps the authentication path for the next leaf and updates it incrementally. The state contains the current `auth` path, a `keep` cache, a set of `treehash` instances that compute future nodes, a stack, a `retain` area for the lower tree levels, and `next_leaf`. On each signature, `xmss_core_sign()` copies the already-computed `auth` path, signs, then calls `bds_round()` and `bds_treehash_update()` to prepare the next path. The expen\n665 High/High Hypertree Capacity for Long-Lived Addresses :: XMSS^MT splits a total height `full_height` into `d` layers of `tree_height = full_height / d`. A signature carries one WOTS+ signature and one authentication path per layer, and the signer advances the leaf index across the layers. Key generation and signing scale with the subtree height, not the full height, so a total height of 20 or 40 is practical. Merklock's single tree has `MAX_HEIGHT = 16`, i.e. 65,536 signatures per address, and even that is impr\n666 Med-High/Med Parameter-Set OID in Keys and Witnesses :: XMSS keys and signatures start with a 32-bit OID. The OID selects the hash function, `n`, Winternitz `w`, tree height, number of layers, and BDS trade-off. `xmss_str_to_oid(\"XMSS-SHA2_10_256\")` returns a stable identifier; `xmss_parse_oid()` populates a `xmss_params` struct that drives every size and validation decision. The implementation never guesses a parameter from context. Merklock's suite prefix identifies the algorithm family (`mkl1`), and the tran\n667 Med-High/Med Versioned Keystore State Blob with Forward Migration :: XMSS does not promise a fixed secret-key layout. The traversal choice changes the state, and the serialized key includes the BDS state. The reference implementation exposes `xmss_xmssmt_core_sk_bytes()` so callers allocate the right size, and its README explicitly warns that the BDS state format may change without changing the public API or output. Merklock's keystore is version 1 with a fixed set of fields: `version`, `kind`, `address`, `suit\n668 Med/Low Hard-Stop Exhaustion and Secret Zeroization :: XMSS treats exhaustion as a hard stop. When the last leaf is reached, `xmss_core_sign()` zeroizes the secret key in memory and returns an error. The test signs exactly `2^h` times, then expects every further attempt to fail. The comment is explicit: production code must make the deletion durable on disk, and the last index may be reserved to avoid an ambiguous \"finished\" state. Merklock's `Wallet.reserve_leaf()` already refuses at capacity with `WalletError\n669 Med-High/Med Generation-fenced solver leases :: Bind every external mining assignment to a lease tuple of tip hash, monotonically increasing generation, salt range and deadline. A new tip increments the generation, drops staged and in-flight work, clears salts and broadcasts a cancel; results carry the generation and are discarded before validation if stale. Give each spawned solver a fresh session token so a restarted process cannot inherit an old lease. Merklock's external backend is one-shot today; fencing m\n670 Med/Med Inclusion-state submission verifier :: After a block or share submission is included, read the canonical slot for that height (the accepted block producer or equivalent state entry) instead of trusting the transaction status. Classify the outcome as accepted, lost race (another producer won the same height) or rejected (no entry written); treat a lost race as stale-round, not invalid work, and do not use a head-state dry run to explain it. A miner integration that reports every included-but-not-winning \n671 Med/Low Tip-readiness gate for miner startup :: Before funding or starting an external miner, poll the node's sync state: accept the first clear answer if no sync was ever observed, require two consecutive clear polls after a sync, keep waiting without a fixed ceiling while block progress is reported, and give up after a bounded unreachable grace. Warn when the node expects peers but has none. Starting a miner against a node that is still importing history wastes work on a stale tip and produces avoidable stale\n672 High/Med Secure-Store Callback Before Signature Release :: liboqs does not write a stateful secret key itself. The application supplies `secure_store_sk`, which receives the serialized key bytes and a context, and the stateful signer calls it before the signature is considered complete. The callback is the storage contract: a deployment can use a file, an HSM, a secure element or an encrypted database without the algorithm knowing. Merklock's `Wallet.save()` is already atomic (temp, fsync, `.bak`, replace), and\n673 Med-High/Low Lock/Unlock/Mutex Triple for Stateful Signing :: A stateful one-time signature is only safe if two signers cannot reserve the same leaf. liboqs makes the application provide all three of a lock function, an unlock function and a mutex. If only part of the triple is set, the lock operation fails rather than silently serializing nothing. Merklock's `Wallet` is a single-process object. The CLI is single-writer, but a daemon, a test harness or two concurrent CLI processes can load the same keystore and\n674 Med-High/Low Suite Support Tier and Upstream Evidence :: liboqs separates three facts that are easy to conflate: the algorithm identifier is known, the algorithm is compiled in, and the project makes a support commitment to it. `ALGORITHMS.md` records upstream maintenance, OQS tier, NIST level, constant-time status, formal verification and optimization targets. `OQS_SIG_alg_is_enabled()` answers the runtime availability question separately from the name list. Merklock's `suites.py` has `suite_id`, `prefix`, `al\n675 High/Med KAT/ACVP Vector Gate for Signature Primitives :: liboqs pins primitive outputs to reviewed known-answer vectors and to NIST ACVP vectors. A KAT failure means the implementation changed its output, not that a test was inconvenient. The stateful KAT is especially relevant: it reads a pre-generated key because key generation can take hours. Merklock has a deterministic WOTS/Merkle construction. A refactor of the hash domain, the Winternitz chain order or the auth-path ordering can change a signature while\n676 Med-High/Low Malformed-Signature Bitflip Corpus :: liboqs does not only test a valid signature. It flips message and signature bits and asserts that verification fails, and it surrounds buffers with magic canaries so an out-of-bounds read or write is visible. The Wycheproof harness adds an external corpus of malformed inputs. Merklock's `verify_signature()` is the consensus boundary. A malformed signature must return false with a typed error, never raise an unexpected exception, read past the buffer or accept a\n677 Med-High/Med Namespace-Scoped State Proof with Completeness :: NMT orders leaves by namespace and proves a complete namespace range: the verifier supplies the leaves, gets their namespaced leaf hashes, and checks that no leaf of that namespace exists outside `[proof.start, proof.end)`. `VerifyNamespace` is therefore stronger than a single inclusion proof. Merklock's account state is sorted by address and committed as a single Merkle root. A light client can prove one account, but cannot prove \"all accounts for \n678 Med-High/Med Absence Proof for Non-Membership :: NMT distinguishes an empty proof, an absence proof with a boundary leaf hash, and a normal inclusion proof. An absence proof shows that the namespace falls inside the covered range but no leaf with that namespace exists; an empty proof covers a namespace outside the root's range. Merklock's `verify_account` can return a zero-valued account record for an unknown address, but that is an assertion by the prover, not a proof of absence. A light client cannot distingu\n679 High/Low Inclusion vs Completeness as Separate Verify Verbs :: NMT makes the difference explicit in the API: `VerifyInclusion` proves that the supplied leaves are in the tree, but it does not prove that no other leaves of the namespace exist. `VerifyNamespace` adds the completeness check. A caller cannot accidentally get \"complete\" from the weaker verb. Merklock's `verify_account` and `verify_inclusion` already return a height/confirmation count, but the names do not say whether the result is a single fact or a\n680 Med-High/Low Proof Envelope Carries Tree Options :: An NMT proof is only meaningful under the tree options used to build it: namespace ID size, the `IgnoreMaxNamespace` policy and the hash function. NMT carries `isMaxNamespaceIDIgnored` in the proof and derives the namespace size from the queried ID; a mismatch with the verifier's tree fails. This is the difference between \"the proof is valid\" and \"the proof is valid for the tree I think I am checking\". Merklock proof documents carry `version`, `chain_id`, `hei\n681 High/Low Proof Structure Validation Before Slicing :: NMT validates a proof's shape before it slices or hashes: the range must be `start < end`, every node must have the hasher's expected length, the root format must be checked before reading its namespace bounds, and a short root must return false rather than panic. Merklock's `verify_proof` and `verify_account_proof` already parse attacker-controlled JSON. They should never raise a raw `IndexError`, `ValueError` or `TypeError` on malformed input. A proof that\n699 Med/Low Batched leaf reservation watermark :: On top of the secure-store contract, persist a reservation watermark for a batch of leaves before signing any of them; sign only below the watermark, and on startup resume at the watermark, burning unused leaves. Choose the batch size to amortize durable writes, and never reserve past tree capacity or across a suite migration. Per-signature fsync is expensive; batching reduces writes without reopening the crash-reuse window, provided the watermark is committed befor\n700 High/Med Peer Watermarks with Grace Period and Protected Peers :: `BasicConnMgr` keeps connections between a low and a high watermark. When the count exceeds the high watermark, it trims down to the low watermark, but it skips new peers still inside a grace period and skips peers with a protected tag. A silence period prevents trim thrash, and a protected peer is only closed when there is no unprotected candidate left. Merklock's `PeerBook` has a hard `MAX_PEERS = 256`; once full, `add()` raises. That rejects a\n701 High/Med Scoped Resource Budgets per Peer and Connection :: The resource manager accounts for resources at nested scopes: a stream belongs to a connection, which belongs to a peer; usage rolls up. Limits can be set at the system, peer, connection, stream, protocol or service level, and an autoscaler derives concrete values from system memory. A limit failure returns a typed error; the caller decides whether to degrade or close. Merklock has global wire limits (`MAX_FRAME_BYTES`, `MAX_MESSAGES_PER_CONNECTION`, `","numLines":62,"startLine":1,"totalLines":123}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9b7e2598-f454-4f79-b7c3-fecae6146a6b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\merklock","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main"}
