{"parentUuid":"ba780fe8-819b-4279-aa2e-a81d355d70f9","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4ce50c82-a8ed-4889-ac0c-4fb42e7bef08","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016hk5drdGm2sx2ZwhKvbmCc","type":"tool_result","content":"63\t702 Med-High/Low Allowlist Reserved Capacity for Eclipse Resistance :: The resource manager allowlist is a reserved lane: allowlisted IP networks (optionally restricted to a specific peer ID) keep working when the normal system limits are exhausted. It is an eclipse-resistance tool: a trusted bootstrap or a known operator address is not pushed out by a flood of ordinary peers. Merklock's `PeerBook` has diversity by address group, but no trusted lane. If the peer book or a resource budget is full, a bootstrap peer o\n64\t703 Med-High/Low Connection Gater Before Dial and Accept :: A connection gater is called before a dial, before an accept and after security/upgrade. It can block a peer ID, a multiaddress or an address range, and it can allow the rest. The gate is cheaper than completing a handshake and then closing, and it centralises policy that would otherwise be scattered across dial and accept paths. Merklock's `PeerLink` connects or accepts directly. There is no central place to refuse a self-connection, a loopback address fr\n65\t704 Med-High/Low Peer Address TTL with Stale-Record Decay :: The peer store attaches a TTL to every address. Expired addresses are removed by a GC pass; an address held by a live connection gets a longer TTL so a session is not torn down. A newer signed peer record can extend the TTL, and an older record cannot. Merklock's `PeerRecord.last_seen` is stored and used for ordering, but it never expires. A peer that moved or went offline stays in the book forever, occupies a slot and can be retried indefinitely. The har\n66\t716 High/Med Versioned State Root with Historical Proof :: IAVL saves each state version with a root hash and keeps the version's root node. `GetImmutable(version)` returns a read-only tree at that version, and a proof can be generated and verified against that version's root. The version is part of the node key, so the same logical key at two versions is two nodes. Merklock stores only the current state root in the latest header. A light client can prove an account against the tip, but not against a past height. A\n67\t717 Med-High/Med Version Pruning with a Retain Window :: IAVL can delete versions below a retain window (`DeleteVersionsTo`) or above a rollback point (`DeleteVersionsFrom`). The tree remains usable; only the pruned historical proofs disappear. This is how a versioned store bounds disk growth without giving up recent history. Merklock currently has no historical state to prune because it keeps only the tip. Once `IDEA-716` adds historical roots, an unbounded cache would grow forever. A retain window gives a clear \"\n68\t718 High/Med Rollback to a Height with Explicit Overwrite :: `LoadVersionForOverwriting` loads a historical version and deletes every newer version. It is an explicit, auditable rollback: the caller names the target height, the tree loads that root, and the future versions are removed. It is not a silent \"load and hope\"; the overwrite is in the name. Merklock replays the chain from genesis on reorg. A deep reorg rebuilds state, but there is no state-level rollback command that says \"discard everything above height \n69\t719 Med-High/Med Deterministic State Snapshot Export and Import :: IAVL exports nodes in a deterministic depth-first post-order and imports them in the same order into an empty tree. The export carries a version; the import version must be at least the highest exported node version. The result is an identical tree, not a re-derived approximation. Merklock has a JSON state file but no portable, deterministic state snapshot with a version and digest. A node migration or a repair copies the whole directory, which is o\n70\t720 Med-High/Low Typed Proof-Op Registry with a Proof Spec :: ICS23 wraps a proof in a typed op: the op has a `type` (for example `ics23:iavl`), a key and opaque data, and a verifier registry maps the type to a proof specification. A light client can support several proof formats without guessing the shape from the data. Merklock proof documents use a `version` and a fixed JSON shape. Adding a namespace, absence or a future state tree means changing the shape; an old client may misinterpret a new document. A typed \n71\t721 High/Med Nonce-chain replacement with absolute-fee compensation :: For account-model transactions, allow a replacement only when it uses the same account nonce, pays at least the absolute fee of the transaction it replaces plus every nonce-dependent successor it evicts, and pays an additional fee at the incremental relay rate for its own size. Bound the replacement to a fixed number of account queues, evict the whole nonce chain, and require that the replacement strictly improve the mempool's fee-versus-size di\n72\t722 Med-High/Med Nonce-chain eviction with rolling minimum fee :: When the mempool exceeds its byte budget or an entry expires, evict the lowest-feerate contiguous nonce chain as a unit, including all successors, rather than individual transactions. Raise a rolling minimum fee to the evicted chain's feerate plus the incremental relay rate, then decay it with a half-life that shortens as the pool empties; expire whole tails so no successor is left orphaned. Evicting a middle transaction strands every higher nonce an\n73\t723 Med-High/Low Codec Registry with Status Lifecycle and Reserved Ranges :: multicodec assigns a numeric code and a lifecycle status. A `draft` code may be reassigned if it does not gain adoption; a `permanent` code may not. The first 127 one-byte codes are reserved for widely used formats, and the private-use range is forbidden in the public table. Merklock's suite IDs are strings (`wots16-merkle-sha256-v1`) with a three-character prefix. There is no numeric code, no reserved range and no promotion rule. A future\n74\t724 Med/Low Canonical Unsigned-Varint Prefix Decoder :: multicodec does not store a fixed-width integer. The code is an unsigned varint, so small codes are one byte and larger codes grow. A decoder must reject an overlong encoding, a truncated varint and a value outside the registry; otherwise two byte strings decode to the same code. Merklock uses text prefixes, so it avoids this today. A future binary transaction or peer envelope would need a compact code. A canonical varint decoder prevents ambiguous encodings a\n75\t725 Med-High/Low Registry Invariant Validator as a Gate :: The multicodec validator treats the registry as data and checks it mechanically: codes ascend, names and codes are unique, names match a regex, codes are hex byte sequences, the private-use range is empty. A bad row fails the build, not a reviewer's memory. Merklock's `SUITES` tuple is code. A new suite is a Python edit; a duplicate prefix, a skipped code or a reserved-range collision is only caught by a test if someone writes that test. A registry validato\n76\t726 Med/Low Generated Constants and Name Normalization :: multicodec names are designed to become constants in many languages: lowercase, alphanumeric, hyphen-separated, underscore only as a secondary separator. The table is the source; each language generates its constants from it instead of hand-copying. Merklock's suite names are long and inconsistent (`wots16-merkle-sha256-v1`, `ml-dsa-65-sha256-v1`, `slh-dsa-sha2-128s-v1`). The CLI, docs and code repeat them; a rename can leave a stale string. A canonical norm\n77\t727 Med-High/Med Self-Describing Identifier Envelope :: The multiformats design puts the identifier inline with the value: the code travels with the bytes, not in an out-of-band function argument. That makes a value self-describing and lets a reader reject an unknown format without a registry lookup by context. Merklock's text addresses carry `mkl1` inline, but its transaction, block and peer envelopes are JSON with a version field and no typed code. A future binary envelope needs the same property: the suite/codec\n78\t743 High/Med Domain-separated Winternitz account tree :: Derive each account's Winternitz keypair from the master seed with HMAC-SHA256 over a structured, fully hardened path (suite, account_index, tree_index), giving each account a disjoint leaf space. Bind the suite, chain ID and account index into every signature digest, and derive the account's public identifier from the same path so a wallet can regenerate accounts without storing an address list. A single seed should recover every account, but Merklock's curr\n79\t744 Med-High/Low Gap-limit account discovery with lookahead :: On restore, derive account 0, scan its external address/leaf range up to a gap limit of N unused leaves, then advance to account 1 only if account 0 has history; stop at the first empty account and warn when the user tries to exceed the gap limit. Keep a lookahead window for the first unused account so new accounts are created only after the previous one is used. Without a gap limit, a restored wallet cannot know when to stop scanning; without the accou\n80\t745 Med-High/Med SSZ-Style Hash Tree Root with a Stable Type Schema :: SSZ gives every container a type: field order, fixed or variable length, and a maximum. The hash tree root is a binary Merkleization of that typed object, not a hash of a JSON string. Two serializers that produce different whitespace or key order still yield the same root. Merklock commits to `transaction.canonical_json(account_record(...))`. Canonical JSON is a good start, but the state root is coupled to the JSON schema: adding a field, changi\n81\t746 Med-High/Med Generalized-Index Field Proof for Account Records :: A generalized index names a position in a Merkleized container: the binary path from the root. A proof reveals one field and its branch, not the whole record. The verifier computes the root from the leaf and branch and compares it with the committed root. Merklock's account proof returns the full account record plus a sibling path. A light client that only needs a balance still receives address, nonce, spent and lock data. A field proof reduces t\n82\t747 High/Med Light-Client Bootstrap from a Trusted Checkpoint :: An Ethereum light client does not replay from genesis. It is configured with a trusted block root, fetches a bootstrap containing the header and the current sync committee (with a Merkle proof), and then follows updates. The trust anchor is explicit and small. Merklock's `LightChain` validates a sequence of headers from a known starting point. A new light client must obtain that starting point out of band, and there is no bootstrap object that binds t\n83\t748 Med-High/Low Weak-Subjectivity Checkpoint with Explicit Expiry :: A weak-subjectivity checkpoint is a recent finalized root that a new node trusts because it has no history to verify. Ethereum documents the period during which that trust is reasonable and the social process for obtaining it. Merklock's light client has no stated trust policy: it accepts a starting header and trusts the chain from there. A checkpoint object with a height, hash, timestamp and expiry makes the trust decision explicit and prevents \n84\t749 Med/Med Fixed/Variable SSZ-Style Transaction Encoding :: SSZ serializes a container as fixed-size fields followed by offsets to variable-size fields, with the variable data appended in field order. The encoding is deterministic and self-delimiting; a decoder can validate lengths before reading. Merklock's transaction wire format is canonical JSON. It is human-readable but larger, and a decoder must parse the whole document before checking field types. A binary SSZ-style form gives a compact canonical encoding w\n85\t750 High/High Hybrid PQ transport profile with a reviewed combiner :: Add an optional transport profile that negotiates a named hybrid group (X25519 + ML-KEM-768), derives both key shares from a transport-only seed, combines the two shared secrets with a labeled hash over both ciphertexts and the handshake transcript, and records the negotiated group in peer connection evidence. Reject ad-hoc concatenation and silent classical fallback; keep classical TLS as an explicit opt-in. Merklock is post-quantum at the signa\n86\t781 High/High Executable state-transition oracle with differential fixtures :: Write a small, independent Python model of Merklock's block and transaction application, and generate deterministic state and blockchain fixtures from it. Run each fixture against the production node and the model, compare post-state root, balances, total supply, nonce and leaf counters, and rejection outcome, then store every divergence as a permanent regression. Merklock's consensus code has no independent oracle; a bug in state applic\n87\t782 Med-High/Med Suite-version validity matrix with transition fixtures :: Tag every consensus test with the suite versions and activation heights it applies to, and generate three fixtures at each boundary: before, at, and after. Include a block that is valid under the old suite and invalid under the new one, and assert the exact rejection class at the boundary. Merklock already plans suite migration (mkl1, mkl2, mkl3) but has no systematic way to prove old and new rules coexist. Boundary fixtures catch off-by-one\n88\t783 Med-High/Low Typed chain-rejection corpus :: Define typed rejection classes for Merklock transactions and blocks (bad nonce, reused leaf, invalid signature, insufficient fee, expired block-to-live, stale parent, invalid state root, supply violation) and attach the expected class to every negative fixture. The runner must verify both rejection and the class, so a node cannot pass by rejecting for an unrelated reason. A negative test that only checks that a block was rejected can hide a missing validation behind \n89\t784 High/Med Incremental Account Witness with Anchor Pinning :: An Orchard note is a leaf in a commitment tree. A spend carries an anchor and a Merkle path; the verifier recomputes the root from the leaf and path and compares it with the anchor. The wallet does not send the whole tree, and it does not rebuild the tree for every proof: an incremental witness is updated as leaves are appended. The anchor pins the witness to a root, so a stale path is rejected rather than silently accepted. Merklock's `proofs.build_ac\n90\t785 High/Med Role-Separated Transaction Package :: A PCZT is a serialized partially created transaction. The Creator fixes the transaction version, flags and anchor; the Constructor adds spends and outputs; the Updater installs witnesses and viewing keys; the IO Finalizer computes the sighash; the Signer adds signatures; the Prover adds proofs; the Verifier checks that the public fields are consistent; the Extractor refuses until every required field is present. Fields that are no longer needed can be redacted, and\n91\t786 High/High KEM-Backed Memo Envelope with Version-Pinned Decryption :: An Orchard output does not contain the recipient address in cleartext. The sender derives a shared secret with the recipient's incoming viewing key, encrypts the note plaintext under it, and publishes the ephemeral public key alongside the ciphertext. The wallet trial-decrypts outputs; a compact 52-byte prefix omits the 512-byte memo so a light client can scan cheaply. The plaintext begins with a version byte, and the domain policy accepts exa\n92\t787 High/Med Scoped View Keys with Outgoing Recovery :: An Orchard full viewing key is a capability set, not a single secret. `to_ivk(scope)` derives a key that detects and decrypts received notes; `to_ovk(scope)` derives a key that decrypts the sender's `out_ciphertext` copy of what was sent. The spend authorizing key is separate. A view-only wallet can therefore audit balances and memos without the ability to sign, and a sender can recover its payment history from the chain even after losing local state. Merklock\n93\t788 High/High Delegated Vesting Claim with Nullifier :: An Orchard note is committed to the tree; spending it reveals a nullifier derived from the note, the nullifier key and the commitment. The nullifier is unique, so a duplicate is a double-spend, and it is unlinkable to the commitment without the viewing key. This is the clean separation between \"the right exists\" (the commitment) and \"the right was exercised\" (the nullifier). Merklock's premine vesting is bound to one address. `state.locked[address] = (allocati\n94\t789 Med-High/Med Bounded-label metrics registry with duplicate-series rejection :: Add a small metrics registry with counters, gauges and histograms; validate metric and label names, reject duplicate timeseries at registration, enforce a per-metric label-name allowlist and a hard cardinality cap with an overflow bucket, and expose a Prometheus-compatible text endpoint on loopback. Keep peer IDs, addresses and other unbounded values out of labels. Merklock has no operator metrics; naive per-peer or per-address label\n95\t790 Med/Med Per-process mmap aggregation for node and miner subprocesses :: In multi-process mode, each node or miner subprocess writes counters and gauges to its own memory-mapped file; the parent exposes a collector that merges the files, adds a pid label only for live-process gauges, and removes files for dead pids. This preserves counters across worker restarts without an IPC hop. Merklock runs a node plus external miner and verifier subprocesses; a single in-process registry would lose their counters or requir\n96\t791 High/Med Detached Winner Signature Outside the Hash Preimage :: NADO separates **block validity** from **producer authentication**. The PoW/weighted draw decides the winner; the winner may attach a detached ML-DSA signature outside the block hash preimage. The signature cannot change the block hash or its weight, but it lets another node prove who produced the block and detect two conflicting signed blocks at the same height and parent. Merklock blocks are anonymous: the coinbase names a miner address, but ther\n97\t792 High/Med Portable Equivocation Evidence Record :: A signature alone is not enough; the useful artifact is a **portable proof of equivocation**: two signed blocks with the same height and parent but different hashes. Anyone can verify the proof without trusting the reporter. NADO uses it to slash a bond; a PoW chain without bonds can still use it to ban a peer, archive evidence, and warn light clients. Merklock's orphan archive (IDEA-656) can hold the two blocks, but there is no canonical evidence record. A `Equ\n98\t793 High/High Snapshot Bootstrap Bound to a Finalized Signed Checkpoint :: A new node can bootstrap from a snapshot instead of replaying every block, but only if the snapshot is bound to a finalized checkpoint: height, state root, and a signature the node already trusts. A snapshot that is internally consistent but does not match the checkpoint is rejected. Merklock replays the full chain on every startup (`_replay()`), which is O(n) and grows without bound. A signed snapshot would make startup O(1) plus the tail, \n99\t794 High/Med Two-Floor Finality: Soft Depth and Hard Floor :: NADO distinguishes a **latency-sensitive depth floor** (`tip - FINALITY_DEPTH`) from a **hard floor** that rollback never crosses. The soft floor feeds the execution layer, pruning and snapshots; the hard floor is backed by a stronger finality rule (FFG/backstop) and a violation is a hard error. Both are monotonic. Merklock currently has a single heaviest-chain rule; `IDEA-661` adds a reorg limit, but one limit has to serve both \"fast UX confirmations\" a\n100\t795 Med/High Zero-Install Browser Node for Wallet, Mine, Explore and Alias :: NADO's adoption thesis is that a node should be one URL: open it and you have a wallet, miner, explorer and alias manager. There is no extension, no sync, no account. The browser page talks to a local node and can generate a post-quantum wallet, mine, send/receive, register an alias and browse the chain. Merklock is a CLI/reference implementation. A local browser UI would lower the barrier to a demo: create a wallet, mine a block, send a \n101\t806 High/High TLA+ model of the signing-state machine :: Specify a small TLA+ state machine for Merklock's wallet: seed-derived leaf space, reserved watermark, signed and revealed leaves, chain tip with reorg, mempool, crash/restart and restore. Model hashes and signatures as uninterpreted constants, then check safety invariants (no leaf signed twice, watermark monotonic, burned leaves permanent, supply constant) with TLC over bounded leaf/block sets; use Apalache for symbolic checks when the state space grows. The\n102\t807 Med-High/Med Counterexample-to-fixture bridge :: When TLC finds an invariant violation, export the trace as a deterministic scenario (initial wallet and chain state, ordered events, expected failure) and add it to the Python test harness. CI runs both the model and the fixture, and a spec change that removes a counterexample must also update the regression. Formal counterexamples are only useful if they become permanent tests; otherwise the same bug can return after the model is refactored. This connects the mo\n103\t823 High/Low Account Derivation Index for Multiple Trees from One Seed :: A master seed derives a per-address chain seed with a 32-bit derivation index. Each index produces an independent tree; the wallet can rotate to a new address when a tree is exhausted without changing the mnemonic/seed. Merklock's `keys.leaf_seed(master_seed, index)` derives leaves within one tree, but there is no account index. When a wallet exhausts its 2^height leaves, the user must create a new wallet and back up a new seed. A derivation \n104\t824 High/Med Parameter Object with Derived WOTS Lengths :: The parameter object derives `ell_1 = ceil(8n/log2(w))`, `ell_2` from the checksum range, and `ell = ell_1 + ell_2`. Keygen, sign, verify and size calculations all read the same object. Changing `n` or `w` changes the signature size and the chain lengths consistently. Merklock's `winternitz.py` uses module constants (`WINDOW=16`, `DIGITS=64`, `CHECKSUM_DIGITS=3`, `CHAINS=67`). That is fine for one suite, but a compact or future suite needs a parameter objec\n105\t825 Med-High/Low Self-Describing Witness Envelope :: The witness is a single serialized object: WOTS signature + leaf index + auth path. `serialize(K)` uses the tree size to choose the index length; `total_size()` includes the control block. The verifier knows `K`/`params` from the address. Merklock's transaction carries `signature`, `auth_path`, and `nonce` as separate fields. That works for one suite, but a compact/legacy suite or a different height needs the verifier to infer parameters. A versioned witness enve\n106\t826 Med/Low Signature-Size and Verification-Cost Estimator :: The reference publishes formulas for witness size, keygen hash count, and verification hash count. The wallet/UI can compare `K` values and parameter sets before choosing a fee. Merklock has `Transaction.weight()` and a fee rate, but a wallet cannot estimate the signature size/verification cost before signing. An estimator would let the wallet choose a height, warn about a large tree, and compute a fee that reflects verification work. The estimator must \n107\t827 Med-High/High Compact `w=256` Parameter Set with a Security/Cost Gate :: WOTS-Tree uses `n=16` (128-bit truncated chain hash), `w=256`, and a full 256-bit Merkle tree hash. The result is a 675-byte witness at `K=1024`, much smaller than a hypertree. The design deliberately places collision resistance on the tree and only second-preimage resistance on the chains. Merklock's current `w=16, n=32` signature is 2,144 bytes. A **compact `w=256, n=32`** variant would be roughly 34 × 32 = 1,088 bytes (plus auth path), \n108\t833 High/Med Effect Hash over Effecting Data with a Pre-Signing Plan :: Penumbra separates what a transaction *does* from how it is *authorized*. The effect hash commits to the state changes (commitments, nullifiers, parameters, memo, detection data) and excludes signatures and proofs. The auth hash covers the effect hash plus the authorization. A `TransactionPlan` can compute the effect hash before any proof or signature exists, so a signer or a hardware device can review the effects and approve exactly that hash.\n109\t834 High/Med Typed Action Envelope with Stateless/Stateful Handler Phases :: A Penumbra transaction is an ordered list of typed actions. Each action is a distinct type with its own data and its own `GasCost`; the parser rejects an unknown variant. Validation is split into a stateless phase (signatures, counts, memo presence, non-empty) and a stateful phase (chain id, expiry, FMD parameters) before execution. A handler registry dispatches by action type, so a new action does not edit the transfer validator. Merklock\n110\t835 Med-High/Med Bloom-Filter Scan Clue for Delegated Detection :: FMD lets a sender attach a clue that a detection key can test. The test always accepts a real recipient and accepts a non-recipient with probability `2^-precision`. A view server can therefore scan for a wallet and return a superset of candidates; the wallet trial-decrypts only those and learns which are real. The server learns a candidate set, not the payment graph. Precision is a consensus parameter with a grace period, so an old clue remains vali\n111\t836 High/Med Multi-Dimensional Gas Meter for Block Validation :: Penumbra prices a transaction on several independent dimensions: block space, compact-block space, verification and execution. An action declares its cost, a block is bounded per dimension, and fees convert gas into tokens. A block full of expensive verification cannot pass just because its bytes fit. Merklock bounds a block by `max_block_transactions` and `max_block_bytes`. Those limits do not capture the cost of verifying a Winternitz signature vers\n112\t837 Med-High/Med Three-Watermark Wallet Sync with Quarantine :: A Penumbra view service reports three heights: the chain tip it knows, the height it has fully scanned, and the height it has partially scanned. A `catching_up` flag distinguishes a syncing service from a stalled one. A client can therefore tell whether a balance is complete or provisional, and resume from the right height after a restart. Merklock's `LightChain` stores validated headers and `Chain` stores state, but the wallet has no scan watermark. `\n113\t892 High/High Sectorized Winternitz tree for multi-device signing :: Split one height-h Merkle tree into 2^(h-s) sectors of height s, each generated from an independent seed and stored with its off-path nodes, while all sectors share the same address root. Assign each signing device a disjoint sector so devices never coordinate a leaf counter; a compromise is contained to one sector, and unused sectors can be activated without changing the address. Merklock's single next_leaf counter assumes one signer. Sectorizati\n114\t893 High/High Cold-root endorsement of hot signing subtrees :: Keep a long-lived cold root tree that signs only the roots of short-lived operational subtrees; hot devices hold a subtree and sign messages, while the root endorses each new subtree. Rotate subtrees before exhaustion or on suspicion, and publish the endorsement chain so verifiers can bind a hot key to the cold root. A hot wallet with a single tree exposes the whole address if the host is compromised. A two-tier hierarchy bounds the damage to one subtre\n115\t894 Med-High/Med Verifiable state handoff with source retirement :: Move a keystore and its leaf state to a new device through a two-step export/import: the source exports the encrypted seed and a signed state summary, the destination imports and proves possession of the same state, and the source irreversibly retires its copy. Record a transfer receipt with the old and new state watermarks; refuse to sign on either device until the handoff completes. Copying a keystore to a second device is the easiest way to reus\n116\t895 Med-High/Low Sign-then-verify gate before signature release :: After generating a Winternitz signature, recover the public key from the signature and verify it against the expected leaf and root before releasing the transaction. Abort and burn the leaf on any mismatch, and log a typed fault event. Apply the same check to external signing backends. Fault injection against the WOTS checksum can turn a single faulty signature into a forgery. A local self-check catches implementation faults and many injected faults\n117\t896 High/Med Log-backed leaf-use audit with consistency proofs :: Append a commitment to every revealed (address, leaf index, txid) to an append-only Merkle log; publish signed tree heads and serve inclusion and consistency proofs. Wallets and auditors can detect two signatures for the same leaf even if they do not see the whole chain, and a log that shows different views is detectable. RFC 10033 notes that verifier-side reuse detection is trivial if all signatures are available. Merklock's tombstones are local; a \n118\t897 Med/Low Hashrate-calibrated confirmation depth :: Derive a confirmation-depth recommendation from an explicit adversary hash-rate fraction and a target double-spend probability, using the Rosenfeld/Nakamoto model. Publish the assumptions and a table, expose the recommendation in proofs and the CLI, and never label it finality. Merklock's proofs accept min_confirmations but there is no quantitative policy. A calibrated table gives merchants and light clients a defensible default without claiming finality.\n119\t898 Med-High/Low Anchor connections across restarts :: Persist two anchor peers across restarts, keep them out of the normal eviction path, rotate feelers through bucket-diverse candidates, and never let a restart start from an empty tried set. Combine with the existing per-group caps and allowlist. Eclipse attacks often begin by filling a node's new/tried tables during restart. Anchor connections preserve a known-good path and make the attack substantially harder.\n120\t900 Med-High/Med Dandelion++ stem-phase transaction relay :: Add an opt-in stem phase for new transactions: forward to one epoch-selected outbound peer, hold a random embargo timer, then fluff-broadcast to all peers. Rotate stem successors per epoch and fall back to direct broadcast on timeout or stem failure. Keep block relay unchanged. Merklock's relay currently broadcasts a transaction to every peer immediately, letting a network observer link origin. Dandelion++ raises the cost of large-scale deanonymization wi\n121\t901 High/High Threshold Winternitz signing with trustee shares :: Use a trusted dealer to split each Winternitz leaf's signing capability among trustees; an untrusted aggregator collects partial signatures from a threshold of trustees, combines them, and publishes one ordinary WOTS signature. Trustees keep only a PRF key and the leaf-use state; no single trustee can sign or reuse a leaf alone. A single hot keystore is a single point of failure. Threshold signing distributes the one-time secret and requires multiple\n122\t902 Med/Med Compact multi-account Merkle multiproof :: Add a compact multiproof that proves k account records in one document, storing only the k leaf indices and the shared intermediate nodes. Use it for multi-account wallets and light clients that need several accounts at one state root; verify all leaves against the same root in one pass. With 743 multi-account wallets, per-account proofs duplicate siblings. A compact multiproof reduces bandwidth and proof verification overhead.\n123\t970 Med-High/Med Constant-Time Secret-Handling Linter :: Lint signing and keystore code for secret-dependent `if`, indexing, early returns, variable-length loops and use of non-constant-time big-integer helpers; require an explicit `constant_time` annotation and a timing-variance test for primitives. Merklock's Winternitz/Merkle code handles long-lived secrets in pure Python, where timing discipline is easy to lose."}]},"uuid":"1db97429-95fc-49c0-a35e-1bf96c89bd80","timestamp":"2026-10-30T12:37:59.570Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\YUNUS-~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\c--Users-Yunus-Emre-Desktop-portable-agent-kit\\05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787\\scratchpad\\new_merklock.txt","content":"702 Med-High/Low Allowlist Reserved Capacity for Eclipse Resistance :: The resource manager allowlist is a reserved lane: allowlisted IP networks (optionally restricted to a specific peer ID) keep working when the normal system limits are exhausted. It is an eclipse-resistance tool: a trusted bootstrap or a known operator address is not pushed out by a flood of ordinary peers. Merklock's `PeerBook` has diversity by address group, but no trusted lane. If the peer book or a resource budget is full, a bootstrap peer o\n703 Med-High/Low Connection Gater Before Dial and Accept :: A connection gater is called before a dial, before an accept and after security/upgrade. It can block a peer ID, a multiaddress or an address range, and it can allow the rest. The gate is cheaper than completing a handshake and then closing, and it centralises policy that would otherwise be scattered across dial and accept paths. Merklock's `PeerLink` connects or accepts directly. There is no central place to refuse a self-connection, a loopback address fr\n704 Med-High/Low Peer Address TTL with Stale-Record Decay :: The peer store attaches a TTL to every address. Expired addresses are removed by a GC pass; an address held by a live connection gets a longer TTL so a session is not torn down. A newer signed peer record can extend the TTL, and an older record cannot. Merklock's `PeerRecord.last_seen` is stored and used for ordering, but it never expires. A peer that moved or went offline stays in the book forever, occupies a slot and can be retried indefinitely. The har\n716 High/Med Versioned State Root with Historical Proof :: IAVL saves each state version with a root hash and keeps the version's root node. `GetImmutable(version)` returns a read-only tree at that version, and a proof can be generated and verified against that version's root. The version is part of the node key, so the same logical key at two versions is two nodes. Merklock stores only the current state root in the latest header. A light client can prove an account against the tip, but not against a past height. A\n717 Med-High/Med Version Pruning with a Retain Window :: IAVL can delete versions below a retain window (`DeleteVersionsTo`) or above a rollback point (`DeleteVersionsFrom`). The tree remains usable; only the pruned historical proofs disappear. This is how a versioned store bounds disk growth without giving up recent history. Merklock currently has no historical state to prune because it keeps only the tip. Once `IDEA-716` adds historical roots, an unbounded cache would grow forever. A retain window gives a clear \"\n718 High/Med Rollback to a Height with Explicit Overwrite :: `LoadVersionForOverwriting` loads a historical version and deletes every newer version. It is an explicit, auditable rollback: the caller names the target height, the tree loads that root, and the future versions are removed. It is not a silent \"load and hope\"; the overwrite is in the name. Merklock replays the chain from genesis on reorg. A deep reorg rebuilds state, but there is no state-level rollback command that says \"discard everything above height \n719 Med-High/Med Deterministic State Snapshot Export and Import :: IAVL exports nodes in a deterministic depth-first post-order and imports them in the same order into an empty tree. The export carries a version; the import version must be at least the highest exported node version. The result is an identical tree, not a re-derived approximation. Merklock has a JSON state file but no portable, deterministic state snapshot with a version and digest. A node migration or a repair copies the whole directory, which is o\n720 Med-High/Low Typed Proof-Op Registry with a Proof Spec :: ICS23 wraps a proof in a typed op: the op has a `type` (for example `ics23:iavl`), a key and opaque data, and a verifier registry maps the type to a proof specification. A light client can support several proof formats without guessing the shape from the data. Merklock proof documents use a `version` and a fixed JSON shape. Adding a namespace, absence or a future state tree means changing the shape; an old client may misinterpret a new document. A typed \n721 High/Med Nonce-chain replacement with absolute-fee compensation :: For account-model transactions, allow a replacement only when it uses the same account nonce, pays at least the absolute fee of the transaction it replaces plus every nonce-dependent successor it evicts, and pays an additional fee at the incremental relay rate for its own size. Bound the replacement to a fixed number of account queues, evict the whole nonce chain, and require that the replacement strictly improve the mempool's fee-versus-size di\n722 Med-High/Med Nonce-chain eviction with rolling minimum fee :: When the mempool exceeds its byte budget or an entry expires, evict the lowest-feerate contiguous nonce chain as a unit, including all successors, rather than individual transactions. Raise a rolling minimum fee to the evicted chain's feerate plus the incremental relay rate, then decay it with a half-life that shortens as the pool empties; expire whole tails so no successor is left orphaned. Evicting a middle transaction strands every higher nonce an\n723 Med-High/Low Codec Registry with Status Lifecycle and Reserved Ranges :: multicodec assigns a numeric code and a lifecycle status. A `draft` code may be reassigned if it does not gain adoption; a `permanent` code may not. The first 127 one-byte codes are reserved for widely used formats, and the private-use range is forbidden in the public table. Merklock's suite IDs are strings (`wots16-merkle-sha256-v1`) with a three-character prefix. There is no numeric code, no reserved range and no promotion rule. A future\n724 Med/Low Canonical Unsigned-Varint Prefix Decoder :: multicodec does not store a fixed-width integer. The code is an unsigned varint, so small codes are one byte and larger codes grow. A decoder must reject an overlong encoding, a truncated varint and a value outside the registry; otherwise two byte strings decode to the same code. Merklock uses text prefixes, so it avoids this today. A future binary transaction or peer envelope would need a compact code. A canonical varint decoder prevents ambiguous encodings a\n725 Med-High/Low Registry Invariant Validator as a Gate :: The multicodec validator treats the registry as data and checks it mechanically: codes ascend, names and codes are unique, names match a regex, codes are hex byte sequences, the private-use range is empty. A bad row fails the build, not a reviewer's memory. Merklock's `SUITES` tuple is code. A new suite is a Python edit; a duplicate prefix, a skipped code or a reserved-range collision is only caught by a test if someone writes that test. A registry validato\n726 Med/Low Generated Constants and Name Normalization :: multicodec names are designed to become constants in many languages: lowercase, alphanumeric, hyphen-separated, underscore only as a secondary separator. The table is the source; each language generates its constants from it instead of hand-copying. Merklock's suite names are long and inconsistent (`wots16-merkle-sha256-v1`, `ml-dsa-65-sha256-v1`, `slh-dsa-sha2-128s-v1`). The CLI, docs and code repeat them; a rename can leave a stale string. A canonical norm\n727 Med-High/Med Self-Describing Identifier Envelope :: The multiformats design puts the identifier inline with the value: the code travels with the bytes, not in an out-of-band function argument. That makes a value self-describing and lets a reader reject an unknown format without a registry lookup by context. Merklock's text addresses carry `mkl1` inline, but its transaction, block and peer envelopes are JSON with a version field and no typed code. A future binary envelope needs the same property: the suite/codec\n743 High/Med Domain-separated Winternitz account tree :: Derive each account's Winternitz keypair from the master seed with HMAC-SHA256 over a structured, fully hardened path (suite, account_index, tree_index), giving each account a disjoint leaf space. Bind the suite, chain ID and account index into every signature digest, and derive the account's public identifier from the same path so a wallet can regenerate accounts without storing an address list. A single seed should recover every account, but Merklock's curr\n744 Med-High/Low Gap-limit account discovery with lookahead :: On restore, derive account 0, scan its external address/leaf range up to a gap limit of N unused leaves, then advance to account 1 only if account 0 has history; stop at the first empty account and warn when the user tries to exceed the gap limit. Keep a lookahead window for the first unused account so new accounts are created only after the previous one is used. Without a gap limit, a restored wallet cannot know when to stop scanning; without the accou\n745 Med-High/Med SSZ-Style Hash Tree Root with a Stable Type Schema :: SSZ gives every container a type: field order, fixed or variable length, and a maximum. The hash tree root is a binary Merkleization of that typed object, not a hash of a JSON string. Two serializers that produce different whitespace or key order still yield the same root. Merklock commits to `transaction.canonical_json(account_record(...))`. Canonical JSON is a good start, but the state root is coupled to the JSON schema: adding a field, changi\n746 Med-High/Med Generalized-Index Field Proof for Account Records :: A generalized index names a position in a Merkleized container: the binary path from the root. A proof reveals one field and its branch, not the whole record. The verifier computes the root from the leaf and branch and compares it with the committed root. Merklock's account proof returns the full account record plus a sibling path. A light client that only needs a balance still receives address, nonce, spent and lock data. A field proof reduces t\n747 High/Med Light-Client Bootstrap from a Trusted Checkpoint :: An Ethereum light client does not replay from genesis. It is configured with a trusted block root, fetches a bootstrap containing the header and the current sync committee (with a Merkle proof), and then follows updates. The trust anchor is explicit and small. Merklock's `LightChain` validates a sequence of headers from a known starting point. A new light client must obtain that starting point out of band, and there is no bootstrap object that binds t\n748 Med-High/Low Weak-Subjectivity Checkpoint with Explicit Expiry :: A weak-subjectivity checkpoint is a recent finalized root that a new node trusts because it has no history to verify. Ethereum documents the period during which that trust is reasonable and the social process for obtaining it. Merklock's light client has no stated trust policy: it accepts a starting header and trusts the chain from there. A checkpoint object with a height, hash, timestamp and expiry makes the trust decision explicit and prevents \n749 Med/Med Fixed/Variable SSZ-Style Transaction Encoding :: SSZ serializes a container as fixed-size fields followed by offsets to variable-size fields, with the variable data appended in field order. The encoding is deterministic and self-delimiting; a decoder can validate lengths before reading. Merklock's transaction wire format is canonical JSON. It is human-readable but larger, and a decoder must parse the whole document before checking field types. A binary SSZ-style form gives a compact canonical encoding w\n750 High/High Hybrid PQ transport profile with a reviewed combiner :: Add an optional transport profile that negotiates a named hybrid group (X25519 + ML-KEM-768), derives both key shares from a transport-only seed, combines the two shared secrets with a labeled hash over both ciphertexts and the handshake transcript, and records the negotiated group in peer connection evidence. Reject ad-hoc concatenation and silent classical fallback; keep classical TLS as an explicit opt-in. Merklock is post-quantum at the signa\n781 High/High Executable state-transition oracle with differential fixtures :: Write a small, independent Python model of Merklock's block and transaction application, and generate deterministic state and blockchain fixtures from it. Run each fixture against the production node and the model, compare post-state root, balances, total supply, nonce and leaf counters, and rejection outcome, then store every divergence as a permanent regression. Merklock's consensus code has no independent oracle; a bug in state applic\n782 Med-High/Med Suite-version validity matrix with transition fixtures :: Tag every consensus test with the suite versions and activation heights it applies to, and generate three fixtures at each boundary: before, at, and after. Include a block that is valid under the old suite and invalid under the new one, and assert the exact rejection class at the boundary. Merklock already plans suite migration (mkl1, mkl2, mkl3) but has no systematic way to prove old and new rules coexist. Boundary fixtures catch off-by-one\n783 Med-High/Low Typed chain-rejection corpus :: Define typed rejection classes for Merklock transactions and blocks (bad nonce, reused leaf, invalid signature, insufficient fee, expired block-to-live, stale parent, invalid state root, supply violation) and attach the expected class to every negative fixture. The runner must verify both rejection and the class, so a node cannot pass by rejecting for an unrelated reason. A negative test that only checks that a block was rejected can hide a missing validation behind \n784 High/Med Incremental Account Witness with Anchor Pinning :: An Orchard note is a leaf in a commitment tree. A spend carries an anchor and a Merkle path; the verifier recomputes the root from the leaf and path and compares it with the anchor. The wallet does not send the whole tree, and it does not rebuild the tree for every proof: an incremental witness is updated as leaves are appended. The anchor pins the witness to a root, so a stale path is rejected rather than silently accepted. Merklock's `proofs.build_ac\n785 High/Med Role-Separated Transaction Package :: A PCZT is a serialized partially created transaction. The Creator fixes the transaction version, flags and anchor; the Constructor adds spends and outputs; the Updater installs witnesses and viewing keys; the IO Finalizer computes the sighash; the Signer adds signatures; the Prover adds proofs; the Verifier checks that the public fields are consistent; the Extractor refuses until every required field is present. Fields that are no longer needed can be redacted, and\n786 High/High KEM-Backed Memo Envelope with Version-Pinned Decryption :: An Orchard output does not contain the recipient address in cleartext. The sender derives a shared secret with the recipient's incoming viewing key, encrypts the note plaintext under it, and publishes the ephemeral public key alongside the ciphertext. The wallet trial-decrypts outputs; a compact 52-byte prefix omits the 512-byte memo so a light client can scan cheaply. The plaintext begins with a version byte, and the domain policy accepts exa\n787 High/Med Scoped View Keys with Outgoing Recovery :: An Orchard full viewing key is a capability set, not a single secret. `to_ivk(scope)` derives a key that detects and decrypts received notes; `to_ovk(scope)` derives a key that decrypts the sender's `out_ciphertext` copy of what was sent. The spend authorizing key is separate. A view-only wallet can therefore audit balances and memos without the ability to sign, and a sender can recover its payment history from the chain even after losing local state. Merklock\n788 High/High Delegated Vesting Claim with Nullifier :: An Orchard note is committed to the tree; spending it reveals a nullifier derived from the note, the nullifier key and the commitment. The nullifier is unique, so a duplicate is a double-spend, and it is unlinkable to the commitment without the viewing key. This is the clean separation between \"the right exists\" (the commitment) and \"the right was exercised\" (the nullifier). Merklock's premine vesting is bound to one address. `state.locked[address] = (allocati\n789 Med-High/Med Bounded-label metrics registry with duplicate-series rejection :: Add a small metrics registry with counters, gauges and histograms; validate metric and label names, reject duplicate timeseries at registration, enforce a per-metric label-name allowlist and a hard cardinality cap with an overflow bucket, and expose a Prometheus-compatible text endpoint on loopback. Keep peer IDs, addresses and other unbounded values out of labels. Merklock has no operator metrics; naive per-peer or per-address label\n790 Med/Med Per-process mmap aggregation for node and miner subprocesses :: In multi-process mode, each node or miner subprocess writes counters and gauges to its own memory-mapped file; the parent exposes a collector that merges the files, adds a pid label only for live-process gauges, and removes files for dead pids. This preserves counters across worker restarts without an IPC hop. Merklock runs a node plus external miner and verifier subprocesses; a single in-process registry would lose their counters or requir\n791 High/Med Detached Winner Signature Outside the Hash Preimage :: NADO separates **block validity** from **producer authentication**. The PoW/weighted draw decides the winner; the winner may attach a detached ML-DSA signature outside the block hash preimage. The signature cannot change the block hash or its weight, but it lets another node prove who produced the block and detect two conflicting signed blocks at the same height and parent. Merklock blocks are anonymous: the coinbase names a miner address, but ther\n792 High/Med Portable Equivocation Evidence Record :: A signature alone is not enough; the useful artifact is a **portable proof of equivocation**: two signed blocks with the same height and parent but different hashes. Anyone can verify the proof without trusting the reporter. NADO uses it to slash a bond; a PoW chain without bonds can still use it to ban a peer, archive evidence, and warn light clients. Merklock's orphan archive (IDEA-656) can hold the two blocks, but there is no canonical evidence record. A `Equ\n793 High/High Snapshot Bootstrap Bound to a Finalized Signed Checkpoint :: A new node can bootstrap from a snapshot instead of replaying every block, but only if the snapshot is bound to a finalized checkpoint: height, state root, and a signature the node already trusts. A snapshot that is internally consistent but does not match the checkpoint is rejected. Merklock replays the full chain on every startup (`_replay()`), which is O(n) and grows without bound. A signed snapshot would make startup O(1) plus the tail, \n794 High/Med Two-Floor Finality: Soft Depth and Hard Floor :: NADO distinguishes a **latency-sensitive depth floor** (`tip - FINALITY_DEPTH`) from a **hard floor** that rollback never crosses. The soft floor feeds the execution layer, pruning and snapshots; the hard floor is backed by a stronger finality rule (FFG/backstop) and a violation is a hard error. Both are monotonic. Merklock currently has a single heaviest-chain rule; `IDEA-661` adds a reorg limit, but one limit has to serve both \"fast UX confirmations\" a\n795 Med/High Zero-Install Browser Node for Wallet, Mine, Explore and Alias :: NADO's adoption thesis is that a node should be one URL: open it and you have a wallet, miner, explorer and alias manager. There is no extension, no sync, no account. The browser page talks to a local node and can generate a post-quantum wallet, mine, send/receive, register an alias and browse the chain. Merklock is a CLI/reference implementation. A local browser UI would lower the barrier to a demo: create a wallet, mine a block, send a \n806 High/High TLA+ model of the signing-state machine :: Specify a small TLA+ state machine for Merklock's wallet: seed-derived leaf space, reserved watermark, signed and revealed leaves, chain tip with reorg, mempool, crash/restart and restore. Model hashes and signatures as uninterpreted constants, then check safety invariants (no leaf signed twice, watermark monotonic, burned leaves permanent, supply constant) with TLC over bounded leaf/block sets; use Apalache for symbolic checks when the state space grows. The\n807 Med-High/Med Counterexample-to-fixture bridge :: When TLC finds an invariant violation, export the trace as a deterministic scenario (initial wallet and chain state, ordered events, expected failure) and add it to the Python test harness. CI runs both the model and the fixture, and a spec change that removes a counterexample must also update the regression. Formal counterexamples are only useful if they become permanent tests; otherwise the same bug can return after the model is refactored. This connects the mo\n823 High/Low Account Derivation Index for Multiple Trees from One Seed :: A master seed derives a per-address chain seed with a 32-bit derivation index. Each index produces an independent tree; the wallet can rotate to a new address when a tree is exhausted without changing the mnemonic/seed. Merklock's `keys.leaf_seed(master_seed, index)` derives leaves within one tree, but there is no account index. When a wallet exhausts its 2^height leaves, the user must create a new wallet and back up a new seed. A derivation \n824 High/Med Parameter Object with Derived WOTS Lengths :: The parameter object derives `ell_1 = ceil(8n/log2(w))`, `ell_2` from the checksum range, and `ell = ell_1 + ell_2`. Keygen, sign, verify and size calculations all read the same object. Changing `n` or `w` changes the signature size and the chain lengths consistently. Merklock's `winternitz.py` uses module constants (`WINDOW=16`, `DIGITS=64`, `CHECKSUM_DIGITS=3`, `CHAINS=67`). That is fine for one suite, but a compact or future suite needs a parameter objec\n825 Med-High/Low Self-Describing Witness Envelope :: The witness is a single serialized object: WOTS signature + leaf index + auth path. `serialize(K)` uses the tree size to choose the index length; `total_size()` includes the control block. The verifier knows `K`/`params` from the address. Merklock's transaction carries `signature`, `auth_path`, and `nonce` as separate fields. That works for one suite, but a compact/legacy suite or a different height needs the verifier to infer parameters. A versioned witness enve\n826 Med/Low Signature-Size and Verification-Cost Estimator :: The reference publishes formulas for witness size, keygen hash count, and verification hash count. The wallet/UI can compare `K` values and parameter sets before choosing a fee. Merklock has `Transaction.weight()` and a fee rate, but a wallet cannot estimate the signature size/verification cost before signing. An estimator would let the wallet choose a height, warn about a large tree, and compute a fee that reflects verification work. The estimator must \n827 Med-High/High Compact `w=256` Parameter Set with a Security/Cost Gate :: WOTS-Tree uses `n=16` (128-bit truncated chain hash), `w=256`, and a full 256-bit Merkle tree hash. The result is a 675-byte witness at `K=1024`, much smaller than a hypertree. The design deliberately places collision resistance on the tree and only second-preimage resistance on the chains. Merklock's current `w=16, n=32` signature is 2,144 bytes. A **compact `w=256, n=32`** variant would be roughly 34 × 32 = 1,088 bytes (plus auth path), \n833 High/Med Effect Hash over Effecting Data with a Pre-Signing Plan :: Penumbra separates what a transaction *does* from how it is *authorized*. The effect hash commits to the state changes (commitments, nullifiers, parameters, memo, detection data) and excludes signatures and proofs. The auth hash covers the effect hash plus the authorization. A `TransactionPlan` can compute the effect hash before any proof or signature exists, so a signer or a hardware device can review the effects and approve exactly that hash.\n834 High/Med Typed Action Envelope with Stateless/Stateful Handler Phases :: A Penumbra transaction is an ordered list of typed actions. Each action is a distinct type with its own data and its own `GasCost`; the parser rejects an unknown variant. Validation is split into a stateless phase (signatures, counts, memo presence, non-empty) and a stateful phase (chain id, expiry, FMD parameters) before execution. A handler registry dispatches by action type, so a new action does not edit the transfer validator. Merklock\n835 Med-High/Med Bloom-Filter Scan Clue for Delegated Detection :: FMD lets a sender attach a clue that a detection key can test. The test always accepts a real recipient and accepts a non-recipient with probability `2^-precision`. A view server can therefore scan for a wallet and return a superset of candidates; the wallet trial-decrypts only those and learns which are real. The server learns a candidate set, not the payment graph. Precision is a consensus parameter with a grace period, so an old clue remains vali\n836 High/Med Multi-Dimensional Gas Meter for Block Validation :: Penumbra prices a transaction on several independent dimensions: block space, compact-block space, verification and execution. An action declares its cost, a block is bounded per dimension, and fees convert gas into tokens. A block full of expensive verification cannot pass just because its bytes fit. Merklock bounds a block by `max_block_transactions` and `max_block_bytes`. Those limits do not capture the cost of verifying a Winternitz signature vers\n837 Med-High/Med Three-Watermark Wallet Sync with Quarantine :: A Penumbra view service reports three heights: the chain tip it knows, the height it has fully scanned, and the height it has partially scanned. A `catching_up` flag distinguishes a syncing service from a stalled one. A client can therefore tell whether a balance is complete or provisional, and resume from the right height after a restart. Merklock's `LightChain` stores validated headers and `Chain` stores state, but the wallet has no scan watermark. `\n892 High/High Sectorized Winternitz tree for multi-device signing :: Split one height-h Merkle tree into 2^(h-s) sectors of height s, each generated from an independent seed and stored with its off-path nodes, while all sectors share the same address root. Assign each signing device a disjoint sector so devices never coordinate a leaf counter; a compromise is contained to one sector, and unused sectors can be activated without changing the address. Merklock's single next_leaf counter assumes one signer. Sectorizati\n893 High/High Cold-root endorsement of hot signing subtrees :: Keep a long-lived cold root tree that signs only the roots of short-lived operational subtrees; hot devices hold a subtree and sign messages, while the root endorses each new subtree. Rotate subtrees before exhaustion or on suspicion, and publish the endorsement chain so verifiers can bind a hot key to the cold root. A hot wallet with a single tree exposes the whole address if the host is compromised. A two-tier hierarchy bounds the damage to one subtre\n894 Med-High/Med Verifiable state handoff with source retirement :: Move a keystore and its leaf state to a new device through a two-step export/import: the source exports the encrypted seed and a signed state summary, the destination imports and proves possession of the same state, and the source irreversibly retires its copy. Record a transfer receipt with the old and new state watermarks; refuse to sign on either device until the handoff completes. Copying a keystore to a second device is the easiest way to reus\n895 Med-High/Low Sign-then-verify gate before signature release :: After generating a Winternitz signature, recover the public key from the signature and verify it against the expected leaf and root before releasing the transaction. Abort and burn the leaf on any mismatch, and log a typed fault event. Apply the same check to external signing backends. Fault injection against the WOTS checksum can turn a single faulty signature into a forgery. A local self-check catches implementation faults and many injected faults\n896 High/Med Log-backed leaf-use audit with consistency proofs :: Append a commitment to every revealed (address, leaf index, txid) to an append-only Merkle log; publish signed tree heads and serve inclusion and consistency proofs. Wallets and auditors can detect two signatures for the same leaf even if they do not see the whole chain, and a log that shows different views is detectable. RFC 10033 notes that verifier-side reuse detection is trivial if all signatures are available. Merklock's tombstones are local; a \n897 Med/Low Hashrate-calibrated confirmation depth :: Derive a confirmation-depth recommendation from an explicit adversary hash-rate fraction and a target double-spend probability, using the Rosenfeld/Nakamoto model. Publish the assumptions and a table, expose the recommendation in proofs and the CLI, and never label it finality. Merklock's proofs accept min_confirmations but there is no quantitative policy. A calibrated table gives merchants and light clients a defensible default without claiming finality.\n898 Med-High/Low Anchor connections across restarts :: Persist two anchor peers across restarts, keep them out of the normal eviction path, rotate feelers through bucket-diverse candidates, and never let a restart start from an empty tried set. Combine with the existing per-group caps and allowlist. Eclipse attacks often begin by filling a node's new/tried tables during restart. Anchor connections preserve a known-good path and make the attack substantially harder.\n900 Med-High/Med Dandelion++ stem-phase transaction relay :: Add an opt-in stem phase for new transactions: forward to one epoch-selected outbound peer, hold a random embargo timer, then fluff-broadcast to all peers. Rotate stem successors per epoch and fall back to direct broadcast on timeout or stem failure. Keep block relay unchanged. Merklock's relay currently broadcasts a transaction to every peer immediately, letting a network observer link origin. Dandelion++ raises the cost of large-scale deanonymization wi\n901 High/High Threshold Winternitz signing with trustee shares :: Use a trusted dealer to split each Winternitz leaf's signing capability among trustees; an untrusted aggregator collects partial signatures from a threshold of trustees, combines them, and publishes one ordinary WOTS signature. Trustees keep only a PRF key and the leaf-use state; no single trustee can sign or reuse a leaf alone. A single hot keystore is a single point of failure. Threshold signing distributes the one-time secret and requires multiple\n902 Med/Med Compact multi-account Merkle multiproof :: Add a compact multiproof that proves k account records in one document, storing only the k leaf indices and the shared intermediate nodes. Use it for multi-account wallets and light clients that need several accounts at one state root; verify all leaves against the same root in one pass. With 743 multi-account wallets, per-account proofs duplicate siblings. A compact multiproof reduces bandwidth and proof verification overhead.\n970 Med-High/Med Constant-Time Secret-Handling Linter :: Lint signing and keystore code for secret-dependent `if`, indexing, early returns, variable-length loops and use of non-constant-time big-integer helpers; require an explicit `constant_time` annotation and a timing-variance test for primitives. Merklock's Winternitz/Merkle code handles long-lived secrets in pure Python, where timing discipline is easy to lose.","numLines":61,"startLine":63,"totalLines":123}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ba780fe8-819b-4279-aa2e-a81d355d70f9","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\merklock","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main"}
