{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/AIKit","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/NeuroCore","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Research-Engine","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Aegean-Licensing","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Aegean-Licensing-Ops","visibility":"private"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Merklock","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/c/Users/Yunus-Emre/Desktop/portable_agent_kit/projects/modelduck","platform":"windows"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6d2d3234-459b-4d21-b4cf-ef64b08e0993","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"1437c78f-b65d-45e4-b13d-8fe7f85acf73","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4ce50c82-a8ed-4889-ac0c-4fb42e7bef08","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FETULWGFnpvxGqrxTsnEwe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t1000 High/Low Rendered-Prompt Fingerprint for Generation and Benchmarks :: NeuroCore snapshots the leading system messages when an agent loop starts, hashes their contents, re-verifies the hash before every model call, restores the pinned prefix if compaction or truncation changed it, and records an `integrity_violation` flag. The governing prompt is treated as a value that must survive every rewrite, not as display text. ModelDuck's benchmark gate is its moat, but the `testHash` covers only `SUITE_VERSION`, the fi\n2\t1001 High/Med Content-Addressed Prompt and Preset Library with Benchmark-Gat :: NeuroCore stores every skill-document change under its SHA-256 content hash, keeps a bounded per-skill index, and writes a rollback as a new version so the rollback itself is auditable. Promotion is eval-gated: the baseline must exist, name its provenance and be perfect; the candidate must be perfect; a regression blocks promotion. ModelDuck's three presets are constants in `src/config.js`, and the README's claim that they \"only earn tr\n3\t1002 High/Med Blocking Load Plan with Finding Codes :: NeuroCore's simulator never executes a node. It checks structure, effects and a cost envelope and returns `{blocking, findings:[{severity, code, message, node_id, fix}], cost, effects, cycles}`. A single pass that cannot fit is blocking; a cycle that may reach the cap is a warning. The editor and the improvement engine gate on `blocking`, not on a vague score. ModelDuck's loader silently tries `[wanted, min(wanted, 3072), 2048, 1024]` and reports a generic out-\n4\t1003 Med-High/Med Shared Request and JIT-Load Budget :: NeuroCore creates one execution budget per top-level flow run and shares it through a context variable, so nested and bridged runs cannot each spend the full allowance. A flow's metadata may lower a limit but never raise it. Exhaustion is a normal, reported failure with a why/fix; `pressure` is a 0–1 signal for how close the run is to a limit. ModelDuck runs one generation at a time by design. `maxTokens` bounds the output, but nothing bounds prompt processing\n5\t1004 Med/Low Self-Calibrating Latency Percentiles and Stall Findings :: NeuroCore times every node attempt and compares it with its own recent history. Once it has 20 samples, a duration above both an absolute floor and three times the prior p99 is recorded as a stall, logged and counted. Detection never aborts a run, because a slow node may still be doing useful work. ModelDuck shows only the last reply's tokens and timings. A slow reply is hard to attribute to prompt processing, first-token latency, generation, m\n6\t1015 High/Med Untrusted-Content Fence and Tool-Call Loop Guard :: NeuroCore fences untrusted text before it reaches a model: a source label, a fresh random nonce, and neutralized tag-shaped text inside. Tool output and retrieved documents are data, not instructions, and the payload cannot close its own block. Its loop guard normalizes tool arguments by removing only transport identifiers (`request_id`, `trace_id`, `nonce`, timestamps) while keeping domain IDs, warns on the third identical call or an A-B-A-B oscilla\n7\t1016 High/Med SSRF-Pinned Hugging Face Fetch with Content Hash :: NeuroCore resolves a hostname once, checks that every resolved address is public, and then connects to the vetted address rather than resolving the name again. The hostname travels in the `Host` header and as TLS SNI, so certificate verification still works. The research gateway applies the same check to every redirect hop, caps the response size and time, and returns a content hash with the source metadata. ModelDuck's curated downloader follows red\n8\t1017 Med/Low Benchmark Trend and Comparability Report :: NeuroCore keeps a bounded sample history, compares the mean of the last 20 samples with the previous 20, requires a meaningful delta, and emits at most one finding per day with a cooldown and persisted dedupe. Drift compares a live snapshot with a measured baseline and returns a score plus reasons, not a bare number. ModelDuck's gate compares one run with a committed baseline and warns on a speed drop over 20%. CPU inference is noisy; a gradual quality or spe\n9\t1018 Med-Low/Low Exact-Phrase Confirmation for Destructive Routes :: NeuroCore requires the exact confirmation phrase on an irreversible route, in addition to the browser dialog. A browser `confirm()` is a usability guard, not an authorization check: any HTTP client can call the endpoint directly. ModelDuck's model delete and conversation delete rely on the UI dialog. An authenticated LAN client can delete a multi-gigabyte model with one request. A server-side phrase makes the irreversible action explicit for every\n10\t1019 Med-High/Med Migration Importer for Ollama and LM Studio :: The fastest way to lose a user is to make them re-download 20 GB. Ollama stores models as manifests and content-addressed blobs; LM Studio stores GGUF files in a model directory. A migration importer can scan both, identify the model files, and offer to import them into ModelDuck by copy or hardlink, preserving the source installation. The importer should be read-only on the source. It reads Ollama manifests, maps the model layer digest to the blob pa\n11\t1020 Med/Med Conversation Branching and Message Editing :: ModelDuck can send, stop and regenerate the last answer, but it cannot edit an earlier user message, fork a conversation, or keep two answers to the same prompt. Every serious chat client offers this; it is how users explore alternatives without losing the original thread. The current linear `messages[]` store makes a branch impossible without overwriting history. A message tree is the minimal model: each message has an `id`, `parentId`, `role`, `content` a\n12\t1021 Med/Low CLI Subcommand Surface with JSON Output :: LM Studio's `lms` CLI makes the local runtime scriptable: `lms status`, `lms ls --json`, `lms ps`, `lms load`, `lms unload`, `lms log stream`. Ollama's CLI is the ecosystem's muscle memory. ModelDuck's CLI only starts the server; every other action requires the browser UI or a raw HTTP call. A thin CLI over the local API would add `serve`, `pull`, `ls`, `ps`, `load`, `unload`, `show`, `rm`, `run`, `status`, `logs` and `bench`, with `--json` and stable exit cod\n13\t1022 High/Med Deterministic Prompt Trace and Replay :: ModelDuck's stated reason to exist is inspectability, yet the UI never shows the exact prompt, token budget or sampler trace. DeepChat markets \"Tape & Trace\" as a way to inspect request context and token budgets; `llama.cpp` exposes `/apply-template` and `/tokenize` for the same reason. ModelDuck's benchmark already records a seed, test hash and timings, so the data model already exists in spirit. A generation trace would record the model file hash, chat-templa\n14\t1023 Med/Med Local Blind Arena for Model Comparison :: ModelDuck already has a deterministic benchmark gate and a commit-linked history. What it lacks is a human, in-the-loop comparison: run the same prompt on two installed models or two presets, hide the labels, vote, and record the result. Open WebUI ships an arena with ELO; Cherry Studio compares multiple models; LM Studio makes A/B chat easy. A local arena can respect the single-context design by running the two candidates sequentially, not in parallel. The UI \n15\t1024 Med-High/Low Single-Instance Host Contract with Graceful Drain :: ModelDuck's current unit of operation is one foreground Node process. If the user double-clicks the launcher twice, the second process either fails with `EADDRINUSE` or, worse, loads nothing and leaves a confusing error. If the machine reboots, the local API is gone until the user remembers the launcher. If the user closes the console during a stream, the model, the KV cache and the port can be torn down without flushing state. The host contract\n16\t1025 Med-High/Med Supervised Server with Crash-Loop Budget :: A local model server is unattended by nature. A transient crash (a native addon fault, a bad request, a Windows file lock) should not leave the user with a dead browser tab, and a deterministic crash should not become an infinite restart loop that burns CPU and fills the log. `modelduck supervise` becomes a small parent process that spawns `modelduck serve` as a child and classifies each exit: - **Clean exit 0** -> supervisor exits `0`, no restart. - **T\n17\t1026 High/Med Versioned Conversation Archive with Interop Import :: Once ModelDuck stores conversations (`IDEA-857`, `IDEA-869`), the next question is ownership. A local-first app should let the user take every conversation out, read it without ModelDuck, move it to another machine, and bring an existing ChatGPT or LibreChat history in. Today the register has branching (`IDEA-1020`) and model migration (`IDEA-1019`), but no user-data archive. The archive is a **directory bundle**, not a ZIP, so the runtime needs no\n18\t1027 Med-High/Low Storage Ledger and Safe Reclamation :: A local model store grows in four ways that users cannot see: full model files, sharded siblings and projectors, abandoned `.part` downloads, and blob/temp files left by a failed import or upgrade. The current downloader checks the final size and performs an atomic rename, but nothing reports what the store costs, which model was last used, or which bytes are safe to reclaim. `modelduck storage` (and a UI \"Storage\" panel) scans the models root and produces a \n19\t1028 Med-High/Med Spec-First Local API with Generated Client :: ModelDuck already exposes an OpenAI-compatible API, but the contract lives in code and prose. Every client integration, test and UI call re-discovers the shapes by hand, and a route change can break a consumer without any test noticing. `openai-node` shows the stronger pattern: the OpenAPI specification is the source, and the client is generated from it. The proposal is a checked-in OpenAPI 3.1 document (`openapi/modelduck.openapi.json`) that describes\n20\t1029 High/Med One-Click Client Wiring and Connection Doctor :: The most common local-API support question is not \"does it stream?\" but \"what URL and model name do I put in this client?\". ModelDuck's protocol work (`IDEA-994`) makes clients *able* to connect; it does not make them *easy* to connect. Ollama's desktop app goes further: it ships installers and binding code for Claude Desktop and Codex, detects an existing installation, and configures it from the app. A \"Connect\" page lists verified presets with the exa\n21\t1030 Med-High/Med Power-Profile Governor :: ModelDuck currently asks `os.availableParallelism()` for a thread count, reports CPU and memory in `systemInfo()`, and otherwise assumes a desktop on mains power. A 7B Q4 model that is pleasant on AC can drain a laptop, trigger thermal throttling, or make the fan the loudest thing in the room. The user then sees a slow model and no explanation. TLP and auto-cpufreq solve the same class of problem for the whole machine: separate profiles per power source, explicit sensor i\n22\t1031 High/Low Model License Card and Acceptance Ledger :: ModelDuck's catalog says `Apache-2.0`, `MIT`, `Llama 3.2 Community License`, but the UI never shows those strings, the download button never asks for acceptance, and a model imported from disk has no license story at all. That is acceptable for a prototype and wrong for a tool that invites a user to download multi-gigabyte weights and run them for years. The mechanism is a license card plus a local, append-only acceptance ledger, not a legal-advice engine. F\n23\t1032 High/Med Isolated Profiles :: `--home` is an escape hatch, not a feature. To keep work chats away from personal chats today, a user must create a second folder, remember its path, close the first server, and relaunch with a long flag. The single home lock then treats the second folder as an unrelated install, so there is no list, no naming, no safe deletion and no way to see which profile owns which disk usage. Joplin and VS Code both show the better shape: a named profile is a first-class identity, a switcher\n24\t1033 Med/Med Library Collections and Metadata Overlay :: The registry is a scanner, not a library. It lists every GGUF it finds, sorted by name, with derived technical metadata. A user with forty models has no favorites, no \"coding\" collection, no way to hide the broken 7B, no note explaining why a quantization was kept, and no way to find a file after moving it. Calibre's answer is a library database beside the books: the files stay untouched, while collections, tags and per-book metadata live in `metadata.db`. Mo\n25\t1034 Med/Med Metered and Off-Peak Download Policy :: ModelDuck can start a 4.3 GB download the moment a user clicks, with no speed limit and no notion of a metered connection. On a hotel Wi-Fi, a phone hotspot or a capped rural link, that is an expensive surprise. The downloader already has the right primitives - `.part`, `Range`, abort, exact size - but it lacks a policy layer that says *when* and *how fast*. aria2 exposes global and per-download limits plus pause/continue; rclone turns a bandwidth limit into a sc\n26\t1035 Med/Med Full-Library Integrity Audit :: A GGUF file can keep its size and magic while losing a block to bit rot, a bad USB cable, a partial copy or a filesystem repair. ModelDuck would still list it, try to load it, and fail with a confusing native error. The downloader verifies the byte count once; the registry checks the magic on every scan; nothing verifies content after that. Restic's model is the right one: a quick check, a deep read-everything check, a recorded baseline, and a repair path that restores f\n27\t1036 High/Med Accessibility and Localization Baseline :: A local chat studio is a reading and writing tool. If the streamed answer is not announced, if a dialog steals focus and never returns it, if the only way to send is a mouse, or if the interface is English-only, a whole class of users is excluded from a tool that otherwise respects their privacy and their hardware. VS Code treats accessibility as a first-class mode rather than a checklist; Joplin ships translations and documents screen-reader behavior. ModelD\n28\t1037 High/Med Paired-Statistics Benchmark Gate with Per-Item Outcomes :: The benchmark history already stores per-prompt rows in `prompt_results`, each with a manifest digest, plus the run environment; the gate still reduces them to a point score and does not compute paired statistics. Fourteen independent tests at alpha = 0.05 give roughly a 51% chance of at least one false alarm per run, and an aggregate score cannot distinguish a model regression from a parser change, a prompt change, or a handful of unlucky sam\n29\t1038 High/Med Reproducible Run Manifest with Thermal and Baseline Expiry :: A benchmark result is only comparable when the environment is part of the result. The existing `runs` history already records CPU, platform, runtime version, test hash, prompt-fingerprint version, and commit identity. Extend that into a first-class `RunManifest` with OS build, physical and logical cores, RAM, GPU backend, Node version, the bundled llama.cpp build when exposed, model SHA-256, quantization, tokenizer and chat-template hashes,\n30\t1039 High/Med Egress Ledger and Claim Ladder :: ModelDuck can claim that inference is local, but its downloader contacts Hugging Face, and any future catalog refresh or update check would add more egress. Add a process-wide network ledger that records every outbound request: timestamp, method, URL, host, initiator, status, bytes, and purpose. Redact query strings and headers that may contain tokens. Expose the ledger through a loopback-only `GET /api/network-ledger` route and a UI panel. Offline Mode sets a global \n31\t1040 High/Med Extractive Citation Verification with Abstention :: A grounded answer should show its work. After retrieval and generation, split the answer into sentences, resolve each citation marker to a retrieved chunk, and search that chunk for an extractive quote that supports the sentence. Use normalized exact matching first, then a bounded fuzzy match over a quote window; label each sentence `supported`, `weak`, `unsupported`, or `no_citation`. If the support rate falls below a configured threshold, or if no \n32\t1041 Med-High/Med Semantic Validation for Constrained Output :: Grammar-constrained decoding guarantees syntax, not meaning. A model can emit a schema-valid object with an empty required field, an out-of-range number, a start date after an end date, or a tool argument outside the allowlist. Add a deterministic semantic-validation layer after parsing: required fields non-empty, enum and range checks, date and number formats, cross-field consistency, tool allowlist, and no unknown arguments. ModelDuck currently stops\n33\t1042 Med-High/Med Editable Fact-Table Memory with Contradiction Detection :: Keep a per-conversation fact table alongside the raw transcript: `{ id, subject, predicate, value, sourceMessageId, createdAt, updatedAt, confidence }`. Extract facts after a turn or on demand, inject the top-k facts relevant to the current query, and let the user edit or delete any fact. Detect contradictions when the same subject and predicate receive a different value; show both values with their source messages and let the user resolve\n34\t1043 Med-High/High N-gram Self-Speculation for Code and Editing :: Speculation does not require a draft model. Build an n-gram index from the prompt and recent context, propose the next tokens from the longest matching suffix, and let the target model verify the proposal in one forward pass. Accept the longest matching prefix and fall back to normal decoding. This is the `ngram-mod` idea: it costs roughly 16 MB, works best on repetitive code and editing output, and adds no draft-model load or memory pressure. The b\n35\t1044 Med-High/Med Benchmark-Gated Model Promotion :: Vendor numbers are strong but there is no independent CPU-GGUF comparison for the 2026 candidates. Add a `benchmarks/candidates.mjs` registry with candidate repository, file name, SHA-256, license, quantization, and chat template. Run the same suite and the `suiteFingerprint` defined in `IDEA-1037` as the incumbent, compare with the paired-statistics gate, and promote a candidate to the curated catalog only when it improves quality by more than 5 percentage point\n36\t1045 High/Med Message-Level Conversation Search with Filters and Snippets :: The current search is a filter over `state.conversations`, and each summary contains only the title and the last message's first 96 characters. A user who remembers an answer but not the conversation title cannot find it. The chat is local, so the full text is already on disk; the missing piece is a queryable view over it. The mechanism is a deterministic, in-memory message index with a small query language: - **Index.** On load, normalize\n37\t1046 High/Med Conversation Notebooks, Tags, Pins and Trash :: The sidebar is a flat list sorted by `updatedAt`, with a delete button. A user with a hundred chats cannot separate \"client work\", \"learning Rust\" and \"recipes\", cannot pin the one chat they reopen every day, and cannot undo a delete. Joplin's model is simple and proven: one notebook per note, many-to-many tags, a trash that restores to the original notebook, and a history that makes deletion less scary. ModelDuck can adopt the organization without adopt\n38\t1047 High/Med Retention Budget with Archive-Before-Evict :: The 200-conversation cap is the most consequential bug-shaped behavior in the chat store. On `create()`, `this.#conversations.length = MAX_CONVERSATIONS` discards the tail. On `load()`, `.slice(0, MAX_CONVERSATIONS)` discards everything beyond the first 200 records. Neither path warns, writes an archive, or asks the user. The tail is insertion order, not necessarily the least recently used conversation, so the app can drop a chat the user updated yesterday\n39\t1048 Med/Med Conversation Outline and Jump Navigator :: A long chat is a single scrolling document. After forty turns, returning to \"the third question where we changed the schema\" means scrolling and skimming. VS Code solves the same problem for long files with an Outline view, breadcrumbs and Sticky Scroll; Joplin solves it with Goto Anything. ModelDuck can derive an outline from the conversation it already has, without an LLM summary. The outline is deterministic: - Each user message is a top-level node, labelle\n40\t1049 Med-Low/Low Message Bookmarks and Stable Deep Links :: A user cannot point at a specific answer. The conversation id is in memory and the message ids exist in `chats.json`, but there is no bookmark, no copy-link action and no route that opens a conversation at a message. Joplin makes internal references a first-class action: \"Link to note\" inserts a link, and Markdown links connect notes. ModelDuck can do the same for a message without exporting the chat. The mechanism has three parts: 1. **Stable route.** Use\n41\t1050 Med/Low Saved Searches and Smart Views :: Once `IDEA-1045` and `IDEA-1046` exist, a user will type the same useful queries repeatedly: `tag:work after:2026-10-01`, `model:qwen role:assistant \"migration\"`, `notebook:scratch -tag:keep`. Joplin and Thunderbird both let users keep those queries as named views instead of retyping them. ModelDuck can store the query, not the results, and evaluate it live. The mechanism is deliberately small: - `savedSearches: [{id, name, query, icon, pinned, order, createdAt}]` in t\n42\t1051 High/Med Host-Approved Pairing Code and QR Device Linking :: Today, a phone user who wants to open ModelDuck on the LAN must enable the LAN bind, restart, copy a long `mdk_...` key from the desktop, type it into the phone, and hope the key was not pasted into a chat or a screenshot. The key is global, so every device gets the same authority. That is not pairing; it is manual key sharing. The pairing flow has one trust anchor: **the host user approves the device**. Possession of a code is necessary but not suff\n43\t1052 High/Med Device Registry with Scoped, Revocable Tokens :: The current API keys are global capabilities. If a phone is paired for chatting, it can also list models, change server settings, download a 4 GB model or revoke the desktop key, because every key is all-or-nothing. A lost device means rotating the one key every client uses. Jupyter separates authentication (\"who is this?\") from authorization (\"may this identity read this resource?\"), and Syncthing treats a lost device as an identity to revoke. ModelDuc\n44\t1053 High/Med Per-Install TLS with Fingerprint Pinning :: The current LAN mode is plain HTTP. A bearer token, a chat message, or a streamed answer can be read by any device that can observe the local network, and a browser on a coffee-shop Wi-Fi has no way to tell whether it reached the right host. Syncthing's answer is to make the certificate itself the identity: generate a keypair, self-sign a certificate, hash it, and compare that hash to a known value. ModelDuck can use the same idea without a public CA. The me\n45\t1054 Med/Low Network Exposure Doctor :: A single checkbox changes ModelDuck from a loopback app to a network service. The user gets no inventory of what changed: which interfaces are listening, whether authentication is on, whether CORS is open, whether TLS is active, or whether the port is reachable from the guest Wi-Fi. The dangerous combination is easy to create: LAN bind + no auth + CORS `*` lets any web page on the network call the local model. The doctor makes that combination visible before it is saved. `GET\n46\t1055 Med/Med Conversation Handoff to a Paired Device :: A desktop chat is often the wrong screen for the next ten minutes: the user wants to keep reading on a phone while making coffee, or show an answer on a tablet. Copying the URL works only if the phone is already on the same network, authenticated, and the URL includes the message anchor. ModelDuck can make \"continue on this device\" a deliberate, scoped action. The handoff flow: 1. From a conversation or a message, the host clicks **Continue on device**. The ap\n47\t1056 High/Med Hardware-Fit Starter Recommendation :: The catalog is a list, not a recommendation. A new user sees SmolLM2 135M, Qwen 0.5B/1.5B, Llama 3.2 3B, Phi-3.5 3.8B and Mistral 7B with byte sizes, but no statement about which one will feel usable on *this* machine at *this* context. The result is predictable: the biggest model looks like the best model, and the first experience is a memory squeeze or a slow stream. The recommender uses measurements the app already has plus the catalog's declared values: - **I\n48\t1057 Med/Med Verified First-Run Walkthrough :: The README's quick start is good, but it is outside the app. A new user who opens the browser sees an empty chat, a model panel and a settings dialog with dozens of controls. There is no in-app sequence that says \"do this, then this, and here is how you know it worked\". VS Code's walkthroughs solve this with ordered steps, completion events and a dismissible checklist; Home Assistant's onboarding solves it with a short sequence before the main interface. The walkthroug\n49\t1058 High/Med Measured Performance Explainer :: \"Replies are very slow\" is the most common local-model complaint, and the current answer is a README paragraph about memory bandwidth. The app already has the measurements to do better: prompt tokens, time to first token, output tokens per second, context size, thread count, model quant, free RAM, GPU layers, queue wait, power state and cache reuse. The missing piece is a deterministic explanation that names the likely bottleneck and one action. The explainer runs aft\n50\t1059 Med/Med Symptom-to-Fix Troubleshooting Wizard :: When ModelDuck fails, the error is usually accurate but isolated: a native load error, a 507 disk error, a 502 download error, or a stream that stops. A user does not know which of the dozens of settings and environment factors matter. Home Assistant's Repairs turns a detected problem into a short issue with a fix action; Ollama's troubleshooting guide groups failures by symptom. ModelDuck can combine both into a small, read-only wizard. The wizard starts from o\n51\t1060 Med/Low Redacted Support Bundle :: A local-first app has no telemetry, which is good for privacy and bad for support. When a user reports a bug, the maintainer needs versions, OS, CPU/RAM, GPU backend, settings, model metadata, the last error and a few log lines. Asking the user to find and paste all of that is slow and error-prone, and asking for a whole log file can leak prompts or paths. The support bundle is a single JSON file, generated on demand: - `manifest`: app version, build/commit, platform, arch, N\n52\t852 High/Med Content-Addressed Model Manifest with SHA-256 Verification :: Ollama never trusts a filename or an advertised byte count as the identity of a model. A manifest is JSON with a config layer and a list of layers; every layer carries `mediaType`, `digest` and `size`. `NewLayer` hashes the bytes while copying them into a temporary file, names the resulting blob `sha256-<hex>`, and atomically renames it into the blob store. If a blob with that digest already exists, the new copy is discarded and the existing\n53\t853 Med-High/Med Multi-Part Resumable Download with Stall Recovery :: Ollama downloads large blobs as up to 16 ranged parts. `Prepare` reads any existing `-partial-*` part files, rebuilds the completed byte count, and creates new parts only after a `HEAD` request establishes the total size. Each part writes its own JSON progress file after every successful chunk, and `downloadChunk` uses `Range: bytes=start-end` with an `io.NewOffsetWriter`, so a restart can resume every part independently. A stalled part is detect\n54\t854 Med-High/Low Idle Keep-Alive with Bounded Request Queue :: Ollama's scheduler keeps a loaded runner until its `sessionDuration` expires. When a request finishes, the scheduler starts or resets a timer; if another request arrives first, the timer is reset. A request can override the duration with `keep_alive` (zero unloads immediately, a negative value keeps the runner indefinitely). The scheduler also bounds the pending queue (`ErrMaxQueue`) and evicts a runner to make room when the maximum loaded-model count i\n55\t855 Med/Low Capability and Provenance Introspection :: Ollama describes a model from the same data the inference path uses. `describeModelFromManifest` reads the manifest and GGUF metadata, then fills `ModelDetails` (family, parameter size, quantization, context length, embedding length) and `Capabilities` (chat, tools, vision, thinking, embedding). The list endpoint and the show endpoint use the same `Model.Capabilities()` method, so the UI and the API cannot disagree about what a model can do. The capability set "}]},"uuid":"3f3a402e-0049-4e2e-a5f6-dfe5369f190a","timestamp":"2026-10-30T12:40:05.938Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\YUNUS-~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\c--Users-Yunus-Emre-Desktop-portable-agent-kit\\05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787\\scratchpad\\new_modelduck.txt","content":"1000 High/Low Rendered-Prompt Fingerprint for Generation and Benchmarks :: NeuroCore snapshots the leading system messages when an agent loop starts, hashes their contents, re-verifies the hash before every model call, restores the pinned prefix if compaction or truncation changed it, and records an `integrity_violation` flag. The governing prompt is treated as a value that must survive every rewrite, not as display text. ModelDuck's benchmark gate is its moat, but the `testHash` covers only `SUITE_VERSION`, the fi\n1001 High/Med Content-Addressed Prompt and Preset Library with Benchmark-Gat :: NeuroCore stores every skill-document change under its SHA-256 content hash, keeps a bounded per-skill index, and writes a rollback as a new version so the rollback itself is auditable. Promotion is eval-gated: the baseline must exist, name its provenance and be perfect; the candidate must be perfect; a regression blocks promotion. ModelDuck's three presets are constants in `src/config.js`, and the README's claim that they \"only earn tr\n1002 High/Med Blocking Load Plan with Finding Codes :: NeuroCore's simulator never executes a node. It checks structure, effects and a cost envelope and returns `{blocking, findings:[{severity, code, message, node_id, fix}], cost, effects, cycles}`. A single pass that cannot fit is blocking; a cycle that may reach the cap is a warning. The editor and the improvement engine gate on `blocking`, not on a vague score. ModelDuck's loader silently tries `[wanted, min(wanted, 3072), 2048, 1024]` and reports a generic out-\n1003 Med-High/Med Shared Request and JIT-Load Budget :: NeuroCore creates one execution budget per top-level flow run and shares it through a context variable, so nested and bridged runs cannot each spend the full allowance. A flow's metadata may lower a limit but never raise it. Exhaustion is a normal, reported failure with a why/fix; `pressure` is a 0–1 signal for how close the run is to a limit. ModelDuck runs one generation at a time by design. `maxTokens` bounds the output, but nothing bounds prompt processing\n1004 Med/Low Self-Calibrating Latency Percentiles and Stall Findings :: NeuroCore times every node attempt and compares it with its own recent history. Once it has 20 samples, a duration above both an absolute floor and three times the prior p99 is recorded as a stall, logged and counted. Detection never aborts a run, because a slow node may still be doing useful work. ModelDuck shows only the last reply's tokens and timings. A slow reply is hard to attribute to prompt processing, first-token latency, generation, m\n1015 High/Med Untrusted-Content Fence and Tool-Call Loop Guard :: NeuroCore fences untrusted text before it reaches a model: a source label, a fresh random nonce, and neutralized tag-shaped text inside. Tool output and retrieved documents are data, not instructions, and the payload cannot close its own block. Its loop guard normalizes tool arguments by removing only transport identifiers (`request_id`, `trace_id`, `nonce`, timestamps) while keeping domain IDs, warns on the third identical call or an A-B-A-B oscilla\n1016 High/Med SSRF-Pinned Hugging Face Fetch with Content Hash :: NeuroCore resolves a hostname once, checks that every resolved address is public, and then connects to the vetted address rather than resolving the name again. The hostname travels in the `Host` header and as TLS SNI, so certificate verification still works. The research gateway applies the same check to every redirect hop, caps the response size and time, and returns a content hash with the source metadata. ModelDuck's curated downloader follows red\n1017 Med/Low Benchmark Trend and Comparability Report :: NeuroCore keeps a bounded sample history, compares the mean of the last 20 samples with the previous 20, requires a meaningful delta, and emits at most one finding per day with a cooldown and persisted dedupe. Drift compares a live snapshot with a measured baseline and returns a score plus reasons, not a bare number. ModelDuck's gate compares one run with a committed baseline and warns on a speed drop over 20%. CPU inference is noisy; a gradual quality or spe\n1018 Med-Low/Low Exact-Phrase Confirmation for Destructive Routes :: NeuroCore requires the exact confirmation phrase on an irreversible route, in addition to the browser dialog. A browser `confirm()` is a usability guard, not an authorization check: any HTTP client can call the endpoint directly. ModelDuck's model delete and conversation delete rely on the UI dialog. An authenticated LAN client can delete a multi-gigabyte model with one request. A server-side phrase makes the irreversible action explicit for every\n1019 Med-High/Med Migration Importer for Ollama and LM Studio :: The fastest way to lose a user is to make them re-download 20 GB. Ollama stores models as manifests and content-addressed blobs; LM Studio stores GGUF files in a model directory. A migration importer can scan both, identify the model files, and offer to import them into ModelDuck by copy or hardlink, preserving the source installation. The importer should be read-only on the source. It reads Ollama manifests, maps the model layer digest to the blob pa\n1020 Med/Med Conversation Branching and Message Editing :: ModelDuck can send, stop and regenerate the last answer, but it cannot edit an earlier user message, fork a conversation, or keep two answers to the same prompt. Every serious chat client offers this; it is how users explore alternatives without losing the original thread. The current linear `messages[]` store makes a branch impossible without overwriting history. A message tree is the minimal model: each message has an `id`, `parentId`, `role`, `content` a\n1021 Med/Low CLI Subcommand Surface with JSON Output :: LM Studio's `lms` CLI makes the local runtime scriptable: `lms status`, `lms ls --json`, `lms ps`, `lms load`, `lms unload`, `lms log stream`. Ollama's CLI is the ecosystem's muscle memory. ModelDuck's CLI only starts the server; every other action requires the browser UI or a raw HTTP call. A thin CLI over the local API would add `serve`, `pull`, `ls`, `ps`, `load`, `unload`, `show`, `rm`, `run`, `status`, `logs` and `bench`, with `--json` and stable exit cod\n1022 High/Med Deterministic Prompt Trace and Replay :: ModelDuck's stated reason to exist is inspectability, yet the UI never shows the exact prompt, token budget or sampler trace. DeepChat markets \"Tape & Trace\" as a way to inspect request context and token budgets; `llama.cpp` exposes `/apply-template` and `/tokenize` for the same reason. ModelDuck's benchmark already records a seed, test hash and timings, so the data model already exists in spirit. A generation trace would record the model file hash, chat-templa\n1023 Med/Med Local Blind Arena for Model Comparison :: ModelDuck already has a deterministic benchmark gate and a commit-linked history. What it lacks is a human, in-the-loop comparison: run the same prompt on two installed models or two presets, hide the labels, vote, and record the result. Open WebUI ships an arena with ELO; Cherry Studio compares multiple models; LM Studio makes A/B chat easy. A local arena can respect the single-context design by running the two candidates sequentially, not in parallel. The UI \n1024 Med-High/Low Single-Instance Host Contract with Graceful Drain :: ModelDuck's current unit of operation is one foreground Node process. If the user double-clicks the launcher twice, the second process either fails with `EADDRINUSE` or, worse, loads nothing and leaves a confusing error. If the machine reboots, the local API is gone until the user remembers the launcher. If the user closes the console during a stream, the model, the KV cache and the port can be torn down without flushing state. The host contract\n1025 Med-High/Med Supervised Server with Crash-Loop Budget :: A local model server is unattended by nature. A transient crash (a native addon fault, a bad request, a Windows file lock) should not leave the user with a dead browser tab, and a deterministic crash should not become an infinite restart loop that burns CPU and fills the log. `modelduck supervise` becomes a small parent process that spawns `modelduck serve` as a child and classifies each exit: - **Clean exit 0** -> supervisor exits `0`, no restart. - **T\n1026 High/Med Versioned Conversation Archive with Interop Import :: Once ModelDuck stores conversations (`IDEA-857`, `IDEA-869`), the next question is ownership. A local-first app should let the user take every conversation out, read it without ModelDuck, move it to another machine, and bring an existing ChatGPT or LibreChat history in. Today the register has branching (`IDEA-1020`) and model migration (`IDEA-1019`), but no user-data archive. The archive is a **directory bundle**, not a ZIP, so the runtime needs no\n1027 Med-High/Low Storage Ledger and Safe Reclamation :: A local model store grows in four ways that users cannot see: full model files, sharded siblings and projectors, abandoned `.part` downloads, and blob/temp files left by a failed import or upgrade. The current downloader checks the final size and performs an atomic rename, but nothing reports what the store costs, which model was last used, or which bytes are safe to reclaim. `modelduck storage` (and a UI \"Storage\" panel) scans the models root and produces a \n1028 Med-High/Med Spec-First Local API with Generated Client :: ModelDuck already exposes an OpenAI-compatible API, but the contract lives in code and prose. Every client integration, test and UI call re-discovers the shapes by hand, and a route change can break a consumer without any test noticing. `openai-node` shows the stronger pattern: the OpenAPI specification is the source, and the client is generated from it. The proposal is a checked-in OpenAPI 3.1 document (`openapi/modelduck.openapi.json`) that describes\n1029 High/Med One-Click Client Wiring and Connection Doctor :: The most common local-API support question is not \"does it stream?\" but \"what URL and model name do I put in this client?\". ModelDuck's protocol work (`IDEA-994`) makes clients *able* to connect; it does not make them *easy* to connect. Ollama's desktop app goes further: it ships installers and binding code for Claude Desktop and Codex, detects an existing installation, and configures it from the app. A \"Connect\" page lists verified presets with the exa\n1030 Med-High/Med Power-Profile Governor :: ModelDuck currently asks `os.availableParallelism()` for a thread count, reports CPU and memory in `systemInfo()`, and otherwise assumes a desktop on mains power. A 7B Q4 model that is pleasant on AC can drain a laptop, trigger thermal throttling, or make the fan the loudest thing in the room. The user then sees a slow model and no explanation. TLP and auto-cpufreq solve the same class of problem for the whole machine: separate profiles per power source, explicit sensor i\n1031 High/Low Model License Card and Acceptance Ledger :: ModelDuck's catalog says `Apache-2.0`, `MIT`, `Llama 3.2 Community License`, but the UI never shows those strings, the download button never asks for acceptance, and a model imported from disk has no license story at all. That is acceptable for a prototype and wrong for a tool that invites a user to download multi-gigabyte weights and run them for years. The mechanism is a license card plus a local, append-only acceptance ledger, not a legal-advice engine. F\n1032 High/Med Isolated Profiles :: `--home` is an escape hatch, not a feature. To keep work chats away from personal chats today, a user must create a second folder, remember its path, close the first server, and relaunch with a long flag. The single home lock then treats the second folder as an unrelated install, so there is no list, no naming, no safe deletion and no way to see which profile owns which disk usage. Joplin and VS Code both show the better shape: a named profile is a first-class identity, a switcher\n1033 Med/Med Library Collections and Metadata Overlay :: The registry is a scanner, not a library. It lists every GGUF it finds, sorted by name, with derived technical metadata. A user with forty models has no favorites, no \"coding\" collection, no way to hide the broken 7B, no note explaining why a quantization was kept, and no way to find a file after moving it. Calibre's answer is a library database beside the books: the files stay untouched, while collections, tags and per-book metadata live in `metadata.db`. Mo\n1034 Med/Med Metered and Off-Peak Download Policy :: ModelDuck can start a 4.3 GB download the moment a user clicks, with no speed limit and no notion of a metered connection. On a hotel Wi-Fi, a phone hotspot or a capped rural link, that is an expensive surprise. The downloader already has the right primitives - `.part`, `Range`, abort, exact size - but it lacks a policy layer that says *when* and *how fast*. aria2 exposes global and per-download limits plus pause/continue; rclone turns a bandwidth limit into a sc\n1035 Med/Med Full-Library Integrity Audit :: A GGUF file can keep its size and magic while losing a block to bit rot, a bad USB cable, a partial copy or a filesystem repair. ModelDuck would still list it, try to load it, and fail with a confusing native error. The downloader verifies the byte count once; the registry checks the magic on every scan; nothing verifies content after that. Restic's model is the right one: a quick check, a deep read-everything check, a recorded baseline, and a repair path that restores f\n1036 High/Med Accessibility and Localization Baseline :: A local chat studio is a reading and writing tool. If the streamed answer is not announced, if a dialog steals focus and never returns it, if the only way to send is a mouse, or if the interface is English-only, a whole class of users is excluded from a tool that otherwise respects their privacy and their hardware. VS Code treats accessibility as a first-class mode rather than a checklist; Joplin ships translations and documents screen-reader behavior. ModelD\n1037 High/Med Paired-Statistics Benchmark Gate with Per-Item Outcomes :: The benchmark history already stores per-prompt rows in `prompt_results`, each with a manifest digest, plus the run environment; the gate still reduces them to a point score and does not compute paired statistics. Fourteen independent tests at alpha = 0.05 give roughly a 51% chance of at least one false alarm per run, and an aggregate score cannot distinguish a model regression from a parser change, a prompt change, or a handful of unlucky sam\n1038 High/Med Reproducible Run Manifest with Thermal and Baseline Expiry :: A benchmark result is only comparable when the environment is part of the result. The existing `runs` history already records CPU, platform, runtime version, test hash, prompt-fingerprint version, and commit identity. Extend that into a first-class `RunManifest` with OS build, physical and logical cores, RAM, GPU backend, Node version, the bundled llama.cpp build when exposed, model SHA-256, quantization, tokenizer and chat-template hashes,\n1039 High/Med Egress Ledger and Claim Ladder :: ModelDuck can claim that inference is local, but its downloader contacts Hugging Face, and any future catalog refresh or update check would add more egress. Add a process-wide network ledger that records every outbound request: timestamp, method, URL, host, initiator, status, bytes, and purpose. Redact query strings and headers that may contain tokens. Expose the ledger through a loopback-only `GET /api/network-ledger` route and a UI panel. Offline Mode sets a global \n1040 High/Med Extractive Citation Verification with Abstention :: A grounded answer should show its work. After retrieval and generation, split the answer into sentences, resolve each citation marker to a retrieved chunk, and search that chunk for an extractive quote that supports the sentence. Use normalized exact matching first, then a bounded fuzzy match over a quote window; label each sentence `supported`, `weak`, `unsupported`, or `no_citation`. If the support rate falls below a configured threshold, or if no \n1041 Med-High/Med Semantic Validation for Constrained Output :: Grammar-constrained decoding guarantees syntax, not meaning. A model can emit a schema-valid object with an empty required field, an out-of-range number, a start date after an end date, or a tool argument outside the allowlist. Add a deterministic semantic-validation layer after parsing: required fields non-empty, enum and range checks, date and number formats, cross-field consistency, tool allowlist, and no unknown arguments. ModelDuck currently stops\n1042 Med-High/Med Editable Fact-Table Memory with Contradiction Detection :: Keep a per-conversation fact table alongside the raw transcript: `{ id, subject, predicate, value, sourceMessageId, createdAt, updatedAt, confidence }`. Extract facts after a turn or on demand, inject the top-k facts relevant to the current query, and let the user edit or delete any fact. Detect contradictions when the same subject and predicate receive a different value; show both values with their source messages and let the user resolve\n1043 Med-High/High N-gram Self-Speculation for Code and Editing :: Speculation does not require a draft model. Build an n-gram index from the prompt and recent context, propose the next tokens from the longest matching suffix, and let the target model verify the proposal in one forward pass. Accept the longest matching prefix and fall back to normal decoding. This is the `ngram-mod` idea: it costs roughly 16 MB, works best on repetitive code and editing output, and adds no draft-model load or memory pressure. The b\n1044 Med-High/Med Benchmark-Gated Model Promotion :: Vendor numbers are strong but there is no independent CPU-GGUF comparison for the 2026 candidates. Add a `benchmarks/candidates.mjs` registry with candidate repository, file name, SHA-256, license, quantization, and chat template. Run the same suite and the `suiteFingerprint` defined in `IDEA-1037` as the incumbent, compare with the paired-statistics gate, and promote a candidate to the curated catalog only when it improves quality by more than 5 percentage point\n1045 High/Med Message-Level Conversation Search with Filters and Snippets :: The current search is a filter over `state.conversations`, and each summary contains only the title and the last message's first 96 characters. A user who remembers an answer but not the conversation title cannot find it. The chat is local, so the full text is already on disk; the missing piece is a queryable view over it. The mechanism is a deterministic, in-memory message index with a small query language: - **Index.** On load, normalize\n1046 High/Med Conversation Notebooks, Tags, Pins and Trash :: The sidebar is a flat list sorted by `updatedAt`, with a delete button. A user with a hundred chats cannot separate \"client work\", \"learning Rust\" and \"recipes\", cannot pin the one chat they reopen every day, and cannot undo a delete. Joplin's model is simple and proven: one notebook per note, many-to-many tags, a trash that restores to the original notebook, and a history that makes deletion less scary. ModelDuck can adopt the organization without adopt\n1047 High/Med Retention Budget with Archive-Before-Evict :: The 200-conversation cap is the most consequential bug-shaped behavior in the chat store. On `create()`, `this.#conversations.length = MAX_CONVERSATIONS` discards the tail. On `load()`, `.slice(0, MAX_CONVERSATIONS)` discards everything beyond the first 200 records. Neither path warns, writes an archive, or asks the user. The tail is insertion order, not necessarily the least recently used conversation, so the app can drop a chat the user updated yesterday\n1048 Med/Med Conversation Outline and Jump Navigator :: A long chat is a single scrolling document. After forty turns, returning to \"the third question where we changed the schema\" means scrolling and skimming. VS Code solves the same problem for long files with an Outline view, breadcrumbs and Sticky Scroll; Joplin solves it with Goto Anything. ModelDuck can derive an outline from the conversation it already has, without an LLM summary. The outline is deterministic: - Each user message is a top-level node, labelle\n1049 Med-Low/Low Message Bookmarks and Stable Deep Links :: A user cannot point at a specific answer. The conversation id is in memory and the message ids exist in `chats.json`, but there is no bookmark, no copy-link action and no route that opens a conversation at a message. Joplin makes internal references a first-class action: \"Link to note\" inserts a link, and Markdown links connect notes. ModelDuck can do the same for a message without exporting the chat. The mechanism has three parts: 1. **Stable route.** Use\n1050 Med/Low Saved Searches and Smart Views :: Once `IDEA-1045` and `IDEA-1046` exist, a user will type the same useful queries repeatedly: `tag:work after:2026-10-01`, `model:qwen role:assistant \"migration\"`, `notebook:scratch -tag:keep`. Joplin and Thunderbird both let users keep those queries as named views instead of retyping them. ModelDuck can store the query, not the results, and evaluate it live. The mechanism is deliberately small: - `savedSearches: [{id, name, query, icon, pinned, order, createdAt}]` in t\n1051 High/Med Host-Approved Pairing Code and QR Device Linking :: Today, a phone user who wants to open ModelDuck on the LAN must enable the LAN bind, restart, copy a long `mdk_...` key from the desktop, type it into the phone, and hope the key was not pasted into a chat or a screenshot. The key is global, so every device gets the same authority. That is not pairing; it is manual key sharing. The pairing flow has one trust anchor: **the host user approves the device**. Possession of a code is necessary but not suff\n1052 High/Med Device Registry with Scoped, Revocable Tokens :: The current API keys are global capabilities. If a phone is paired for chatting, it can also list models, change server settings, download a 4 GB model or revoke the desktop key, because every key is all-or-nothing. A lost device means rotating the one key every client uses. Jupyter separates authentication (\"who is this?\") from authorization (\"may this identity read this resource?\"), and Syncthing treats a lost device as an identity to revoke. ModelDuc\n1053 High/Med Per-Install TLS with Fingerprint Pinning :: The current LAN mode is plain HTTP. A bearer token, a chat message, or a streamed answer can be read by any device that can observe the local network, and a browser on a coffee-shop Wi-Fi has no way to tell whether it reached the right host. Syncthing's answer is to make the certificate itself the identity: generate a keypair, self-sign a certificate, hash it, and compare that hash to a known value. ModelDuck can use the same idea without a public CA. The me\n1054 Med/Low Network Exposure Doctor :: A single checkbox changes ModelDuck from a loopback app to a network service. The user gets no inventory of what changed: which interfaces are listening, whether authentication is on, whether CORS is open, whether TLS is active, or whether the port is reachable from the guest Wi-Fi. The dangerous combination is easy to create: LAN bind + no auth + CORS `*` lets any web page on the network call the local model. The doctor makes that combination visible before it is saved. `GET\n1055 Med/Med Conversation Handoff to a Paired Device :: A desktop chat is often the wrong screen for the next ten minutes: the user wants to keep reading on a phone while making coffee, or show an answer on a tablet. Copying the URL works only if the phone is already on the same network, authenticated, and the URL includes the message anchor. ModelDuck can make \"continue on this device\" a deliberate, scoped action. The handoff flow: 1. From a conversation or a message, the host clicks **Continue on device**. The ap\n1056 High/Med Hardware-Fit Starter Recommendation :: The catalog is a list, not a recommendation. A new user sees SmolLM2 135M, Qwen 0.5B/1.5B, Llama 3.2 3B, Phi-3.5 3.8B and Mistral 7B with byte sizes, but no statement about which one will feel usable on *this* machine at *this* context. The result is predictable: the biggest model looks like the best model, and the first experience is a memory squeeze or a slow stream. The recommender uses measurements the app already has plus the catalog's declared values: - **I\n1057 Med/Med Verified First-Run Walkthrough :: The README's quick start is good, but it is outside the app. A new user who opens the browser sees an empty chat, a model panel and a settings dialog with dozens of controls. There is no in-app sequence that says \"do this, then this, and here is how you know it worked\". VS Code's walkthroughs solve this with ordered steps, completion events and a dismissible checklist; Home Assistant's onboarding solves it with a short sequence before the main interface. The walkthroug\n1058 High/Med Measured Performance Explainer :: \"Replies are very slow\" is the most common local-model complaint, and the current answer is a README paragraph about memory bandwidth. The app already has the measurements to do better: prompt tokens, time to first token, output tokens per second, context size, thread count, model quant, free RAM, GPU layers, queue wait, power state and cache reuse. The missing piece is a deterministic explanation that names the likely bottleneck and one action. The explainer runs aft\n1059 Med/Med Symptom-to-Fix Troubleshooting Wizard :: When ModelDuck fails, the error is usually accurate but isolated: a native load error, a 507 disk error, a 502 download error, or a stream that stops. A user does not know which of the dozens of settings and environment factors matter. Home Assistant's Repairs turns a detected problem into a short issue with a fix action; Ollama's troubleshooting guide groups failures by symptom. ModelDuck can combine both into a small, read-only wizard. The wizard starts from o\n1060 Med/Low Redacted Support Bundle :: A local-first app has no telemetry, which is good for privacy and bad for support. When a user reports a bug, the maintainer needs versions, OS, CPU/RAM, GPU backend, settings, model metadata, the last error and a few log lines. Asking the user to find and paste all of that is slow and error-prone, and asking for a whole log file can leak prompts or paths. The support bundle is a single JSON file, generated on demand: - `manifest`: app version, build/commit, platform, arch, N\n852 High/Med Content-Addressed Model Manifest with SHA-256 Verification :: Ollama never trusts a filename or an advertised byte count as the identity of a model. A manifest is JSON with a config layer and a list of layers; every layer carries `mediaType`, `digest` and `size`. `NewLayer` hashes the bytes while copying them into a temporary file, names the resulting blob `sha256-<hex>`, and atomically renames it into the blob store. If a blob with that digest already exists, the new copy is discarded and the existing\n853 Med-High/Med Multi-Part Resumable Download with Stall Recovery :: Ollama downloads large blobs as up to 16 ranged parts. `Prepare` reads any existing `-partial-*` part files, rebuilds the completed byte count, and creates new parts only after a `HEAD` request establishes the total size. Each part writes its own JSON progress file after every successful chunk, and `downloadChunk` uses `Range: bytes=start-end` with an `io.NewOffsetWriter`, so a restart can resume every part independently. A stalled part is detect\n854 Med-High/Low Idle Keep-Alive with Bounded Request Queue :: Ollama's scheduler keeps a loaded runner until its `sessionDuration` expires. When a request finishes, the scheduler starts or resets a timer; if another request arrives first, the timer is reset. A request can override the duration with `keep_alive` (zero unloads immediately, a negative value keeps the runner indefinitely). The scheduler also bounds the pending queue (`ErrMaxQueue`) and evicts a runner to make room when the maximum loaded-model count i\n855 Med/Low Capability and Provenance Introspection :: Ollama describes a model from the same data the inference path uses. `describeModelFromManifest` reads the manifest and GGUF metadata, then fills `ModelDetails` (family, parameter size, quantization, context length, embedding length) and `Capabilities` (chat, tools, vision, thinking, embedding). The list endpoint and the show endpoint use the same `Model.Capabilities()` method, so the UI and the API cannot disagree about what a model can do. The capability set ","numLines":55,"startLine":1,"totalLines":106}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1437c78f-b65d-45e4-b13d-8fe7f85acf73","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main"}
