:"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"4d07f0a8-a72d-4480-89a4-10a7ba29ffb5","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4ce50c82-a8ed-4889-ac0c-4fb42e7bef08","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V5mUFaCX71uufEWQg8wZXv","type":"tool_result","content":"56\t856 Med/Low Model Alias and Tag Resolution :: Ollama gives every model a structured name: optional host, optional namespace, model, tag (default `latest`), and an optional digest. `ParseName` normalizes a user string into that structure; `IsValid` rejects unsafe parts; `DisplayShortest` chooses the shortest unambiguous form for display. The name resolves to a manifest, and a manifest list can select a child by runner or digest. The HTTP layer parses the requested name once and returns a structured error when it is \n57\t857 High/Low Wrapper-Owned Chat History with System Prompt Seeding :: `LlamaChatSession` resolves a chat wrapper from the model. With the default `chatWrapper: \"auto\"`, `resolveChatWrapper(model)` reads the GGUF architecture and the tokenizer's `chat_template` and returns a specialized or Jinja wrapper. The constructor then calls `chatWrapper.generateInitialChatHistory({systemPrompt})`, which places the system prompt in the exact segment format that model expects. `setChatHistory()` replaces the entire history, and\n58\t858 High/Med Token-Metered Context Window with Context Shift :: node-llama-cpp does not trim chat history by character count. `compressHistoryToFitContextSize` asks the chat wrapper to render the full context, tokenizes that rendered text with the model's tokenizer, and compares the token count with `contextSize - contextShiftSize`. If it does not fit, it runs a context-shift strategy. The default `eraseFirstResponseAndKeepFirstSystem` removes the oldest model response while keeping the first system message, then r\n59\t859 Med-High/Med JSON-Schema Constrained Decoding :: `llama.createGrammarForJsonSchema(schema)` compiles a JSON schema into a GBNF grammar. Passing that grammar to `session.promptWithMeta(..., {grammar})` constrains the sampler so the model can only emit tokens that keep the output valid against the schema. `LlamaJsonSchemaGrammar.parse(json)` then validates the result against the original schema. This is generation-level enforcement, not a post-hoc \"please return JSON\" prompt. ModelDuck's OpenAI layer passes only \n60\t860 Med-High/High Native Function Calling with a Local Allowlist :: `defineChatSessionFunction({description, params, handler})` declares a function with a JSON-schema parameter shape and a handler. Passing `functions` to `session.promptWithMeta` makes the chat wrapper document the functions to the model, constrain the function name and parameter generation with grammars, execute the handler, append the result to the history, and continue the loop. The response exposes `functionCalls` and `metadata.stopReason === \"f\n61\t861 Med/Med Embeddings Endpoint and Local Semantic Search :: `model.createEmbeddingContext({contextSize, batchSize})` creates a context dedicated to embeddings. `getEmbeddingFor(text)` returns a `LlamaEmbedding` with a frozen vector, and `calculateCosineSimilarity(other)` compares two vectors. The model exposes `embeddingVectorSize` and `fileInfo.architectureMetadata.embedding_length`. This is a separate context from the chat context, with its own memory and batch settings. ModelDuck is chat-only. Its conversation \n62\t862 High/Med Content-Addressed Model Gallery with Mirrors and OCI :: A gallery entry is more than a URL and a byte size: it can carry a digest, alternative mirrors, and an OCI reference. The installer downloads from the best mirror, verifies the digest, and can verify a signature before accepting the artifact. ModelDuck's catalog verifies only the final byte size. A truncated file with the right length, a swapped file, or a malicious mirror would be accepted. A content-addressed entry (`sha256`, size, mirrors, opti\n63\t863 High/High Backend/Engine Manager with Install, Upgrade and Delete :: LocalAI treats a backend as an installable component with a version, a digest, a library entry and a health check. It can install, upgrade, delete, list, and route a model to a compatible backend. A backend can be pinned to a release; an upgrade is explicit. ModelDuck depends on one native `node-llama-cpp` version. When the dependency is missing or incompatible, the app fails at import. A small backend manager would let ModelDuck detect the in\n64\t864 Med/Med Durable Install Jobs with Status Polling :: A model/backend install is an asynchronous job with an ID and a status that can be polled. The UI does not hold a single long request; it starts a job, polls progress, and can reconnect after a reload. ModelDuck's `DownloadManager` keeps jobs in memory. A server restart loses the job list, and the UI cannot tell whether a `.part` is still valid. A durable job record (model ID, received bytes, status, error, updated time) lets the UI resume after a restart and \n65\t865 High/Med Global Admission Control and Bounded Queue :: A global admission controller bounds how many backend requests run at once. A request either acquires a slot or gets a typed busy error; the in-flight count is observable. This prevents a burst from exhausting memory or CPU. ModelDuck currently returns 409 \"already answering\" when a second request arrives. That is a hard rejection, not a queue. On a CPU, running two generations is bad, but a bounded queue with a position/estimate is friendlier: the second r\n66\t866 Med/Med Request Tracing, PII Redaction and Token Metrics :: A local server still needs observability: request ID, route, model, tokens, duration, outcome, and a redacted error. LocalAI traces backend calls and redacts PII in request/response logs. Token metrics expose usage without logging content. ModelDuck logs only a warning on failure. A local debug log with request ID, duration, token counts, and a redacted prompt/response would make performance and failures diagnosable without leaking chat content. The UI\n67\t867 High/Med Pluggable AI Engine Registry :: An engine is registered by provider name and retrieved by the model/provider. Local and remote engines share a chat-completion request shape (messages, tools, sampling, stream), so the UI does not care which engine runs. The engine manager is a small map, not a framework. ModelDuck hard-codes `node-llama-cpp` and `LlamaChatSession`. A registry would let it add a GPU/MLX engine, a remote OpenAI-compatible backend, or a fake engine for tests without changing the server/UI.\n68\t868 Med/Med Download Provider Extension with Catalog and Progress :: Jan's download extension is a provider around a model catalog: list models, start a download, report progress, and hand the installed file to the engine extension. The engine extension owns model presets/readiness/settings; the download extension owns acquisition. ModelDuck has a single catalog + downloader module. Separating **catalog provider** from **engine** would let it add a Hugging Face search provider, a local file import, or a mirror with\n69\t869 Med/Med Assistant, Thread and Message Entities :: Jan separates an **assistant** (instructions/model/settings), a **thread** (conversation), and **messages** (events). Each has an entity, an event, and an interface, so the UI and storage share a typed model. A message can be updated/streamed via events. ModelDuck's `ChatStore` has conversations with messages, but no assistant/thread separation and no event model. A thread/assistant model would let a user keep multiple conversations per assistant, change setting\n70\t870 High/High Local RAG with a Vector DB Extension :: Jan's RAG extension attaches documents to a thread, chunks them, embeds them through an embedding engine, stores vectors in a vector DB extension, and exposes retrieval as a tool. The UI can cite the retrieved chunks. ModelDuck is a chat studio, not a RAG app. A local RAG path would let a user attach a PDF/text file and ask questions about it, all offline. The minimal version needs: local file ingestion, chunking, an embedding engine, a vector store, and a retri\n71\t871 Med-High/High MCP Tool Extension with Tool Calls :: Jan models an MCP server as an extension with tools; the engine request carries `tools`, `tool_choice`, and `tool_calls`; the assistant can call a tool and feed the result back. MCP gives a standard way to add local tools (filesystem, search, databases) without hard-coding each one. ModelDuck has no tool calling. A minimal MCP client would let the model call a local tool, show the call in the chat, and continue with the result. This is a large feature; it need\n72\t872 High/Med Slot-Based Request Queue with Fair Cancellation :: Requests are tasks in a queue, not direct calls into the model. A worker schedules tasks onto context slots; a task can be deferred, cancelled, or cleaned up. The response reader waits for a set of tasks and supports stop. This decouples HTTP concurrency from inference concurrency. ModelDuck rejects a second chat with 409 and has no queue. A bounded queue with a task ID, slot assignment, cancellation and fair ordering would let the UI/API wait instead \n73\t873 High/Med Prompt Cache and Common-Prefix Reuse :: The server keeps a prompt cache per slot: a new request that shares a prefix with the previous prompt reuses the evaluated KV state instead of re-evaluating the whole prompt. This is a large latency win for a fixed system prompt and a long chat history. ModelDuck recreates a `LlamaChatSession` per conversation and truncates history by characters. It does not exploit a common prefix across requests. A prefix cache keyed by `(model, system prompt, initial messages)\n74\t874 High/Med JSON-Schema and Grammar-Constrained Output :: The server accepts a JSON schema/grammar and constrains decoding so the output is syntactically valid. This is how local models produce reliable JSON for tool calls, extraction or structured chat. ModelDuck's OpenAI endpoint ignores `response_format`/JSON schema. A local app that can return valid JSON is much more useful for automation. `node-llama-cpp` exposes `LlamaJsonSchemaGrammar`, so the wrapper can support `response_format: {type:\"json_object\"|\"json_\n75\t875 High/High Multi-Model Scheduler with Memory-Aware Load/Unload :: The server can keep multiple models loaded, track memory per model, and load/unload based on memory pressure. A request for a model that is not loaded may evict another model; a model that does not fit is refused. ModelDuck loads exactly one model and keeps it until the user swaps. On a laptop, a user may switch between a small chat model and a code model; reloading from disk each time is slow, but keeping both may OOM. A memory-aware LRU (keep on\n76\t876 High/Med Resumable SSE Stream Sessions with Cancel :: A stream session stores the generated chunks so a reconnecting client can read from an offset, not just from the live socket. It is bounded (max bytes), finalized, cancellable, and garbage-collected. This is how a UI can survive a reload or a flaky connection. ModelDuck streams SSE directly to the response. If the tab reloads, the partial answer is gone. A stream session keyed by conversation/request with an offset lets the UI reconnect and replay, and a can\n77\t882 High/Low Process-Unique Incomplete Downloads with Atomic Promotion :: Hugging Face Hub does not let two processes append to the same partial file. Each download writes to a unique temporary path, checks disk space for both the temporary and destination directories, and only then renames the complete file into place. If a lock is broken, the worst case is duplicated bandwidth, not a corrupted cache. The comment in the source is explicit: a shared `.incomplete` file corrupts the cache whenever the surrounding loc\n78\t883 High/Med Content-Addressed Model Blob Store with Revision Pinning :: Hugging Face Hub stores file content once, under a blob name derived from the ETag, and creates snapshot paths that point at the blob for a specific commit. Two revisions that share a file share the blob; a new revision adds a new snapshot directory without duplicating unchanged files. The revision is pinned at download time, so a later branch move does not silently change what a snapshot contains. ModelDuck currently stores models by catalog \n79\t884 High/Low Offline Mode with Local-First Resolution and Typed Cache Miss :: When offline mode is enabled, the Hub client resolves files only from the local cache. If a file is missing, it raises `LocalEntryNotFoundError`, a typed error that says \"this may exist on the Hub, but it is not on disk and the network is disabled\". It does not hang on a request or pretend the file is absent remotely. ModelDuck is a local-first app, but its catalog download path assumes the network is available. A user on a plane, a locke\n80\t885 Med-High/Med Selective Artifact Download with Allow/Ignore Patterns and Bou :: A Hub snapshot can contain many files: multiple quantizations, tokenizers, configs, READMEs and images. `snapshot_download` lets the caller select only the files it needs with allow/ignore patterns, caps concurrent workers, and can mirror the result into a local directory. The selection is declared before the transfer, so the client does not download a 40 GB repo to get one 4 GB GGUF. ModelDuck's catalog is a curated single-file list\n81\t886 High/Low ETag/Commit-Bound Integrity Verification with Mismatch Retry :: Hub downloads are bound to metadata resolved before the transfer: the ETag identifies the file version, the commit hash identifies the repository snapshot, and the expected size catches truncation. The downloader verifies the received size and raises a consistency error that tells the user to retry with `force_download=True`. The metadata call itself is retried separately from the byte transfer. ModelDuck's catalog pins an exact byte size,\n82\t899 Med-High/Med Public Benchmark Tracks with Pinned Slices :: Add a second, opt-in benchmark tier (`npm run bench:public`) that runs small, pinned slices of recognized benchmarks through the same `LlamaEngine` the UI uses: | Track | Source / metric | Slice | Runtime signal | |---|---|---|---| | `ifeval` | Verifiable instruction checkers; prompt-level strict accuracy | 30 prompts across the 25 instruction types | Instruction following | | `gsm8k` | Final-number exact match | 25 problems, fixed order | Arithmetic re\n83\t903 High/Med Quantization-Aware Memory Fit Preflight :: Compute tensor bytes, KV-cache bytes for the configured context, and runtime overhead from GGUF metadata; block a load that exceeds a configurable budget and suggest a smaller quant or context. CPU-first users hit opaque OOM or swap; a preflight turns a crash into an actionable choice.\n84\t904 High/Med Speculative Decoding with a Tiny Draft Model :: Pair a target GGUF with a tiny draft model of the same tokenizer; accept a draft token when the target agrees, otherwise fall back; expose the acceptance rate and disable automatically when it is low. CPU decoding is memory-bandwidth-bound; a draft model can improve tokens/s for some pairs.\n85\t905 Med-High/High Paged KV Cache with Prefix Sharing :: Allocate the KV cache in fixed blocks, share common-prefix blocks across chats, and copy on write; cap total blocks and evict least-recently-used prefixes. Long chats and repeated system prompts waste memory in a contiguous cache.\n86\t906 Med-High/High Continuous Batching for Local Multi-User Serving :: Admit multiple chat requests into one generation loop, add/remove sequences per token step, and bound batch size by memory; keep a fair queue. The local API may serve several tools or family members; request-level serialisation wastes CPU.\n87\t907 Med-High/Med Quality-Aware Quantization Escalation :: Start with the smallest quant that fits the budget; run a small quality probe; escalate to a larger quant only when the probe fails and memory allows; record the chosen quant and probe result. Users should not have to guess between Q2, Q4 and Q8.\n88\t923 High/Med Attention-Sink Streaming for Unbounded Chat :: StreamingLLM observes that transformers devote a large attention score to the first few tokens even when they are not semantically important. Keeping those \"sink\" tokens plus a rolling recent window lets a model trained on a finite window run on very long conversations without fine-tuning. H2O adds a complementary observation: a small set of heavy-hitter tokens carries most of the attention value, so an eviction policy should retain both recent and high-at\n89\t924 High/Low Per-Launch Capability Token for the Local API :: ModelDuck already validates the Host header and Origin, which blocks the classic DNS-rebinding path where an attacker hostname resolves to 127.0.0.1. But any local process can still call the OpenAI-compatible API, and a browser page served from a loopback origin could act with ambient authority if the checks are ever weakened. The confused-deputy literature says the service should require an explicit capability, not rely on the caller's location. A per-l\n90\t925 High/Med Chunk-Level Merkle Verification for Resumable Downloads :: IPFS stores a file as content-addressed blocks linked by a Merkle DAG. Each block can be verified independently, and a root hash commits to the whole file. A resumable download can therefore verify every chunk as it arrives, resume from the last verified chunk, and detect corruption before the file is promoted. ModelDuck currently resumes from a `.part` file and checks the final size. A same-size corruption or a bad range response can survive u\n91\t926 Med-High/Med Warm-Cache Model Loading with Prefetch Hints :: ServerlessLLM treats model loading as the dominant cold-start cost. It keeps checkpoints on local storage, uses a loading-optimized format, exploits the storage hierarchy, and schedules a model to the server where its checkpoint is already local. The result is 10–200× lower latency across workloads. ModelDuck loads a GGUF from disk on every model switch. A warm-cache loader would keep the file in the OS page cache, issue prefetch hints before `loadMod\n92\t927 Med-High/Low CPU Thread Topology and NUMA-Aware Thread Budget :: The CPU inference literature shows that CPU deployment is constrained by memory capacity and bandwidth, and that a specialized runtime with optimized kernels and INT4 weights is needed to make it practical. On a multi-socket or hybrid-core machine, the thread count alone is not enough: thread placement, NUMA locality and avoiding oversubscription matter as much as the number of threads. ModelDuck exposes a raw `threads` setting and clamps it to 1–\n93\t938 High/Med TUF-Style Signed Model Catalog with Rollback Protection :: TUF separates update metadata into roles (root, targets, snapshot, timestamp), each with its own keys and thresholds. A compromised target key cannot rewrite the root; a compromised root can be rotated with an overlap window; snapshot and timestamp metadata prevent an attacker from serving an old but valid catalog (rollback) or withholding updates (freeze). The design survives key compromise rather than assuming a single trusted signer. ModelDu\n94\t939 High/Med Hierarchical Conversation Summarization with Token Budget :: Recursive summarization compresses a dialogue in stages: summarize a small window, then combine that summary with the next window to produce a new memory. The chatbot conditions on the latest memory instead of the full transcript, which keeps a bounded token budget while preserving long-range consistency. The paper shows the method complements both long-context and retrieval-augmented models. ModelDuck stores the full chat history and sends a\n95\t940 High/Med KV Cache Quantization with Per-Channel Keys :: KIVI's central finding is that key and value caches have different distributions: keys should be quantized per channel, values per token. A 2-bit cache with this asymmetric scheme preserves quality while cutting KV memory by more than half, enabling larger batches or longer contexts. KVQuant extends the idea to very long contexts with outlier-aware quantization. ModelDuck's KV cache is stored at the model's native precision. On a CPU with limited RAM, the \n96\t941 Med-High/High Tree-Based Speculative Decoding with Medusa Heads :: Medusa avoids the separate draft model that makes speculative decoding operationally heavy. It attaches several lightweight decoding heads to the target model, predicts multiple future tokens, builds a tree of candidate continuations, and verifies the tree in one forward pass with a tree-attention mask. The result is lossless acceleration with a single model. ModelDuck already has an idea for draft-model speculation. Medusa is a different trade-\n97\t942 Med-High/Med Tool-Call Schema Validation with Error-Feedback Retry :: Gorilla shows that models often hallucinate API arguments and usage; retrieval over current documentation and a correctness-focused evaluation materially improve API-call accuracy. ToolLLM scales the problem to 16K+ APIs and uses a search-based decision tree plus an automatic evaluator. The common lesson is that a tool call should be validated against a schema before it is returned or executed, and the model should see a structured error when\n98\t953 Med/Med Draft-Model Pairing with Acceptance-Rate Autotune :: ModelDuck scans the local GGUF inventory, proposes same-tokenizer draft candidates by vocab hash and size ratio, and benchmarks validated/refuted tokens plus net tokens per second on the actual CPU. It tunes draft length and minimum confidence, persists the winning pairing per target model, and auto-disables speculation when break-even is not met. Acceptance statistics surface in response metadata and the UI. CPU decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, so a\n99\t954 Med-High/Med KV-Cache Budget Contract with Quantized-Cache Presets :: Before loading a GGUF, ModelDuck computes KV bytes per token from GGUF metadata (layers, KV heads, head dimension) for candidate context sizes and cache types. It rejects configurations above a declared RAM ceiling with a typed error and the smallest feasible preset, then exposes the chosen budget in /v1/models and response headers. Quantized K/V presets are opt-in, smoke-tested, and fall back to f16 on failure. CPU users hit OOM or silently \n100\t967 High/Med Hostile-GGUF Admission Control :: Add a dependency-free `src/gguf-guard.js` that validates the header before `readGgufFileInfo()` or `loadModel()`. Read through a fixed 64 KiB window, never allocate from declared lengths, and enforce: version 2 or 3; `tensor_count` and `metadata_kv_count` bounded by remaining file size and configurable caps; string/key/name limits; array/nesting caps; `n_dimensions <= 4`; positive dimensions; alignment a nonzero multiple of 8 and `<= 1 MiB`; every tensor offset aligned\n101\t994 High/Med Multi-Protocol Compatibility Surface :: The ecosystem has settled on a small protocol set. Ollama's API is the de-facto local standard; `llama.cpp` serves OpenAI chat/completions, OpenAI Responses, embeddings, rerank and Anthropic Messages; LocalAI advertises OpenAI, Anthropic and Ollama compatibility; KoboldCpp exposes Ollama-compatible endpoints alongside OpenAI. A client written for any of these can use a server that implements the same shapes, with no ModelDuck-specific adapter. ModelDuck currently\n102\t995 High/Med Hugging Face URI Resolver for Arbitrary GGUF :: Ollama made any Hugging Face GGUF one command away: `ollama run hf.co/user/repo:Q4_K_M`. The Hugging Face guide documents quant tags, full filenames and private repositories. `llama.cpp` has the same idea with `-hf`. ModelDuck's catalogue is excellent for a first run, but it is a closed list; a user who wants a newer model must download it manually and import the file. A resolver would parse `hf.co/user/repo[:quant]`, query the Hugging Face model API for \n103\t996 Med-High/Med Sharded GGUF and Projector Grouping :: Larger GGUF models ship as multiple shards, and vision models ship a separate projector. The `llama.cpp` router expects a directory containing either a single file, a `*-00001-of-0000N.gguf` set, or a directory with `mmproj-*.gguf` beside the model. ModelDuck scans every `.gguf` as an independent model and reads metadata with `spliceSplitFiles: false`, so a sharded model appears as several partial files and a projector appears as a nonsense model. A bundle lay\n104\t997 Med-High/Med Native Thinking Segment Pipeline :: ModelDuck's `reasoning` setting is a prompt instruction, not model-native reasoning. It appends \"Think through the problem carefully\" to the system prompt. Qwen3-style models and other recent families expose a real thinking mode through the chat template (`enable_thinking`, `reasoning_effort`, or a thinking token), and `node-llama-cpp` can stream `thought` segments separately from the final answer. Ollama already exposes `think` as a boolean or a level and return\n105\t998 Med-High/Med Rerank Endpoint for Local Retrieval :: Reranking is Ollama's most-upvoted open feature request. `llama.cpp` exposes a rerank endpoint that takes a query and documents and returns relevance scores; `node-llama-cpp` exposes a `LlamaRankingContext` with `rankAll()` and long-document chunking. A reranker is small, fast and CPU-friendly, and it materially improves retrieval quality over cosine similarity alone. ModelDuck has no embeddings server and no reranker. IDEA-861 covers embeddings, and IDEA-870 \n106\t999 High/High Portable Mode and Single-Folder Distribution :: KoboldCpp ships one executable; llamafile ships one file containing the runtime and model; Foundry Local ships a ~20 MB runtime. ModelDuck still needs Node, `npm install`, a native addon and a default state folder in the user's home. The home-directory complaint is one of Ollama's most upvoted issues, and it is exactly the kind of friction a portable app can remove. The first phase is low risk: a `--portable` flag that resolves `home` and `models` next t"}]},"uuid":"34f82e7c-1894-4e5a-8dba-7850a11b13ba","timestamp":"2026-10-30T12:40:13.016Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\YUNUS-~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\c--Users-Yunus-Emre-Desktop-portable-agent-kit\\05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787\\scratchpad\\new_modelduck.txt","content":"856 Med/Low Model Alias and Tag Resolution :: Ollama gives every model a structured name: optional host, optional namespace, model, tag (default `latest`), and an optional digest. `ParseName` normalizes a user string into that structure; `IsValid` rejects unsafe parts; `DisplayShortest` chooses the shortest unambiguous form for display. The name resolves to a manifest, and a manifest list can select a child by runner or digest. The HTTP layer parses the requested name once and returns a structured error when it is \n857 High/Low Wrapper-Owned Chat History with System Prompt Seeding :: `LlamaChatSession` resolves a chat wrapper from the model. With the default `chatWrapper: \"auto\"`, `resolveChatWrapper(model)` reads the GGUF architecture and the tokenizer's `chat_template` and returns a specialized or Jinja wrapper. The constructor then calls `chatWrapper.generateInitialChatHistory({systemPrompt})`, which places the system prompt in the exact segment format that model expects. `setChatHistory()` replaces the entire history, and\n858 High/Med Token-Metered Context Window with Context Shift :: node-llama-cpp does not trim chat history by character count. `compressHistoryToFitContextSize` asks the chat wrapper to render the full context, tokenizes that rendered text with the model's tokenizer, and compares the token count with `contextSize - contextShiftSize`. If it does not fit, it runs a context-shift strategy. The default `eraseFirstResponseAndKeepFirstSystem` removes the oldest model response while keeping the first system message, then r\n859 Med-High/Med JSON-Schema Constrained Decoding :: `llama.createGrammarForJsonSchema(schema)` compiles a JSON schema into a GBNF grammar. Passing that grammar to `session.promptWithMeta(..., {grammar})` constrains the sampler so the model can only emit tokens that keep the output valid against the schema. `LlamaJsonSchemaGrammar.parse(json)` then validates the result against the original schema. This is generation-level enforcement, not a post-hoc \"please return JSON\" prompt. ModelDuck's OpenAI layer passes only \n860 Med-High/High Native Function Calling with a Local Allowlist :: `defineChatSessionFunction({description, params, handler})` declares a function with a JSON-schema parameter shape and a handler. Passing `functions` to `session.promptWithMeta` makes the chat wrapper document the functions to the model, constrain the function name and parameter generation with grammars, execute the handler, append the result to the history, and continue the loop. The response exposes `functionCalls` and `metadata.stopReason === \"f\n861 Med/Med Embeddings Endpoint and Local Semantic Search :: `model.createEmbeddingContext({contextSize, batchSize})` creates a context dedicated to embeddings. `getEmbeddingFor(text)` returns a `LlamaEmbedding` with a frozen vector, and `calculateCosineSimilarity(other)` compares two vectors. The model exposes `embeddingVectorSize` and `fileInfo.architectureMetadata.embedding_length`. This is a separate context from the chat context, with its own memory and batch settings. ModelDuck is chat-only. Its conversation \n862 High/Med Content-Addressed Model Gallery with Mirrors and OCI :: A gallery entry is more than a URL and a byte size: it can carry a digest, alternative mirrors, and an OCI reference. The installer downloads from the best mirror, verifies the digest, and can verify a signature before accepting the artifact. ModelDuck's catalog verifies only the final byte size. A truncated file with the right length, a swapped file, or a malicious mirror would