{"parentUuid":"ba13bf63-84f8-4177-ac7f-507de82da513","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYZXg3mjXD6gJcqSTrKt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four approved modelduck ideas are still unfixed at the latest commit (`1c9876d`). For 1054, the code check found it's worse than I recorded: it calls for a stronger fix than the one I approved. Nothing has been changed or committed. The modelduck ledger is clean, but another agent committed there earlier today.\n\n## 1054 (P1, security): another website can control ModelDuck\n\n**What I recorded:** with CORS on and authentication off, any web page open in your browser can call the API, because CORS allows every origin.\n\n**What the code also shows** ([src/server.js:232-262](projects/modelduck/src/server.js#L232-L262), [src/http.js:44-80](projects/modelduck/src/http.js#L44-L80)):\n1. **Turning on network sharing switches off the website check.** Normally `isLoopbackRequest` refuses a request that comes from another website. But once LAN sharing or CORS is on, it returns early and never checks which site the request came from.\n2. **Authentication doesn't cover the host's own browser.** `requiresApiAuth` asks for a key on `/api/*` only when the request comes from another machine. A website open in your own browser connects from this machine, so with CORS on and auth on, it can still use `/api/*` without a key. That includes deleting models, changing server settings and creating API keys.\n3. **No CORS needed for a basic attack.** `readJsonBody` doesn't check the content type. So with only LAN sharing on, a website can send a plain-text POST containing JSON, which browsers send without asking permission first. It can't read the replies, but it can start downloads or unload models.\n\n**Proposed fix:**\n- Always refuse `/api/*` requests that come from another website, even with LAN sharing or CORS on.\n- Apply CORS only to `/v1`.\n- Require a JSON content type on POST and PUT requests.\n- Refuse to save LAN or CORS settings while authentication is off, with an explanation and fix, and show the exposure in the UI.\n\nSaved settings that already have LAN on and auth off also need handling. I'd switch LAN and CORS off on load and log a warning, but that's your call.\n\n## 1047 (P1, data loss): chats silently disappear past 200\n\nThis is in [src/chat-store.js:82, 109-110](projects/modelduck/src/chat-store.js#L82).\n- **What happens:** chats are dropped by the order they were created, not by when you last used them. Adding a message updates the chat's timestamp but doesn't move it in the list. So an old chat you used yesterday is the first one dropped when you create chat number 201, with no warning. Loading the app does the same thing.\n- **Proposed fix:** never delete chats.\n  - Move chats over the limit, least recently used first, into an archive file.\n  - Show how many are archived.\n  - Add a failing test first that shows the current drop.\n\n## 967 (P2, security): unsafe GGUF files reach the native parser\n\nThis is in [src/model-registry.js:156-162](projects/modelduck/src/model-registry.js#L156-L162).\n- **What happens:** the only check before llama.cpp parses a model file is the 4-byte \"GGUF\" signature. Any downloaded or imported file that passes it goes straight to the native parser.\n- **Proposed fix:** first read the header in plain JavaScript. Check the version and the tensor and metadata counts. Make sure every declared string or array fits inside the file's size. Refuse the file with an explanation if it doesn't pass.\n\n## 862 (P2): downloads aren't checked against a content hash\n\nThis is in [src/downloader.js:140-235](projects/modelduck/src/downloader.js#L140) and [src/model-catalog.js](projects/modelduck/src/model-catalog.js).\n- **What happens:** downloads follow redirects, and the only check is that the final byte count matches the five catalog entries.\n- **Proposed fix:**\n  - Add a SHA-256 to each catalog entry.\n  - Hash the file while it streams in. A resumed download re-hashes the partial file first.\n  - Refuse to use the file if the hash doesn't match.\n- **Getting the hashes:** the real values have to come from Hugging Face's file metadata, which means going online once.\n\n## Suggested order\n\n1054 first, then 1047: one is a live security hole and the other loses data. Both are small and self-contained, and each would be a separate milestone with a failing test first. 967 and 862 can follow.\n\nShould I start on 1054 and 1047? If so, decide how to handle saved settings that already have LAN on and auth off."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":520,"cache_read_input_tokens":74266,"output_tokens":2235,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":734},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":520,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2235,"cache_read_input_tokens":74266,"cache_creation_input_tokens":520,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":520},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"38d74d88-5e17-4265-9ecf-1e9132ac2bc4","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgYZXfgxGTcEdWGUZ3P8n","requestRef":"36d339b2-aa9a-476c-997c-fe19c4bd9cad","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"ffe29a9a-0795-4403-bc73-17c4b8316609","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:15:14.810Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","versi