{"parentUuid":"48e7b500-eb80-4e6e-b4ac-0fc471cd5043","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYZkkHdS6Av2Fipgo8Xn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I tested all four approved modelduck ideas by running ModelDuck's actual code on the latest commit (`1c9876d`), and every one is a real bug. Two are worse than I recorded, and two of my earlier statements were wrong. Nothing in modelduck was changed. The test scripts and their temporary data are in my scratchpad and have been cleaned up.\n\n## 1054: a website can take control of ModelDuck (confirmed, worse than recorded)\n\nI started the real server and sent requests as if from another website (`https://evil.example`):\n\n| Settings | Request from the other website | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Defaults | Read server settings | 403, refused (correct) |\n| CORS on, auth off | Read settings / create an API key | 200 / 201, and the website can read the reply, **including the new API key** |\n| **CORS on, auth on**, no key | `/v1/models` | 401 (correct) |\n| **CORS on, auth on**, no key | Read settings / create an API key | **200 / 201, the key is returned** |\n| **LAN on, auth on** | A plain-text POST to create a chat | **201, the chat was created** |\n| Normal settings save | LAN and CORS on with auth off | 200, accepted without a warning |\n\n- **Turning on authentication doesn't help.** Requests to `/api/*` from your own computer never need a key. A website open in your browser counts as your own computer, so with CORS on it can create an API key and read it back.\n- **LAN sharing alone is enough for a basic attack.** It switches off the check on which website a request came from. A website can then send plain-text requests that change things, even though it can't read the replies.\n- **Correction:** last time I said a website could delete models or change server settings. The server does accept those requests, but a real browser blocks them, because the CORS headers only allow GET and POST. Reading settings, creating keys, chatting and starting downloads are what's actually exposed.\n\n## 1047: chats silently disappear (confirmed)\n\n| Test | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Create 200 chats, write in the first one, then create chat 201 | The chat you just used is **gone** |\n| Load a saved file with 250 chats | 200 are kept, and the most recently used is **gone** |\n\nChats are dropped in the order they were created, regardless of when you last used them.\n\n## 967: one bad model file can crash ModelDuck at every start (confirmed, my earlier explanation was wrong)\n\nI said the danger was llama.cpp's native code. That was wrong: the scan uses node-llama-cpp 3.22.2's JavaScript parser. The actual risk is a crash or a hang. I gave the parser hostile files of about 100 bytes, each in a separate process with limited memory:\n\n| Hostile file | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Claims 2^62 tensors | **Process killed**: out of memory |\n| Claims 2^62 metadata entries | **Hangs**: no result after 20 seconds |\n| Claims a first key 2^40 bytes long | Caught error (safe) |\n\nModelDuck scans the models folder when it starts ([src/main.js:117](projects/modelduck/src/main.js#L117)) and every time the model list is refreshed. An out-of-memory error can't be caught, so a single bad `.gguf` in that folder would crash the app at every launch until someone removes the file. The fix stays the same: check the header's size claims against the real file before parsing it.\n\n## 862: downloads aren't verified (confirmed)\n\nI simulated the download server and sent back a file of exactly the right size containing only the letter \"A\". The download was marked **complete** and saved as `SmolLM2-135M-Instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf`, even though the file doesn't start with the GGUF signature. No catalog entry has a hash field.\n\nTogether with 967, this means anything sitting between you and the download server could hand you a file that crashes ModelDuck at every start.\n\n## Changes to the order\n\n1054, then 967 (upgraded to P1), then 1047, then 862. I haven't updated the register text for 1054 and 967 yet. Should I correct it to these findings and start on 1054? The open question from before still stands: should saved settings that already have LAN on and auth off be switched off at startup?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":534,"cache_read_input_tokens":91922,"output_tokens":1564,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":177},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":534,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1564,"cache_read_input_tokens":91922,"cache_creation_input_tokens":534,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":534},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"b722a949-7bf0-4294-ab36-b2777bdc9c7d","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgYZkjyH4YU53TKVuUi8P","requestRef":"995b52f5-c257-4db6-a99c-4de84fbc1fa7","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"b4a00c9a-fa9e-4211-879b-9202955fdf02","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:18:06.612Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main","slug":"hidden-watching-bengio"}
