; ADR-014 remains as the historical generation-only decision.\r\n[PASS] test (1.8s, detected): npm test\r\n[PASS] hygiene (1.4s, built-in): C:\\Python314\\python.exe C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\scripts\\check_project_hygiene.py --workspace C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck\r\n[PASS] guidelines (0.2s, built-in): C:\\Python314\\python.exe C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\scripts\\update_knowledge_base.py --workspace C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck --check\r\n[PASS] secrets (0.1s, built-in): C:\\Python314\\python.exe C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\scripts\\check_secrets.py --workspace C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck\r\n4/4 gates passed for 'modelduck'\r\nManual: record 'checklists: passed' or the failed or skipped item IDs in the task's latest checkpoint before the next commit (PRINCIPLE-001 R8, PRINCIPLE-011 R8).\r\n[WARN] the project has uncommitted non-bookkeeping changes\r\n[WARN] UI surface 'app' has no finished UI task matching its current files; finish a new UI task (records before ADR-169 have no digest)\r\n[DELIVERY] NOT READY: 7 modified, 0 untracked, 0 conflicted, UI contract not delivery-ready; run `check --strict` before pushing.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck\\.aikit\\decisions.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":135,"newStart":1,"newLines":142,"lines":["-# Decisions","-","-Why this project is built the way it is, oldest first. One entry per decision that changes a","-contract, a dependency, a data format or the architecture; IDs are consecutive and never reused.","-","-<!-- Entry format:","-### ADR-NNN: <decision in one line>","-- **Date**: YYYY-MM-DD","-- **Status**: Accepted","-- **Context**: <what forced the decision>","-- **Decision**: <what was decided>","-- **Consequences**: <what follows, including what it costs>","--->","-","-","-### ADR-001: Ship a local web app instead of a desktop shell","-- **Date**: 2026-10-30","-- **Status**: Accepted","-- **Context**: The user needs a dependable replacement for LM Studio and Ollama, and asked for the easiest possible install. Electron or Tauri would add a second runtime, packaging pipeline and hundreds of megabytes before the first chat works.","-- **Decision**: Serve the UI from the same Node process that runs inference. On Windows, start.bat opens it in an installed Edge/Chrome app window; otherwise the default browser opens. There is no build step and no UI framework.","-- **Consequences**: One `npm install` and one `npm start` are enough. Closing the browser window leaves the local server running until Ctrl+C, which the README states. A future native shell can wrap the same HTTP surface without changing the engine.","-","-### ADR-002: One CPU-first llama.cpp context, one generation at a time","-- **Date**: 2026-10-30","-- **Status**: Accepted","-- **Context**: A 16 GB machine without a usable GPU must load small GGUF models predictably. Multiple contexts multiply KV-cache memory, and parallel generations on the same CPU make every reply slower.","-- **Decision**: Use node-llama-cpp with GPU disabled unless `MODELDUCK_GPU=auto`; load one model and one context, reject a second concurrent generation with an actionable 409, and rebuild the chat session from the atomic conversation store when the active conversation changes. Context creation retries at smaller sizes before failing.","-- **Consequences**: RAM use stays close to one model plus one context. Switching conversations loses the KV cache and pays prompt processing again. The UI and OpenAI endpoint share one fair queue and cannot corrupt each other's session.","-","-### ADR-003: Benchmark quality and speed on three model classes","-- **Date**: 2026-10-30","-- **Status**: Accepted","-- **Context**: Token throughput alone cannot show whether a change improved the product. The user requires every generation-affecting change to exercise at least three small, different model types and compare the result.","-- **Decision**: Add `npm run bench`, a deterministic nine-task suite (instruction following, arithmetic, facts, reasoning, JSON, JavaScript syntax, formatting, list cardinality, long-context recall) run through the real LlamaEngine on SmolLM2 135M, Qwen2.5 0.5B and Qwen2.5 Coder 1.5B. Quality is compared exactly with `benchmarks/baseline.json`; speed is the median generated tokens per second and warns beyond a 20% drop.","-- **Consequences**: The gate takes minutes and needs the three model files, so it is an explicit pre-checkpoint step rather than part of `npm test`. Generated code is syntax-checked by `node --check`, never executed. A deliberate quality change requires a reviewed baseline update.","-","-","-### ADR-004: Keep benchmark history in SQLite with an append-only JSONL export","-- **Date**: 2026-10-30","-- **Status**: Accepted","-- **Context**: Quality and speed only help if each change can be compared with the commit that produced it. A single baseline file has no trend, and a binary database alone is not reviewable in a diff.","-- **Decision**: `npm run bench` records each run in a local `benchmarks/history.sqlite` database (`runs`, `model_results`, `prompt_results`, `commits`) and appends the same run to `benchmarks/history.jsonl`. A run made before its commit is marked pending; `npm run bench:link` attaches it to the new HEAD. The JSONL file is tracked and can rebuild the database with `npm run bench:report -- --rebuild`.","-- **Consequences**: Progress is queryable per model and per commit without storing model weights. The SQLite file is ignored so binary churn stays out of git, while the tracked JSONL keeps the record reviewable. Node 22+ is needed for `node:sqlite` in the benchmark tooling; the app itself remains Node 20+.","-","-","-### ADR-005: Separate model-reload and model-usage benchmark gates","-- **Date**: 2026-10-30","-- **Status**: Accepted","-- **Context**: Loading parameters and sampling parameters change different things. Mixing them hides whether a quality delta came from the model file, the KV cache, the context configuration, the sampler or a changed test.","-- **Decision**: `npm run bench:model` runs one-factor reload variants and `npm run bench:chat` runs generation/sampling variants on the configuration chosen by the first phase. Both record a SHA-256 test hash over prompts, checks and matrix entries; the changelog plus `SUITE_VERSION` distinguishes test edits from parameter changes.","-- **Consequences**: A model change is judged before sampling tuning. Baselines are separate. A test edit requires a deliberate baseline update and cannot masquerade as a model improvement.","-","-### ADR-006: API server settings, keys, CORS and JIT loading","-- **Date**: 2026-10-30","-- **Status**: Accepted","-- **Context**: LM Studio-style local serving needs a controllable port, optional API keys, LAN access, CORS and lazy model loading without making the browser UI depend on them.","-- **Decision**: Persist `server-settings.json` with port, auth, LAN, CORS, JIT and idle policy. Store only SHA-256 hashes of API keys, return a raw key once, protect `/v1/*`, and keep JIT unload from touching a busy engine. MCP toggles are stored but no MCP transport is claimed.","-- **Consequences**: Auth/CORS and JIT are opt-in and configurable without restarting the UI. Port/LAN changes need a restart. The local UI remains loopback-first.","-","-### ADR-007: GPU backend selection with CPU fallback","-- **Date**: 2026-10-30","-- **Status**: Accepted","-- **Context**: node-llama-cpp can use CUDA, Vulkan and Metal, but a missing driver or build must not prevent local chat. ROCm is not exposed by the binding.","-- **Decision**: Add `gpuBackend` (`cpu`, `auto`, `cuda`, `vulkan`, `metal`) and `gpuLayers`. Explicit backends fail with an actionable error; `auto` falls back to CPU. AMD uses Vulkan because ROCm is unavailable in this runtime.","-- **Consequences**: CPU remains the default. GPU builds may require vendor toolchains, which is reported rather than hidden.","+# Decisions\r","+\r","+Why this project is built the way it is, oldest first. One entry per decision that changes a\r","+contract, a dependency, a data format or the architecture; IDs are consecutive and never reused.\r","+\r","+<!-- Entry format:\r","+### ADR-NNN: <decision in one line>\r","+- **Date**: YYYY-MM-DD\r","+- **Status**: Accepted\r","+- **Context**: <what forced the decision>\r","+- **Decision**: <what was decided>\r","+- **Consequences**: <what follows, including what it costs>\r","+-->\r","+\r","+\r","+### ADR-001: Ship a local web app instead of a desktop shell\r","+- **Date**: 2026-10-30\r","+- **Status**: Accepted\r","+- **Context**: The user needs a dependable replacement for LM Studio and Ollama, and asked for the easiest possible install. Electron or Tauri would add a second runtime, packaging pipeline and hundreds of megabytes before the first chat works.\r","+- **Decision**: Serve the UI from the same Node process that runs inference. On Windows, start.bat opens it in an installed Edge/Chrome app window; otherwise the default browser opens. There is no build step and no UI framework.\r","+- **Consequences**: One `npm install` and one `npm start` are enough. Closing the browser window leaves the local server running until Ctrl+C, which the README states. A future native shell can wrap the same HTTP surface without changing the engine.\r","+\r","+### ADR-002: One CPU-first llama.cpp context, one generation at a time\r","+- **Date**: 2026-10-30\r","+- **Status**: Accepted\r","+- **Context**: A 16 GB machine without a usable GPU must load small GGUF models predictably. Multiple contexts multiply KV-cache memory, and parallel generations on the same CPU make every reply slower.\r","+- **Decision**: Use node-llama-cpp with GPU disabled unless `MODELDUCK_GPU=auto`; load one model and one context, reject a second concurrent generation with an actionable 409, and rebuild the chat session from the atomic conversation store when the active conversation changes. Context creation retries at smaller sizes before failing.\r","+- **Consequences**: RAM use stays close to one model plus one context. Switching conversations loses the KV cache and pays prompt processing again. The UI and OpenAI endpoint share one fair queue and cannot corrupt each other's session.\r","+\r","+### ADR-003: Benchmark quality and speed on three model classes\r","+- **Date**: 2026-10-30\r","+- **Status**: Accepted\r","+- **Context**: Token throughput alone cannot show whether a change improved the product. The user requires every generation-affecting change to exercise at least three small, different model types and compare the result.\r","+- **Decision**: Add `npm run bench`, a deterministic nine-task suite (instruction following, arithmetic, facts, reasoning, JSON, JavaScript syntax, formatting, list cardinality, long-context recall) run through the real LlamaEngine on SmolLM2 135M, Qwen2.5 0.5B and Qwen2.5 Coder 1.5B. Quality is compared exactly with `benchmarks/baseline.json`; speed is the median generated tokens per second and warns beyond a 20% drop.\r","+- **Consequences**: The gate takes minutes and needs the three model files, so it is an explicit pre-checkpoint step rather than part of `npm test`. Generated code is syntax-checked by `node --check`, never executed. A deliberate quality change requires a reviewed baseline update.\r","+\r","+\r","+### ADR-004: Keep benchmark history in SQLite with an append-only JSONL export\r","+- **Date**: 2026-10-30\r","+- **Status**: Accepted\r","+- **Context**: Quality and speed only help if each change can be compared with the commit that produced it. A single baseline file has no trend, and a binary database alone is not reviewable in a diff.\r","+- **Decision**: `npm run bench` records each run in a local `benchmarks/history.sqlite` database (`runs`, `model_results`, `prompt_results`, `commits`) and appends the same run to `benchmarks/history.jsonl`. A run made before its commit is marked pending; `npm run bench:link` attaches it to the new HEAD. The JSONL file is tracked and can rebuild the database with `npm run bench:report -- --rebuild`.\r","+- **Consequences**: Progress is queryable per model and per commit without storing model weights. The SQLite file is ignored so binary churn stays out of git, while the tracked JSONL keeps the record reviewable. Node 22+ is needed for `node:sqlite` in the benchmark tooling; the app itself remains Node 20+.\r","+\r","+\r","+### ADR-005: Separate model-reload and model-usage benchmark gates\r","+- **Date**: 2026-10-30\r","+- **Status**: Accepted\r","+- **Context**: Loading parameters and sampling parameters change different things. Mixing them hides whether a quality delta came from the model file, the KV cache, the context configuration, the sampler or a changed test.\r","+- **Decision**: `npm run bench:model` runs one-factor reload variants and `npm run bench:chat` runs generation/sampling variants on the configuration chosen by the first phase. Both record a SHA-256 test hash over prompts, checks and matrix entries; the changelog plus `SUITE_VERSION` distinguishes test edits from parameter changes.\r","+- **Consequences**: A model change is judged before sampling tuning. Baselines are separate. A test edit requires a deliberate baseline update and cannot masquerade as a model improvement.\r","+\r","+### ADR-006: API server settings, keys, CORS and JIT loading\r","+- **Date**: 2026-10-30\r","+- **Status**: Accepted\r","+- **Context**: LM Studio-style local serving needs a controllable port, optional API keys, LAN access, CORS and lazy model loading without making the browser UI depend on them.\r","+- **Decision**: Persist `server-settings.json` with port, auth, LAN, CORS, JIT and idle policy. Store only SHA-256 hashes of API keys, return a raw key once, protect `/v1/*`, and keep JIT unload from touching a busy engine. MCP toggles are stored but no MCP transport is claimed.\r","+- **Consequences**: Auth/CORS and JIT are opt-in and configurable without restarting the UI. Port/LAN changes need a restart. The local UI remains loopback-first.\r","+\r","+### ADR-007: GPU backend selection with CPU fallback\r","+- **Date**: 2026-10-30\r","+- **Status**: Accepted\r","+- **Context**: node-llama-cpp can use CUDA, Vulkan and Metal, but a missing driver or build must not prevent local chat. ROCm is not exposed by the binding.\r","+- **Decision**: Add `gpuBackend` (`cpu`, `auto`, `cuda`, `vulkan`, `metal`) and `gpuLayers`. Explicit backends fail with an actionable error; `auto` falls back to CPU. AMD uses Vulkan because ROCm is unavailable in this runtime.\r","+- **Consequences**: CPU remains the default. GPU builds may require vendor toolchains, which is reported rather than hidden.\r"," "," ### ADR-008: Fingerprint every generation and every changed benchmark input"," - **Date**: 2026-10-30"," - **Status**: Accepted"," - **Context**: The benchmark test hash covered prompts, checks and most matrix fields, but not the global or per-variant system prompt, promptIds, the GGUF chat template, the runtime version or the truncated history the model actually received. A score change could therefore be attributed to settings when the governing prompt, template or runtime had changed."," - **Decision**: Add a generation manifest in `src/generation-manifest.js`. `engine.chat` builds the effective system prompt, the exact bounded history and the repeated prompt through the same helpers and returns a SHA-256 manifest covering model identity, chat-template hash, runtime version, prompt/history digests and sampling settings. Chat messages and the SSE done event store a digest-only summary; OpenAI responses expose the short digest as `system_fingerprint`; benchmark prompt results store manifest, prompt, history and template digests. The test hash now includes the global and per-variant system prompts, promptIds, effective sampling defaults and the manifest version. Baselines record runtime and manifest versions and refuse a mismatch unless `--update-baseline` is deliberately passed.","-- **Consequences**: Two runs are comparable only when the test definition, runtime and manifest version match. Regenerating a baseline after a runtime or test change is explicit and logged. Message files and history rows grow by a small digest summary, not by a second copy of the prompt or history.","+- **Consequences**: Two runs are comparable only when the test definition, runtime and manifest version match. Regenerating a baseline after a runtime or test change is explicit and logged. Message files and history rows grow by a small digest summary, not by a second copy of the prompt or history.\r"," "," ### ADR-009: LAN auth covers UI routes; session reuse is gated by exact history"," - **Date**: 2026-10-30"," - **Status**: Accepted"," - **Context**: Authentication only covered `/v1/*`, so enabling LAN serving exposed `/api/*` and the browser UI to unauthenticated state changes. The engine also had a `historyLength` reuse check that was never advanced, so every multi-turn request rebuilt the chat session and lost KV-cache reuse."," - **Decision**: When authentication is enabled, `/v1/*` always needs a key and `/api/*` needs one when the TCP peer is not loopback. The loopback UI stays key-free; a LAN browser receives 401, is prompted once, and sends `X-API-Key` from session storage. Session reuse is allowed only when the bounded history is complete (no 40-message/48k-character truncation), the system prompt/context signature matches, and the stored history length equals the prior message count. A completed turn advances that count; aborts, errors and truncated histories dispose the session. JIT keep-last documentation now states that disabling it refuses a second model because the engine intentionally holds one.","-- **Consequences**: LAN clients can use the UI or API with a key while the host needs none. Long conversations rebuild rather than risk a manifest/session mismatch. Reused sessions keep the KV cache and still match the generation manifest, which is derived from the same bounded history.","+- **Consequences**: LAN clients can use the UI or API with a key while the host needs none. Long conversations rebuild rather than risk a manifest/session mismatch. Reused sessions keep the KV cache and still match the generation manifest, which is derived from the same bounded history.\r"," "," ### ADR-010: Read-only benchmark report inside the app"," - **Date**: 2026-10-30"," - **Status**: Accepted"," - **Context**: Per-test percentages, runtime, suite/test identity and linked commits were only visible through the CLI. The user asked for the benchmark results to be visible as part of the product, and the existing baselines/JSONL already contain the data."," - **Decision**: Add `src/benchmark-report.js`, a read-only summary over `baseline-model.json`, `baseline-chat.json` and `history.jsonl`. `GET /api/benchmarks` returns both phases with quality, speed, runtime, per-test percentages, suite/test hash and linked commit, plus recent runs. A **Bench** dialog renders it. Missing baselines return an empty report; the route never writes files, loads a model or touches SQLite.","-- **Consequences**: The panel always reflects the last deliberate baseline, not an in-progress run. It works on Node 20 without `node:sqlite`. LAN auth applies because the route is under `/api/*`. No benchmark suite or test hash changes, so existing baselines stay valid.","+- **Consequences**: The panel always reflects the last deliberate baseline, not an in-progress run. It works on Node 20 without `node:sqlite`. LAN auth applies because the route is under `/api/*`. No benchmark suite or test hash changes, so existing baselines stay valid.\r"," "," ### ADR-011: Surface detected GPU backends in Settings"," - **Date**: 2026-10-30"," - **Status**: Accepted"," - **Context**: `/api/gpu` could report which backends the installed runtime sees, but the Settings dialog never asked for it. Users had to guess whether CUDA, Vulkan or Metal was actually available before selecting a backend."," - **Decision**: On opening Settings, request `/api/gpu` and show the detected backend names, mapping the runtime's `false` entry to CPU and adding the ROCm note. Detection failures fall back to a CPU-only message. The CPU default and stored backend choice do not change.","-- **Consequences**: The dialog is read-only and reflects the current runtime, not a benchmark. A missing or broken backend is visible before a failed model load. Detection happens only when Settings opens, so it adds no startup work.","+- **Consequences**: The dialog is read-only and reflects the current runtime, not a benchmark. A missing or broken backend is visible before a failed model load. Detection happens only when Settings opens, so it adds no startup work.\r"," "," ### ADR-012: Verify manifests against a real model without changing baselines"," - **Date**: 2026-10-30"," - **Status**: Accepted"," - **Context**: Unit tests prove the history helpers, but the benchmark runs one isolated prompt per conversation. Nothing exercised the live `engine.chat` path for session reuse, 40-message truncation, prompt repetition and template/runtime identity against a GGUF file."," - **Decision**: Add `npm run verify:manifest -- --models-dir <folder>`. It defaults to Qwen2.5 0.5B Q4 (or the first installed model), patches `setChatHistory` only to count calls, and checks: the second turn reuses the session; a 45-pair history truncates to 40 messages with the expected digest; prompt repetition duplicates the text; the manifest carries the GGUF template hash and node-llama-cpp version. It writes no history and changes no baseline.","-- **Consequences**: The diagnostic needs an installed model and takes a few prompts, so it stays opt-in. It fails with exit 1 on a mismatch, while the benchmark suite and baseline hashes remain untouched.","+- **Consequences**: The diagnostic needs an installed model and takes a few prompts, so it stays opt-in. It fails with exit 1 on a mismatch, while the benchmark suite and baseline hashes remain untouched.\r"," "," ### ADR-013: Add OpenAI text completions without changing chat behavior"," - **Date**: 2026-10-30"," - **Status**: Accepted"," - **Context**: The OpenAI-compatible surface exposed models and chat completions only. LM Studio-style clients and simple prompt tools often call `/v1/completions`, which needs raw continuation rather than a chat template."," - **Decision**: Add `LlamaEngine.complete()` using `LlamaCompletion` with a fresh context sequence, the same one-generation guard, sampling settings, abort handling and manifest builder as chat. Add `POST /v1/completions` with non-streaming and SSE `text_completion` responses, single-string prompt validation, usage, finish reason and `system_fingerprint`. Chat remains on `LlamaChatSession`.","-- **Consequences**: Clients can use either OpenAI endpoint; completions still enter the same CPU queue and cannot overlap a chat. The completion manifest has no system prompt or history, so it records empty values plus the prompt/settings/model identity. Prompt arrays and token-ID arrays are rejected with an actionable error rather than silently misread.","+- **Consequences**: Clients can use either OpenAI endpoint; completions still enter the same CPU queue and cannot overlap a chat. The completion manifest has no system prompt or history, so it records empty values plus the prompt/settings/model identity. Prompt arrays and token-ID arrays are rejected with an actionable error rather than silently misread.\r"," "," ### ADR-014: Preserve actionable generation errors"," - **Date**: 2026-10-30"," - **Status**: Accepted"," - **Context**: Chat and text completion wrapped every thrown error in a generic generation failure, so a deliberate 413 context overflow or invalid-schema error reached clients as a 500. A failed LlamaCompletion constructor could also leave its context sequence undisposed."," - **Decision**: Move the generation error mapping into `src/generation-error.js`. It returns an existing AppError unchanged and only wraps unexpected failures as 500 or 507 memory errors. `engine.chat` and `engine.complete` use it. If completion construction fails before the completion object exists, dispose the context sequence explicitly.","-- **Consequences**: API and SSE clients receive the original status, code, why and fix for actionable failures. Generic runtime failures keep the friendly message. Sequence memory is released on the constructor-failure path.","+- **Consequences**: API and SSE clients receive the original status, code, why and fix for actionable failures. Generic runtime failures keep the friendly message. Sequence memory is released on the constructor-failure path.\r"," "," ### ADR-015: Single-model lookup and cached model listing"," - **Date**: 2026-10-30"," - **Status**: Accepted"," - **Context**: OpenAI clients expect `GET /v1/models/{id}`, and the list route rescanned the model folder on every request even though the registry already keeps a scan cache."," - **Decision**: Serve `/v1/models` from `registry.listPublic()`, add `GET /v1/models/{id}` with id or file-name lookup and a 404 for unknown models, and return quant, size and chat-template hash alongside the standard OpenAI model fields.","-- **Consequences**: Model listing no longer touches disk on each request; a new model becomes visible after the normal registry refresh. Single-model lookups are cheap and unknown ids fail cleanly.","+- **Consequences**: Model listing no longer touches disk on each request; a new model becomes visible after the normal registry refresh. Single-model lookups are cheap and unknown ids fail cleanly.\r"," "," ### ADR-016: Release idle chat sequences before a completion"," - **Date**: 2026-10-30"," - **Status**: Accepted"," - **Context**: With the default `sequences=1`, a lingering UI chat session owns the only context sequence. A following `/v1/completions` request failed with \"No sequences left\", even though generation is serialized."," - **Decision**: Before allocating a completion sequence, if `context.sequencesLeft` is zero, dispose idle chat sessions. A completion has no chat history to preserve, and the next chat turn rebuilds its session from the atomic store.","-- **Consequences**: Completions and chat can alternate with the default single sequence. Alternating endpoints loses the chat KV cache; the generation manifest remains exact because the rebuilt session uses the same bounded history.","+- **Consequences**: Completions and chat can alternate with the default single sequence. Alternating endpoints loses the chat KV cache; the generation manifest remains exact because the rebuilt session uses the same bounded history.\r"," "," ### ADR-017: Unify load and generation error mapping"," - **Date**: 2026-10-30"," - **Status**: Accepted"," - **Context**: ADR-014 preserved actionable generation errors, but model loading still wrapped GPU-backend and configuration AppErrors as generic model_load_failed 500s. Keeping two separate mappers also risked the same divergence."," - **Decision**: Rename the mapper module to `src/engine-errors.js` and expose `toLoadError` alongside `toGenerationError`. Both return an existing AppError unchanged and only wrap unexpected failures into the friendly 500/507 messages for their operation.","-- **Consequences**: Explicit GPU-backend failures keep `gpu_backend_failed`, memory failures keep 507 codes, and unexpected load/generation failures stay friendly. The old `generation-error.js` was removed; ADR-014 remains as the historical generation-only decision.","+- **Consequences**: Explicit GPU-backend failures keep `gpu_backend_failed`, memory failures keep 507 codes, and unexpected load/generation failures stay friendly. The old `generation-error.js` was removed; ADR-014 remains as the historical generation-only decision.\r","+","+### ADR-018: Refuse cross-site and rebound requests; exposure needs authentication","+- **Date**: 2026-10-30","+- **Status**: Accepted","+- **Context**: IDEA-1054. A live probe showed that with Enable CORS on, any web page in the host's browser could read server settings and mint a readable API key even with Require Authentication on, because `/api/*` trusts loopback sockets and CORS applied to every route. LAN mode skipped the Origin and Host checks, so cross-site text/plain POSTs and DNS-rebound pages reached `/api/*`. Settings accepted LAN or CORS with authentication off.","+- **Decision**: `isCrossSiteRequest` refuses any request whose Origin differs from its Host (`403 cross_site_refused`), except `/v1/*` while CORS is on; CORS headers and preflight apply to `/v1/*` only. A loopback socket must always name a loopback Host; only LAN mode admits other machines. `ServerSettingsStore.update` refuses LAN or CORS without Require Authentication (`400 auth_required_for_exposure`), and `load` switches such a saved file's LAN and CORS off with a warning and saves it.","+- **Consequences**: The same-origin UI (loopback or LAN) and Origin-less API clients are unchanged. Local pages on another port can no longer call ModelDuck unless CORS is on with a key. Contract change: the tests `server settings clamp values...` and `CORS preflight is answered only when enabled` now enable authentication alongside CORS, because CORS without it is no longer a valid setting."]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck\\.aikit\\decisions.md"]}},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"d78547d8-24bd-4100-a186-63ba97e01633","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/c/Users/Yunus-Emre/Desktop/portable_agent_kit/projects/modelduck","root":"/c/Users/Yunus-Emre/Desktop/portable_agent_kit/projects/modelduck","branch":"master","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":7,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/AIKit","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/NeuroCore","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Research-Engine","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Aegean-Licensing","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Aegean-Licensing-Ops","visibility":"private"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Merklock","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/c/Users/Yunus-Emre/Desktop/portable_agent_kit/projects/modelduck","platform":"windows"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1bb11dbd-baf4-4fb0-9da7-68268d33a247","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main","slug":"hidden-watching-bengio"}
