{"parentUuid":"1cd9252c-1184-4664-9408-16be611aa594","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"77902c58-ab0d-45be-9a54-10ce222b753c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RzJqfWA12S5vfpr6dXhMov","type":"tool_result","content":" 28041 reports/ModelDuck idea briefs.md\n 53403 reports/ModelDuck local LLM literature review.md\n 31480 reports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md\n 21162 reports/research-engine-project-mining.md\n 32953 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/edge_and_cpu_inference.md\n 41222 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/evaluation_and_benchmarking.md\n 37132 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/inference_time_features.md\n 33905 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/local_first_product_and_security.md\n 34524 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/quantization_quality_tradeoffs.md\n 36221 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/small_model_capabilities.md\n350043 total\n=== reports/ModelDuck idea briefs.md\n# ModelDuck idea briefs\n\nThese briefs expand the 15-row “Ideas from the articles” table in\n[ModelDuck local LLM literature review](ModelDuck%20local%20LLM%20literature%20review.md).\nEach brief is written as an implementation idea, not as a literature summary. The\nevidence tags match the review: **T1** peer-reviewed, **T2** preprint or technical\nreport, **T3** official documentation or rule, **T4** vendor claim, **T5**\nindependent benchmark, **T6** practitioner report.\n\nThe ordering is deliberate. Ideas 1–8 are safe to adopt now because they either\nremove a measurement error or protect a claim ModelDuck already makes. Ideas 9–12\nare prototypes with a clear experiment and a kill criterion. Ideas 13–15 are\ndocumented as defer or reject so they do not get re-litigated every roadmap cycle.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Per-item logging, `suiteFingerprint`, and paired statistics\n\n**Recommendation:** Adopt now · **Rank:** 1 · **Evidence:** T1 + T3\n\n**Problem.** The current gate stores a score per task, not an outcome per item. That\nmakes it impossible to separate a model regression from a parser change, a prompt\nchange, or a handful of unlucky samples. Fourteen independent tests at α = 0.05 also\ngive roughly a 51% chance of at least one false alarm per run.\n\n**Proposal.** Write one JSONL record per item containing `item_id`, `task`,\n`prompt_hash`, `expected`, `raw_output`, `parsed_output`, `correct`, `parse_fail`,\n`latency_ms`, `suite_fingerprint`, and the run manifest. Compute paired McNemar\ntests against the baseline, Wilson confidence intervals for accuracy, and Holm\ncorrection across tasks. Keep the per-item file as the primary artifact; the summary\n=== reports/ModelDuck local LLM literature review.md\n# ModelDuck should measure more and claim less\r\n\r\nThe literature supports a narrow conclusion: ModelDuck's exact operating point — 0.5B–3B Q4 GGUF, one user, batch 1, an 8 GB Windows CPU laptop — is almost unmeasured. The rigorous work sits at 7B and above on GPUs, where throughput is batching-limited; the edge work sits on Raspberry Pis and Apple Silicon, where memory is unified and power budgets differ. What transfers is physics and method: decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, quality is a property of a specific quantization artifact and prompt path, small models fail compositionally, and small unpaired benchmarks cannot detect the effects that matter. ModelDuck's defensible advantage is therefore not its model catalog but its measurement apparatus: per-item logging, a pinned suite fingerprint, separate TTFT and time-per-output-token, a memory budget that fails closed, and claims scoped to what was measured on the target machine. The cheapest high-value features are prompt and KV caching, a 4–8K context budget with q8 KV, grammar-constrained final answers, retrieval with a 22M reranker and extractive citations, and a hardened loopback API with an honest egress ledger. The expensive traps are 32K context, autonomous agents, draft-model speculation on CPU, q4_0 KV, large rerankers, and 2B+ multimodal.\r\n\r\n## The evidence base stops short of ModelDuck's operating point\r\n\r\nEfficient-inference surveys are organized around GPU serving: KV management, continuous batching, tensor parallelism, and multi-request goodput (Zhou et al., 2024; Zhen et al., 2025). The credible CPU anchors are edge studies on 0.5B–2.6B models and one systems paper on a 3B BitNet model (Wei et al., 2025; Husom et al., 2025). Neither uses GGUF Q4 on Windows x86, and the GPU papers use 7B–70B at batch sizes above one, so their speedups describe a different bottleneck. **There is no rigorous head-to-head study of 0.5B–3B Q4 GGUF on an 8 GB Windows laptop with RAM bandwidth, thread count, context depth, and energy disclosed.** That absence is the central epistemic fact of this review.\r\n\r\n| Tag | Meaning | Representative sources | Use |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| T1 | Peer-reviewed | T-MAC, KIVI, HELM, RULER, FormatSpread | Mechanisms and measurement standards |\r\n| T2 | Preprint or technical report | GreenBench, Kurt, Kumar, Fang, SmolLM2 | Directional claims and hypotheses |\r\n| T3 | Official documentation or rule | GGUF spec, llama.cpp, node-llama-cpp, MLPerf | Implementation contract |\r\n| T4 | Vendor claim | AMD, Qwen, Ollama, LM Studio, Meta, Microsoft, Google | Hypothesis generation only |\r\n| T5 | Independent benchmark | OpenBenchmarking, Artificial Analysis, NobodyWho, Gaessler | Order-of-magnitude calibration |\r\n| T6 | Practitioner report | GitHub discussions, consumer benchmarks, forensic blogs | Failure modes |\r\n\r\nWhere the sources agree, the agreement is strong enough to plan around. Decode is bandwidth-bound at batch 1, so tokens per second approximate effective bandwidth divided by active bytes, with real systems reaching 50–80% of that ceiling (Juravsky et al., 2023; Gaessler, 2024a). Four-bit weights are near-optimal at a fixed total bit budget (Dettmers & Zettlemoyer, 2023). Q8_0 KV cache is close to free, while symmetric q4_0 KV is a cliff (Gaessler, 2024b; llama.cpp discussion #23470, 2026). Small models are limited by reliability and scope, not by a missing faculty, and prompt format can move scores more than a size upgrade (Lu et al., 2025; Sclar et al., 2024).\r\n\r\nThe collisions are substantive. Precision-scaling work says heavily overtrained small models are more fragile to quantization, and OmniQuant reports moderate-to-high loss at 3–4 bits especially for 1–10B models, while a code-robustness study finds models below 3B handle aggressive quantization better under adversarial prompts (Kumar et al., 2024; OmniQuant, 2024; Fang et al., 2025). The constructs differ: clean accuracy versus adversarial robustness. Q4 speed is also contested, with a 41% generation advantage over Q8_0 at 8B in llama.cpp's reference bench and 2% on a 96-core Xeon (llama.cpp documentation, 2026; Kurt, 2026).\r\n\r\n## Memory bandwidth sets the ceiling; every feature pays rent in bytes\r\n\r\nThe most useful planning equation is `tokens per second ≈ effective memory bandwidth ÷ active bytes per token`, discounted to 50–80% for real systems (Juravsky et al., 2023; Gaessler, 2024a). At batch 1, FLOPs are nearly irrelevant; decode is a streaming problem. This explains why a 3B Q4 model lands around 10–18 tokens per second on a mid-range 8 GB laptop while a 0.5B lands at 40–80, and why a draft model usually loses on CPU: it doubles the bytes moved per speculative step. It also explains why context length is a performance knob, not just a memory knob.\r\n\r\n| Model class | Q4_K_M weights | KV per token, FP16 | KV at 4K | KV at 8K | Expected decode | 8 GB verdict |\r\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\r\n| Qwen2.5-0.5B | ~0.31 GB | ~12 KB | ~49 MB | ~98 MB | 40–80 tok/s | Comfortable at 8K, even 32K |\r\n| Qwen2.5-1.5B | ~0.92 GB | ~28 KB | ~115 MB | ~235 MB | 25–40 tok/s | Comfortable at 8K; 32K tight |\r\n| Llama-3.2-3B | ~1.83 GB | ~112 KB | ~460 MB | ~920 MB | 10–18 tok/s | 8K tight; 4K safer; 32K no |\r\n=== reports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md\n# ModelDuck — What to Borrow from NeuroCore\n\n**Date:** 2026-10-30\n**Target:** `projects/modelduck` (v0.1.0, `node-llama-cpp` 3.22.2)\n**Source:** `projects/neurocore` (FastAPI + HTMX agent framework, 21 modules)\n**Scope:** transferable mechanics, not architecture\n**Constraint:** every idea must preserve ModelDuck's positioning: one small Node process,\nCPU-first, no account or telemetry, inspectable state, benchmark-gated generation changes.\n\n> **Collision warning:** ModelDuck has an in-flight `matrix-server-v2` task at phase 2\n> (`.aikit/task_ledger.json`) touching `engine.js`, `server-settings.js`, `jit.js`,\n> `benchmarks/run.mjs`, `suite.mjs`, `history.mjs`, `report.mjs`, presets and the UI.\n> Finish or park it before implementing anything below. The first two ideas touch the\n> same files.\n\n---\n\n## Executive summary\n\n1. **Do not port NeuroCore's architecture.** It is a multi-module Python control plane with\n   flows, a brain, memory, messaging and a marketplace. ModelDuck is one Node process and a\n   browser UI. What transfers is a handful of **invariants** that NeuroCore learned to enforce.\n2. **The strongest fit is benchmark integrity.** ModelDuck's `testHash` covers the suite and\n   matrix settings, but not the system prompt, per-variant system prompt, GGUF chat template,\n   or `node-llama-cpp` version. A change there can move quality without the gate noticing.\n   A **generation manifest / prompt fingerprint** closes that gap and reuses the existing\n   `engine.chat` path for both the app and the benchmark.\n3. **Second: versioned prompts and presets with benchmark-gated trust.** NeuroCore records\n   every prompt-material change under its SHA-256, bounds history, and treats rollback as a\n   new version. ModelDuck's presets are hardcoded and nothing records that a preset earned\n=== reports/research-engine-project-mining.md\n# Research Engine — Cross-Project Mechanic Mining\n\n**Date:** 2026-10-30\n**Target:** `projects/research-engine`\n**Scope:** all 45 sibling projects under `projects/` (every project except the target)\n**Filter:** Python 3.10+ standard library only, SQLite, CLI-first, no network calls, human judgment\nstays in the loop, no literature crawling.\n\n## Method\n\n1. Read every project README and extracted the module/class/function inventory.\n2. Deep-read the modules that implement provenance, storage, evidence, statistics, budgeting,\n   verification or agent-facing surfaces.\n3. Compared each mechanic against the research engine's current schema v10, `engine/*.py` and the\n   existing research-engine idea backlog.\n4. Kept only mechanics that add a new invariant, not a second copy of an existing one.\n\n## Current state (important before any implementation)\n\n- The engine is **not on a green baseline**. `python -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` currently\n  reports 404 tests with one failure: `tests/test_docs.py` requires `README.md` to be under 22,500\n  characters, but it is 23,255 (HEAD is already 23,229). This predates the mining work.\n- There is an **in-flight task** `T-015-discovery-calibration` in\n  `projects/research-engine/.aikit/task_ledger.json`, with uncommitted `engine/search.py`,\n  `engine/calibration.py`, their tests and CLI/report wiring. The `source duplicates` parser exists\n  but has no handler. Treat search, calibration and source-duplicate detection as already owned.\n- Any new implementation should first finish or explicitly park T-015, then restore the README\n  budget and the full suite.\n\n## Coverage: every sibling project","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d552c43d-3ad2-428e-8dcd-1c2d64845f60","timestamp":"2026-10-30T15:01:48.567Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":" 28041 reports/ModelDuck idea briefs.md\n 53403 reports/ModelDuck local LLM literature review.md\n 31480 reports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md\n 21162 reports/research-engine-project-mining.md\n 32953 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/edge_and_cpu_inference.md\n 41222 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/evaluation_and_benchmarking.md\n 37132 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/inference_time_features.md\n 33905 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/local_first_product_and_security.md\n 34524 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/quantization_quality_tradeoffs.md\n 36221 research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/small_model_capabilities.md\n350043 total\n=== reports/ModelDuck idea briefs.md\n# ModelDuck idea briefs\n\nThese briefs expand the 15-row “Ideas from the articles” table in\n[ModelDuck local LLM literature review](ModelDuck%20local%20LLM%20literature%20review.md).\nEach brief is written as an implementation idea, not as a literature summary. The\nevidence tags match the review: **T1** peer-reviewed, **T2** preprint or technical\nreport, **T3** official documentation or rule, **T4** vendor claim, **T5**\nindependent benchmark, **T6** practitioner report.\n\nThe ordering is deliberate. Ideas 1–8 are safe to adopt now because they either\nremove a measurement error or protect a claim ModelDuck already makes. Ideas 9–12\nare prototypes with a clear experiment and a kill criterion. Ideas 13–15 are\ndocumented as defer or reject so they do not get re-litigated every roadmap cycle.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Per-item logging, `suiteFingerprint`, and paired statistics\n\n**Recommendation:** Adopt now · **Rank:** 1 · **Evidence:** T1 + T3\n\n**Problem.** The current gate stores a score per task, not an outcome per item. That\nmakes it impossible to separate a model regression from a parser change, a prompt\nchange, or a handful of unlucky samples. Fourteen independent tests at α = 0.05 also\ngive roughly a 51% chance of at least one false alarm per run.\n\n**Proposal.** Write one JSONL record per item containing `item_id`, `task`,\n`prompt_hash`, `expected`, `raw_output`, `parsed_output`, `correct`, `parse_fail`,\n`latency_ms`, `suite_fingerprint`, and the run manifest. Compute paired McNemar\ntests against the baseline, Wilson confidence intervals for accuracy, and Holm\ncorrection across tasks. Keep the per-item file as the primary artifact; the summary\n=== reports/ModelDuck local LLM literature review.md\n# ModelDuck should measure more and claim less\r\n\r\nThe literature supports a narrow conclusion: ModelDuck's exact operating point — 0.5B–3B Q4 GGUF, one user, batch 1, an 8 GB Windows CPU laptop — is almost unmeasured. The rigorous work sits at 7B and above on GPUs, where throughput is batching-limited; the edge work sits on Raspberry Pis and Apple Silicon, where memory is unified and power budgets differ. What transfers is physics and method: decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, quality is a property of a specific quantization artifact and prompt path, small models fail compositionally, and small unpaired benchmarks cannot detect the effects that matter. ModelDuck's defensible advantage is therefore not its model catalog but its measurement apparatus: per-item logging, a pinned suite fingerprint, separate TTFT and time-per-output-token, a memory budget that fails closed, and claims scoped to what was measured on the target machine. The cheapest high-value features are prompt and KV caching, a 4–8K context budget with q8 KV, grammar-constrained final answers, retrieval with a 22M reranker and extractive citations, and a hardened loopback API with an honest egress ledger. The expensive traps are 32K context, autonomous agents, draft-model speculation on CPU, q4_0 KV, large rerankers, and 2B+ multimodal.\r\n\r\n## The evidence base stops short of ModelDuck's operating point\r\n\r\nEfficient-inference surveys are organized around GPU serving: KV management, continuous batching, tensor parallelism, and multi-request goodput (Zhou et al., 2024; Zhen et al., 2025). The credible CPU anchors are edge studies on 0.5B–2.6B models and one systems paper on a 3B BitNet model (Wei et al., 2025; Husom et al., 2025). Neither uses GGUF Q4 on Windows x86, and the GPU papers use 7B–70B at batch sizes above one, so their speedups describe a different bottleneck. **There is no rigorous head-to-head study of 0.5B–3B Q4 GGUF on an 8 GB Windows laptop with RAM bandwidth, thread count, context depth, and energy disclosed.** That absence is the central epistemic fact of this review.\r\n\r\n| Tag | Meaning | Representative sources | Use |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| T1 | Peer-reviewed | T-MAC, KIVI, HELM, RULER, FormatSpread | Mechanisms and measurement standards |\r\n| T2 | Preprint or technical report | GreenBench, Kurt, Kumar, Fang, SmolLM2 | Directional claims and hypotheses |\r\n| T3 | Official documentation or rule | GGUF spec, llama.cpp, node-llama-cpp, MLPerf | Implementation contract |\r\n| T4 | Vendor claim | AMD, Qwen, Ollama, LM Studio, Meta, Microsoft, Google | Hypothesis generation only |\r\n| T5 | Independent benchmark | OpenBenchmarking, Artificial Analysis, NobodyWho, Gaessler | Order-of-magnitude calibration |\r\n| T6 | Practitioner report | GitHub discussions, consumer benchmarks, forensic blogs | Failure modes |\r\n\r\nWhere the sources agree, the agreement is strong enough to plan around. Decode is bandwidth-bound at batch 1, so tokens per second approximate effective bandwidth divided by active bytes, with real systems reaching 50–80% of that ceiling (Juravsky et al., 2023; Gaessler, 2024a). Four-bit weights are near-optimal at a fixed total bit budget (Dettmers & Zettlemoyer, 2023). Q8_0 KV cache is close to free, while symmetric q4_0 KV is a cliff (Gaessler, 2024b; llama.cpp discussion #23470, 2026). Small models are limited by reliability and scope, not by a missing faculty, and prompt format can move scores more than a size upgrade (Lu et al., 2025; Sclar et al., 2024).\r\n\r\nThe collisions are substantive. Precision-scaling work says heavily overtrained small models are more fragile to quantization, and OmniQuant reports moderate-to-high loss at 3–4 bits especially for 1–10B models, while a code-robustness study finds models below 3B handle aggressive quantization better under adversarial prompts (Kumar et al., 2024; OmniQuant, 2024; Fang et al., 2025). The constructs differ: clean accuracy versus adversarial robustness. Q4 speed is also contested, with a 41% generation advantage over Q8_0 at 8B in llama.cpp's reference bench and 2% on a 96-core Xeon (llama.cpp documentation, 2026; Kurt, 2026).\r\n\r\n## Memory bandwidth sets the ceiling; every feature pays rent in bytes\r\n\r\nThe most useful planning equation is `tokens per second ≈ effective memory bandwidth ÷ active bytes per token`, discounted to 50–80% for real systems (Juravsky et al., 2023; Gaessler, 2024a). At batch 1, FLOPs are nearly irrelevant; decode is a streaming problem. This explains why a 3B Q4 model lands around 10–18 tokens per second on a mid-range 8 GB laptop while a 0.5B lands at 40–80, and why a draft model usually loses on CPU: it doubles the bytes moved per speculative step. It also explains why context length is a performance knob, not just a memory knob.\r\n\r\n| Model class | Q4_K_M weights | KV per token, FP16 | KV at 4K | KV at 8K | Expected decode | 8 GB verdict |\r\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\r\n| Qwen2.5-0.5B | ~0.31 GB | ~12 KB | ~49 MB | ~98 MB | 40–80 tok/s | Comfortable at 8K, even 32K |\r\n| Qwen2.5-1.5B | ~0.92 GB | ~28 KB | ~115 MB | ~235 MB | 25–40 tok/s | Comfortable at 8K; 32K tight |\r\n| Llama-3.2-3B | ~1.83 GB | ~112 KB | ~460 MB | ~920 MB | 10–18 tok/s | 8K tight; 4K safer; 32K no |\r\n=== reports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md\n# ModelDuck — What to Borrow from NeuroCore\n\n**Date:** 2026-10-30\n**Target:** `projects/modelduck` (v0.1.0, `node-llama-cpp` 3.22.2)\n**Source:** `projects/neurocore` (FastAPI + HTMX agent framework, 21 modules)\n**Scope:** transferable mechanics, not architecture\n**Constraint:** every idea must preserve ModelDuck's positioning: one small Node process,\nCPU-first, no account or telemetry, inspectable state, benchmark-gated generation changes.\n\n> **Collision warning:** ModelDuck has an in-flight `matrix-server-v2` task at phase 2\n> (`.aikit/task_ledger.json`) touching `engine.js`, `server-settings.js`, `jit.js`,\n> `benchmarks/run.mjs`, `suite.mjs`, `history.mjs`, `report.mjs`, presets and the UI.\n> Finish or park it before implementing anything below. The first two ideas touch the\n> same files.\n\n---\n\n## Executive summary\n\n1. **Do not port NeuroCore's architecture.** It is a multi-module Python control plane with\n   flows, a brain, memory, messaging and a marketplace. ModelDuck is one Node process and a\n   browser UI. What transfers is a handful of **invariants** that NeuroCore learned to enforce.\n2. **The strongest fit is benchmark integrity.** ModelDuck's `testHash` covers the suite and\n   matrix settings, but not the system prompt, per-variant system prompt, GGUF chat template,\n   or `node-llama-cpp` version. A change there can move quality without the gate noticing.\n   A **generation manifest / prompt fingerprint** closes that gap and reuses the existing\n   `engine.chat` path for both the app and the benchmark.\n3. **Second: versioned prompts and presets with benchmark-gated trust.** NeuroCore records\n   every prompt-material change under its SHA-256, bounds history, and treats rollback as a\n   new version. ModelDuck's presets are hardcoded and nothing records that a preset earned\n=== reports/research-engine-project-mining.md\n# Research Engine — Cross-Project Mechanic Mining\n\n**Date:** 2026-10-30\n**Target:** `projects/research-engine`\n**Scope:** all 45 sibling projects under `projects/` (every project except the target)\n**Filter:** Python 3.10+ standard library only, SQLite, CLI-first, no network calls, human judgment\nstays in the loop, no literature crawling.\n\n## Method\n\n1. Read every project README and extracted the module/class/function inventory.\n2. Deep-read the modules that implement provenance, storage, evidence, statistics, budgeting,\n   verification or agent-facing surfaces.\n3. Compared each mechanic against the research engine's current schema v10, `engine/*.py` and the\n   existing research-engine idea backlog.\n4. Kept only mechanics that add a new invariant, not a second copy of an existing one.\n\n## Current state (important before any implementation)\n\n- The engine is **not on a green baseline**. `python -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` currently\n  reports 404 tests with one failure: `tests/test_docs.py` requires `README.md` to be under 22,500\n  characters, but it is 23,255 (HEAD is already 23,229). This predates the mining work.\n- There is an **in-flight task** `T-015-discovery-calibration` in\n  `projects/research-engine/.aikit/task_ledger.json`, with uncommitted `engine/search.py`,\n  `engine/calibration.py`, their tests and CLI/report wiring. The `source duplicates` parser exists\n  but has no handler. Treat search, calibration and source-duplicate detection as already owned.\n- Any new implementation should first finish or explicitly park T-015, then restore the README\n  budget and the full suite.\n\n## Coverage: every sibling project","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"5f2441b0-1fc8-40a7-bcb6-7f70880845aa","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/c/Users/Yunus-Emre/Desktop/portable_agent_kit","root":"/c/Users/Yunus-Emre/Desktop/portable_agent_kit","branch":"main","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":5,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":2},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/AIKit","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/AIKit","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Aegean