nt\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:129:### 2. Versioned prompt and preset library with benchmark-gated trust\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:165:### 3. Pre-flight load plan with blocking and warning findings\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:207:### 4. Per-request budget and JIT load budget\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:240:## Tier 2 — after the surface settles\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:242:### 5. Live latency monitor with p99 stall detection\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:272:### 6. Untrusted fence and loop guard — build before MCP/RAG\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:308:### 7. SSRF-safe model acquisition\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:342:### 8. Benchmark trend and comparability report\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:372:### 9. Server-side confirmation for destructive routes\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:395:## Tier 3 — selective or later\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:410:## Sequencing\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:425:## Benchmark and test plan\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:458:## Open decisions\nreports/modelduck-neurocore-research.md:474:## Sources\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:9:## Method\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:18:## Current state (important before any implementation)\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:30:## Coverage: every sibling project\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:80:## Tier 1 — recommended next\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:82:### 1. Immutable analysis records with re-analysis (`muse`)\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:109:### 2. Random-effects meta-analysis + forest plot (`lattice`)\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:134:### 3. Semantic snapshot verification (`primordia`)\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:156:### 4. Mass-missing environment guard (`photo-harbor`)\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:179:### 5. Preview + confirmation digest for destructive commands (`aegean-licensing-ops`)\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:202:### 6. Offline bibliography import: BibTeX, RIS, CSL-JSON (`lattice`)\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:222:## Tier 2 — high value, larger or narrower\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:238:## Tier 3 — useful, not next\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:268:## Do not implement\nreports/research-engine-project-mining.md:283:## Recommended sequence\n119:## Ideas from the articles: eight adopt, four prototype, one defer, two reject\n120-\n121-The ranking below blends evidence strength, user benefit, and fit to a single Node process on 8 GB. \"Evidence strength\" uses the tags from Table 1, and \"smallest credible experiment\" is the minimum test that could change the decision.\n122-\n123-| Rank | Idea | What the evidence says | Expected benefit | Cost/complexity | CPU/8 GB fit | Evidence strength | Smallest credible experiment | Recommendation |\n124-|---:|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n125-| 1 | Per-item logging, `suiteFingerprint`, paired statistics | HELM and MLPerf version the whole task; Miller (2024) shows paired tests are the only affordable power; 14 unpaired tasks give ~51% false-alarm risk | Unlocks every quality claim; separates mo\n126-| 2 | Memory-budget fit card and fail-closed policy | 3B/8K is 6.2–7.2 GB before a browser; KV is the binding constraint; q8 KV is near-free | Prevents the swap cliff and \"why is it slow?\" support burden | Low–medium: estimator and three-state verdict \n127-| 3 | Prompt/KV cache with cache-aware ordering | Prompt Cache reports 8–60× TTFT; llama.cpp reuses 43 tokens as 1; context shift destroys the cache | Multi-turn usability; avoids minute-long re-prefill | Low: cache metrics, stable prefix, checkpoint po\n128-| 4 | Loopback security baseline and LAN opt-in | 0.0.0.0 Day, Ollama CVE, llama.cpp CORS `*`, and cross-client state restore show loopback is not a boundary | Blocks browser and LAN attacks; makes \"local\" credible | Low: bind, Host/Origin, token, CORS off\n129-| 5 | Claim hierarchy, Offline Mode, network ledger | Survey and CHI work show trust is conditional; Ollama's audit shows update and catalog traffic; forensics show traces | Credible differentiation; auditability | Low–medium: egress ledger and offline t\n130-| 6 | Extractive citations, abstention, verification UI | Only 51.5% of sentences fully supported; citations raise trust even when wrong; Trust-Align improves refusal | Reduces overreliance; makes grounded mode trustworthy | Medium: quote matching, support\n131-| 7 | Grammar-constrained final answer plus semantic validation | Validity rises to 100%; semantic failures remain; constrained TPOT can be ~2×; format restrictions hurt reasoning | Reliable extraction and API output | Low; possible 2× TPOT | Strong | T1\n132-| 8 | Capability-floor quantization policy | Q8_0 is F16-equivalent at 8B; Q6/Q5 near-noise; Q4 measurable but task-shaped; Q3 GSM8K −9.3; small-model evidence contested | Quality per byte; honest model cards | Low: per-model tiers and a Q8 reference | S\n133-| 9 | Hybrid retrieval with a 22M reranker | BEIR finds reranking strong but costly; 22M is ~91 MB; semantic chunking adds no consistent gain | Document QA without blowing RAM | Low–medium: ~0.1–0.3 GB | Strong | T1/T2 | 10 docs, 100 queries; recall@5,\n134-| 10 | Editable memory: summary, fact table, retrieval | LongMemEval shows a 30% drop; EMG-RAG reports ~10% gain; MemGPT's small-model benefit unproven | Continuity across sessions | Low–medium: storage and UI | Strong | T1/T2 | 12 sessions, 15 facts; su\n135-| 11 | n-gram self-speculation (`ngram-mod`) on code/editing | REST and n-gram methods help repetitive output; consumer CPU shows 1.96× code but 1.01× chat; AdaptiveSD warns of bandwidth saturation | Faster code and editing | Very low: ~16 MB | Strong | \n136-| 12 | Evaluate Qwen3.5-2B, SmolLM3-3B, Phi-4-mini | Vendor numbers are strong but there is no