{"parentUuid":"2601d804-340d-4cd7-a548-4895844545c8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"70a75a41-7888-4c77-bc13-5ad4f9e80b7c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NKJUhiqm8DUCM3bN3ZoLBv","type":"tool_result","content":"### 2. Versioned prompt and preset library with benchmark-gated trust\n**What NeuroCore does.** `SkillVersionStore` stores every content change under its SHA-256,\nkeeps a bounded 50-version index per skill, and writes a rollback as a new version so the\nrollback itself is auditable (`modules/skills/versions.py:1-149`). Promotion of an improvement\nrequires a perfect same-flow baseline with provenance, a perfect candidate and no regression;\nthe promoted version is recorded and can be rolled back (`modules/brain/improvement.py:279-376`).\n**Why it fits ModelDuck.** The three presets are hardcoded and the README says they \"fill the\nsettings form, never apply themselves, and only earn trust after both phases pass\"\n(`README.md:120-121`). Nothing records that a preset actually passed, and a user cannot save a\ngood system prompt at all. A versioned library turns the claim into state that can be inspected.\n**What to add.**\n- `prompts.json` and `presets.json` under `--home`, written through the existing atomic helper\n  (`src/fs-atomic.js`) with quarantine on corruption.\n- A version record: `{ n, sha256, createdAt, author, note, benchmarkRunUid, suiteVersion, testHash,\n  promptFingerprint }`. Keep at most 50; prune oldest; never prune the active version. Rollback\n  copies an old body into a new version.\n- Trust state: `candidate` → `trusted` only when a run UID exists and its `suiteVersion`,\n  `testHash` and `promptFingerprint` still match the current preset content. Any edit or\n  prompt-affecting setting change resets it to `candidate`.\n- Built-in presets stay immutable; \"Clone\" creates a user preset. A trusted preset only fills\n  the settings form — it never applies itself.\n- UI: list, diff, rollback, \"Benchmark this preset\" (runs both phases or attaches an existing\n  run), \"Use\".\n- Tests: pruning, rollback-as-new-version, trust invalidation on edit, refusal to trust without\n  a run, stable ordering.\n**Effort:** S–M (2–3 days). **Impact:** high for the benchmark moat and onboarding.\n**Benchmark:** no generation change; if a default preset changes, run the gate. **Risk:** keep\nthe JSON human-readable; no database.\n---\n### 3. Pre-flight load plan with blocking and warning findings\n**What NeuroCore does.** `simulate_flow` analyses structure, effects and a cost envelope without\nexecuting a node. It returns `{ blocking, findings:[{severity, code, message, node_id, fix}],\ncost, effects, cycles }`. A single pass that cannot fit is blocking; a cycle that *may* reach\nthe cap is a warning (`core/flow_simulation.py:1-321`, especially `290-320`). The editor and the\nimprovement engine gate on `blocking`, not on a score.\n**Why it fits ModelDuck.** `engine.load` silently retries `[wanted, min(wanted, 3072), 2048, 1024]`\n(`src/engine.js:257-287`) and only surfaces a generic OOM (`engine.js:59-75`). The user never\nsees which context was planned, which fallback succeeded, or why. The GitHub research already\nasks for a fit report (item 3); NeuroCore supplies the deterministic report shape.\n**What to add.**\n- `src/load-plan.js`, a pure function `planLoad({ entry, settings, system })` returning:\n  - `blocking`: file missing or unreadable, incomplete shard set (`*-00001-of-0000N.gguf` with\n    missing parts), bad GGUF magic, not enough disk for the model, requested context impossible\n    at the smallest fallback.\n  - `warning`: requested context above the trained context, `kvCache` `q4_0`/`q8_0` without\n    flash attention (the engine forces it, so an info), GPU layers > 0 with the CPU backend,\n    `mmap off` with a large model and low RAM, `sequences > 1` with a single context, an\n    `mmproj-*.gguf` file selected directly, missing chat template, `maxTokens` close to the\n    context, recent load failures with these settings.\n  - `info`: effective context after fallback, estimated KV bytes, recommended threads, model\n    size/quant.\n- `GET /api/models/:id/plan?context=&kv=&backend=` returns the report without loading. The UI\n  shows a \"Load plan\" drawer; warnings have \"Load anyway\". Record which fallback context\n  actually succeeded in the response.\n- Return the same report from a failed `POST /api/models/:id/load` instead of only the generic\n  error.\n- After `matrix-server-v2` lands, reuse the shape for `bench:model --plan`: number of variants,\n  downloads, estimated time from history, variants likely to fail. A single pass that cannot\n  fit is blocking; a matrix that may exceed a time budget is a warning.\n**Effort:** S–M (2–3 days). **Impact:** medium-high — fewer support tickets and a stronger\n\"inspectable\" story. **Benchmark:** no generation change; if the plan changes the default\ncontext selection, run the gate. **Risk:** memory estimates are guesses; label them and never\nblock a user who accepts a warning. Only impossible states are blocking.\n---\n### 4. Per-request budget and JIT load budget\n**What NeuroCore does.** `ExecutionBudget` bounds nodes, LLM calls, tokens, tool calls and wall\ntime. One budget is created per top-level run and shared by nested runs; flow metadata may\nlower a limit but never raise it. `spend` raises `BudgetExceeded` with a why/fix; `pressure`\nis a 0–1 signal; `snapshot()` is JSON-safe (`core/execution_budget.py:1-161`). `SelfBudget`\nreserves a unit before the work starts and resets at UTC midnight\n(`modules/brain/self_budget.py:1-154`).\n**Why it fits ModelDuck.** ModelDuck runs one generation at a time by design. `maxTokens` bounds\nthe output, but nothing bounds prompt processing or wall time, and nothing prevents an API\nclient from forcing repeated JIT loads of different models (`src/jit.js:34-95`). A LAN client\ncan monopolize the single context.\n**What to add.**\n- `src/budget.js`: `createBudget({ maxPromptTokens, maxOutputTokens, maxSeconds,\n  maxJitLoadsPerHour })` with `spend`, `checkTime`, `pressure`, `snapshot`.\n- Per request: request `max_tokens`/`max_completion_tokens` may only lower `maxOutputTokens`;\n  check `promptTokens + maxTokens <= context` (already in `engine.js:429-439`); a timer aborts\n  with `budget_exceeded` and a why/fix. Add `budget` to `stats` and `/api/status`.\n- JIT: a persisted `jit-loads.json` ledger counts loads in a rolling hour. `JitManager.ensure`\n  reserves before loading and releases on failure; a denied reservation returns 429 with\n  `Retry-After`. Add `maxJitLoadsPerHour` (default 6) to server settings.\n- Tests: fake clock, lower-only override, wall-time abort, pressure snapshot, ledger reset,\n  release on failed load.\n**Effort:** S–M (2 days). **Impact:** medium — robustness for LAN/API use. **Benchmark:** no\nquality change; the wall-time cap only affects runaway requests. **Risk:** keep default\n`maxSeconds` generous (600) so normal long prompts are unaffected; make it configurable.\n---","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c1098994-ce12-408c-b260-21cfd7a7455b","timestamp":"2026-10-30T15:04:03.849Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"### 2. Versioned prompt and preset library with benchmark-gated trust\n**What NeuroCore does.** `SkillVersionStore` stores every content change under its SHA-256,\nkeeps a bounded 50-version index per skill, and writes a rollback as a new version so the\nrollback itself is auditable (`modules/skills/versions.py:1-149`). Promotion of an improvement\nrequires a perfect same-flow baseline with provenance, a perfect candidate and no regression;\nthe promoted version is recorded and can be rolled back (`modules/brain/improvement.py:279-376`).\n**Why it fits ModelDuck.** The three presets are hardcoded and the README says they \"fill the\nsettings form, never apply themselves, and only earn trust after both phases pass\"\n(`README.md:120-121`). Nothing records 