{"parentUuid":"8aabfb31-265b-40d7-97e3-81ffa269da0b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"70a75a41-7888-4c77-bc13-5ad4f9e80b7c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YUEf7w4iX2X2A4DVNuxLDS","type":"tool_result","content":"38\t## Quantization is an artifact, not a label\n39\t\n40\tOnly one quantization family executes natively in GGUF on CPU. GPTQ uses second-order layer-wise reconstruction; AWQ protects activation-salient weights with per-channel scaling; SmoothQuant migrates activation outliers for W8A8; LLM.int8() keeps outlier dimensions in FP16; QLoRA's NF4 is a training format (Frantar et al., 2023; Lin et al., 2024; Xiao et al., 2023; Dettmers et al., 2022; Dettmers et al., 2023). Their quality numbers do not transfer to a `.gguf` file because the artifacts are produced by different algorithms and executed by different kernels. GGUF K-quants are data-free block quantization with a 256-weight super-block split into eight sub-blocks of 32, plus quantized 6-bit scales and mins.\n41\t\n42\tThe 8B evidence is the best-controlled GGUF data available. On WikiText-2, F16 perplexity is 6.2332; Q8_0 is 6.2343 (+0.04%, KLD 0.0014, RMS Δp 1.20%); Q6_K is 6.2534 (+0.32%, KLD 0.0055); Q5_K_M is 6.2886 (+0.89%, KLD 0.0108, RMS 3.16%); Q4_K_M is 6.4071 (+2.8%, KLD 0.0313, RMS **5.52%**); Q4_0 is 6.7001 (+7.5%); Q3_K_S is 7.8638 (+26%); and Q2_K is 9.7516 (+56%) (llama.cpp documentation, 2026). A 2.8% perplexity rise hides a 5.5% RMS shift in the next-token distribution, an order of magnitude above Q6_K.\n43\t\n44\tThe small-model story is contested. Precision-scaling laws fitted on 465 runs and validated to 1.7B parameters and 26B tokens find that post-training quantization loss increases as models are trained on more data, to the point that additional pretraining data can be \"actively harmful\" after quantization (Kumar et al., 2024). OmniQuant independently notes that 3–4 bit weight quantization can cause moderate-to-high losses, \"especially for smaller models (1–10B)\" (OmniQuant, 2024). Against this, a study of 350M–33B code models finds quantized models were more robust than full precision in 51.59% versus 42.86% of adversarial prompt experiments, and concludes that models below 3B handle aggressive quantization better than larger ones (Fang et al., 2025).\n45\t\n46\tOn 8 GB, weights are not the binding constraint; the KV cache and the OS are. That makes \"highest precision that fits\" mostly right for 0.5B–1.5B, while 3B is forced to Q4 by the context budget. A 135M model should ship Q8_0 at roughly 0.14 GB because Q4 saves about 60 MB, less than a browser tab, while removing an already thin margin. A 0.5B should default to Q6_K at roughly 0.41 GB, with Q8_0 at roughly 0.53 GB when RAM allows and Q4_K_M only as an explicit low-memory toggle.\n47\t\n48\t| Model class | Default | Acceptable alternative | Avoid | KV | Rationale |\n49\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n50\t| 135M | Q8_0 (~0.14 GB) | Q6_K | ≤Q5_K_M | q8_0 | Q4 saves ~60 MB, less than a browser tab |\n51\t| 0.5B | Q6_K (~0.41 GB) | Q8_0 (~0.53 GB) | Q4_K_M except low-memory mode | q8_0 | Contested sensitivity; Q8 fits |\n52\t| 1.5B general | Q5_K_M (~1.07 GB) | Q4_K_M (~0.92 GB) | Q4_0, Q3, Q2 | q8_0 | Quality-per-byte sweet spot |\n53\t| 3B general | Q4_K_M (~1.83 GB) floor | Q5_K_M (~2.14 GB) at 4K | ≤Q3 | q8_0 | 8 GB plus context forces Q4 |\n54\t| 1.5B–3B code | Q5_K_M or Q6_K | Q4_K_M as floor | ≤Q4_0 | q8_0 | Format-sensitive; pass@1 drops are visible |\n55\t\n56\tKV quantization is the dominant memory lever at long context. Ollama describes q8_0 as \"a very small loss in precision\" and q4_0 as \"a small-medium loss... more noticeable at higher context sizes\" (Ollama, 2026). A GPU measurement on a 30B model at 128K found FP16 KV at 768 MiB, q8_0 at 408 MiB (−47%), and q4_0 at 216 MiB (−72%); prompt throughput was unchanged, but q4_0 generation fell 11.9% at 24K and **36.8% at 110K** (llama.cpp discussion #20969, 2026).\n57\t\n58\t## Small models are reliability-limited, not faculty-limited\n59\t\n60\tThe sub-4B gains from 2024–2026 came mainly from over-training on filtered and synthetic data plus distillation from 7B–70B teachers, not from new architectures. SmolLM2-1.7B used roughly 11 trillion tokens in a multi-stage web, math, code, and instruction mix (Allal et al., 2025). A survey of 70 small models finds recent models typically use more than 1.5 trillion tokens, far above Chinchilla's roughly 20 tokens per parameter, and above about 1 trillion tokens data quality outweighs token count (Lu et al., 2025).\n61\t\n62\tThe failure modes are compositional. Small models handle single-step transformations and local pattern matching, then collapse on multi-step reasoning, long-horizon tool use, factual recall, non-English tasks, and safety. Qwen2.5-1.5B scores 68.5 on GSM8K but 35.0 on MATH; Qwen2.5-0.5B scores 41.6 versus 19.5; Phi-3.5 scores 86.2 versus 48.5; Llama 3.2 3B scores 77.7 versus 48.0 (Qwen Team, 2024; Microsoft, 2024; Meta, 2024).\n63\t\n64\tThe capability contract follows. A 135M model is for autocomplete, tone or format rewriting, keyword or field extraction, and classification — not chat, facts, arithmetic, code, or multi-turn memory. A 0.5B is for short replies, one-paragraph rewrites, summarising under 500 words, and flat key–value extraction — not facts, arithmetic, nested JSON, non-English, or more than two or three simultaneous constraints. A 1.5B is for paragraph drafting, email replies, short code with a code-tuned variant, single-turn RAG with citations, and one-level JSON — not multi-step math, tool calls, long context, or non-English nuance.\n65\t\n66\tAdvertised context is an upper bound. RULER evaluated 17 long-context models on 13 tasks and found almost all degrade sharply with length; although all claimed at least 32K, only half maintained satisfactory performance at 32K (Hsieh et al., 2024). Llama 3.2 1B reports 96.8 exact match on single-needle NIAH but only 20.3 F1 on InfiniteBench En.QA, and Phi-3.5 Mini's RULER average falls from 94.3 at 4K to 63.6 at 128K, while Llama 3.1 8B retains 77.0 at 128K (Meta, 2024; Microsoft, 2024).\n67\t\n68\tThe 2026 candidates are credible but vendor-reported. Qwen3.5-2B is Apache 2.0, 262K context, with thinking and non-thinking modes; its 2B is the quality sweet spot at MMLU-Pro 55.3/66.5, IFEval 61.2/78.6, and BFCL-V4 43.6, while the 0.8B is much weaker (Qwen Team, 2026). SmolLM3-3B is fully open weights, data, and recipe, 11.2 trillion tokens, 64K context, six languages, hybrid think/no-think, IFEval 76.7, and RULER-64K 67.9 (Hugging Face, 2025). Phi-4-mini is 3.8B, MIT-licensed, 5 trillion tokens, 128K context, MMLU 67.3, MMLU-Pro 52.8, GSM8K 88.6, and MATH 64.0 (Microsoft, 2025).\n69\t\n70\t## Evaluation is the product's only defensible source of truth\n71\t\n72\tThe field's best practice converges on six controls: version and pin every pipeline element, disclose the prompt protocol verbatim, report more than accuracy, publish uncertainty, retain per-item outputs, and build in anti-contamination and anti-gaming mechanisms. HELM evaluated 30 models across 42 scenarios with seven metrics and released all raw prompts and completions; before HELM, models averaged coverage of only 17.9% of its core scenarios (Liang et al., 2023).\n73\t\n74\tDecontamination by exact match does not work because paraphrased items evade n-gram filters; refresh-based benchmarks such as LiveBench are a mitigation, and a metric can be reliable and still measure the wrong thing (Yang et al., 2023; Xu et al., 2023; White et al., 2024; Jacobs & Wallach, 2021). The statistical problem is worse than most teams assume.\n75\t\n76\tLaptop measurement has a several-percent noise floor from the environment, not the model. Environment-only differences such as environment-block size and link order produced up to 8% performance change with identical source (Mytkowicz et al., 2009). Rigorous evaluation needs confidence intervals over independent repetitions and explicit separation of warm-up from steady state (Georges et al., 2007). `llama-bench` reports mean plus standard deviation and separates prompt processing from token generation, while its markdown table drops commit and variance; a published laptop methodology uses a trimmed mean of 15 runs (llama.cpp documentation, 2026; Mozilla.ai, 2025).\n77\t\n78\tA 20% speed threshold is roughly 7σ at CV = 3%, so it will miss the 8–15% regressions users notice, and the full config cross-product wastes hours on timing-only permutations while missing that flash-attention kernel selection perturbs numerics and mmap changes page-cache behavior. The three highest-leverage changes are per-item logging, splitting TTFT from TPOT at a 7% warning threshold, and recasting the quality fail as an absolute floor plus a confirmed large delta. Per-item logging unlocks every paired statistic for the cost of one file format; splitting the speed gate converts a blind threshold into a useful one; and the floor-plus-delta removes most flakiness while still catching breakage.\n79\t\n80\t| Element | Current state | Verdict | Change | Target |\n81\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n82\t| Suite size | 14 tasks, item counts unstated | Coverage strength, low power | Add a stratified 2,000-item subset across four domains | ≥2,000 items; ≥120 per task |\n83\t| Scoring | Point accuracy | Underpowered | Store per-item outcome: correct, parse-fail, schema-fail | 100% logged |\n84\t| Statistics | None stated | Major gap | Paired McNemar, Wilson CI, Holm, wild cluster bootstrap | α = 0.05; always report CI |\n85\t| `testHash` | SHA-256 of test content | Necessary, insufficient | `suiteFingerprint` over rendered prompts, answers, metric hash, few-shot pool, tokenizer hash, model hash, sampler, runtime commit | One hash, eight components |\n86\t| Baseline | Single versioned number | Insufficient | Seven runs: median, MAD, 95% CI, environment manifest; expires on OS, driver, or runtime change | ≥7 runs; 90-day expiry |\n87\t| Quality gate | Hard fail on any drop | Too sensitive | Floor at baseline − 5 pp, plus 5 pp delta at p < 0.001 confirmed on a second run | δ = 5 pp; p = 0.001 |\n88\t| Per-task gate | Per-task | Flaky | 15 pp warn; two consecutive runs fail; never fail on n < 120 | 15 pp, two runs |\n89\t| Parse failures | Untracked | Silent bias | First-class metric; +1 pp over baseline is T0 | ≤1% |\n90\t| Speed gate | Warn at 20% | Insensitive | Split TTFT and TPOT; warn 7%/10%, fail 20%/25% on two runs | Warn 7%; fail 20% |\n91\t| Repetitions | Unknown | Likely too few | One warm-up plus ten measured; median and MAD; CV ≤ 5% | 10 reps |\n92\t| Thermal | Unhandled | Confound | Three blocks; flag if block 3 exceeds block 1 by 5% | ≤5% drift |\n93\t| Config matrix | Full cross-product | Wasteful | Quality axes quant/KV/context; speed axes threads/batch/mmap; 200-item smoke | 200-item smoke |\n94\t| Contamination | None | Unaddressed | One private holdout; refresh 10% quarterly; canary string | One holdout |\n95\t| Comparability | Implicit | Fragile | Run manifest: hardware, OS, runtime commit, model hash, sampler, suite fingerprint | Manifest required |\n96\t\n97\t## Privacy is an engineering property, not a slogan\n98\t\n99\tLocal-first software is about ownership of mutable user documents, not computation. Kleppmann and colleagues treat the local copy as primary and servers as secondary replicas; their seven ideals are no spinners, work not trapped on one device, network optional, seamless collaboration, the Long Now, security and privacy by default, and ultimate ownership (Kleppmann et al., 2019). The technical core is CRDTs for mergeable documents, notes, and editors, not model artifacts. The Long Now ideal assumes the last released software keeps running and formats stay readable; for AI, behavior also depends on tokenizer, chat template, quantization, runtime kernels, and hardware.\n100\t\n101\tThe perception evidence is double-edged. In a controlled VR study with 60 participants, one group was told they were using cloud models and another local or on-premise models, while both were local; the \"local\" framing produced significantly higher UX and perceived response quality, and perceived data security rose significantly (p = .009, d = −0.52), while trust after interaction did not differ (VR framing study, 2026). A CHI EA 2026 on-device study found \"conditional trust\": participants reported epistemic uncertainty about data protection despite technically secure local deployment, and concluded that \"invisible security measures are insufficient to foster trust\" (CHI EA, 2026).\n102\t\n103\tThe technical evidence says local inference is not a confidentiality boundary. \"The Illusion of Local Privacy\" tested model loading, runtime memory, wrapper persistence, and serving; a 24-hour AFL++ campaign with more than 12 million executions found no parser crashes or malformed GGUF loads, but recovered prompts from plaintext allocator memory, found wrappers persisting prompts in plaintext, found a llama.cpp flaw letting one authenticated client restore another's saved state in **200 out of 200 trials**, and found a prompt-prefix remote timing oracle (Ibrahim et al., 2026).\n104\t\n105\tLicensing is part of the trust story because weights are not code. The Llama 3.1 license grants use, reproduction, distribution, and modification, but distribution requires a copy of the agreement, a prominent \"Built with Llama\" notice, the specified notice text, and, for derivatives, \"Llama\" at the beginning of the model name; it has a 700 million monthly active user threshold and terminates on breach with a deletion requirement (Meta, 2024). Gemma redistribution requires the use restrictions as an enforceable provision, a copy of the agreement, prominent modification notices, and a notice file, and Google reserves the right to restrict usage remotely or otherwise (Google, 2026).\n106\t\n107\tThe claim hierarchy is the copy deck. L0 is \"inference runs on this computer,\" which almost every local tool can claim; L1 is \"we operate no service that receives prompts,\" which most can claim; L2 is \"no analytics, telemetry, or crash SDK,\" which many can claim; L3 is \"normal use makes no network requests,\" which few can honestly claim because update checks and downloads violate it; L4 is \"air-gapped mode verified,\" which is rare. ModelDuck should claim L0–L2, offer L3 through an Offline Mode that blocks catalog refresh, update checks, and telemetry, and claim L4 only after a tested air-gap mode.\n108\t\n109\t## Inference-time features: cheap wins, expensive traps\n110\t\n111\tRAG is the most credible usefulness upgrade, but direct evidence at 0.5B–3B is thinner than at 7B+. On CRAG, most advanced LLMs answered at most 34% correctly; straightforward RAG raised this only to 44%, and state-of-the-art industry RAG answered 63% without hallucination (Yang et al., 2024). Long context generally beat RAG on QA, while RAG was better for dialogue and general queries; across 20 LLMs, quality rose then fell as retrieved passages increased, with hard negatives a key cause (Li et al., 2024; Jin et al., 2024).\n112\t\n113\tTool use is narrower than the demos suggest. On BFCL v4, `xLAM-2-1b-fc-r`, a 1B model fine-tuned for function calling, scores 30.44 overall, with agentic sub-scores in the low single digits (Patil et al., 2025). A 2025 BFCL-based edge study found 1–3B models significantly outperformed sub-1B models, reaching up to 65.74% overall and 55.62% multi-turn accuracy with hybrid SFT, PEFT, RL, and DPO optimization, while TinyAgent reports that a 1.1B model with curated function-calling data, tool retrieval, and quantization can achieve or surpass GPT-4-Turbo's function calling for a local Mac assistant, but that is an authors' claim on a narrow task (Haque et al., 2025; Erdogan et al., 2024).\n114\t\n115\tPrompt and KV caching are the best latency lever. Prompt Cache reports time-to-first-token improvements from 8× on GPU to **60× on CPU** for long, overlapping prompts, without changing outputs (Gim et al., 2024). llama.cpp supports prefix reuse and slot persistence: `cache_prompt` defaults true, `--cache-reuse N` controls minimum chunk reuse, and `/slots/{id}?action=save|restore` persists state, while node-llama-cpp exposes `saveStateToFile()` and `loadStateFromFile()` plus a custom `contextShift` strategy (llama.cpp discussion #13606, 2025; node-llama-cpp, 2026). The KV cache is the memory constraint: a Llama-3.2-3B-class model needs about 112 KiB per token in FP16, roughly 450 MB at 4K and 900 MB at 8K; q8_0 roughly halves this.\n116\t\n117\tTrust features are cheap and evidence-backed, but they can backfire. A human audit of four generative search engines found only **51.5% of generated sentences fully supported by citations** and only 74.5% of citations supporting their sentence, and on ELI5 even the best systems lacked complete citation support 50% of the time (Liu et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2023). A roughly 12,000-query, seven-country experiment with a preregistered US sample found reference links and citations significantly increased trust in generative search **even when links or citations were incorrect or hallucinated**, while uncertainty highlighting reduced willingness to trust and share (Li & Aral, 2025).\n118\t"}]},"uuid":"68b3e531-c7f2-4311-a50c-7a5dd462642e","timestamp":"2026-10-30T15:04:24.404Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"c:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\reports\\ModelDuck local LLM literature review.md","content":"## Quantization is an artifact, not a label\n\nOnly one quantization family executes natively in GGUF on CPU. GPTQ uses second-order layer-wise reconstruction; AWQ protects activation-salient weights with per-channel scaling; SmoothQuant migrates activation outliers for W8A8; LLM.int8() keeps outlier dimensions in FP16; QLoRA's NF4 is a training format (Frantar et al., 2023; Lin et al., 2024; Xiao et al., 2023; Dettmers et al., 2022; Dettmers et al., 2023). Their quality numbers do not transfer to a `.gguf` file because the artifacts are produced by different algorithms and executed by different kernels. GGUF K-quants are data-free block quantization with a 256-weight super-block split into eight sub-blocks of 32, plus quantized 6-bit scales and mins.\n\nThe 8B evidence is the best-controlled GGUF data available. On WikiText-2, F16 perplexity is 6.2332; Q8_0 is 6.2343 (+0.04%, KLD 0.0014, RMS Δp 1.20%); Q6_K is 6.2534 (+0.32%, KLD 0.0055); Q5_K_M is 6.2886 (+0.89%, KLD 0.0108, RMS 3.16%); Q4_K_M is 6.4071 (+2.8%, KLD 0.0313, RMS **5.52%**); Q4_0 is 6.7001 (+7.5%); Q3_K_S is 7.8638 (+26%); and Q2_K is 9.7516 (+56%) (llama.cpp documentation, 2026). A 2.8% perplexity rise hides a 5.5% RMS shift in the next-token distribution, an order of magnitude above Q6_K.\n\nThe small-model story is contested. Precision-scaling laws fitted on 465 runs and validated to 1.7B parameters and 26B tokens find that post-training quantization loss increases as models are trained on more data, to the point that additional pretraining data can be \"actively harmful\" after quantization (Kumar et al., 2024). OmniQuant independently notes that 3–4 bit weight quantization can cause moderate-to-high losses, \"especially for smaller models (1–10B)\" (OmniQuant, 2024). Against this, a study of 350M–33B code models finds quantized models were more robust than full precision in 51.59% versus 42.86% of adversarial prompt experiments, and concludes that models below 3B handle aggressive quantization better than larger ones (Fang et al., 2025).\n\nOn 8 GB, weights are not the binding constraint; the KV cache and the OS are. That makes \"highest precision that fits\" mostly right for 0.5B–1.5B, while 3B is forced to Q4 by the context budget. A 135M model should ship Q8_0 at roughly 0.14 GB because Q4 saves about 60 MB, less than a browser tab, while removing an already thin margin. A 0.5B should default to Q6_K at roughly 0.41 GB, with Q8_0 at roughly 0.53 GB when RAM allows and Q4_K_M only as an explicit low-memory toggle.\n\n| Model class | Default | Acceptable alternative | Avoid | KV | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 135M | Q8_0 (~0.14 GB) | Q6_K | ≤Q5_K_M | q8_0 | Q4 saves ~60 MB, less than a browser tab |\n| 0.5B | Q6_K (~0.41 GB) | Q8_0 (~0.53 GB) | Q4_K_M except low-memory mode | q8_0 | Contested sensitivity; Q8 fits |\n| 1.5B general | Q5_K_M (~1.07 GB) | Q4_K_M (~0.92 GB) | Q4_0, Q3, Q2 | q8_0 | Quality-per-byte sweet spot |\n| 3B general | Q4_K_M (~1.83 GB) floor | Q5_K_M (~2.14 GB) at 4K | ≤Q3 | q8_0 | 8 GB plus context forces Q4 |\n| 1.5B–3B code | Q5_K_M or Q6_K | Q4_K_M as floor | ≤Q4_0 | q8_0 | Format-sensitive; pass@1 drops are visible |\n\nKV quantization is the dominant memory lever at long context. Ollama describes q8_0 as \"a very small loss in precision\" and q4_0 as \"a small-medium loss... more noticeable at higher context sizes\" (Ollama, 2026). A GPU measurement on a 30B model at 128K found FP16 KV at 768 MiB, q8_0 at 408 MiB (−47%), and q4_0 at 216 MiB (−72%); prompt throughput was unchanged, but q4_0 generation fell 11.9% at 24K and **36.8% at 110K** (llama.cpp discussion #20969, 2026).\n\n## Small models are reliability-limited, not faculty-limited\n\nThe sub-4B gains from 2024–2026 came mainly from over-training on filtered and synthetic data plus distillation from 7B–70B teachers, not from new architectures. SmolLM2-1.7B used roughly 11 trillion tokens in a multi-stage web, math, code, and instruction mix (Allal et al., 2025). A survey of 70 small models finds recent models typically use more than 1.5 trillion tokens, far above Chinchilla's roughly 20 tokens per parameter, and above about 1 trillion tokens data quality outweighs token count (Lu et al., 2025).\n\nThe failure modes are compositional. Small models handle single-step transformations and local pattern matching, then collapse on multi-step reasoning, long-horizon tool use, factual recall, non-English tasks, and safety. Qwen2.5-1.5B scores 68.5 on GSM8K but 35.0 on MATH; Qwen2.5-0.5B scores 41.6 versus 19.5; Phi-3.5 scores 86.2 versus 48.5; Llama 3.2 3B scores 77.7 versus 48.0 (Qwen Team, 2024; Microsoft, 2024; Meta, 2024).\n\nThe capability contract follows. A 135M model is for autocomplete, tone or format rewriting, keyword or field extraction, and classification — not chat, facts, arithmetic, code, or multi-turn memory. A 0.5B is for short replies, one-paragraph rewrites, summarising under 500 words, and flat key–value extraction — not facts, arithmetic, nested JSON, non-English, or more than two or three simultaneous constraints. A 1.5B is for paragraph drafting, email replies, short code with a code-tuned variant, single-turn RAG with citations, and one-level JSON — not multi-step math, tool calls, long context, or non-English nuance.\n\nAdvertised context is an upper bound. RULER evaluated 17 long-context models on 13 tasks and found almost all degrade sharply with length; although all claimed at least 32K, only half maintained satisfactory performance at 32K (Hsieh et al., 2024). Llama 3.2 1B reports 96.8 exact match on single-needle NIAH but only 20.3 F1 on InfiniteBench En.QA, and Phi-3.5 Mini's RULER average falls from 94.3 at 4K to 63.6 at 128K, while Llama 3.1 8B retains 77.0 at 128K (Meta, 2024; Microsoft, 2024).\n\nThe 2026 candidates are credible but vendor-reported. Qwen3.5-2B is Apache 2.0, 262K context, with thinking and non-thinking modes; its 2B is the quality sweet spot at MMLU-Pro 55.3/66.5, IFEval 61.2/78.6, and BFCL-V4 43.6, while the 0.8B is much weaker (Qwen Team, 2026). SmolLM3-3B is fully open weights, data, and recipe, 11.2 trillion tokens, 64K context, six languages, hybrid think/no-think, IFEval 76.7, and RULER-64K 67.9 (Hugging Face, 2025). Phi-4-mini is 3.8B, MIT-licensed, 5 trillion tokens, 128K context, MMLU 67.3, MMLU-Pro 52.8, GSM8K 88.6, and MATH 64.0 (Microsoft,