lity fail as an absolute floor plus a confirmed large delta. Per-item logging unlocks every paired statistic for the cost of one file format; splitting the speed gate converts a blind threshold into a useful one; and the floor-plus-delta removes most flakiness while still catching breakage.\n\n| Element | Current state | Verdict | Change | Target |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Suite size | 14 tasks, item counts unstated | Coverage strength, low power | Add a stratified 2,000-item subset across four domains | ≥2,000 items; ≥120 per task |\n| Scoring | Point accuracy | Underpowered | Store per-item outcome: correct, parse-fail, schema-fail | 100% logged |\n| Statistics | None stated | Major gap | Paired McNemar, Wilson CI, Holm, wild cluster bootstrap | α = 0.05; always report CI |\n| `testHash` | SHA-256 of test content | Necessary, insufficient | `suiteFingerprint` over rendered prompts, answers, metric hash, few-shot pool, tokenizer hash, model hash, sampler, runtime commit | One hash, eight components |\n| Baseline | Single versioned number | Insufficient | Seven runs: median, MAD, 95% CI, environment manifest; expires on OS, driver, or runtime change | ≥7 runs; 90-day expiry |\n| Quality gate | Hard fail on any drop | Too sensitive | Floor at baseline − 5 pp, plus 5 pp delta at p < 0.001 confirmed on a second run | δ = 5 pp; p = 0.001 |\n| Per-task gate | Per-task | Flaky | 15 pp warn; two consecutive runs fail; never fail on n < 120 | 15 pp, two runs |\n| Parse failures | Untracked | Silent bias | First-class metric; +1 pp over baseline is T0 | ≤1% |\n| Speed gate | Warn at 20% | Insensitive | Split TTFT and TPOT; warn 7%/10%, fail 20%/25% on two runs | Warn 7%; fail 20% |\n| Repetitions | Unknown | Likely too few | One warm-up plus ten measured; median and MAD; CV ≤ 5% | 10 reps |\n| Thermal | Unhandled | Confound | Three blocks; flag if block 3 exceeds block 1 by 5% | ≤5% drift |\n| Config matrix | Full cross-product | Wasteful | Quality axes quant/KV/context; speed axes threads/batch/mmap; 200-item smoke | 200-item smoke |\n| Contamination | None | Unaddressed | One private holdout; refresh 10% quarterly; canary string | One holdout |\n| Comparability | Implicit | Fragile | Run manifest: hardware, OS, runtime commit, model hash, sampler, suite fingerprint | Manifest required |\n\n## Privacy is an engineering property, not a slogan\n\nLocal-first software is about ownership of mutable user documents, not computation. Kleppmann and colleagues treat the local copy as primary and servers as secondary replicas; their seven ideals are no spinners, work not trapped on one device, network optional, seamless collaboration, the Long Now, security and privacy by default, and ultimate ownership (Kleppmann et al., 2019). The technical core is CRDTs for mergeable documents, notes, and editors, not model artifacts. The Long Now ideal assumes the last released software keeps running and formats stay readable; for AI, behavior also depends on tokenizer, chat template, quantization, runtime kernels, and hardware.\n\nThe perception evidence is double-edged. In a controlled VR study with 60 participants, one group was told they were using cloud models and another local or on-premise models, while both were local; the \"local\" framing produced significantly higher UX and perceived response quality, and perceived data security rose significantly (p = .009, d = −0.52), while trust after interaction did not differ (VR framing study, 2026). A CHI EA 2026 on-device study found \"conditional trust\": participants reported epistemic uncertainty about data protection despite technically secure local deployment, and concluded that \"invisible security measures are insufficient to foster trust\" (CHI EA, 2026).\n\nThe technical evidence says local inference is not a confidentiality boundary. \"The Illusion of Local Privacy\" tested model loading, runtime memory, wrapper persistence, and serving; a 24-hour AFL++ campaign with more than 12 million executions found no parser crashes or malformed GGUF loads, but recovered prompts from plaintext allocator memory, found wrappers persisting prompts in plaintext, found a llama.cpp flaw letting one authenticated client restore another's saved state in **200 out of 200 trials**, and found a prompt-prefix remote timing oracle (Ibrahim et al., 2026).\n\nLicensing is part of the trust story because weights are not code. The Llama 3.1 license grants use, reproduction, distribution, and modification, but distribution requires a copy of the agreement, a prominent \"Built with Llama\" notice, the specified notice text, and, for derivatives, \"Llama\" at the beginning of the model name; it has a 700 million monthly active user threshold and terminates on breach with a deletion requirement (Meta, 2024). Gemma redistribution requires the use restrictions as an enforceable provision, a copy of the agreement, prominent modification notices, and a notice file, and Google reserves the right to restrict usage remotely or otherwise (Google, 2026).\n\nThe claim hierarchy is the copy deck. L0 is \"inference runs on this computer,\" which almost every local tool can claim; L1 is \"we operate no service that receives prompts,\" which most can claim; L2 is \"no analytics, telemetry, or crash SDK,\" which many can claim; L3 is \"normal use makes no network requests,\" which few can honestly claim because update checks and downloads violate it; L4 is \"air-gapped mode verified,\" which is rare. ModelDuck should claim L0–L2, offer L3 through an Offline Mode that blocks catalog refresh, update checks, and telemetry, and claim L4 only after a tested air-gap mode.\n\n## Inference-time features: cheap wins, expensive traps\n\nRAG is the most credible usefulness upgrade, but direct evidence at 0.5B–3B is thinner than at 7B+. On CRAG, most advanced LLMs answered at most 34% correctly; straightforward RAG raised this only to 44%, and state-of-the-art industry RAG answered 63% without hallucination (Yang et al., 2024). Long context generally beat RAG on QA, while RAG was better for dialogue and general queries; across 20 LLMs, quality rose then fell as retrieved passages increased, with hard negatives a key cause (Li et al., 2024; Jin et al., 2024).\n\nTool use is narrower than the demos suggest. On BFCL v4, `xLAM-2-1b-fc-r`, a 1B model fine-tuned for function calling, scores 30.44 overall, with agentic sub-scores in the low single digits (Patil et al., 2025). A 2025 BFCL-based edge study found 1–3B models significantly outperformed sub-1B models, reaching up to 65.74% overall and 55.62% multi-turn accuracy with hybrid SFT, PEFT, RL, and DPO optimization, while TinyAgent reports that a 1.1B model with curated function-calling data, tool retrieval, and quantization can achieve or surpass GPT-4-Turbo's function calling for a local Mac assistant, but that is an authors' claim on a narrow task (Haque et al., 2025; Erdogan et al., 2024).\n\nPrompt and KV caching are the best latency lever. Prompt Cache reports time-to-first-token improvements from 8× on GPU to **60× on CPU** for long, overlapping prompts, without changing outputs (Gim et al., 2024). llama.cpp supports prefix reuse and slot persistence: `cache_prompt` defaults true, `--cache-reuse N` controls minimum chunk reuse, and `/slots/{id}?action=save|restore` persists state, while node-llama-cpp exposes `saveStateToFile()` and `loadStateFromFile()` plus a custom `contextShift` strategy (llama.cpp discussion #13606, 2025; node-llama-cpp, 2026). The KV cache is the memory constraint: a Llama-3.2-3B-class model needs about 112 KiB per token in FP16, roughly 450 MB at 4K and 900 MB at 8K; q8_0 roughly halves this.\n\nTrust features are cheap and evidence-backed, but they can backfire. A human audit of four generative search engines found only **51.5% of generated sentences fully supported by citations** and only 74.5% of citations supporting their sentence, and on ELI5 even the best systems lacked complete citation support 50% of the time (Liu et al., 2023; Gao et al., 2023). A roughly 12,000-query, seven-country experiment with a preregistered US sample found reference links and citations significantly increased trust in generative search **even when links or citations were incorrect or hallucinated**, while uncertainty highlighting reduced willingness to trust and share (Li & Aral, 2025).\n","numLines":81,"startLine":38,"totalLines":274}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8aabfb31-265b-40d7-97e3-81ffa269da0b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main","slug":"hidden-watching-bengio"}
