{"parentUuid":"3dee7f71-0eea-45ae-b562-07df1489c0f4","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"70a75a41-7888-4c77-bc13-5ad4f9e80b7c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018RjLqUYZBpRxXc77eiySEX","type":"tool_result","content":"143\t## Claims ModelDuck should stop making or qualify\n144\t\n145\tThe fastest quality improvement available is to stop making claims the evidence cannot support. Table 6 lists the claims most likely to appear in a local-LLM README, why each fails, and a defensible rewrite.\n146\t\n147\t| Claim | Why it fails | Defensible rewrite | Evidence |\n148\t|---|---|---|---|\n149\t| \"Q4 is within 1–3% of FP16\" or \"Q4 is lossless\" | No source defines the denominator; 8B Q4_K_M is +2.8% PPL, 5.5% RMS token-probability shift, ~1 pt MMLU; 3-bit GSM8K −9.3; 0.5B–3B GGUF task data absent | \"On Llama-3.1-8B with llama.cpp b7600, Q4_K_M was within noise on HellaSwag, IFEval, and TruthfulQA, about 1 point lower on MMLU, and +0.24 PPL; at 0.5B–3B we report measured deltas per artifact.\" | T2: Kurt, 2026; T3: llama.cpp, 2026 |\n150\t| \"Runs on 8 GB\" without context, quant, KV, or browser state | 3B/8K is 6.2–7.2 GB before a browser; 32K is not viable; swap turns 15 tok/s into <1 | \"3B Q4_K_M at 4K with q8 KV fits 8 GB with headroom; 8K is tight; 32K does not fit.\" | T1: Husom et al., 2025; T3: llama.cpp, 2026 |\n151\t| \"128K context\" or \"long context\" | Advertised window is an upper bound; RULER degrades; InfiniteBench 20.3 F1 at 96.8 needle; 3B/32K needs ~3.6 GB KV | \"4–8K working context at 3B, 8–16K at 1.5B; use retrieval for more.\" | T1: Hsieh et al., 2024; T4: Meta, 2024 |\n152\t| \"OpenAI-compatible\" without a tested matrix | Tools, JSON schema, vision, streaming, and error shapes differ across local servers | \"OpenAI-compatible chat/completions/models with streaming; tools, JSON, vision, and errors are covered by a published conformance matrix.\" | T3: LM Studio, 2026; T3: Ollama, 2026 |\n153\t| \"No telemetry,\" \"100% private,\" \"your data never leaves your device\" | Update checks, catalog refreshes, downloads, plaintext traces, localhost attacks, and LAN mode complicate the claim | \"Inference is local; we operate no prompt service; no analytics or crash SDK. Network requests are limited to downloads you start, catalog refresh, and update checks; Offline Mode blocks all three.\" | T2/T6: Ibrahim et al., 2026; Local AI Scope, 2026 |\n154\t| \"GPT-4-like\" or \"rivals 7B\" | Vendor benchmarks are selected; cross-vendor numbers are incomparable; 3B is not an agent | State the capability contract: drafting, extraction, short code, grounded QA; not multi-step reasoning or autonomous tool use. | T4: Qwen Team, 2024; T4: Microsoft, 2024; T1: Lu et al., 2025 |\n155\t| \"Reliable function calling\" or \"agents\" | BFCL agentic scores are low single digits at 1B; τ-bench GPT-4o is <50% success; multi-step tool use is not reliable at 3B | \"Single-turn tools with 3–5 functions, schema-validated arguments, and explicit confirmation; no autonomous multi-step loops.\" | T1: Patil et al., 2025; T2: Yao et al., 2024 |\n156\t| \"Lossless Q8\" | Q8_0 is within 0.04% PPL and ≤0.1 pt at 8B, not mathematically exact; small-model behavior unmeasured | \"Q8_0 is F16-equivalent within measurement error at 8B on our suite.\" | T2: Kurt, 2026; T3: llama.cpp, 2026 |\n157\t| \"Q4 is faster\" | 41% faster in llama.cpp's reference bench, 2% on a 96-core Xeon; hardware and kernel dependent | \"Q4 saves bytes; on this CPU we measure the actual speed difference and publish it.\" | T2: Kurt, 2026; T3: llama.cpp, 2026 |\n158\t| \"Open source\" for Llama or Gemma weights | Licenses restrict use, naming, redistribution, and termination; they are not OSI-open | \"Open weights under the Llama/Gemma license; see the provenance card for terms.\" | T4: Meta, 2024; T4: Google, 2026 |\n159\t| \"Local-first AI\" | Local-first concerns ownership of mutable documents; inference behavior depends on runtime, quant, template, and hardware | \"Local-first data: chats, attachments, settings, and model files are local and exportable; inference is local on this machine.\" | T1: Kleppmann et al., 2019 |\n160\t| \"Energy efficient\" without a meter | GreenBench's advantage is an M4 Pro preprint; x86 laptop energy is unmeasured; modelled energy is not measurement | Omit, or say \"measured on this machine with a meter; boundary: package or system.\" | T2: GreenBench, 2026; T1: Husom et al., 2025 |\n161\t| \"Deterministic benchmark\" | Temperature 0 is not deterministic; batching, prefix caching, and chunked prefill move scores up to 15 pp | \"Pinned, seeded, batch size 1; the noise floor is reported and the run manifest is published.\" | T2: Ouyang et al., 2025; T5: Thinking Machines Lab, 2025 |\n162\t| \"Delete removes all traces\" | WAL, backups, exports, swap, and SSD wear-leveling can retain copies; cryptographic erasure is not guaranteed | \"Scoped delete with a documented residue map; no cryptographic-erasure promise.\" | T2: Murtuza, 2026 |\n163\t| \"Enterprise-grade security\" | No localhost-specific standard; local serving has cross-client state restore and timing-oracle flaws; auth is not tenant isolation | Publish the threat model and the tests: loopback binding, Host/Origin, token, CORS, LAN, tools, supply chain. | T2: Ibrahim et al., 2026; T3: OWASP REST, 2026 |\n164\t\n165\t## A 30/60/90-day roadmap for one maintainer\n166\t\n167\tThe roadmap assumes one maintainer, a small Node codebase, and no new inference backend. It sequences evidence before features, because every later decision depends on the gate being trustworthy; each 30-day block has one primary workstream and two supporting ones.\n168\t\n169\t| Horizon | Workstream | Deliverable | Exit criterion |\n170\t|---|---|---|---|\n171\t| Days 0–30 | Evidence | Per-item logging, eight-component `suiteFingerprint`, run manifest, TTFT/TPOT split, 10 repetitions, thermal blocks | Ten A/A runs show false-alarm rate below 5%; baseline has at least seven runs and a 95% CI |\n172\t| Days 0–30 | Trust | Verify loopback binding, Host/Origin allowlist, per-install token, CORS off, JSON-only state changes, LAN opt-in | Cross-origin fetch, DNS rebinding, Host fuzzing, and cross-token state restore all fail |\n173\t| Days 0–30 | Product | Fit card, fail-closed 70–75% RAM policy, 4K/8K defaults, q8 KV | 3B/8K and 1.5B/8K measured on the target laptop; refusal works at threshold |\n174\t| Days 0–30 | Claims | Rewrite README, homepage, and API docs using Table 6; add evidence tags to model cards | Every public claim has a measured qualifier, an artifact hash, or a license link |\n175\t| Days 31–60 | Quality | Capability-floor quant policy; paired Q4/Q5/Q8 evaluation on 0.5B, 1.5B, 3B | CIs reported; no \"1–3%\" claim remains; per-model defaults updated |\n176\t| Days 31–60 | Retrieval | Hybrid BM25 plus dense retrieval, 22M reranker, extractive citations, abstention; grounded mode | Support rate and false-answer rate improve versus no-RAG; RSS stays within budget |\n177\t| Days 31–60 | Structure | Grammar-constrained final answer, semantic validation, one retry; TPOT measured | 100% schema validity; field accuracy reported; TPOT cost disclosed |\n178\t| Days 31–60 | Gate | Floor at baseline − 5 pp, confirmed 5 pp delta at p < 0.001, per-task 15 pp, parse-failure T0 | Gate catches a seeded regression and stays below 5% false alarms on A/A |\n179\t| Days 61–90 | Onboarding | Hardware scan, 2–3 recommendations, license, download, benchmark, first chat | Time-to-first-chat under 10 minutes on an 8 GB laptop |\n180\t| Days 61–90 | Provenance | Provenance card, license gate, SHA-256 pinning, export/import, scoped delete | Hash-mismatch and license-acceptance tests pass; export round-trips in CI |\n181\t| Days 61–90 | Privacy UX | Offline Mode, network ledger, API compatibility matrix and conformance tests | Zero unexpected egress in offline mode; chat, streaming, and error shapes pass |\n182\t| Days 61–90 | Catalog | Evaluate Qwen3.5-2B, SmolLM3-3B, Phi-4-mini on the same gate; test `ngram-mod` on code | Promotion only if >5 pp with CI; n-gram adopted only if ≥1.2× on code and no chat regression |\n183\t\n184\t## Methods, limitations, and unresolved contradictions\n185\t\n186\tThis review synthesizes six research notes compiled on 30 October 2026: edge and CPU inference; GGUF quantization quality trade-offs; small-model capabilities; evaluation and benchmarking; inference-time features; and local-first product and security. The notes draw mainly on 2022–2026 sources, with older measurement and local-first work where relevant, and include peer-reviewed papers, preprints, official documentation, vendor claims, independent benchmarks, and practitioner reports. The search scope was deliberately narrow: local and edge inference of 0.5B–4B models, GGUF and post-training quantization, small-model capability and failure modes, evaluation methodology for single-machine deployments, privacy and security of local serving, and inference-time features that fit an 8 GB CPU laptop.\n187\t\n188\tSource quality is uneven. The strongest peer-reviewed evidence covers mechanisms (bandwidth-bound decode, KV quantization, attention), evaluation practice (HELM, BIG-bench, MLPerf, McNemar), small-model failure modes (RULER, GSM1k, MMLU-CF, FormatSpread), and local-first principles. The weakest evidence covers the exact product regime: no peer-reviewed study measures 0.5B–3B Q4 GGUF on an 8 GB Windows laptop; no peer-reviewed study measures CPU speculative decoding in that band; no study measures the KV-quantization decode penalty on CPU; and no independent benchmark covers the shortlist with identical prompts, templates, quantization, and CPU decoding. Several recent sources are preprints whose venues could not be verified, including GreenBench (2026), Kurt (2026), Kumar et al. (2024), Fang et al. (2025), Chavan (2026), SubTernary (2026), AdaptiveSD (2026), Speculative Illusion (2026), Ibrahim et al. (2026), Murtuza (2026), Weiss et al. (2026), and Zou (2026).\n189\t\n190\tThe unresolved contradictions are substantive. On small-model quantization, Kumar and OmniQuant imply more caution below 1B, while Fang implies more robustness under adversarial prompts; the current judgment is that clean accuracy is likely more fragile and adversarial robustness can improve, which only a paired clean-and-adversarial evaluation at 0.5B, 1.5B, and 3B can settle. On Q4 speed, llama.cpp's 41% advantage and the Xeon's 2% difference are both real on their hardware, so no universal speed promise is possible without a per-CPU paired benchmark.\n191\t\n192\t## Conclusion\n193\t\n194\tThe literature's most important finding for ModelDuck is not a model, a quant, or a feature. It is that the product's operating point is under-measured, and that the measurement apparatus is itself the differentiator. A local chat studio that can say \"this artifact, on this laptop, at this context, lost 1.2 points on MMLU with a 95% interval, and here is the run manifest\" has something no vendor benchmark can match. A studio that repeats \"Q4 is lossless, 128K context, 100% private\" has three claims it cannot defend and a support queue it cannot close.\n195\t"}]},"uuid":"c7a3d7c5-99a2-4b28-bf74-ef941ffd73bd","timestamp":"2026-10-30T15:04:40.150Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"c:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\reports\\ModelDuck local LLM literature review.md","content":"## Claims ModelDuck should stop making or qualify\n\nThe fastest quality improvement available is to stop making claims the evidence cannot support. Table 6 lists the claims most likely to appear in a local-LLM README, why each fails, and a defensible rewrite.\n\n| Claim | Why it fails | Defensible rewrite | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \"Q4 is within 1–3% of FP16\" or \"Q4 is lossless\" | No source defines the denominator; 8B Q4_K_M is +2.8% PPL, 5.5% RMS token-probability shift, ~1 pt MMLU; 3-bit GSM8K −9.3; 0.5B–3B GGUF task data absent | \"On Llama-3.1-8B with llama.cpp b7600, Q4_K_M was within noise on HellaSwag, IFEval, and TruthfulQA, about 1 point lower on MMLU, and +0.24 PPL; at 0.5B–3B we report measured deltas per artifact.\" | T2: Kurt, 2026; T3: llama.cpp, 2026 |\n| \"Runs on 8 GB\" without context, quant, KV, or browser state | 3B/8K is 6.2–7.2 GB before a browser; 32K is not viable; swap turns 15 tok/s into <1 | \"3B Q4_K_M at 4K with q8 KV fits 8 GB with headroom; 8K is tight; 32K does not fit.\" | T1: Husom et al., 2025; T3: llama.cpp, 2026 |\n| \"128K context\" or \"long context\" | Advertised window is an upper bound; RULER degrades; InfiniteBench 20.3 F1 at 96.8 needle; 3B/32K needs ~3.6 GB KV | \"4–8K working context at 3B, 8–16K at 1.5B; use retrieval for more.\" | T1: Hsieh et al., 2024; T4: Meta, 2024 |\n| \"OpenAI-compatible\" without a tested matrix | Tools, JSON schema, vision, streaming, and error shapes differ across local servers | \"OpenAI-compatible chat/completions/models with streaming; tools, JSON, vision, and errors are covered by a published conformance matrix.\" | T3: LM Studio, 2026; T3: Ollama, 2026 |\n| \"No telemetry,\" \"100% private,\" \"your data never leaves your device\" | Update checks, catalog refreshes, downloads, plaintext traces, localhost attacks, and LAN mode complicate the claim | \"Inference is local; we operate no prompt service; no analytics or crash SDK. Network requests are limited to downloads you start, catalog refresh, and update checks; Offline Mode blocks all three.\" | T2/T6: Ibrahim et al., 2026; Local AI Scope, 2026 |\n| \"GPT-4-like\" or \"rivals 7B\" | Vendor benchmarks are selected; cross-vendor numbers are incomparable; 3B is not an agent | State the capability contract: drafting, extraction, short code, grounded QA; not multi-step reasoning or autonomous tool use. | T4: Qwen Team, 2024; T4: Microsoft, 2024; T1: Lu et al., 2025 |\n| \"Reliable function calling\" or \"agents\" | BFCL agentic scores are low single digits at 1B; τ-bench GPT-4o is <50% success; multi-step tool use is not reliable at 3B | \"Single-turn tools with 3–5 functions, schema-validated arguments, and explicit confirmation; no autonomous multi-step loops.\" | T1: Patil et al., 2025; T2: Yao et al., 2024 |\n| \"Lossless Q8\" | Q8_0 is within 0.04% PPL and ≤0.1 pt at 8B, not mathematically exact; small-model behavior unmeasured | \"Q8_0 is F16-equivalent within measurement error at 8B on our suite.\" | T2: Kurt, 2026; T3: llama.cpp, 2026 |\n| \"Q4 is faster\" | 41% faster in llama.cpp's reference bench, 2% on a 96-core Xeon; hardware and kernel dependent | \"Q4 saves bytes; on this CPU we measure the actual speed difference and publish it.\" | T2: Kurt, 2026; T3: llama.cpp, 2026 |\n| \"Open source\" for Llama or Gemma weights | Licenses restrict use, naming, redistribution, and termination; they are not OSI-open | \"Open weights under the Llama/Gemma license; see the provenance card for terms.\" | T4: Meta, 2024; T4: Google, 2026 |\n| \"Local-first AI\" | Local-first concerns ownership of mutable documents; inference behavior depends on runtime, quant, template, and hardware | \"Local-first data: chats, attachments, settings, and model files are local and exportable; inference is local on this machine.\" | T1: Kleppmann et al., 2019 |\n| \"Energy efficient\" without a meter | GreenBench's advantage is an M4 Pro preprint; x86 laptop energy is unmeasured; modelled energy is not measurement | Omit, or say \"measured on this machine with a meter; boundary: package or system.\" | T2: GreenBench, 2026; T1: Husom et al., 2025 |\n| \"Deterministic benchmark\" | Temperature 0 is not deterministic; batching, prefix caching, and chunked prefill move scores up to 15 pp | \"Pinned, seeded, batch size 1; the noise floor is reported and the run manifest is published.\" | T2: Ouyang et al., 2025; T5: Thinking Machines Lab, 2025 |\n| \"Delete removes all traces\" | WAL, backups, exports, swap, and SSD wear-leveling can retain copies; cryptographic erasure is not guaranteed | \"Scoped delete with a documented residue map; no cryptographic-erasure promise.\" | T2: Murtuza, 2026 |\n| \"Enterprise-grade security\" | No localhost-specific standard; local serving has cross-client state restore and timing-oracle flaws; auth is not tenant isolation | Publish the threat model and the tests: loopback binding, Host/Origin, token, CORS, LAN, tools, supply chain. | T2: Ibrahim et al., 2026; T3: OWASP REST, 2026 |\n\n## A 30/60/90-day roadmap for one maintainer\n\nThe roadmap assumes one maintainer, a small Node codebase, and no new inference backend. It sequences evidence before features, because every later decision depends on the gate being trustworthy; each 30-day block has one primary workstream and two supporting ones.\n\n| Horizon | Workstream | Deliverable | Exit criterion |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Days 0–30 | Evidence | Per-item logging, eight-component `suiteFingerprint`, run manifest, TTFT/TPOT split, 10 repetitions, thermal blocks | Ten A/A runs show false-alarm rate below 5%; baseline has at least seven runs and a 95% CI |\n| Days 0–30 | Trust | Verify loopback binding, Host/Origin allowlist, per-install token, CORS off, JSON-only state changes, LAN opt-in | Cross-origin fetch, DNS rebinding, Host fuzzing, and cross-token state restore all fail |\n| Days 0–30 | Product | Fit card, fail-closed 70–75% RAM policy, 4K/8K defaults, q8 KV | 3B/8K and 1.5B/8K measured on the target laptop; refusal works at threshold |\n| Days 0–30 | Claims | Rewrite README, homepage, and API docs using Table 6; add evidence tags to model cards | Every public claim has a measured qualifier, an artifact hash, or a license link |\n| Days 31–60 | Quality | Capability-floor quant policy; paired Q4/Q5/Q8 evaluation on 0.5B, 1.5B, 3B | CIs reported; no \"1–3%\" claim remains; per-model defaults updated |\n| Days 31–60 | Retrieval | Hybrid BM25 plus dense retrieval, 22M reranker, extractive citations, abstention; grounded mode | Support rate and false-answer rate improve versus no-RAG; RSS stays within budget |\n| Days 31–60 | Structure | Grammar-constrained final answer, semantic validation, one retry; TPOT measured | 100% schema validity; field accuracy reported; TPOT cost disclosed |\n| Days 31–60 | Gate | Floor at baseline − 5 pp, confirmed 5 pp delta at p < 0.001, per-task 15 pp, parse-failure T0 | Gate catches a seeded regression and stays below 5% false alarms on A/A |\n| Days 61–90 | Onboarding | Hardware scan, 2–3 recommendations, license, download, benchmark, first chat | Time-to-first-chat under 10 minutes on an 8 GB laptop |\n| Days 61–90 | Provenance | Provenance card, license gate, SHA-256 pinning, export/import, scoped delete | Hash-mismatch and license-acceptance tests pass; export round-trips in CI |\n| Days 61–90 | Privacy UX | Offline Mode, network ledger, API compatibility matrix and conformance tests | Zero unexpected egress in offline mode; chat, streaming, and error shapes pass |\n| Days 61–90 | Catalog | Evaluate Qwen3.5-2B, SmolLM3-3B, Phi-4-mini on the same gate; test `ngram-mod` on code | Promotion only if >5 pp with CI; n-gram adopted only if ≥1.2× on code and no chat regression |\n\n## Methods, limitations, and unresolved contradictions\n\nThis review synthesizes six research notes compiled on 30 October 2026: edge and CPU inference; GGUF quantization quality trade-offs; small-model capabilities; evaluation and benchmarking; inference-time features; and local-first product and security. The notes draw mainly on 2022–2026 sources, with older measurement and local-first work where relevant, and include peer-reviewed papers, preprints, official documentation, vendor claims, independent benchmarks, and practitioner reports. The search scope was deliberately narrow: local and edge inference of 0.5B–4B models, GGUF and post-training quantization, small-model capability and failure modes, evaluation methodology for single-machine deployments, privacy and security of local serving, and inference-time features that fit an 8 GB CPU laptop.\n\nSource quality is uneven. The strongest peer-reviewed evidence covers mechanisms (bandwidth-bound decode, KV quantization, attention), evaluation practice (HELM, BIG-bench, MLPerf, McNemar), small-model failure modes (RULER, GSM1k, MMLU-CF, FormatSpread), and local-first principles. The weakest evidence covers the exact product regime: no peer-reviewed study measures 0.5B–3B Q4 GGUF on an 8 GB Windows laptop; no peer-reviewed study measures CPU speculative decoding in that band; no study measures the KV-quantization decode penalty on CPU; and no independent benchmark covers the shortlist with identical prompts, templates, quantization, and CPU decoding. Several recent sources are preprints whose venues could not be verified, including GreenBench (2026), Kurt (2026), Kumar et al. (2024), Fang et al. (2025), Chavan (2026), SubTernary (2026), AdaptiveSD (2026), Speculative Illusion (2026), Ibrahim et al. (2026), Murtuza (2026), Weiss et al. (2026), and Zou (2026).\n\nThe unresolved contradictions are substantive. On small-model quantization, Kumar and OmniQuant imply more caution below 1B, while Fang implies more robustness under adversarial prompts; the current judgment is that clean accuracy is likely more fragile and adversarial robustness can improve, which only a paired clean-and-adversarial evaluation at 0.5B, 1.5B, and 3B can settle. On Q4 speed, llama.cpp's 41% advantage and the Xeon's 2% difference are both real on their hardware, so no universal speed promise is possible without a per-CPU paired benchmark.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe literature's most important finding for ModelDuck is not a model, a quant, or a feature. It is that the product's operating point is under-measured, and that the measurement apparatus is itself the differentiator. A local chat studio that can say \"this artifact, on this laptop, at this context, lost 1.2 points on MMLU with a 95% interval, and here is the run manifest\" has something no vendor benchmark can match. A studio that repeats \"Q4 is lossless, 128K context, 100% private\" has three claims it cannot defend and a support queue it cannot close.\n","numLines":53,"startLine":143,"totalLines":274}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3dee7f71-0eea-45ae-b562-07df1489c0f4","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main","slug":"hidden-watching-bengio"}
