{"parentUuid":"3779599a-c301-4141-8c14-894c2431eb1f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"70a75a41-7888-4c77-bc13-5ad4f9e80b7c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KCcfFXE5K4zYY7ksPSDS4B","type":"tool_result","content":"## 2. Memory-budget fit card and fail-closed policy\n**Recommendation:** Adopt now · **Rank:** 2 · **Evidence:** T1 mechanism + T6 measurements\n**Problem.** “Runs on 8 GB” is not a property of a model; it is a property of a model,\nquantization, context length, KV-cache type, runtime, and how much browser the user\nhas open. A 3B Q4 model at 8K is roughly 6.2–7.2 GB before the browser is counted.\nWhen it crosses the line, the machine swaps and the user experiences a hang, not a\nclean error.\n**Proposal.** Before load, read the GGUF header and estimate: weights, KV cache at the\nselected context and KV precision, runtime buffers, and an OS/browser reserve. Show a\nthree-state card — **Comfortable**, **Tight**, **Will not fit** — with the largest safe\ncontext for that machine. Enforce the verdict at 70–75% of available RAM: refuse the\nload with a specific remedy (“reduce context to 4K,” “use q8 KV,” “close the browser”)\nrather than allowing swap.\nExpose the same estimate in the model picker and in the API error path. Record the\nactual peak RSS after load and compare it with the estimate so the estimator can be\ncalibrated over time.\n**Evidence.** The review’s arithmetic puts 3B/8K at 6.2–7.2 GB before a browser,\nwhile 1.5B/8K and 3B/4K sit in a safer range. KV cache, not weight bits, is the\nbinding constraint at long context: a Llama-3.2-3B-class model needs about 112 KiB\nper token in FP16, roughly 0.9 GB at 8K and 3.6 GB at 32K. q8_0 KV roughly halves\nthat with near-free quality loss; symmetric q4_0 KV is a quality cliff.\n**Smallest credible experiment.** On the target 8 GB laptop, load 3B/8K, 3B/4K, and\n1.5B/8K. Record peak RSS, swap, time-to-first-token, and tokens per second. Verify\nthat the 70–75% refusal triggers before the OS swaps.\n**Pass gate.** No configuration that passes the card swaps or drops below 5 tokens\nper second; every refusal names a concrete remedy; the estimate is within 15% of\nmeasured peak RSS.\n**Cost and risk.** Low to medium. The estimator needs model-architecture metadata and\na small amount of calibration. Be conservative: a false “comfortable” verdict is much\nworse than a false “tight” verdict.\n---\n## 3. Prompt/KV cache with cache-aware ordering\n**Recommendation:** Adopt now · **Rank:** 3 · **Evidence:** T1 + T3 + T6\n**Problem.** Multi-turn chat re-prefills the same system prompt, retrieved context,\nand conversation prefix on every turn. On CPU that can turn a short reply into a\nminute of prefill, especially at 4K–8K context. The user perceives the model as slow\neven when decode speed is fine.\n**Proposal.** Keep a stable prefix: system prompt, tool/schema instructions, and\nlong-lived retrieved context first; volatile turns last. Reuse the KV cache for the\nunchanged prefix, expose cache-hit tokens and TTFT in the UI, and persist a\nconversation checkpoint so reopening a chat does not re-prefill from zero.\nUse llama.cpp’s prefix reuse and slot persistence (`cache_prompt`, `--cache-reuse`,\n`/slots/{id}?action=save|restore`) or node-llama-cpp’s `saveStateToFile()` /\n`loadStateFromFile()`. Choose a context-shift policy deliberately: shifting the\nwindow destroys the cache, so summarize or truncate at a turn boundary instead of\nsilently shifting mid-conversation.\n**Evidence.** Prompt Cache reports 8× TTFT improvement on GPU and up to 60× on CPU\nfor long overlapping prompts without changing outputs (Gim et al., 2024). llama.cpp\ncan reuse a 43-token prefix as one cached block. The review’s own tests show context\nshift is the enemy of cache reuse.\n**Smallest credible experiment.** Run a 20-turn scripted chat with cache on and off.\nMeasure TTFT, cache-hit tokens, peak RSS, and answer quality on the same prompts.\n**Pass gate.** Median TTFT drops by at least 2× after the first turn; cache-hit tokens\nare visible; no answer changes on a fixed-seed comparison; RSS stays within the\nmemory card’s budget.\n**Cost and risk.** Low. The main risk is stale cache after a settings change; version\nthe cache key by model hash, sampler, and prefix hash.\n---\n## 4. Loopback security baseline and LAN opt-in\n**Recommendation:** Adopt now · **Rank:** 4 · **Evidence:** T5/T6 + T3\n**Problem.** “Bound to 127.0.0.1” is not a security boundary. A malicious website can\nattempt cross-origin requests, DNS rebinding can reach localhost, and a local server\nthat trusts any `Origin` or stores state per client can leak one client’s data to\nanother. Local tools have already shipped CORS `*` defaults and cross-client state\nrestore bugs.\n**Proposal.** Enforce a small, testable baseline:\n- bind to `127.0.0.1` by default; require an explicit LAN opt-in and a warning;\n- validate `Host` against an allowlist and reject unexpected `Origin` values;\n- require a per-install bearer token for `/v1/*` when authentication is enabled;\n- keep CORS off by default; allow only explicit origins;\n- accept JSON content types for state-changing routes and reject simple-form CSRF;\n- isolate saved slots/state by token, not just by slot id;\n- put LAN mode behind TLS and a separate threat-model page.\n**Evidence.** The review documents “0.0.0.0 Day,” an Ollama CVE, llama.cpp CORS `*`,\nand a llama.cpp flaw that let one authenticated client restore another client’s saved\nstate in 200 of 200 trials (Ibrahim et al., 2026). OWASP’s REST guidance supports\nexplicit origin checks, token auth, and content-type enforcement.\n**Smallest credible experiment.** Run four negative tests: cross-origin `fetch`,\nDNS-rebinding host header, Host-header fuzzing, and cross-token state restore. All\nmust fail closed. Then run the same tests with LAN mode explicitly enabled and verify\nthe warning and TLS requirements.\n**Pass gate.** All four attacks fail; the server logs the rejected origin/host; no\nstate is readable across tokens; the LAN opt-in cannot be enabled accidentally.\n**Cost and risk.** Low. The main risk is breaking legitimate local integrations;\nprovide a documented token and a clear error message.\n---\n## 5. Claim hierarchy, Offline Mode, and a network ledger\n**Recommendation:** Adopt now · **Rank:** 5 · **Evidence:** T1/T2/T6\n**Problem.** “No telemetry” and “your data never leaves your device” are not the same\nclaim, and neither is automatically true. Update checks, catalog refreshes, model\ndownloads, crash reporters, and browser requests can all create egress. Users cannot\nverify a claim they cannot see.\n**Proposal.** Adopt a five-level claim hierarchy (L0–L4):\n- **L0:** inference runs on this computer;\n- **L1:** we operate no service that receives prompts;\n- **L2:** no analytics, telemetry, or crash SDK;\n- **L3:** normal use makes no network requests;\n- **L4:** air-gapped mode is verified.\nClaim L0–L2 plainly. Offer an **Offline Mode** that blocks catalog refresh, update\nchecks, and downloads, and only then claim L3 for that mode. Claim L4 only after a","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"3fee9d7d-f084-43bf-93bd-c67b7664ac69","timestamp":"2026-10-30T15:04:59.280Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## 2. Memory-budget fit card and fail-closed policy\n**Recommendation:** Adopt now · **Rank:** 2 · **Evidence:** T1 mechanism + T6 measurements\n**Problem.** “Runs on 8 GB” is not a property of a model; it is a property of a model,\nquantization, context length, KV-cache type, runtime, and how much browser the user\nhas open. A 3B Q4 model at 8K is roughly 6.2–7.2 GB before the browser is counted.\nWhen it crosses the line, the machine swaps and the user experiences a hang, not a\nclean error.\n**Proposal.** Before load, read the GGUF header and estimate: weights, KV cache at the\nselected context and KV precision, runtime buffers, and an OS/browser reserve. Show a\nthree-state card — **Comfo