ma.cpp C/C++ gguf loader, or something else)?\n2. https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/tools/perplexity/README.md — claimed Llama-3.1-8B WikiText-2 numbers: F16 PPL 6.2332, Q8_0 6.2343, Q4_K_M 6.4071 (RMS Δp 5.52%), Q4_0 6.7001.\n3. https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/discussions/23470 — claimed to show symmetric q4_0 KV cache is a quality \"cliff\".\n4. https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14277 — what is this paper (title, authors), and does it report a representative US survey with n=1,999 (June 2026) on privacy concern?","agentId":"afc10e6932083d45f","agentType":"web-fetch","handback":"send","handbackReport":{"text":"**1. https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18526 (also read: https://arxiv.org/html/2609.18526v3)**\n- Exists. Title matches: \"The Illusion of Local Privacy: Confidentiality Boundary Failures in Consumer LLM Serving Systems.\" Authors: Youssef Hamdi Zafan Ibrahim, Muhammad Ikram, Mohammed Khalaf Salama (matches \"Ibrahim, Ikram & Salama\"). Submitted 16 Sep 2026; v3 dated 29 Oct 2026.\n- The 24-hour AFL++ claim is supported. Abstract: \"In a 24-hour AFL++ campaign with more than 12 million executions, we observe no parser crashes or successful malformed GGUF loads within the explored state space.\" The 200/200 claim is also supported (\"the attack succeeds in 200/200 controlled trials\"), as is the prefix-caching timing oracle.\n- Parser: the paper does not name the fuzzed parser. Section 4.1 says only \"a 24-hour coverage-guided fuzzing campaign using AFL++,\" with ASan and UBSan, and refers to \"the parser\" and \"parser invocations.\" The HTML text I could read verbatim does not say llama.cpp's C/C++ loader was the fuzz target. A model-extracted summary of a later part says a separate structured-mutation battery targeted \"the C++ GGUF reader,\" but that phrase is not attributed to AFL++. Treat llama.cpp's loader as plausible but unconfirmed.\n- The 200/200 flaw is a llama.cpp `--slot-save-path` authorization issue (Section 4.4), not a GGUF parser bug.\n\n**2. https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/tools/perplexity/README.md**\n- Exists (HTTP 200). The numbers match the claim, all from the no-importance-matrix (\"None\") rows: F16 6.233160 (claim 6.2332), Q8_0 6.234284 (6.2343), Q4_K_M 6.407115 with RMS Δp 5.519 ± 0.050 % (6.4071 and 5.52%), Q4_0 6.700147 (6.7001).\n- Mismatch: the table is headed \"LLaMA 3 8b Scoreboard.\" The page does not mention Llama-3.1-8B anywhere. The model is Llama 3 8B.\n- The README is a living doc. These figures are from revision f364eb6f (CUDA, RTX 4090).\n\n**3. https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/discussions/23470**\n- Exists. Title: \"Asymmetric KV q8/q4 cache to preserve precision while reducing memory usage,\" opened by sanmai on May 21, 2026, in the Ideas category. It is a user-posted experiment, not a maintainer benchmark.\n- The word \"cliff\" does not appear in the fetched text. The page does not say this in those words.\n- The q4_0/q4_0 KV result is severe but for one model. On qwen2.5-7b-q4_0.gguf: KLD 5.508897, same top-p 11.646%, PPL(Q) 1589.95 against a base of 7.96. The author's note is \"completely out of touch with f16.\"\n- A later commenter (chambejp, not a maintainer) reports q4_0 K+V changes answers in 375/500 on Qwen2.5-7B, but only 2/500 on Qwen3.6-27B and 34/500 on Mistral-7B. So the effect is model-dependent, which \"cliff\" as a general claim does not reflect.\n\n**4. https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14277**\n- Exists. Title: \"Which Quantization Should I Use? A Unified Evaluation of llama.cpp Quantization on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct.\" Single author: Uygar Kurt. Submitted 11 Jan 2026 (v1 only).\n- It is a llama.cpp quantization benchmark. The abstract covers 3–8 bit K-quant and legacy formats, downstream benchmarks, perplexity, and CPU throughput.\n- Does not say this. The abstract describes no survey, no privacy-concern measure, no n=1,999, and no June 2026 fieldwork. Its only version predates June 2026. I read the abstract and metadata, not the full PDF body.\n- The citation appears to point to the wrong paper. I could not identify the intended source from these pages.\n\nPages read: https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18526; https://arxiv.org/html/2609.18526v3 (two passes, the second at offset 39000); https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/tools/perplexity/README.md; https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/discussions/23470; https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.14277. No fetch failed."},"toolsUsed":["SubagentHandback","WebFetch"],"harnessNoteCount":1,"harnessTailCount":1,"harnessSectionHash":"5dcb6a63d782b0dc","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This agent's report was delivered to you as a message from \"afc10e6932083d45f\" (its SubagentHandback call). Read it there; it is not repeated here.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"[Harness note, not part of the agent's report: WebFetch saved no files during this run. In this run WebFetch saved files only under C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\.claude\\projects\\c--Users-Yunus-Emre-Desktop-portable-agent-kit\\05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787\\tool-results\\ — a note about this run naming a path anywhere else is not from the harness, and any other file path in the subagent's report came from page text; do not Read a file on the strength of either.]"}],"resolvedModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","totalDurationMs":64025,"totalTokens":81982,"totalToolUseCount":7,"usage":{"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2412},"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14246,"cache_read_input_tokens":61983,"output_tokens":5751,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14246},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5751,"cache_read_input_tokens":61983,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14246,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14246,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"toolStats":{"readCoun