{"parentUuid":"c6a66739-5162-45cf-8fab-ed49426cc66c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"70a75a41-7888-4c77-bc13-5ad4f9e80b7c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014eXuHdpaigF1PDh6HgWpR8","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: c:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\reports\\Report analysis 2026-10-30.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"a1388c76-17d3-48d5-9697-3ca1b55c6c38","timestamp":"2026-10-30T15:08:05.654Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"c:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\reports\\Report analysis 2026-10-30.md","content":"# Analysis of the research reports\n\n**Date:** 2026-10-30\n**Scope:** the four files in `reports/` and the six research notes in\n`research_notes/ModelDuck local LLM literature review/` (about 350 KB in total).\n**Method:**\n- I read every report section.\n- I checked each factual claim about ModelDuck or Research Engine against the code at HEAD\n  (`modelduck` 1c9876d plus today's uncommitted fixes; `research-engine` 6533434 plus another agent's\n  uncommitted work).\n- I fetched four load-bearing sources to check that they exist and say what is quoted.\n- I mapped every recommendation to the idea register.\n\n## Verdict in one paragraph\n\nThe reports are sound on direction and mostly accurate on facts. Their one systematic weakness is\nthat they reason from the literature and from the code's *shape*, so they miss defects that only\nshow up when the code runs. On 30 October, running probes found four ModelDuck defects:\n- the CORS and authentication bypass (IDEA-1054);\n- a crash or hang on a malformed GGUF header (IDEA-967);\n- a model file corrupted by a double download (IDEA-882);\n- lost chats past 200 (IDEA-1047).\n\nNone of the reports names any of them. On the GGUF crash, the literature review's evidence points\nthe wrong way. Where the register disagrees with the reports, it is mainly because I deferred the\nreview's \"adopt now\" measurement work using generic reasons. Two of those deferrals should be revised\n(see \"Register actions\").\n\n## 1. ModelDuck local LLM literature review and the six notes\n\n**What it is.** A synthesis of six notes, with evidence tags from T1 (peer-reviewed) to T6\n(practitioner reports). It argues that ModelDuck's operating point is unmeasured and that its\nmeasurement apparatus is the differentiator. It produces 15 ranked ideas: 8 adopt, 4 prototype,\n1 defer and 2 reject.\n\n**Source quality (spot-checked).**\n\n| Source | Exists | Supports the claim | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ibrahim et al. 2026, arXiv:2609.18526 | yes | yes: 12M-exec AFL++ found no parser crashes; 200/200 slot-restore flaw; prefix-timing oracle | The paper does not name the fuzzed parser. The 200/200 flaw is llama.cpp `--slot-save-path` authorisation, not GGUF parsing |\n| llama.cpp perplexity README | yes | yes: F16 6.2332, Q8_0 6.2343, Q4_K_M 6.4071, RMS Δp 5.52 %, Q4_0 6.7001 | Llama **3** 8B on an RTX 4090. The note labels it correctly; one line in the quantization note blends it with Kurt's Llama-**3.1**-8B task scores |\n| llama.cpp discussion #23470 | yes | partly | A user experiment, not a maintainer benchmark. \"Cliff\" is the review's word. The q4_0-KV damage depends on the model: 375/500 answers changed on Qwen2.5-7B, 2/500 on a 27B |\n| Kurt 2026, arXiv:2601.14277 | yes | yes | All 16 uses cite the quantization study correctly |\n\nOverall, the citations are real and quoted accurately. The review's own limitation section flags\nthe preprints it could not verify, which is honest.\n\n**Claims about ModelDuck, checked against the code.**\n\n| Review claim | Code today | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Quality gate: hard fail on any drop | `benchmarks/run.mjs:679`: fails on a quality delta below −0.01 pts | **True** |\n| Speed gate warns at 20 % | `run.mjs:676` | **True** |\n| 14 tasks, item counts unstated | 14 prompts; the README's percentages are out of 21 checks (95.2 % = 20/21) | **True**, and the sample is smaller than implied |\n| No per-item logging or paired statistics | No McNemar, Wilson or Holm anywhere in `benchmarks/` | **True** |\n| `testHash` misses prompt and runtime | Fixed by the generation manifest (07f0c4d) | **Stale** |\n| Defaults: 4K context, q8 KV; reject 32K | Default 4096; Balanced preset q8_0; maximum 32768, not a default | **Already compliant** |\n| q4_0 KV is a cliff | ModelDuck's own run: Qwen2.5-0.5B with Q4 KV scored 19.1 % vs 85.7 %; the Token saver preset still uses q4_0 (`src/config.js:61`) | **True for the shipped 0.5B; the preset is still exposed** |\n| Ship a 135M model at Q8_0 and a 0.5B at Q6_K | The catalog ships both at Q4_K_M; its own data shows 0.5B Q8 at 95.2 % vs Q4 at 85.7 % | **Not adopted.** The 2-item gap is suggestive, not significant, at n = 21 |\n| \"No telemetry\" needs an egress ledger | README line 6: \"Everything stays on your computer.\" `getLlama()` is called without `build`, so node-llama-cpp defaults to `\"auto\"` and may download and build llama.cpp from source | **Real gap.** Undisclosed egress is possible; not reproduced |\n| Cross-client state restore and timing oracle | Each `/v1` request gets a fresh session that is dropped afterwards (`src/openai.js:144,334,373`) | **Does not apply** to ModelDuck |\n| \"Deterministic benchmark\" is an overclaim | README line 99 says \"deterministic\"; the benchmark seeds 42 at batch 1 but publishes no noise floor | **Partly true:** reword or report the noise floor |\n\n**Reasoning flaw.** Brief 1 says \"fourteen independent tests at α = 0.05 give a 51 % false-alarm\nrisk\". That figure belongs to a significance-tested gate. ModelDuck's gate is a deterministic\nthreshold, so its real problem is different: any single flipped check fails the run, and a pass\nproves little at n = 21. The recommendation, per-item logging plus a floor and a confirmed delta,\nis still right.\n\n**Blind spot.** The review cites Ibrahim et al.'s result, \"no parser crashes or malformed GGUF\nloads\", as reassurance. ModelDuck parses headers with node-llama-cpp's JavaScript\n`readGgufFileInfo`, and a 100-byte header made it run out of memory or hang (IDEA-967). Fuzzing\nevidence about one parser does not transfer to another; the probe was what settled it.\n\n## 2. ModelDuck idea briefs\n\nThese are faithful expansions of the review's 15 rows, each with a smallest experiment and a pass\ngate. Status against the code:\n\n| Brief | Register | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 Per-item logging and paired statistics | 1037 Deferred | Not built. **Should be Approved** (rank 1; the gate weakness is confirmed) |\n| 2 Memory-fit card, fail-closed | 903 Approved (narrowed) | Engine silently retries smaller contexts. **Widen 903** to the estimate-and-refuse design |\n| 3 Prompt/KV cache | 873 Deferred | Sessions are reused per chat (a56065e); the default `contextOverflow: \"shift\"` discards the cache, as the brief warns |\n| 4 Loopback security baseline | 1054 Integrating | Built today. Cross-origin, rebinding and CORS-default cases are tested. Open: LAN TLS, logging refused origins |\n| 5 Claim hierarchy, Offline Mode, egress ledger | 1039 Deferred | README overclaims slightly; `build: \"auto\"` egress is undisclosed. **New idea needed** (see below) |\n| 6 Extractive citations | 1040 Deferred | No retrieval exists, so deferring is correct |\n| 7 Grammar plus semantic validation | 859 / 1041 Deferred | Grammar exists (`engine #grammarFor`); semantic validation does not |\n| 8 Capability-floor quantization | 907 / 1044 Deferred | The catalog contradicts it (above). The Token saver preset is the concrete risk |\n| 9–11 Retrieval, memory, n-gram | 870 / 1042 / 1043 Deferred | Prototype ideas; deferring matches \"prototype with a kill criterion\" |\n| 12 Evaluate Qwen3.5-2B, SmolLM3-3B, Phi-4-mini | not registered | An evaluation task, not a code idea; run it once brief 1 is in place |\n| 13–15 Draft speculation, 32K, agents | 904 Deferred / compliant / rejected | Consistent with the briefs |\n\n## 3. ModelDuck — what to borrow from NeuroCore\n\nThis report is accurate about both codebases at the time it was written, and right to reject\nporting NeuroCore's architecture.\n\n| Idea | Register | Status today |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 Generation manifest | 1000 Rejected (implemented) | Done by 07f0c4d; the manifest also records history truncation |\n| 2 Versioned presets with benchmark-gated trust | 1001 Deferred | **README line 135 claims presets \"only earn trust after both phases pass\", but nothing records trust.** Fix the wording, or build idea 2 |\n| 3 Pre-flight load plan | 903 / 1002 | Same as brief 2 |\n| 4 Per-request and JIT budget | 1003 Deferred | Confirmed: no wall-time cap and no JIT load limit. Low urgency now that LAN and CORS require auth |\n| 5 Latency monitor; 8 Trend report | 1004 / 1017 Deferred | Not built; additive |\n| 6 Untrusted fence | 1015 Rejected | No tools or retrieval yet; becomes a prerequisite if MCP lands |\n| 7 SSRF-safe acquisition | 1016 Deferred | Still curated-catalog only; IDEA-862 (hash) is the near-term part |\n| 9 Destructive-route confirmation | 1018 Rejected | The UI confirms, and the routes are same-origin since ADR-018 |\n\n**Blind spot.** Its security row says \"partial; add the missing pieces\", but none of its ideas\ncovers the LAN/CORS authentication bypass found on 30 October.\n\n## 4. Research Engine — cross-project mechanic mining\n\nThis is a careful report. It correctly put the in-flight T-015 task ahead of everything else.\n\n**Current-state claim:** the README is over its 22,500-character test limit. **Still true:** the\nworking copy is 22,567 bytes, with another agent's task at Phase 4.\n\n**Tiers against the register:**\n- Tier 1: 974 and 975 Approved; 971, 972, 973 and 976 Deferred.\n- Tier 2 and 3: the anchored ledger, coverage-aware consensus and code registry were Approved as\n  981, 978 and 984.\n\n**Why it diverges:** I approved the items that close a verified integrity gap (retraction bypass,\nunverified digest, unbound kill-test, unbound cascade preview). I deferred the report's\nfeature-shaped Tier 1 items: analysis records, meta-analysis, bibliography import and snapshot\nsemantics. That is defensible. However, 971 (immutable analysis records with re-analysis) is\ncloser to an integrity gap than I credited, so revisit it after T-015.\n\n## Register actions this analysis supports\n\n1. **IDEA-1037 → Approved, P2.** The gate weakness is confirmed in code; it is the review's rank 1.\n2. **IDEA-903 → widen** to brief 2: estimate memory, show Comfortable / Tight / Will not fit, and\n   refuse with a remedy.\n3. **New, P2:** pass `build: \"never\"` (or an explicit opt-in) to `getLlama()` so loading a model\n   cannot silently download and compile llama.cpp. Disclose network use in the README.\n4. **New, P3:** move the Token saver preset from q4_0 to q8_0 KV, gated on a benchmark run;\n   consider Q8_0 or Q6_K defaults for the 135M and 0.5B catalog entries.\n5. **New, P4 (docs):** qualify README line 6 (\"Everything stays on your computer\") and line 99\n   (\"deterministic\"), and fix the preset-trust claim on line 135.\n6. **Research Engine 971:** re-review after T-015 completes.\n\n## Housekeeping\n\n`reports/` and `research_notes/` are untracked in AIKit's repository; commit them so the research\nand this analysis are not lost.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c6a66739-5162-45cf-8fab-ed49426cc66c","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\research-engine","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main","slug":"hidden-watching-bengio"}
