{"parentUuid":"ff2bc4ba-4966-4b54-adfd-375cf071c637","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYdb711tmzmt66DdPMQB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've analyzed all four reports and the six research notes, and written the results to [reports/Report analysis 2026-10-30.md](reports/Report%20analysis%202026-10-30.md). The register is unchanged until you decide on the six actions below.\n\n**How reliable they are:** they hold up well on direction and facts. I checked four sources the review leans on: all exist and are quoted accurately. Two of the review's points about them needed correcting:\n- **The fuzzing paper doesn't name the parser it tested.**\n- **The \"q4_0 KV cliff\" comes from one user's experiment,** and the effect varies a lot by model. ModelDuck's own run (19.1% at Q4 KV, against 85.7% normally) shows it does apply to its 0.5B model.\n\n**Their weak spot:** they reason from literature and the code's structure rather than running it. None of the four defects that today's probes found shows up in the reports (website access, the bad-file crash, double-download corruption, lost chats). On the crash, the review cites a fuzzing study that found \"no parser crashes\" as reassurance. That study tested a different parser from the JavaScript one ModelDuck uses, which crashed on a 100-byte file.\n\n**The reports' factual claims about ModelDuck, checked against the code:**\n- **Confirmed:**\n  - the benchmark fails on any drop of more than 0.01 points;\n  - it has only 21 checks and no per-item statistics;\n  - the speed warning triggers at 20%;\n  - the Token saver preset still uses the 4-bit KV setting;\n  - the catalog ships Q4 for the 135M and 0.5B models, against both the review and ModelDuck's own Q8 result.\n- **Out of date:** the prompt-fingerprint gap was closed by commit `07f0c4d`.\n- **Doesn't apply:** the cross-client cache leak, because each API request gets its own session.\n\n**New problems the analysis turned up:**\n- **Possible silent downloads.** ModelDuck lets node-llama-cpp download and compile llama.cpp when no prebuilt binary fits, while the README says \"Everything stays on your computer.\" This comes from the library's documented default; I haven't triggered it.\n- **An unsupported README claim.** It says presets \"only earn trust after both phases pass\", but nothing records that.\n- **Research-engine still fails its own test.** Its README is still over the 22,500-character limit, as the research-engine report warned.\n\n**Where my verdicts and the reports disagree:** I deferred the review's top-ranked measurement work (1037) with a generic reason, and narrowed the memory-fit idea (903). For research-engine, I approved verified integrity gaps over the report's feature-style tier 1 items, which I think is defensible. 971 (immutable analysis records) deserves a second look once that project's active task finishes.\n\n**Register changes the analysis supports:**\n1. **1037: approve as P2.** It's the review's #1, and the code confirms the gate is a raw threshold.\n2. **903: widen it** to estimate memory, show whether the model fits, and refuse with a fix when it doesn't.\n3. **New, P2:** set `build: \"never\"` in ModelDuck so loading a model can never silently download and compile llama.cpp.\n4. **New, P3:** move the Token saver preset to 8-bit KV, after a benchmark run, and reconsider the 135M and 0.5B default quantizations.\n5. **New, P4 (docs):** qualify three README claims: \"Everything stays on your computer\", \"deterministic\", and presets \"earning trust\".\n6. **Research-engine 971:** re-review once its active task completes.\n\nShould I apply these six register changes? Separately, I'd also recommend committing `reports/` and `research_notes/`, which are still untracked."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5375,"cache_read_in