rity risks, or architectural friction observed in the source repo or inherent to the pattern.>\n\n#### How to Combat These\n<Concrete engineering mitigations we must apply when integrating into our target project (e.g., stdlib-only adaptation, bounded queues, atomic fsync, strict schema validation, timeout caps).>\n\n#### Cold-Start Agent Implementation Blueprint\n1:# Dossier: Evidence-Grade Measurement, Trust and Small-Model Features (ModelDuck)\n8:## 1. Executive Summary & Core Architecture\n16:### Coverage map: the review's fifteen ideas\n38:## 2. Target Routing Matrix\n55:## 3. Extracted Ideas & Cold-Start Implementation Blueprints\n57:### `IDEA-1037`: Paired-Statistics Benchmark Gate with Per-Item Outcomes\n# Dossier: Evidence-Grade Measurement, Trust and Small-Model Features (ModelDuck)\n\n- **Source URL**: `reports/ModelDuck local LLM literature review.md` (local synthesis, 2026-10-30)\n- **Source License**: `Local research report; cited papers and specifications retain their own licenses`\n- **Analyzed Commit / Date**: `working tree` / `2026-10-30`\n- **Primary Stack**: Node.js 20+ ESM, standard-library HTTP server, vanilla HTML/CSS/JS UI, `node-llama-cpp` CPU GGUF inference, OpenAI-compatible chat API.\n\n## 1. Executive Summary & Core Architecture\n\nThe 2026 ModelDuck review reaches a narrow conclusion: the product's exact operating point - 0.5B-3B Q4 GGUF, one user, batch 1, an 8 GB Windows CPU laptop - is almost unmeasured in the literature. The rigorous systems work is at 7B and above on GPUs, where throughput is batching-limited; the edge work uses different hardware and power budgets. What transfers is physics and method: decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, quality belongs to a specific quantization artifact and prompt path, small models fail compositionally, and small unpaired benchmarks cannot detect the effects that matter.\n\nMost of the review's fifteen ranked ideas already have register entries: `IDEA-873`/`905`/`940`/`954` cover prompt and KV caching; `IDEA-903`/`1002` cover memory-fit preflight and blocking load plans; `IDEA-924`/`968` cover loopback protection; `IDEA-884` covers offline mode; `IDEA-907` covers quantization policy; `IDEA-870`/`998` cover retrieval and reranking; `IDEA-939` covers conversation summarization; `IDEA-904`/`953` cover draft-model speculation; `IDEA-923`/`954` cover context limits; and `IDEA-860`/`871`/`1015` cover tools and agents. This dossier therefore extracts only the ideas that are not already represented, or whose mechanism is materially different from the existing entry.\n\nThree gaps remain. First, the benchmark gate records per-prompt results but reduces them to point estimates, so it has low statistical power and cannot separate a model regression from parser or prompt drift. Second, privacy and grounding claims have no evidence surface: there is no network ledger, no claim ladder, no citation-support check, and no semantic validation for schema-valid but wrong output. Third, several evidence-backed small-model features are missing: editable fact-table memory, n-gram self-speculation (blocked on runtime support), and a benchmark-gated model-promotion path.\n\n### Coverage map: the review's fifteen ideas\n\n| Review idea | Register coverage |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Per-item logging, `suiteFingerprint`, paired statistics | `IDEA-1037` (new), with `IDEA-1038` (new run manifest); extends `IDEA-1000` and `IDEA-1017` |\n| 2. Memory-budget fit card and fail-closed policy | `IDEA-903`, `IDEA-1002` |\n| 3. Prompt/KV cache with cache-aware ordering | `IDEA-873`, `IDEA-905`, `IDEA-940`, `IDEA-954` |\n| 4. Loopback security baseline and LAN opt-in | `IDEA-924`, `IDEA-968` |\n| 5. Claim hierarchy, Offline Mode, network ledger | `IDEA-1039` (new); extends `IDEA-884` |\n| 6. Extractive citations, abstention, verification UI | `IDEA-1040` (new); composes with `IDEA-870` and `IDEA-998` |","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"6bf85168-1db9-4d90-99ee-94c6f0586c15","timestamp":"2026-10-30T15:09:47.302