{"parentUuid":"7dc1ffe9-55e3-43b2-8720-2e35ffd7c74a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"b4c33e6c-c70d-4e0d-847f-0a7096f64eb3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013nSgghkyB4eGj4mRi9JKnG","type":"tool_result","content":"# ModelDuck benchmark\n\nModelDuck has two benchmark gates. They answer two different questions:\n\n1. **Model reload phase** — which model file and loading configuration should we\n   use? It varies model family, quantization, KV cache type, context length,\n   batch size, CPU threads, thread pool, sequences, flash attention, mmap, GPU\n   layers and context checkpoints.\n2. **Model usage phase** — once a model configuration is fixed, which sampling\n   and prompting setup gives the best answers? It varies temperature, top-k,\n   top-p, min-p, repeat penalty, repeated user prompts, reasoning directives,\n   system prompts and JSON-schema structured output.\n\n```bash\nnpm run bench:model -- --download   # first run: fetch missing models\nnpm run bench:chat\nnpm run bench:update                # deliberately replace both baselines\nnpm run bench:report\nnpm run bench:link                  # after commit: run once per pending phase\n```\n\nThe suite runs through the same `LlamaEngine` the UI uses. It starts with\nSmolLM2 135M, Qwen2.5 0.5B and Qwen2.5 Coder 1.5B, and adds Qwen2.5 1.5B,\nLlama 3.2 3B and Phi-3.5 Mini plus Q4/Q5/Q8 variants for the reload phase.\nThe fourteen tasks cover exact instruction following, arithmetic, factual\nrecall, reasoning, multiple-choice HellaSwag/MMLU-style questions, a GSM8K-style\nword problem, JSON shape, JavaScript parsing, lowercase formatting, list\ncardinality, reading comprehension, long-context recall and bug repair.\n\nQuality is the percentage of deterministic checks passed. Speed is the median\ngenerated tokens per second. `runtimeMs` is the whole variant's wall-clock time.\nThe model phase runs one-factor-at-a-time on representative models; the chat\nphase runs generation-only variants on the model configuration chosen by the\nfirst phase. Temperature is 0 for the model phase and 42-seed sampling for the\nchat phase.\n\n## Test identity\n\n`benchmarks/suite.mjs` exports `SUITE_VERSION`. Every result also carries a\nSHA-256 `testHash` over the prompts, checks, global and per-variant system\nprompts, `promptIds` and effective matrix defaults. A baseline with a different\nhash is not comparable: the runner refuses to claim a quality change until the\ntest changelog is reviewed and the baseline is deliberately updated. A different\nnode-llama-cpp version or manifest version is refused the same way.\nSee [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md).\n\nEvery model result records its GGUF chat-template hash, and every prompt result\nstores the generation manifest digest, prompt digest and truncated-history\ndigest. That makes a result traceable to the exact system prompt, history,\ntemplate and sampling settings the model saw, independently from the test hash.\nA baseline with a different model file size or chat-template hash is refused\nbefore the run starts; `--update-baseline` is the deliberate override.\n\n## Unsupported by node-llama-cpp 3.22.2\n\nThese are recorded in every result's `capabilities.unsupported` field:\n\n- physical batch size (`n_ubatch` is not exposed separately from `batchSize`)\n- unified KV cache (`kv_unified` is not exposed)\n- RoPE frequency base and scale\n\nROCm is not a node-llama-cpp backend. AMD GPUs use Vulkan; NVIDIA uses CUDA;\nApple uses Metal. A backend build may require the vendor toolchain.\n\n## History database\n\nEvery run writes:\n\n- `benchmarks/history.jsonl` — tracked, append-only run/link events and the\n  durable record that can rebuild the database.\n- `benchmarks/history.sqlite` — ignored local DB with `runs`, `model_results`,\n  `prompt_results` and `commits` tables; `phase`, `test_hash` and every variant\n  setting are queryable.\n\nA run made before its commit is pending. `npm run bench:link` attaches it to the\nnew HEAD; `npm run bench:report` shows quality, speed, runtime and the\n`v<suite>:<hash>` identity per commit. Node 22+ is required for `node:sqlite`;\nthe app itself remains Node 20+. The app's **Bench** button reads the two\nbaseline files and this JSONL history to show the same per-test percentages,\nruntime and linked commit without a terminal.\n  501 benchmarks/history.mjs\n   99 benchmarks/link.mjs\n  316 benchmarks/matrix.mjs\n  243 benchmarks/report.mjs\n  713 benchmarks/run.mjs\n  312 benchmarks/suite.mjs\n   75 benchmarks/test-hash.mjs\n  250 benchmarks/verify-manifest.mjs\n 2509 total\nlatest.json\n\nThe test definitions are versioned separately from the application and from the\nmodel settings. Every run records `suiteVersion` and a SHA-256 `testHash` of the\nprompts, checks and matrix entries. If those definitions change, the hash changes\nand the baseline must be updated deliberately; a result delta is then visibly a\ntest change, not a model/settings regression.\n\n| Version | Date | Change |\n|---|---|---|\n| v1 | 2026-10-30 | Initial 9 tasks: exact word, arithmetic, factual, reasoning, JSON, JavaScript, lowercase, list, needle. |\n| v2 | 2026-10-30 | Added HellaSwag-style choice, MMLU-style choice, GSM8K-style word problem, reading comprehension and bug-fix tasks. |\n| v3 | 2026-10-30 | Split into model-reload and chat-usage phases; added quantization, KV cache, context/batch/thread/sequence/flash/mmap/checkpoint variants and sampling/system/reasoning/prompt-repeat/structured-output variants. |\n| v4 | 2026-10-30 | Hash the global and per-variant system prompts, `promptIds`, effective sampling defaults and the generation-manifest version. Every run also records node-llama-cpp/Node versions, model chat-template hashes and a per-generation manifest digest; a runtime, model-file size or chat-template mismatch is refused like a test-hash mismatch. |\n\nWhen adding or changing a prompt, check or matrix variant:\n\n1. Bump `SUITE_VERSION` in `benchmarks/suite.mjs`.\n2. Add a row to this table.\n3. Run both phases with `--update-baseline` deliberately.\n4. Do not claim a quality gain until the new baseline and test hash are committed.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5f1a62da-0889-4d24-acb8-21a31911e7ba","timestamp":"2026-10-30T15:24:41.149Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# ModelDuck benchmark\n\nModelDuck has two benchmark gates. They answer two different questions:\n\n1. **Model reload phase** — which model file and loading configuration should we\n   use? It varies model family, quantization, KV cache type, context length,\n   batch size, CPU threads, thread pool, sequences, flash attention, mmap, GPU\n   layers and context checkpoints.\n2. **Model usage phase** — once a model configuration is fixed, which sampling\n   and prompting setup gives the best answers? It varies temperature, top-k,\n   top-p, min-p, repeat penalty, repeated user prompts, reasoning directives,\n   system prompts and JSON-schema structured output.\n\n```bash\nnpm run bench:model -- --download   # first run: fetch missing models\nnpm run bench:chat\nnpm run bench:update                # deliberately replace both baselines\nnpm run bench:report\nnpm run bench:link                  # after commit: run once per pending phase\n```\n\nThe suite runs through the same `LlamaEngine` the UI uses. It starts with\nSmolLM2 135M, Qwen2.5 0.5B and Qwen2.5 Coder 1.5B, and adds Qwen2.5 1.5B,\nLlama 3.2 3B and Phi-3.5 Mini plus Q4/Q5/Q8 variants for the reload phase.\nThe fourteen tasks cover exact instruction following, arithmetic, factual\nrecall, reasoning, multiple-choice HellaSwag/MMLU-style questions, a GSM8K-style\nword problem, JSON shape, JavaScript parsing, lowercase formatting, list\ncardinality, reading comprehension, long-context recall and bug repair.\n\nQuality is the percentage of deterministic checks passed. Speed is the median\ngenerated tokens per second. `runtimeMs` is the whole variant's wall-clock time.\nThe model phase runs one-factor-at-a-time on representative models; the chat\nphase runs generation-only variants on the model configuration chosen by the\nfirst phase. Temperature is 0 for the model phase and 42-seed sampling for the\nchat phase.\n\n## Test identity\n\n`benchmarks/suite.mjs` exports `SUITE_VERSION`. Every result also carries a\nSHA-256 `testHash` over the prompts, checks, global and per-variant system\nprompts, `promptIds` and effective matrix defaults. A baseline with a different\nhash is not comparable: the runner refuses to claim a quality change until the\ntest changelog is reviewed and the baseline is deliberately updated. A different\nnode-llama-cpp version or manifest version is refused the same way.\nSee [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md).\n\nEvery model result records its GGUF chat-template hash, and every prompt result\nstores the generation manifest digest, prompt digest and truncated-history\ndigest. That makes a result traceable to the exact system prompt, history,\ntemplate and sampling settings the model saw, independently from the test hash.\nA baseline with a different model file size or chat-template hash is refused\nbefore the run starts; `--update-baseline` is the deliberate override.\n\n## Unsupported by node-llama-cpp 3.22.2\n\nThese are recorded in every result's `capabilities.unsupported` field:\n\n- physical batch size (`n_ubatch` is not exposed separately from `batchSize`)\n- unified KV cache (`kv_unified` is not exposed)\n- RoPE frequency base and scale\n\nROCm is not a node-llama-cpp backend. AMD GPUs use Vulkan; NVIDIA uses CUDA;\nApple uses Metal. A backend build may require the vendor toolchain.\n\n## History database\n\nEvery run writes:\n\n- `benchmarks/history.jsonl` — tracked, append-only run/link events and the\n  durable record that can rebuild the database.\n- `benchmarks/history.sqlite` — ignored local DB with `runs`, `model_results`,\n  `prompt_results` and `commits` tables; `phase`, `test_hash` and every variant\n  setting are queryable.\n\nA run made before its commit is pending. `npm run bench:link` attaches it to the\nnew HEAD; `npm run bench:report` shows quality, speed, runtime and the\n`v<suite>:<hash>` identity per commit. Node 22+ is required for `node:sqlite`;\nthe app itself remains Node 20+. The app's **Bench** button reads the two\nbaseline files and this JSONL history to show the same per-test percentages,\nruntime and linked commit without a terminal.\n  501 benchmarks/history.mjs\n   99 benchmarks/link.mjs\n  316 benchmarks/matrix.mjs\n  243 benchmarks/report.mjs\n  713 benchmarks/run.mjs\n  312 benchmarks/suite.mjs\n   75 benchmarks/test-hash.mjs\n  250 benchmarks/verify-manifest.mjs\n 2509 total\nlatest.json\n\nThe test definitions are versioned separately from the application and from the\nmodel settings. Every run records `suiteVersion` and a SHA-256 `testHash` of the\nprompts, checks and matrix entries. If those definitions change, the hash changes\nand the baseline must be updated deliberately; a result delta is then visibly a\ntest change, not a model/settings regression.\n\n| Version | Date | Change |\n|---|---|---|\n| v1 | 2026-10-30 | Initial 9 tasks: exact word, arithmetic, factual, reasoning, JSON, JavaScript, lowercase, list, needle. |\n| v2 | 2026-10-30 | Added HellaSwag-style choice, MMLU-style choice, GSM8K-style word problem, reading comprehension and bug-fix tasks. |\n| v3 | 2026-10-30 | Split into model-reload and chat-usage phases; added quantization, KV cache, context/batch/thread/sequence/flash/mmap/checkpoint variants and sampling/system/reasoning/prompt-repeat/structured-output variants. |\n| v4 | 2026-10-30 | Hash the global and per-variant system prompts, `promptIds`, effective sampling defaults and the generation-manifest version. Every run also records node-llama-cpp/Node versions, model chat-template hashes and a per-generation manifest digest; a runtime, model-file size or chat-template mismatch is refused like a test-hash mismatch. |\n\nWhen adding or changing a prompt, check or matrix variant:\n\n1. Bump `SUITE_VERSION` in `benchmarks/suite.mjs`.\n2. Add a row to this table.\n3. Run both phases with `--update-baseline` deliberately.\n4. Do not claim a quality gain until the new baseline and test hash are committed.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ae9f203f-6681-4c85-bd73-fd9b061197e1","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/c/Users/Yunus-Emre/Desktop/portable_agent_kit/projects/modelduck","root":"/c/Users/Yunus-Emre/Desktop/portable_agent_kit/projects/modelduck","branch":"master","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":16,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":5},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/AIKit","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/NeuroCore","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Research-Engine","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Aegean-Licensing","visibility":"public"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Aegean-Licensing-Ops","visibility":"private"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"AegeanWorks/Merklock","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/c/Users/Yunus-Emre/Desktop/portable_agent_kit/projects/modelduck","platform":"windows"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7dc1ffe9-55e3-43b2-8720-2e35ffd7c74a","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"C:\\Users\\Yunus-Emre\\Desktop\\portable_agent_kit\\projects\\modelduck","sessionId":"05aa96e5-db7a-4aed-bad4-f0f3cebd2787","version":"2.1.312","gitBranch":"main","slug":"hidden-watching-bengio"}
